their side of the house, you have to choose a thematic theme, right? If you want to learn about space, right? But if you later decide to learn about, I don\'t know, political science, that\'s gonna be a lot worse on their side of the house. Why? Because you have spent some years getting educated in one field and then you have to switch, right?','other',131.5,149.3),(1017,39,5416,5418,'Why do they do this? A, because they are kids, their neofrontal cortex is not developed enough just simply by biology. But second, also because they might be lazy, they might have distractions around them, they might have like, I don\'t know, there\'s this girl in class that I really like and I wanna go in her class and whatnot because I have a crush on her, but also because they wanna, I don\'t know, spend time outside, they can be lazy and whatnot.','other',149.5,173.5),(1018,39,5419,5420,'But thirdly, they\'re also influenceable by other people, specifically by parents. And I think this is worse on their side of the house.','other',174,181.2),(1019,39,5421,5424,'Why or in what ways are they influenceable by parents? I think A, there are certain norms in societies about certain professions that are more successful than others because they are paid more. So for example, you\'re told, oh, be a doctor, be a lawyer, do this so you can end up in school. This might lead a kid to choose a subject that they don\'t really like. That\'s bad.','other',181.5,203.4),(1020,39,5425,5428,'On the comparative, on our side of the house, what happens is that if you really, like you study other subjects, right? You study math, chemistry, physics, but you also study literature. And if you decide at 18, at 18 you have more autonomy to actually go against that parent pressure. This is crucial, because the kid who actually wants to go to art school is more likely to go to art school on their side of the house and be more happy in the long term, even if they don\'t receive like a lawyer salary.','other',203.6,229.5),(1021,39,5429,5432,'But also, secondly, there\'s also a lot of pressure from parents to like, be self-sufficient, right? To leave the house, to like earn your salary, to not depend on them anymore financially, because they will grow old or whatnot. And in some countries even to support them financially later on, right? This is bad because probably this will lead them to make decisions influenced by their parents on their side of the house.','other',230,252),(1022,39,5433,5434,'On our side of the house, sure, maybe they will have math and chemistry too, but they can still take that literature course. And even if they don\'t ultimately pursue that literature study, at least their time in high school is better off because they get one hour to talk about Shakespeare, whereas on their side of the house there\'s no such time, meaning that their educational experience overall is bigger in quality.','other',252.2,272.8),(1023,39,5435,5435,'Cool, then why are progressive education worse?','other',273.2,278),(1024,39,5436,5436,'First, because of the choice you make in the student psychology, as I said.','other',278.3,281.4),(1025,39,5437,5441,'Secondly, I think the lack of supervision, I think the quality of your education gets worse the moment that the teacher only facilitates, right? So you do peer learning, if somebody does a PowerPoint, right, with things they googled about Iraq, versus somebody who does a, no thank you, who does a teacher who does a presentation about Iraq that has actually studied that, obviously you\'re better off because they have more deeper knowledge. Closing? You can engage. So if I don\'t wanna do that. And lastly, I think you have more quality education because the teacher now has the ability to give you lectures and to study, to have studied that subject in detail versus your peers probably are not well educated in that topic.','other',281.8,333.8),(1026,39,5442,5442,'Okay, wait, why is this the most important thing in the debate?','other',334,338.7),(1027,39,5443,5446,'First, it\'s about how you learn, right? So if you get more information, and if you get more clear information, earlier in your life, it\'s good because you\'re at the age where you can orient yourself, right, so it\'s easier to make choices once you have more information, right? That\'s the time where you have the ability to explore all of these options on our side of the house. Secondly, note that this is a secure impact. Why?','other',338.8,362.9),(1028,39,5447,5450,'Because it\'s about other things in life later on, like uni admissions. Probably, if you don\'t study math, it\'s very difficult to take a uni exam in math because you\'re doing experimental learning for four years in something like literature, for instance, right? So you have more capacity to go to uni. This impacts you passing your exam, but also access to a career later on, social mobility, and the things that I explained are important to you.','other',363.2,387.5),(1029,39,5451,5451,'I think that my speech, it reminded me that it\'s important to accept things about your choice, and with that, I would like to welcome the leader of opposition, here. Thank you. Thank you.','other',390.1,401.3),(1030,39,5452,5452,'Do you want to follow up or something? It\'s fine.','other',415.1,417.9),(1031,41,5543,5547,'I want you to think of the first time you had to explain to your teammate in a debate about an IR case you didn\'t know about, about some random country you first heard about and how the economy over there is going or how the people over there are going. And you\'re going to quickly see that that\'s probably how people are going to go in these classes and try to explain to their peers how things are going about subjects they don\'t know about in a way that they don\'t necessarily care about because it might be within their area of specialization but they don\'t care about each case study, especially if it\'s assigned to them by the teacher rather than they be able to choose some things, particularly they be able to choose, in physics I want to focus on how a spaceship works or I want to focus on how the rainbow works. If their teacher says, you only have these two choices and you\'re not that passionate about either of them, then you choose one and you have to figure out how it works and you have to explain it. That\'s kind of how this works.','other',944.5,993.7),(1032,41,5548,5548,'The problem is a couple of things with this.','other',993.9,997),(1033,41,5549,5549,'So I\'m going to give you four clear reasons as to why the way I want to explain to you is better for these kids to learn, then I\'m going to weigh it off and then I\'m going to clarify whatever I think is cool.','other',997.1,1006.3),(1034,41,5550,5550,'One, I think because it\'s less confusing, it\'s better.','other',1006.7,1009.3),(1035,41,5551,5553,'I think the idea of compartmentalizing things by topic rather than having a holistic approach is better for you to be able to understand particular things about that topic. So when I say this thing, like me being in this room works like this because my body physically works like this and then there\'s the emotional side of my psychology and how it affects me and then there\'s the part about finances and how I was able to get here and everything. That\'s a lot of information, especially for a kid to process.','other',1009.7,1039),(1036,41,5554,5558,'They say holistic is good because then they learn to have engaging stuff and so on. But what you don\'t engage with is that then they need to be able to understand general rules that take from all of these things and apply them to different cases as well. When they learn the human body works like this and they say this is the biology part because this is the component in the body from there and then this is the chemistry reaction happens in your brain because that\'s the part and then this is the physics of it because your brain is, I don\'t know, heavy or something. All of these things, they need to be able to then compartmentalize that they have general rules. I think this is difficult for a kid to do even if it may be desirable that they do it because they need to have example A, example B, example C and they go on with all the letters of the alphabet until they understand that that\'s not just an isolated case but that\'s an area of expertise that that is a general rule that applies.','other',1039.3,1090.3),(1037,41,5559,5560,'And yes, the teacher might say it is, but no, there\'s less time for the teachers to be able to themselves be able to explain these things, to be able to themselves be able to make the kids understand how these rules work and that they apply in multiple cases. So I think over time, they might get their impact but it\'s about how short term and closer when you first explain these things, the kids are able to get it.','other',1090.5,1111.1),(1038,41,5561,5561,'Why is this important?','other',1111.3,1112.1),(1039,41,5562,5564,'One, because of how they are able to understand the things and be interested in them. If I don\'t understand something from the get go, especially kids with a shorter attention span and that\'s what I characterize that might have different interests as well, they are likely to disengage with that topic and not want to learn further on, right? It has compounding impacts in the longer term if you don\'t understand from the get go.','other',1112.6,1134.4),(1040,41,5565,5568,'Two, because you might be able to disengage with the topic as a whole. So you might say, I don\'t like maths because of the way that maths was explained to me in this particular example of the spaceship because it was a too complicated equation for me to get. And instead of being open to the idea of learning general simpler rules of maths, you are close to the idea just because that particular topic didn\'t interest you. I think this is more likely in kids because again, as what I characterize, they are maybe impulsive, they don\'t compartmentalize things as well because they don\'t have abstract thinking, et cetera.','other',1134.5,1163.9),(1041,41,5569,5576,'Not on the comparative, you\'re likely to. .. still be able to engage with simple, basic things first. When you start a general thing, you don\'t have to engage with the level of complexity of the weird example of the rainbows if the law of physics in there is too complicated. You can start with the basic. Here is how gravity works. It\'s m times g. It\'s one simple, small equation. And that might be easier for kids to process.','other',1164.2,1187.3),(1042,41,5577,5578,'Second thing, I think depth and clarity is important. And this is all about us mechanization and why teachers do this well.','other',1187.7,1193.7),(1043,41,5579,5582,'They say, oh, but there\'s good teachers, bad teachers. That\'s a strong man response. When a case points to you, it\'s about how much the teachers are able to explain and how less reliance you have on the students explaining these things when they don\'t have the same capacity comparatively to be able to explain it well or don\'t have the same passion comparatively to explain it to their peers. That creates a little trickle-down impact that what I was talking about.','other',1193.8,1218.6),(1044,41,5583,5583,'One, it\'s about how your exams are going to go, right?','other',1218.8,1221.5),(1045,41,5584,5589,'Because if you understand things somewhat comparatively less, you might have a worse upgrade. And margins matter in university choices. Margins matter a lot, particularly in high school choices and how you rank students against each other, right? And yes, it\'s so standardized. We should be able to see globally how the average is and figure out if you are better off in a university in this country or you are just below the threshold and you won\'t get there. If you don\'t have the good margins, you\'re not there.','other',1221.6,1247.8),(1046,41,5590,5590,'Two, I think it\'s about the stress levels of the kids.','other',1248.1,1250.3),(1047,41,5591,5595,'All the one that talks to you about social norms is not just symmetric, but it\'s about how the kids are able to then deal with that, right? It\'s not just about the influence of choices themselves, but it\'s about the kids knowing, I feel certain enough that the way I\'m handling the subject is good, that I understand what\'s going on and that I will pass my exams. So it\'s a continuous impact throughout their school journey, but it\'s also a continuous impact in the long term when they are facing the exam. Do they feel like they have just isolated examples or do they actually understand the equation because that is what will be asked of them? And they say, sure, they will teach it on their side as well.','other',1250.6,1284.7),(1048,41,5596,5597,'The comparative is how much time do you spend on teaching the standardized things that will be asked of you in the exam and how certain are you that the way it was explained to you was clear because it was a teacher and not your colleague who didn\'t give a shit about it when they explained it to you and so you are confused about whether or not it works this way or that way and they had a muddy explanation, closing, opening.','other',1285,1306.1),(1049,41,5598,5598,'Yes, sure, if we could just start from the basic stuff as if you obviously don\'t give children theory of relativity to learn about, we start with basic math concepts you apply to the homework.','poi',1308.7,1316.3),(1050,41,5599,5600,'Okay, cool, but maybe you don\'t. That\'s not comparative.','other',1317.5,1321),(1051,41,5601,5601,'First thing, no, fourth thing why you should care about this more, I think standardized education makes it relatable and I think this is important.','other',1321.7,1329.9),(1052,41,5602,5603,'Instead of having isolated cases of physics that you talk about with a student from another school that has a different teacher or with your parent as well went to the same education system, I think it\'s easier for you to be able to help each other with homework or to just understand that you\'re going through the same thing like the tests are somewhat similar because you use similar textbooks that go through the same things, right? These things are comparatively more relatable and that brings enjoyment and maybe capacity to build more friendships and also just have better connected learning outside of school as well which tends to happen plenty when you have to in high school because there\'s pressure from your parents to be able to do well and to go to university.','other',1330.2,1367),(1053,41,5603,5603,'These things are comparatively more relatable and that brings enjoyment and maybe capacity to build more friendships and also just have better connected learning outside of school as well which tends to happen plenty when you have to in high school because there\'s pressure from your parents to be able to do well and to go to university.','other',1349.7,1367),(1054,41,5604,5604,'Why is all that we\'ve told you most important in the debate?','other',1367.4,1370.4),(1055,41,5605,5608,'I think one, because it\'s prior to any happiness in your life later on. This ties to your career choices and you feeling like you are showing yourself. This ties to the margins of you getting into university. This ties into the margins of you being able to win stuff later. Go team.','other',1370.4,1386.2),(1056,43,5728,5730,'I think conveniently, opposition just tries to ignore that this is a motion to prefer worlds. We\'re talking about secondary education systems that are going to change. But their size, we\'re working through the status quo of universities.','other',1921.8,1931.2),(1057,43,5731,5733,'My company government quite clearly tells you how they\'re going to impact better the students and how it\'s likely for the system to make them better just because of being a generalist. If they\'re going to tell me that it\'s going to be interesting for them, they\'re going to take the lack of it. If you have a world in which they have agency, they have to accept this.','other',1931.7,1945.5),(1058,43,5734,5736,'It\'s going to take a long time. If their case is well understanding of the topics, it\'s going to be a matter of taking more time to explain it, having more resources in which you\'re trying to get them to understand it. And furthermore, we have a lack of opportunities to generalize some topics that are likely going to be tested in the future.','other',1945.6,1958.5),(1059,43,5737,5737,'Secondly, I\'m going to be really clear on my structure here for my extension.','other',1958.9,1961.5),(1060,43,5738,5742,'I think inherently in this debate, there\'s a clear principle. We\'re talking about the education. It\'s a mandatory thing the system must ensure for every one of their citizens. We believe government doesn\'t actively tackle on why this is important and how a system that actually changes this for a experimental system, firstly, clearly ignores the inequality that exists around the world for educational systems. But secondly, how do we believe they have to ensure basic needs for all their necessities?','other',1962,1984.9),(1061,43,5743,5743,'I\'ll take a few ideas from the whole thing whenever you want to take it for engagement.','other',1985.2,1988),(1062,43,5744,5744,'Let\'s start with the learning structure first.','other',1988.3,1990.6),(1063,43,5745,5746,'I think education veils the potential of every kid that passes through these doors. We have an obligation as users to not rob the inequality path.','other',1990.9,1997.4),(1064,43,5747,5749,'Firstly, what\'s the learning structure like and what do we think is important? We refer to this as a progressive overload of content. I\'m giving you every single thing along the way that you\'re able to get the tools to do whatever you want with this content and this knowledge.','other',1997.7,2007.9),(1065,43,5750,5753,'And specifically, this looks like I\'m giving you algebra in first grade or secondary school. And then I\'m giving you calculus at last because I know you need the building steps to do it. What happens on the other side is this progressive overload of content A looks extremely different because we\'re focusing on them learning, not memorizing. And we believe when we don\'t tackle why memorizing inherently, even though we think it may be bad, is what gets you a job, is what gets you a university, every type of impact that we can claim on government or opposition about the kid comes from where they get after secondary school.','other',2008.1,2036.9),(1066,43,5754,5755,'If I ensure that\'s better, I think we\'re going on that path. But then secondly, I think they accept specialized schools who are generalized.','other',2037.1,2044.2),(1067,43,5756,5756,'So basically, these are characterizations.','other',2044.3,2045.4),(1068,43,5757,5759,'I\'m going to give the kid everything they want to give, like geography, history, even though you know that you want to go into peer math. So why does progressive fall in structure? Three main points.','other',2045.7,2054.2),(1069,43,5760,5765,'I think firstly, there\'s a sidelining of the teacher. This is an extension to the mechanism I hope to assess. We believe sidelining a teacher is not like taking them out of the classroom. We believe inherently from the motion, when you\'re giving them a facilitator role, they lack the academic empowerment they get when they\'re the ones giving knowledge to the kids, to the students. When there\'s a professor that teaches me something I don\'t know, in my brain, I recognize them as someone powerful or someone that I can get as a role. And specifically this, it makes it easy or accessible the way that it\'s structured to get more learning in the person.','other',2054.3,2084.5),(1070,43,5766,5767,'But then secondly, I think there\'s more diversified in the curriculum. Inherently, when you give students the possibility or agency to change the style of school or what they\'re learning, there\'s more priority in which classes they can take.','other',2084.6,2094.6),(1071,43,5768,5769,'This is exactly what I mean by not allowing them to get all the basic needs, for example, the basic requirements for university. Give me a break.','other',2094.8,2101.7),(1072,43,5770,5772,'So if there\'s more variety, there\'s also just a greater chance that they\'re going to cover the stuff that they\'re going to get tested on in university, directly. It is pretty square off from opposition to try and claim that they\'re going to have all the experience on the field and also the knowledge from my side. If that\'s the case, there\'s no agency in opposition.','other',2102.1,2116.3),(1073,43,5773,5775,'We have to understand that there\'s a possibility of them actually learning something the way they want it to. They\'re lacking certain spaces, like my OG said quite clearly, in which they\'re going to keep these other classes. If we are specific in the way that we give classes to the kids, we lack the agency to teach them the other things they don\'t need or are quite non-obligatory as they try to mitigate, but also important if they want to change careers.','other',2116.8,2137.8),(1074,43,5776,5778,'But then thirdly, I think the interconnected of topics that I agree with relates to this. I think the lack of agency for the kids is extremely bad. So it works, the lack of agency is quite important to have, firstly, for the specialised education, secondly, because a well understood other topic is memorised and I think that\'s important, I think I already explained it.','other',2138.1,2153.2),(1075,43,5779,5780,'Let\'s go over the idea of a principle. We think education is a mandatory obligation.','other',2153.4,2156.8),(1076,43,5781,5784,'We think here in society, we value that the government pays self-education for their citizens, because it\'s the only mechanism in which we can scale, not only economically, but socially. I think, fairly, one, it has to be available to everyone, that\'s what we have in public education, but then secondly, it has to meet the basic needs of every one of its citizens that passes through it. This is a mechanism that unless someone only goes to obligatory education, every single one of us is going to school. We need a system that works and not impairs people by the opportunities it has.','other',2157.8,2183.1),(1077,43,5785,5787,'There is a clear piece of characterisation that none of the open cameras have touched, as specifically, what we believe in education is unequal, and how does your side make it more unequal. We think, firstly, we\'re not going to tell you that education on my side is available completely at the same level for everyone, but we believe in the worst case scenario when we have public education, and it\'s just a basic curriculum the government allows or gives the professors to follow, is meeting the basic needs of something that may be needed in the future, like math, like science, and being a generalist, like social sciences and geography and history.','other',2183.1,2213.9),(1078,43,5788,5791,'If I decide to change careers, if I decide to not be the thing I wanted when I was 15, like for example, I\'m an engineer, I wanted to be a medic, this system allows me and gives me the tools to do so. In their side, when I\'m getting a specialist, I\'m going to have to learn better, I\'m going to be way better at understanding certain mathematical concepts, but I\'m going to be lacking the agency to switch from that. When we don\'t allow people to actually move and decipher themselves in an academic setting, but more so in the future, we\'re failing them as a government. We think it\'s way better on our side than how?','other',2214.2,2240.6),(1079,43,5792,5794,'Firstly, because we don\'t have the problem of accessibility, we think even in the worst case scenario where resources are lacking, and we don\'t have the opportunities to give every single student a great textbook, we\'re allowing them to steal the opportunities of a teacher that has experience, a curriculum that has worked for 35 plus years, and specifically, and the clear-cut case that makes the government win against the position for now, is that we allow them to fit into university, because that\'s not the system that they likely are going to change. In both sides, university still sucks, and I still get an exam that decides my future.','other',2240.6,2266.8),(1080,43,5795,5795,'Closing out, thank you, if you have anything.','other',2267,2268.1),(1081,43,5796,5796,'Okay, let\'s do this as a comparative, this is not a concession, this is an exercise of thought.','other',2268.1,2289.8),(1082,43,5797,5799,'Let\'s assume that exercise of them being more capable of understanding deep scientific textbooks or knowledge, because they\'ve spent more time doing so. I still believe my side is better, when I\'m allowing more quantity of people to have more abilities to have just the basic needs met, and not having them to go to a different opportunity or a different system, or having to learn by myself all the things that I need in the system. Just because of quantity of people helped, and just because of my side, the education needs and obligatory, mandatory, let\'s say, things that the government has to do, are being met, I\'m really proud to propose.','other',2289.9,2321.1),(1083,45,5894,5895,'I know it\'s just broad extension, but I really want to be clear about how your position has completely ignored my prioritization. Even though it\'s kind of dirty, I still believe it\'s not new material, and I do believe they have to engage in this.','other',2879.7,2890.8),(1084,45,5896,5896,'Education is inherently not accessible for everyone.','other',2891.1,2893.1),(1085,45,5897,5899,'This is a statement that I did. I have opportunities for them to do the FUI, and they are able to do so now. I want to explain how this is the angular stone in which my case lands, and even the worst case scenario where my principle is founded on the practical, and you buy on my OGS extension, I think I\'ll have a second.','other',2893.1,2907),(1086,45,5900,5903,'Let\'s explain, firstly, the accessibility principle, and how do we believe that inherently, when we have this all amazing experimental learning, you\'re not allowing everyone to get the same. I think, firstly, there\'s a clear understanding that when people don\'t have the economic resources, they\'re dependent on the system. So specifically, lower class families and kids, in which they have less opportunities to complement their kids\' education, as they get into an amazing travel to the CERN to learn about nuclear fission, and how maybe another person in the same society could be able to, is an obstacle they\'re not able to get across that already exists on my side. What happens in the comparative is that on my side, I\'m able to give them a basic system that\'s at least going to give them the basic tools to get out from society.','other',2907.4,2947.9),(1087,45,5903,5905,'What happens in the comparative is that on my side, I\'m able to give them a basic system that\'s at least going to give them the basic tools to get out from society. In my worst case scenario, in which families don\'t have the resources, they\'re still able to get better. Then, when we show the basic needs, is that we\'re actually allowing the principle to happen.','other',2940.3,2957.1),(1088,45,5906,5907,'We believe the principle, as I said in my session, is a mandatory obligation of the system. When we allow it, is that we believe it\'s inherently good.','other',2957.4,2963.7),(1089,45,5908,5908,'I\'ll take it now.','other',2963.9,2964.4),(1090,45,5909,5909,'Both resources are.','other',2965.3,2967.1),(1091,45,5910,5910,'.. Same exercise as before.','other',2983.6,2984.6),(1092,45,5911,5914,'Let\'s assume that people are better at learning, and let\'s say they\'re better at understanding complex topics, and they have amazing teachers, as well as trying to get there. That first is expensive. Not everyone is accessible to that. That\'s the main point of my case.','other',2984.8,2995.5),(1093,45,5915,5917,'Understanding that not everyone has the same opportunities. But then, secondly, that in this case scenario, when we\'re allowing them to be specific, we\'re not allowing them to be better on the future. Their case is specifically dependent on how are we going to get better education, completely oversized, what the next step is going to be, as well as closing up position.','other',2995.5,3012.7),(1094,45,5918,5921,'In which the university, even if reform is available, the university is still not going to change. If we\'re going to allow people to be better and be smart, it\'s not going to be a system up for everyone. We\'re going to have people better prepared, getting into all these universities, and not allowing for other people to get these kinds of opportunities. We\'re hindering people on their side of the case.','other',3013,3027.9),(1095,45,5922,5922,'Now let\'s take a quick look at the comparative and how I may be able to do the case.','other',3028.2,3031),(1096,45,5923,5926,'I think with closing up position, closing up position tries to lay about how reform may be possible, and how inherently we\'re going to get a better education thanks to this. I believe, firstly, a better education is dependent on how it\'s going to affect. What\'s the value of this education? If we explain that this education, like it\'s not going to help the person get a better job, or get a better development in society, it\'s inherently not doing anything for us.','other',3031.2,3051.8),(1097,45,5927,5928,'But on my side, if I\'m sitting a kid in the geography class, but at least he\'s there and he\'s following a system, it\'s following something better in their future, even if the teacher sucks. Notice that in the worst case scenario, where the education sucks, I\'m still debating the closing up position idea of getting better education, getting better resources, better teachers, better opportunities.','other',3051.8,3069.9),(1098,45,5929,5929,'Let\'s take my opening, and I\'ll take your POI after.','other',3070.2,3072.8),(1099,45,5930,5931,'I think, first of all, my opening, it\'s the same principle again. They try to say that things are still changing, that things are still obligatory, as a mitigation to the opportunities that people may have, and that inherently there are still going to be things that they\'re going to be doing.','other',3073.2,3085),(1100,45,5932,5933,'I believe that the deafness is the specific mechanism that differentiates these two models. In my side, I\'m going to be able to sit down with students, I give them two classes a week for a year about geography.','other',3085.4,3095),(1101,45,5934,5937,'In their side, I\'m not going to be able to, or if I do, it\'s going to be twice or triple the amount of time. The compromise of time and the deafness of topics is different in both educational systems. In my side, I\'m able to at least have an understanding in the basic levels, even in my worst case, memorizing, where at least the content is there, and they\'re able to recall it if necessary. But on their side, even when they\'re better, they\'re not able to do all the things that I still believe are necessary as a government to give their kids an education system.','other',3095.4,3121.6),(1102,45,5938,5938,'I\'ll take your POI closing. Sorry.','other',3122.3,3124),(1103,45,5939,5939,'Why students love memorizing.','poi',3124.8,3126.5),(1104,45,5940,5942,'They don\'t love memorizing. Your cause of charge is to explain to me why even if I\'m memorizing, even if it\'s words, I\'m telling you it\'s going to be better for the future. I don\'t care if this gets better development, if it does not directly mean that everyone is getting more opportunities at university.','other',3127,3141.8),(1105,45,5943,5945,'In my side, it still allows, even for our system that is archaic, it\'s a system that\'s archaic after secondary school. It\'s the same for your side. You\'re not explaining how the reform is also going to affect the university.','other',3142,3150.9),(1106,45,5946,5947,'Understanding that, with my opening, I think in my opening, it clearly characterized the main things of learning structure. I\'m filling in voids that I feel are important, specifically the interconnected topics and how inherent it does not allow for a better understanding of it.','other',3151.3,3163.7),(1107,45,5948,5949,'But I believe the principle stands. If the principle is somehow filled by a bad analysis, I believe that we may explain their case a little bit better.','other',3163.9,3171.2),(1108,45,5950,5950,'For all their reasons, I\'m sorry, I\'m proud to propose.','other',3171.4,3174.2),(1109,48,6111,6113,'Explaining two simple things in opening opposition. First, why leadership is better. Secondly, why the world in which these student leaders engage outside the democratic system is far worse.','other',434.5,480.8),(1110,48,6114,6114,'I\'m going to prove how you get instability that OJ thinks is so important as well as better policies.','other',480.8,484.8),(1111,48,6115,6115,'So firstly, why don\'t we get better leadership within the system?','other',484.8,487.8),(1112,48,6116,6119,'So we agree with the premise of opening government that these student leaders are likely to win because they\'re all uniquely inspiring. Many people supported the movement in the first place in order to overcome the dictatorship, indicating a large effect of support. The current leader of the provincial government, Mohamed Younis, is likely to back these individuals as well. They\'re just generally relatable to youth and uniquely inspiring because they represented the movement that gave them democracy, that represented the fall of the dictatorship that oppressed so many individuals.','other',487.8,511.5),(1113,48,6120,6121,'Why is this so good? I have a couple of reasons why people make better leaders that are uniquely able to represent the youth and make their voices important in the political sphere in the first place.','other',511.5,519.7),(1114,48,6122,6123,'And the premise here directly challenges what we get in an opening government that says there will be incentives to represent the youth on either side of the house. If we prove that youth otherwise would be politically disengaged, this means there\'s less political incentives when we cater to them because you don\'t see them as viable voters to the upper house in the first place.','other',519.7,534.6),(1115,48,6124,6124,'Why is this the case?','other',534.6,535.6),(1116,48,6125,6127,'Firstly, youth deeply distrust the establishment not only due to decades of oppression under a dictatorship or a pseudo-dictatorship and a legacy of corruption associated with other parties like the BNP as well, but also that youth is generally associated with the state itself as a source of oppression, but also that structurally youth often have incentives not to engage in rational ignorance where you believe that your vote doesn\'t matter and you have less incentives therefore to be politically engaged. It\'s not worth it to put in the efforts to vote and to try and learn about political parties if you don\'t think your vote is going to matter in the first place.','other',535.6,564.2),(1117,48,6128,6130,'And even when you do consume media, it often feeds you negative narratives about, for example, why democracy is a sham, why horrible things in your country are happening. It\'s not worth voting because media often sensationalizes the worst things that happen because it\'s the most eye-catching. Which means youth that often disproportionately, who appeal to these things, often have no incentives to want to engage politically otherwise.','other',564.2,581.7),(1118,48,6131,6132,'So it\'s uniquely only the leaders that are presented in transition to democracy that you are going to fight to be passed or that make you engage in the political system and have some degree of trust to want to vote in the first place. On their side, they have no doubt or challenge.','other',581.7,592.4),(1119,48,6133,6133,'The second reason why youth are going to be disproportionately not politically represented is that they are economically weak as well.','other',592.4,597.6),(1120,48,6134,6135,'For A, this is just likely because youth are quite young, they have less time to accumulate wealth in the long term which gives you political and economic leverage. This time itself hurts.','other',597.6,607),(1121,48,6136,6138,'But secondly, Bangladesh is currently going through an economic transition where they are going to be relatively poor, to a more middle and upper income country which has resulted in large amounts of unemployment in the short term. Because as the country becomes richer, as wages rise, it means that as a country gets to transition, many people are left behind and left unemployed. This disproportionately harms the youth because their lack of experience and time means they are not likely to be, for example, high skilled and middle manager type people and more likely to be workers which has left many of them economically in the dust.','other',607,634.5),(1122,48,6139,6141,'This means that they have less, for example, lobbying power but also less economic power to be engaged in the system, to be able to take time off to vote as well and to do things like attend political rallies. Especially in the context of a post-revolution society where a lot of their will to engage and their will to protest has been exhausted as well. This means that in the absence of these individuals, they get no representation for the youth.','other',634.5,655.2),(1123,48,6142,6142,'Furthermore, I want to explain why youth leaders themselves are likely to be incredibly good.','other',655.2,660),(1124,48,6143,6143,'This is because there is likely to be a selection effect where the youth leaders are likely to be people that have strong liberal democratic values to the extent that they were willing to fight and potentially be incarcerated or die for these values in an oppressive regime in the first place which suggests a deep ideological attachment to things like democracy and the liberalization of society.','other',660,677.1),(1125,48,6144,6144,'What is the impact of all of this?','other',677.1,678.9),(1126,48,6145,6147,'I think it\'s first and foremost good that we have good democratic liberal values against corruption and pro-things like meritocracy. But also on a political policy level, it\'s good that we have demands to cater to people that have long term interests, i.e. the youth that are going to live for much longer than people who are not the youth.','other',678.9,693.4),(1127,48,6148,6149,'This is important because this demands for example a transition away from textiles and other forms of low skilled industry which is bad in the long term even if it is desirable for politicians in the short term. Why are these things bad in the long term?','other',693.4,705.2),(1128,48,6150,6153,'Things like for example textiles or other forms of manufacturing often are unsustainable as Bangladesh becomes more economically wealthy. As incomes rise, exports become less competitive because you have higher costs of inputs. This is bad because it\'s hard to outcompete other industries. But also things like automation and the rise of automation would otherwise force these countries like Bangladesh to pretty much really de-industrialize as well.','other',705.2,725.5),(1129,48,6154,6154,'Why is it the case that alternatives are bad and are going to care for these short term interests?','other',725.5,729),(1130,48,6155,6155,'First, let\'s begin by characterizing who these alternatives are.','other',729,731.6),(1131,48,6156,6157,'Firstly, it\'s likely to be for example the BNP, an old establishment party which is likely to have the resources and the legitimacy to succeed by being an opposition party to the Obama league. And this is a problem because they have a long history of corruption and institutionalized corruption which is likely to continue because as a leader of a party that is engaged in corruption and tax economics, they adapt to have large amounts of wealth as a source.','other',731.6,753),(1132,48,6158,6162,'But also B, are likely to be politically short termists as well. They\'re likely to have connections with, because as an old establishment party, you have large connections with industry, for example manufacturing or textiles, which is often the tipping point for you catering to their interests. Not only because they have personal connections, but because they\'re self-competing on alternative incentives. So for example, many other workers in society are somewhat short termists, going to keep their job in the short term because it\'s bad for the country in the long term. So these two aspects of the Bangladesh are often the tipping points for alternative parties like the BNP to overvalue the short term and prioritize things like textiles as opposed to adapting and investing in better long term economic plans.','other',753,786.4),(1133,48,6163,6164,'Secondly, another alternative is likely to be Islamist parties as well because Bangladesh has a large Islamist population, or Muslim population, which is likely to be incredibly oppressive towards Hindu minorities. This is likely to be the case because Islamist parties have used this as a primary means of differentiation as a political party against other parties, which is likely to lead to a more extremist type of rhetoric and policy stance.','other',786.4,808),(1134,48,6165,6165,'Secondly, I want to explain why it is worse for these leaders to engage outside the political system before that political movement.','other',808,814),(1135,48,6166,6166,'UNIS has been a powerful democratizing and anti-corruption force, even just in August, and over time has seen it with the cause of momentum, which can be channeled to and strengthen existing political institutions, which young people now, and not in the past, have desired.','other',814,827.9),(1136,48,6167,6168,'Right, but the problem is it\'s associated with these student leaders alongside UNIS, who are seen as a front for pro-democracy movements when they don\'t run. They\'re also likely to do things like be critical of the otherwise democratic system, where the upper parties and the counter-factionals are going to exploit now.','other',827.9,842.4),(1137,48,6169,6170,'In the short run, we think in a world where these people do not run, it leads to more instability, where, for example, these student leaders are more likely to do things like stop protests, because they believe these other, alternative parties are bad and anti-democratic, which they\'re also likely to be, which we proved in our previous point. So they do things like protest on the streets, even if it\'s not all of you, it\'s likely to be the leaders and the close followers who now are not choosing to run but still have the original passion and desire to be engaged.','other',842.4,863.4),(1138,48,6171,6171,'This is a problem because these types of protests, firstly, lead to things like democratic backsliding and the stress of the government that OG thinks is so harmful, but also creates instability itself, which leads to things like investor flights.','other',863.4,872.9),(1139,48,6172,6173,'Finally, I think the overwhelming dominance of the student party that would run on our side is good in terms of avoiding gridlock that occurs more when you have other plurality of parties on their steeping-hoes that are likely to have an overwhelming majority, and gridlock itself is often inherently bad when you have one, for example, like parties that have an incentive to sabotage the main party in a coalition because they want them to succeed less, they can win in the next electoral cycle, which is really harmful because you don\'t pass basic infrastructure bills that the big banks of the nation need in a closed public society.','other',872.9,898.1),(1140,48,6174,6174,'Thank you very much.','other',898.1,933.1),(1141,50,6271,6272,'First, I want to claim out the BN claim on the likelihood of a coup. It\'s extremely unlikely you\'d get a military coup.','other',1506.7,1517.2),(1142,50,6273,6277,'One, the military lacks funding. FDI is declining. The military cares about funding because democratic institutions actually favor the military. When parties compete against each other on the potential of a democracy, they are also likely to compete for the military\'s favor, which is why they are likely to give the military funding. However, this depends on having access to foreign investment.','other',1517.2,1532),(1143,50,6278,6281,'Second, the youth movement is a substantial unifying force. And the military doesn\'t want to go against the entire unifying force of student leaders and the young youth movement. That\'s why the military chief, Wako Uzuman, gave concessions to the transitional government under U.S. This is why the youth movement are specifically less likely to be anti-military after this point, meaning the military struggles to challenge the youth movement.','other',1532,1550.1),(1144,50,6282,6283,'In contrast, the military probably has substantial capacity to challenge the BNP that doesn\'t have the same degree of legitimacy that the youth movement has. As a consequence, a military coup is, if anything, more likely on their side.','other',1550.1,1559.6),(1145,50,6284,6288,'Third, there\'s likely to be Western leverage. Bangladesh is in the middle of a $4. 7 billion IMF playoff. And the biggest market for its goods is the EU, which is looking for a trade deal. Because in 2029, the EU\'s favorable trade conditions are set to expire, meaning Bangladesh can\'t export to them.','other',1559.6,1573),(1146,50,6289,6292,'However, the West will not use its leverage against a coup if the BNP is in power. This is because Kamala Zia\'s administration was extremely unpopular among the West, precisely for engaging in the same kinds of democratic backsliding as Sheikh Zainab. So a military coup might actually be favorable for China or the West in the presence of the BNP, but not in the presence of a transitional government. So, if anything, a military coup is more likely on their side.','other',1573,1593.5),(1147,50,6293,6294,'No, for this flip to work, we don\'t have to prove that a military coup is more likely than not. The risk of a military coup is a massive detrimental impact to Bangladesh, because investors are terrified of military governments, and they pull out capital, meaning Bangladesh struggles to transition its economy in expected value terms that is larger than any of the other impacts of the opening government.','other',1593.5,1610.4),(1148,50,6295,6295,'Let\'s talk about the plausible alternatives in this debate, beginning with Kamala Zia\'s BNP.','other',1610.4,1614.3),(1149,50,6296,6298,'The first point I want to make is the BNP will not face political competition in the short run. So I think Zain agrees the BNP will indeed be in power, but critically, it will not be held accountable. It will not face political competition, and there\'s also a structural risk of youth leaders re-entering politics, because of how the BNP will establish itself as having an overwhelming advantage in politics.','other',1614.3,1631.7),(1150,50,6299,6301,'Reason one, they have a leg up in terms of funding. They have name recognition and existing stockpiles of funding, plus large amounts of corruption from being in opposition for a while. This funding is critical to finance electoral campaigns.','other',1631.7,1641.9),(1151,50,6302,6306,'Here\'s the thing. Youth leaders have a unique moment of legitimacy right now after the July revolution, but people\'s memories of revolution are short-lived. The reason why is Bangladesh is facing an economic transition. It is at the risk of a foreign exchange crisis, which is why it is experiencing a bailout from the IMF. Youth unemployment is in record highs.','other',1641.9,1659.1),(1152,50,6307,6309,'What does this mean? It means when the BNP has substantial amounts of funding, youth leaders, if they re-enter politics in a future moment, when people experience economic crisis and don\'t have needed memories of the July revolution, so they don\'t just look at a youth leader and automatically think they\'re good, funding is extremely important in elections. Funding is also important because voting is hard in this economic situation.','other',1659.1,1678.4),(1153,50,6310,6314,'Liz\'s argument was not just young people aren\'t motivated to vote. It\'s that they don\'t have the capacity to vote. Because if youth unemployment is high, you don\'t have the time to take time off work in order to stand in line to vote. This is ours and ours, and the BNP probably doesn\'t want to encourage young voters, precisely because, as Ryan\'s argument concedes, it is scared of them. So the BNP is lucky to make voting specifically hard, the same tactics that the Awami League engaged in.','other',1678.4,1699.4),(1154,50,6315,6317,'But the youth aren\'t terrified to engage in a second protest. The protests already led to massive humanitarian losses. It was literally gunning down protesters.','other',1699.4,1705.8),(1155,50,6318,6320,'Even if it led to success at the end, the youth aren\'t tired of continuing to engage in future protests. So there isn\'t a substantial risk of a protest. What this means is, young people will struggle to turn up to vote, and the BNP\'s political power is likely to be entrenched, because when it is in power, it will continue gaining access to funding by being in power and by being the incumbent party, meaning future political competition is unlikely to threaten it.','other',1705.8,1725.8),(1156,50,6321,6321,'No, thank you.','other',1725.8,1726.5),(1157,50,6322,6323,'Third, the BNP\'s existing voter block is much less fractured. Historically, people who are worried about Indian infrastructure in Bangladesh\'s politics are people with more of an Islamist name have sided with the BNP, and their voter block is unlikely to fracture in the future, meaning other political parties will simply be absorbed by the power of the BNP.','other',1726.5,1741.9),(1158,50,6324,6325,'As a consequence, the BNP is unlikely to face large political competition, and in their best case, the coup does not happen. Why does this matter?','other',1741.9,1748.5),(1159,50,6326,6328,'Because the BNP is, first, because at least all the PM\'s arguments on political competition are wrong. Political competition is symmetric. But second, then you listen to the BNP\'s arguments on young people and why the BNP will cater to them.','other',1748.5,1759.1),(1160,50,6329,6329,'First, the BNP will not cater to them.','other',1759.1,1761.2),(1161,50,6330,6333,'What are their arguments here? One, they say youth voters are energized for the protests. This is an assertion. I\'ve demonstrated the substantial exhaustion among youth voters after the protests, combined with complacency about Bangladeshi politics because the specific actions of the Awami League, for example, reservations for military members from the 1971 revolution are gone.','other',1761.2,1777.8),(1162,50,6334,6335,'There is a combination of complacency and exhaustion, which means youth voters aren\'t energized and aren\'t as likely to turn up. Their second mechanism is security organizers have credibility, and they have incentives to tell them to turn up and vote.','other',1777.8,1788.5),(1163,50,6336,6338,'Here\'s the problem. Youth leaders are likely to back the BNP. Why is this likely to be true in the absence of them coming into positions of power?','other',1788.5,1795.4),(1164,50,6339,6340,'Because the BNP has a unique narrative. Kavita Zia was arrested by Sheikh Hasina, which means they\'re able to use the rhetoric of saying, we are the BNP, the party that was persecuted by the Awami League.','other',1795.4,1805),(1165,50,6341,6343,'This power gives a substantial amount of influence among young voters. So the initial involvement and endorsement of security organizers is for the BNP, plus the BNP has power, so it has the ability to do things like pay the pop, especially in this environment that\'s often what they want. They are likely to continue spreading their values or encouraging young people to turn up, plus unexplained why young people don\'t have the capacity to, even if they want to turn up, which is why youth turn up in Bangladesh continues to be low.','other',1805,1827.4),(1166,50,6344,6345,'Third, they see there\'s a risk of coming out of retirement. And it\'s clear why in the long run, which is the potential time period that they will come out of retirement, one, they have damaged their credibility because they have made a promise to exit politics, and two, they will not be seen in the same way because memories will be short-lived.','other',1827.4,1840.5),(1167,50,6346,6347,'All of this means there will not be a meaningful challenge to the power of the BNP. The BNP will crush political competition.','other',1840.5,1846.2),(1168,50,6348,6349,'Here\'s the thing. Youth leaders will be better than the BNP.','other',1846.2,1849.3),(1169,50,6350,6350,'Their argument is youth leaders will be totally insulated from political competition so why would they give a fuck? Here\'s why.','other',1849.3,1854.4),(1170,50,6351,6352,'Because youth leaders aren\'t a monolithic group. The fact that even some youth leaders are potentially ideological faces substantial amounts of pressure, no thank you, to ensure that basically youth leaders push for democratization and similar norms. Closing? You haven\'t? Yeah.','other',1854.4,1867.1),(1171,50,6353,6354,'Your own argument on why these leaders are non-monolithic is the precise reason why there\'s likely to remain political competition on our side given that different factions of the organization usually come back to their parties, including the Islamist parties described in the Halal. No, but I\'ve just given you a bunch of reasons why the BNP will beat all these potential parties.','other',1867.3,1884.2),(1172,50,6355,6355,'In a first-class proposal system, strategic voting means always back a single party.','other',1884.2,1888),(1173,50,6356,6358,'This and the BNP will act as a selling point. Thus, they will use Brexit to get them in even if they have differing views. However, if they are in politics, even if they are a temporary monolith, if one of them starts engaging in democratic backsliding, other youth leaders can turn against them and form parties of their own which can drive political competition which can help foundation.','other',1888,1903.6),(1174,50,6359,6361,'Here\'s the argument that they totally missed from Liz\'s speech. The BNP has strong economic connections with existing textile industries. Textile industries have backed them for a substantial amount of time, so they depend on them for their resources.','other',1903.6,1915.5),(1175,50,6362,6363,'They owe them favors. They have family members appointed to top positions in a bunch of textile industries that didn\'t want the Iwami League to win.','other',1915.5,1922.3),(1176,52,6444,6445,'Firstly on the future political system of Bangladesh. Secondly on stability and why the BNP would be bad for it as well as incredibly bad governments.','other',2456.2,2506.3),(1177,52,6446,6450,'Let\'s start with the future political system of Bangladesh. Because we think the biggest of the fault in this debate isn\'t about who wins government the next time. Because what\'s happening at the moment is we have an interim government and before we go to another general election, they\'re going to have an election before that to form the special assembly to rewrite the Bangladeshi constitution and reform the government institutions of that country. And the most important thing in this debate is who has power in that to form the long term constitution of Bangladesh, what the formation of government is going to look like. And that very intuitively outweighs the rest of the debate.','other',2506.3,2535.4),(1178,52,6451,6451,'So they\'re talking about all of the government structures that inform every single election into the future.','other',2535.4,2539.7),(1179,52,6452,6455,'What are the problems with Bangladesh\'s political system at the moment? First of all, the massive rigging of elections. What was supposed to happen is that the government prior to an election would give up power to a caretaker government such that they weren\'t in power during that election so that they couldn\'t rig it. The Awami League refused to do that and that\'s why the BNP boycotted almost every election in the last decade.','other',2539.7,2558),(1180,52,6456,6457,'So, secondly, there\'s an enormous amount of corruption in Bangladesh\'s government. That\'s to say there\'s an enormous amount of embezzlement, an enormous amount of people who are appointed bureaucrats who continue to preserve their own image.','other',2558,2568.5),(1181,52,6458,6461,'There\'s also an enormous amount of judicial problems. Judicial actors, members of the judiciary essentially act in line with political parties. All of them were supposed to mass resign in protest because they were trying to bring back Shere Khasana after she had fled to India. There\'s an enormous amount of problems that need to be solved in this constitutional assembly.','other',2568.5,2584.1),(1182,52,6462,6462,'And currently, the interim government has established a large number of commissions under Mohammed Hassan and also with the involvement of the student leaders to reform these policies.','other',2584.1,2593.4),(1183,52,6463,6463,'Why is it dead?','other',2593.4,2594.5),(1184,52,6464,6464,'Yeah, Yunus has a notable primacy.','poi',2594.5,2596.7),(1185,52,6465,6468,'Do you think that the international actors are likely to run and rotate within or are you averse to this trend? Hassan is in general running the next election. We believe he\'s turning a caretaker, which is what he did in the last caretaker election about 20 years ago. He\'s very explicitly a caretaker.','other',2596.7,2609.4),(1186,52,6469,6469,'He\'s not here to be a long-term political actor in Bangladesh.','other',2609.4,2612.1),(1187,52,6470,6471,'So, why is it that it is necessary to have these people involved in the constitutional assembly? First of all, the existing forces in politics benefited from each of these systems.','other',2612.1,2622.2),(1188,52,6472,6477,'All of their allies, they were put in place by the existing political system. So they\'re incredibly unlikely to want to change it. But secondly, without a force, without a specifically large institutional force against this, it\'s likely that any smaller forces that OG talks about, like individuals or small groups of two or three running, would be able to get this change through. They\'re likely to be bought off or horse-traded by the larger parties. They\'re less likely to have an agreement on what this reform should look like. So even if they all agree there should be reform, if they don\'t agree on it, they\'re on net and are going to get outvoted.','other',2622.2,2649),(1189,52,6478,6480,'And also, they weren\'t involved in the creation of it. The student leaders got Mohammed Hassan in. They served in roles with those commissions developing it.','other',2649,2656.2),(1190,52,6481,6481,'So, they are the ones who are involved in the development and can get it passed through.','other',2656.2,2660.1),(1191,52,6482,6483,'Why is it that they can only get this done if they are a party? First of all, being a part of a party is necessary for branding and selling your candidate.','other',2660.1,2666.7),(1192,52,6484,6485,'And secondly, elections are big business. You need operatives, you need money, you need ads, you need institutional capacity.','other',2666.7,2671.6),(1193,52,6486,6487,'You can\'t do this with two or three individuals. The next thing I want to prove is that other institutional power in Bangladesh is very, very rare.','other',2671.6,2678.5),(1194,52,6488,6489,'Because they can always go to other institutions where you might support. But there\'s good structural reasons to believe there aren\'t a lot of other large institutions in Bangladesh that can form themselves into political parties.','other',2678.5,2689.4),(1195,52,6490,6493,'First of all, it\'s an authoritarian country. Secondly, the Oman, which meant that they were going to shut down any alternative institutional power base. Secondly, the Omani League tried to position itself as a big tent party that could include anyone in. So, they had an incentive to try and bring in any other institutions and align themselves with the Omani League.','other',2689.4,2706.1),(1196,52,6494,6495,'And thirdly, Bangladesh has been in chaos for many years. The initial protest against the quota system started in 2018.','other',2706.1,2710.7),(1197,52,6496,6497,'And in that context of chaos, it\'s very difficult to form institutional power. So, that means that there aren\'t a lot of other large institutions that could step into this gap.','other',2710.7,2718.6),(1198,52,6498,6506,'The best comparison then is the BNP or other smaller parties. Open government always says, well, students are seen as bad, they\'re seen as irrational, they\'re seen as too young. But the problem with this is that this party isn\'t just going to run students. They\'re going to bring other people in. For instance, they brought in Hassan in the short run. Also, they brought in a large number of university professors who actually designed the original system of reform. They worked with their own professors. And military leaders, as Open Opposition points out, military leaders who told the military to put down their arms, not to oppose this. And those people have almost nothing in common, except for the fact that they were brought together by an anti-discrimination student movement.','other',2718.6,2752),(1199,52,6507,6509,'That is why they\'re likely to be taken seriously. And they\'re also robots closing government, because, like, well, they\'re incredibly terrible. But they brought in a large amount of support.','other',2752,2760),(1200,52,6510,6514,'The key thing is that they are important for bringing those people together. But I also want to note here, not only is it important that they\'re part of the Constitutional Assembly to solve all those problems, but it\'s also important that they are the first government afterwards. Because as I allude to, the entire Supreme Court of Bangladesh was forced to resign because they were seen as trying to bring back Sheikh Hasani. That means that they\'re going to get to appoint the next set of justices who will make the first interpretations of the new Constitution that will serve as the baseline precedent against which that Constitution is interpreted. And that\'s incredibly important.','other',2760,2787.9),(1201,52,6515,6517,'They also set the norms of how it\'s interpreted. And if another party wins, they have incentive to show that the new rules that they don\'t like are very bad and very difficult in order to manufacture consent to get them taken away. It\'s very important that they are not only involved in the right to do it, but that they are the first party to interpret it and to appoint the justices who do so.','other',2787.9,2805.6),(1202,52,6518,6523,'What are the benefits of this? It means that you get a government that functions, which is important for your economics. It also means you get rid of the quota system, which is not only un-democratic, but also incredibly bad for the functioning of government, because you appoint a large number of freedom fighters to the system, not because they\'re good. And it makes, and crucially, it makes the Bangladesh Public Service not politically neutral, because the freedom fighters that are appointed have political allegiances. And it means you don\'t have a neutral public service. That pretty much weighs pretty intuitively over the rest of the debate. Second extension.','other',2805.6,2833.3),(1203,52,6524,6524,'Why is it that you get more stability?','other',2833.3,2835.9),(1204,52,6525,6527,'Opening government says you get the military coup. Opening office pretty astutely points out that the military can explicitly allow a moment to come through. You\'re very unlikely to get the military coup.','other',2835.9,2845),(1205,52,6528,6529,'Why is it that the BNP would be incredibly bad at this ability? First of all, their leader is not somebody who can legitimately run, he\'s been charged with anti-corruption, and different courts have split over his actual legitimacy as a candidate.','other',2845,2857),(1206,52,6530,6531,'So, that means that they\'re incredibly likely to be protested. Secondly, there\'s very likely to be hindered protests, where this aggressively pro-Muslim party wins, who have done terrible things to women and minorities in the past.','other',2857,2867.6),(1207,52,6532,6532,'And thirdly, your relationship with India is likely to be substantially worse, and they\'re likely to foment unrest on the ground.','other',2867.6,2873.2),(1208,52,6533,6534,'We already saw Indian forces in the country protecting Sheikh Hasana on her side during the last revolution, and they have an intention to foment chaos in the country in order to re-establish their influence. And I know these aren\'t the only reasons why you don\'t get stability.','other',2873.2,2886.4),(1209,52,6535,6538,'There are also reasons why the BNP is bad. Like, they are anti-Hindu, we should care about Hindu. They are anti-India, we should care about your relationship with India, your trade relationship, your security relationship. They have done corruption, which is not only that you can\'t legitimately run, but also, you know, corruption is bad.','other',2886.4,2899.8),(1210,52,6539,6540,'Additionally, I think it\'s important that these student leaders are there to be able to tamp down protests. Open Opposition says, well, student leaders might do protest, but I think that\'s the lower impact argument, because their protests are naturally pretty good and pretty reasonable.','other',2899.8,2912.1),(1211,52,6541,6544,'What\'s more important is the bad, more aggressive sets of protests that might come from other actors, which we think they\'re uniquely good at tamping down and stopping the Indian violence. First of all, people are going to feel lied to if these people don\'t follow through on their promises. Secondly, they are the powerful figures who can come out persuasively and urge people to tamp down. But thirdly, the protests are more willing to negotiate with them when they\'re in government, because they have a history of being receptive to protest, rather than turning to violence because they feel like the government will never listen, because protests will never work.','other',2912.1,2939.8),(1212,52,6545,6545,'That was good.','other',2939.8,2984.9),(1213,54,6634,6635,'Firstly, closing government, opening government, the way in which closing government and opening opposition will be interpreted in their news. Firstly, closing government, we now ask two questions.','other',3493.8,3541.8),(1214,54,6636,6637,'Firstly, will the youth leaders win elections? And secondly, what policies are they going to implement when they leave the government?','other',3541.8,3546.8),(1215,54,6638,6638,'Firstly, will the youth leaders win elections?','other',3546.8,3548.7),(1216,54,6639,6641,'The first reason why I think this is pretty obvious, is that they have already proven themselves to be highly effective political organisers. This seems dismissive about the amount of effort they\'ve applied, because the protests have been placed for a long period of time, and involved more than just organising people to vote on the streets, but organising highly complex networks of people to operate around an infrastructure system that cracked down on those students, and then after that, being able to negotiate the process of establishing a relatively democratic transitional government in order to manage the process of moving towards democracy, and establishing a series of missions in order to deal with and analyse the problems that existed in the previous system, which could suggest to me that these protests are efficient in organising, are likely to be good in winning elections.','other',3548.7,3585.7),(1217,54,6642,6644,'Secondly, we think that the BNP themselves have lost a lot of institutional knowledge about winning elections, and think that while they are probably likely to be effective, they are unlikely to be unbeatable. That is, firstly, because they\'ve been disempowered by the army, they\'ve been in long-term opposition for decades, which suggests they haven\'t had that much infrastructure around winning elections. But secondly and additionally, they\'ve boycotted elections for a long period of time, which means that infrastructure around elections is likely to be lost.','other',3585.7,3607.3),(1218,54,6645,6645,'And I\'d like to sort of deny this opening opposition, we agree with opening opposition that in the counterfactual, without students, it\'s likely that this BNP is going to win the election without student opposition.','other',3607.3,3616.4),(1219,54,6646,6647,'What Matt says is not that that is never going to happen, but that if it did happen, it would be bad because the BNP is a mortal party. Thirdly, their unpopular policies are outweighed by a series of very popular figures that are firstly coming from outside the establishment, because although it\'s true that people have some skepticism of students and whether or not they\'ll be anti-democratic, they know for certain that the BNP has been corrupt.','other',3616.4,3635.7),(1220,54,6648,6649,'It\'s led by a person who is currently being charged with money laundering. It has previously, or has been previously, fought out for corruption, which guarantees them some amount of leverage over them.','other',3635.7,3644.4),(1221,54,6650,6650,'Secondly, the people in Bangladesh want democracy.','other',3644.4,3646.5),(1222,54,6651,6652,'Living in a dictatorship sucks. Having at least one party that is working hard towards democracy is a meaningful draw for people, even if they have a series of unpopular policies.','other',3646.5,3655),(1223,54,6653,6654,'Thirdly, the students aren\'t a super racialised religious party, which is a characteristic of a BNP that has alienated a lot of people in Bangladesh who see that racialisation is something that causes division, escalates tensions in the region of India, and is detrimental towards their likelihood of getting trade and economic development in the future. Fourthly and finally, they\'re not children.','other',3655,3673.5),(1224,54,6655,6656,'These are university students as well, pretty old university students, who have been politically organising for a long time, who are experienced in some level of politics at the youth level, where these people are likely to be participating. But Susan Wang, so he says it won\'t work.','other',3673.5,3686.5),(1225,54,6656,6658,'But Susan Wang, so he says it won\'t work. This is obviously going to be a hard thing to achieve. But now is the best time to achieve for any kind in the future.','other',3684.1,3692),(1226,54,6659,6664,'The Awami League is split. The Awami League is on the run, and their leader has left India. They lack a firm opponent towards their leader, someone who can organise them. The students have a lot of momentum at the moment, and the BNP is at a critical point, but it is also unlikely to succeed. For the reasons that I previously explained, it\'s previously explained about lacking the infrastructure around elections for men. So now is the time when it could work, and that\'s when it\'s important.','other',3692,3713.9),(1227,54,6665,6668,'So when you\'re around the likelihood then, first you\'re going to need to prove high likelihood of success. In fact, this is conceded by a Prime Minister, who claims that. They\'ll be so popular and unbeatable, they\'ll have unchecked power, the result of them becoming evil. Which therefore makes the quasi-government a nice, hopeful government as well.','other',3713.9,3728.7),(1228,54,6669,6671,'But secondly, even if they don\'t, it\'s still good for them to have some level of power. Firstly, because it means better accountability on the BNP and the Awami League, if they are the ones who benefit from power. But secondly, it\'s important for building momentum towards future political successes, as you build institutional knowledge around elections, into and towards the future.','other',3728.7,3745.6),(1229,54,6672,6672,'Netcessent talked about whether or not their policies are bad.','other',3745.6,3748),(1230,54,6673,6673,'They outlined that some of these policies might have negative consequences.','other',3748,3750.9),(1231,54,6674,6676,'Firstly, many of the people who they want to remove from power are extremely unpopular. For example, police commissioners in Bangladesh are notorious for being permissive of things like police officers engaging in violence, engaging in rape. They are people who others also want to see removed.','other',3750.9,3763.5),(1232,54,6677,6679,'Secondly, it\'s not necessary to remove these figures in order to remove corruption. A lot of these people are aligned with corrupt political figures from the Awami League, which makes removing them necessary in order to improve the infrastructure that allows corruption to continue. Because even if you change who\'s in power, the BNP comes in power, if you permit the lower levels of corruption to continue to permeate the system, the BNP then becomes the corrupt political party that\'s in power.','other',3763.5,3784.2),(1233,54,6680,6681,'Thirdly, it\'s necessary to get trust in government, to resolve the aforementioned problems, the fact that they are corrupt, and the fact that they are associated with previous members of the Awami League, and the fact that they\'re unpopular. If you want trust in any new government, it needs to remove the infrastructure of the old government, which is unpopular and evil.','other',3784.2,3798),(1234,54,6682,6686,'Fourthly, and most importantly, it\'s important for justice. These people are evil people who deserve punishment. The Student League, Sophie wants to say, is a fucking terrorist organisation. The Awami League\'s youth group should be disseminated and destroyed. They are a group of people who deserve justice to come to them.','other',3798,3811.7),(1235,54,6687,6687,'Could these youth leaders finally then be flexible, even in the instances where these policies are bad?','other',3811.7,3816),(1236,54,6688,6692,'Firstly, they have explicitly, I think in a moment, set up a series of commissions to redesign their policy platform with experts involved. That legitimises changes in refinement of their current policy, not as factors, but as academic refinement on the policies they have established, which makes that change legitimate. But secondly, they\'re likely to believe in that possibility and not be so everyone can use, because they\'re working with a new set of people. They\'re working with people like Hassan, the interim leader of the government, like the figures and academics they have put into these commissions in order to redefine their policy. They have a revealed preference towards redefining and establishing a new political platform that is likely to have positive outcomes, even in the instances where their policies are bad. Fifteen seconds.','other',3816,3854.4),(1237,54,6693,6697,'OTR gave five reasons the DNP is accountable. Students are a newly-entered graduate, and one thing. Why? The DNP raised it before. The DNP must satisfy professors\' demands for colleges and schools of mentoring. They can still operate outside politics, and the DNP needs to prove it has changed from the past.','poi',3854.4,3868.7),(1238,54,6698,6698,'This beats your extension directly. It doesn\'t.','other',3868.7,3871.1),(1239,54,6699,6700,'Both allowing the DNP to come into power at a moment of unique political vacuum and crisis and high instability, with a system of corruption already in place, means that none of those systems of democratic ability exist. They might have potential pressure from young people or from other political parties, but if they rig the next election and after this next election, then they win, they don\'t get any of those better outcomes.','other',3871.1,3890.3),(1240,54,6701,6702,'But if you believe that\'s true, and the DNP should have accountability, they get better accountability when there is more youth representation in parliament. Next to respond to opening government, first they say this is going to empower parties that weaponise scepticism that people have of youth leaders.','other',3890.3,3901.8),(1241,54,6703,6703,'Firstly, directly contradicted by their later claim, which is that student leaders will be so popular they will end up having unchecked power.','other',3901.8,3907.1),(1242,54,6704,6705,'Secondly, these people are likely to be sceptical of anyone protecting the branding of being the consequence of a revolutionary movement. Next they say that student leaders will have too much unchecked power and will become corrupt in the end.','other',3907.1,3916.4),(1243,54,6706,6707,'Firstly, if there is any meaningful degree of scepticism, that explains where the democratic ability on student leaders comes from. Like if you believe that the DNP is likely to be successful or at least push back, like they say, usually that is the pressure that forces them to continue to be democratic because there is someone pointing them out and forcing them to be better.','other',3916.4,3931),(1244,54,6708,6709,'How are they likely to go about gaining their legitimacy? Firstly, doing the things they set out to do, abolishing the quota system, which only Matt explains real harm of.','other',3931,3938.4),(1245,54,6710,6712,'Secondly, gaining the backing and approval of various groups who supported them in the crisis of the revolution, like university academics and the commissions they have set up. Thirdly, maintaining their commitment to democracy into the long term. So if you believe that the DNP is a meaningful opposition, that explains why youth leaders will continue to be given long term gifts everywhere.','other',3938.4,3951.7),(1246,54,6713,6717,'Finally, to prove the DNP wins, that\'s good. The DNP is horrible. Firstly, they are horrible to the communities of minorities. Secondly, they are being led by a corrupt person. Thirdly, they bring anti-unionism, which is likely to escalate political tensions.','other',3951.7,3961),(1247,54,6718,6719,'But also, better competition makes the DNP better. So believe that they are going to bring power to the movement.','other',3961,3965.4),(1248,54,6720,6720,'That\'s a great part of the process.','other',3965.4,3966.4),(1249,54,6720,6720,'That\'s a great part of the process.','other',3965.4,3966.4),(1250,47,6027,6030,'A bit of characterization then. These are liberal university students as organizers who oppose the Hasina\'s authoritarian rule. Specifically, they were opposed to the spoiled system that gave jobs and social benefits to the descendants of people involved in the 1971 liberation war. They took the cause of the street personally, the national military cracked down, and therefore the government has been overthrown ever since.','other',5.8,25.7),(1251,47,6031,6032,'First argument then, democratic consolidation. The goal of this debate is on OG, we want a stable transition to democracy.','other',26,31.9),(1252,47,6033,6037,'Context, Bangladesh is at an extremely enormously pivotal and fragile moment of transition. This is a post-revolutionary state. The military still has huge amounts of influence and large fractions of the military still have ties to the fallen Hasina government. On top of this, Bangladesh is also a relatively impoverished country with high levels of ethnic and religious divisions and tensions. Therefore, there are a lot of instability that\'s currently growing within the region.','other',31.9,54),(1253,47,6038,6040,'What then happens when student organizers directly run for political roles? We say this is quite bad for a couple reasons. One, it actively reduces buy-in to the prospect of a future democratic government, especially the perception of it, which often impacts how it plays out in the long run. Why?','other',54.5,70.7),(1254,47,6041,6043,'Because often you still have religious conservatives and rural populations in Bangladesh. And on top of that, people unsupported through loyalty to the old regime who are deeply skeptical of these people running for office. Why? The specific concern with this crowd of student organizers running is that it alienates a substantial amount of people who fear that these student organizers are not striving for a truly democratic government, but instead, just authoritarianism run by the opposition party of they themselves instead.','other',70.9,97.2),(1255,47,6044,6046,'What is the impact of this mechanism? This often means that there is likely going to be less buy-in for institutions, i.e. for example, people don\'t pay as much taxes.','other',98,105.3),(1256,47,6047,6050,'And that\'s a big problem for Bangladesh right now, by the way. Or second, it is easy for radical opposition parties to characterize student-led parties or governments as radical and violent. If you view these parties as radical and violent, you are more likely to vote for a radical, ultra-nationalist or Islamist party and you often do things like exacerbate ethno-nationalism and politics and you actively bring more instability. Second, this is also bad because it reduces electoral competition.','other',105.5,130.5),(1257,47,6051,6052,'How does this actually happen? Because many people involved in these protests are almost assuredly going to vote for these student leaders when they run.','other',130.9,139.6),(1258,47,6053,6057,'Why is this the case? Because often they have already built cultural personality when organizing these revolutions. They often have a good amount of charisma, which is what allowed them to organically rise to the top of these revolutions in the first place. And there is a track record of their efforts and their success. And, most importantly, these leaders are also likely to run in the same party or same government to actively capitalize on the July Revolution\'s support, which is unprecedentedly large compared to any efforts they have made in the past.','other',139.6,165),(1259,47,6058,6060,'Why is this terrible, then? Because this actively increases the fear of further democratic backsliding. If you are already a person on the fence and skeptical of the revolution, when there is last-line support for these figures that cannot really be challenged in any meaningful way because the Aon government and their credibility is down the drain, you fear they become a missusina even if they never end up like that.','other',165.3,186.6),(1260,47,6061,6064,'This is a comparative argument. Why? Because if you counterfactualize, there are still going to be parties that run to appeal to these students of protesters. But there are going to likely be more competing parties and political figures because they cannot all unite under the silver bullet that we took down the Aon government. They all would have to do so much more to differentiate themselves and to make sure they are passing or they are proposing new policies.','other',186.8,210.6),(1261,47,6065,6070,'But also, second, these leaders face less political accountability on their side. The students who led the protests are, while very affable people, no, not actually infallible. Power tends to corrupt even when there are good intentions in some cases. When you give large amounts of power to a small group, they often have selfish incentives to engage in things like ethnic favoritism because you need to do that. You need some level of majority support to make sure that you can stay in government for a bit to pass the policies and ideals you want to pass because you generally believe ideologically these are good policies and you want to help the Bangladeshi people. You also sometimes might engage in corruption because you believe if there is no way out to get funds, besides actively pander to the military in some cases, even if you think that these are all done for good causes, this often leads to democratic backsliding even when intentions are not harmful.','other',211,254.4),(1262,47,6071,6073,'Often I say that no, no, but these leaders are good. We agree, they are probably good, but when they get into power, they oftentimes change. And we argue that change is likely, especially in these circumstances of large amounts of instability.','other',254.7,265.5),(1263,47,6074,6076,'But third, what this means is that leaders cannot be held accountable to the ballot box. And this is no longer that different from the old regime. This is terrible.','other',266.1,273.6),(1264,47,6077,6079,'The counterfactual is we likely think there is a multiplicity of parties and leaders are likely to contest in these elections. Student leaders likely support a variety of parties. Political competition is good. Why?','other',274,284.8),(1265,47,6080,6081,'Because you often do panders to more groups, more minorities, and so it\'s harder for you to alienate them. You often prevent backsliding and they hold each other accountable, which is crucial in a moment of democratic transition.','other',285.1,293.9),(1266,47,6082,6084,'Second argument, before that closing. Yeah, so if the anti-discrimination student movement doesn\'t climb, what other force has the institutional capacity to form a party? Or is it just going to be lots of small, independent groups of two or three, all split up, fighting against each other?','poi',294.5,307.8),(1267,47,6085,6086,'Well, I mean, I think this is a question to be answered in a round. I\'m not exactly sure right now.','other',307.8,312.4),(1268,47,6087,6087,'Brian will do it.','other',312.7,313.7),(1269,47,6088,6089,'Second argument, this reduces immediate political instability. It is not even clear that we will get elections or when we will get elections.','other',313.9,320.7),(1270,47,6090,6091,'So therefore, we need stability to make sure the transitional government works in the interim because these are lives of people at stake. There\'s families to feed and there\'s people we need in the house.','other',321,329.9),(1271,47,6092,6092,'Why does this actively benefit the transitional government then?','other',330.3,332.5),(1272,47,6093,6096,'Because here\'s the comparative. It is far more likely they get a military coup on opposition. The way you should weigh this argument is that even if this happens, like 30% more likely, that is a huge risk we would not wish to take because often that leads to blood on the streets. Often that means that people\'s livelihoods are destroyed and you lose faith in democracy, which ends up being irreversible on their side.','other',332.8,352.2),(1273,47,6097,6098,'The military currently is very powerful in Bangladesh for inclusive reasons. They\'ve always been given huge levels of control of the Bangladeshi economy since the Cold War.','other',352.2,359.7),(1274,47,6099,6101,'They\'re politically influential with the connections to political parties. Problem, the military backs the interim government, but they have qualms with the leaders of protest movements. Why? They do not want the leaders of protest movements involved because the leaders specifically are people and icons and figureheads of the anti-military movement then when they were fighting in the revolution.','other',359.7,376.7),(1275,47,6102,6103,'When student organizers begin running for office, you view democracy as a threat to Germany\'s inequal grasp of power. This is a perception issue on their side.','other',376.7,384),(1276,47,6104,6107,'The counterfactual by no means is perfect on our side, but student protesters are more able to portray democracy as good for the military. We can better ally with the military rather than go directly against it. And even if it\'s not a coup on our side, they still undermine elections. There\'s more violence against protesters and it\'s all on that far worse.','other',384,399.2),(1277,47,6108,6108,'Very proud to be a post.','other',399.2,400.6),(1278,49,6177,6178,'and the protest movements who need to be represented by their lovely figure, Mugabe. We all know how that story ends, hence not well.','other',996.5,1028.6),(1279,49,6179,6181,'The thesis of opening up is straightforward. Power corrupts. The student protesters did a good thing by toppling Hasina\'s regime. The best thing they can now do is to stay on the outside, to criticize, yes, to protest, as opposed to become corrupted by the political establishment.','other',1028.6,1042.6),(1280,49,6182,6185,'Three points in this speech. First, on youth voting. Second, the counterfactual. Finally, democratic consolidation.','other',1042.6,1048.5),(1281,49,6186,6187,'First, on youth establishment. This is opening opposition\'s main point.','other',1048.5,1052.5),(1282,49,6188,6188,'I have four lines of response.','other',1052.5,1053.5),(1283,49,6189,6191,'Response number one, the premise of opening opposition\'s claim is that the youth are otherwise unlikely to engage in politics. That\'s unlikely because student organizers don\'t disappear on our side of the house. They still exist, they still have charisma, they still have cults of personality, and have strong incentives to encourage youth voters to turn out and vote.','other',1053.5,1068.7),(1284,49,6192,6197,'And the reason why this is probably a message that will be received well by many individuals is for all the reasons that opening opposition themselves name. For example, the fact that these leaders have unique power over student protesters. The fact that these people have charisma. The fact that these individuals are seen as having accomplished a revolutionary movement. Which means even if there is a marginal reduction in the youth vote, it\'s not 60%. Rather, you\'re probably getting a high level of youth turnout on either side of the house mitigating opening opposition\'s claim.','other',1068.7,1096),(1285,49,6198,6199,'Response number two, political parties, most obviously the BNP, have strong structural incentives on our side of the house to appeal to our youth voters. And this is for three reasons.','other',1096,1105),(1286,49,6200,6203,'Reason number one, the August protest movement energized youth votes in a way that was not true historically. So opening opposition is right. Historically, young voters in Bangladesh did not participate in democratic politics. But the fact that they\'ve now been energized and mobilized by the most powerful moment in post-independence Bangladeshi history means that youth voters probably feel empowered to participate in democratic politics, even if their leaders do not run.','other',1105,1128.6),(1287,49,6204,6207,'Reason number two, political parties have strong incentives to appeal to our youth voters to deter any potential violence. Hint, what did student protesters do this August when political parties did not represent them? They turned to the streets. They rioted. Foreign investment plummeted, which means even if the BNP is corrupt and selfish, its corrupt and selfish interests align with appealing towards youth voters to ensure there is not a second July revolution.','other',1128.6,1152.4),(1288,49,6208,6209,'Reason number three, on our side of the house, we have durable fiat to assume these leaders do not suddenly form a political party. But if you\'re the BNP, in the next 5, 10, 15 years, you\'re always concerned that if you do not appeal sufficiently well to youth voters, these guys might come out of retirement and actually run against you, which means the theoretical potential that these organizers could potentially run in elections is a powerful preemptive deterrent against neglecting or ignoring the youth vote.','other',1152.4,1178.4),(1289,49,6210,6211,'Response number three to opening opposition, we\'ll flip their claim. Youth distrust will be worse on their side of the house.','other',1178.4,1184.1),(1290,49,6212,6213,'This is a argument that applies to the longer term prospect, not the immediate term. Both teams agree in the short term that people who participate in the protests vote overwhelmingly for these figures.','other',1184.1,1192.8),(1291,49,6214,6217,'But what happens in the long term? Student protesters are likely to feel enormously disillusioned with Bangladeshi politics. Why is that? Because when these people become political operators, when they run parties, when they\'re in government, they have to make decisions in trade-offs that protesters feel violated the principles they pushed for.','other',1192.8,1210.8),(1292,49,6218,6223,'These people have to accept campaign contributions from corporations. They have to engage in political horse trading to overcome the gridlock opening opposition describes. Here\'s the problem. When the person that you saw leading the revolution is now engaging in the very same kind of political shenanigans that you opposed and you fought against, that is when you are much more likely on their side of the house to feel personally attacked. Now, to be clear, on both sides of the house, some political party has to engage in horse trading, campaign contributions, et cetera. It\'s better when that\'s a mainstream political party like the BNP, as opposed to the figureheads of the movement violating their trust, which flips their claim on engagement long term and also increases the probability of protests on the streets.','other',1210.8,1251.7),(1293,49,6224,6224,'This comprehensively flips opening opposition\'s claim and mitigates it to the point they cannot win on this argument.','other',1251.7,1256.9),(1294,49,6225,6226,'Point number two, what is the counterfactual? Opening opposition says that the likely counterfactual is the Bangladesh Nationalist Party. We agree.','other',1256.9,1264.4),(1295,49,6227,6227,'There\'s three reasons.','other',1264.4,1265.1),(1296,49,6228,6229,'First, this party has existing infrastructure to defend the main opposition movement. Since its rise to power in 2009, they\'ve been the main secondary party in Bangladesh since the 80s.','other',1265.1,1274.5),(1297,49,6230,6234,'Reason number two, the AL is overwhelmingly unlikely to win votes in the upcoming election. Why is that? They\'ve been enormously discredited. Their leader literally fled to, I think, India. The point is that the AL is at an all-time low, which means the BNP has enormous capacity to win the election because they have existing infrastructure.','other',1274.5,1290.3),(1298,49,6235,6235,'Reason number three, just empirically, polling shows that the BNP is probably going to be the largest party, whatever the next election happens to be.','other',1290.3,1297.1),(1299,49,6236,6237,'Now, opening opposition\'s claim here is twofold. First, they say that radical Islamist parties gain power.','other',1297.1,1301.9),(1300,49,6238,6239,'Second, they say the BNP is bad. Let\'s work through both.','other',1301.9,1304.1),(1301,49,6240,6246,'Their first claim on radical Islamist parties doesn\'t work for three reasons. Reason number one, there\'s a huge problem with this argument, which is they\'re right. Islamist parties can get some power. But the groups that are voting for Islamist parties would never be the student protesters on our side of the house. The reason why is that the vast majority of people involved in the student protest movement were liberal, democratic, or university students. They are overwhelmingly secular or not highly religious, which means if the organizers of the protests don\'t run for office, then the people who would have voted for them are not suddenly turning to radical Islamist parties because they do not believe that is a legitimate conception of democracy. Therefore, the likely alternative is they move towards secular parties, like, for example, the BNP.','other',1304.1,1342.4),(1302,49,6247,6249,'Response number two, it\'s unlikely that Islamist parties gain substantial power because there\'s a game theoretic disincentive to vote for these parties. The reason why is that you know that the percentage of votes that goes to any individual Islamist party is low, especially because there are multiple of them and they split the vote. So you as a voter have a game theoretic incentive to vote for a secular party that\'s likely to get a greater share of votes because that\'s more likely to affect the outcome of an election. See ya.','other',1342.4,1363.5),(1303,49,6250,6251,'Why are you drawing a distinction between, why are you saying that the BNP is the secular party? They\'re clearly the non-secular party.','poi',1363.5,1370.4),(1304,49,6252,6254,'No, no, no. So just to be clear, the claim I\'m making here is that the radical, fundamentalist, Islamist parties are unlikely to gain power. Our claim is the BNP is preferable.','other',1370.4,1377.8),(1305,49,6255,6256,'This deals nicely with opening opposition. Second claim, they say the BNP is short-termist and corrupt.','other',1377.8,1381.7),(1306,49,6257,6259,'A lot of problems here. Response number one, the BNP can be held accountable on our side of the house with the specific advocacy criticism and protesting of powerful organized movements. The BNP has incentives to listen to these people, even if they\'re not involved in politics, to ensure there is not another wave of revolution.','other',1381.7,1396.7),(1307,49,6260,6261,'Secondarily, the BNP understands that it has to earn the support of these voters because these voters, for all the reasons opening opposition identifies, are distrustful of mainstream parties, and legitimately so. So the BNP, unlike a student organizer, cannot take these votes for granted, which means the BNP has strong incentives to rebrand itself, to internally purge corrupt members of its party.','other',1396.7,1416.3),(1308,49,6262,6263,'But thirdly, and this is critical, we are fine with gridlock. Here\'s why. Because the biggest problem facing Bangladesh is not that you don\'t have policy.','other',1416.3,1423.9),(1309,49,6264,6267,'The biggest problem facing Bangladesh is that an authoritarian movement in power does not have checks on its capacity to legislate and turns Bangladesh into a secondary authoritarian movement. Gridlock is good because it moderates policy. Even if you\'re on Islamist parties, they are counterbalanced by secular parties. All of which means that it is good to have gridlock on their side of the house to increase the probability of backsliding and military violence repercussed. Thank you. All right.','other',1423.9,1489),(1310,51,6371,6372,'We provide a variety of reasons to believe that even if students are uncorruptible, immoral, politically accountable, they will be ineffectual and they will cause the greatest degree of harm in this day. I\'m gonna start by positioning this extension against the government.','other',1978.4,1996.8),(1311,51,6373,6375,'Open government, I think, assumes that students are very likely to win when they explain that young people will be almost automatically behind them. But then they also explain that there are a series of perception issues that might block young people out of power, which I think kind of mitigates the harms that that can trigger. I think then, subsequently, it provides a series of good potential risks that could occur where students are, for instance, corrupt or overly radical, but I think this potentially requires a relatively uncharitable view of the incentives of these students, who I think, to their credit, are pretty pro-democracy, are pretty enlivened, and subject to kind of counter-billing incentives that both are queen\'s names.','other',1996.8,2030.3),(1312,51,6376,6378,'So, we will make a more modest, certain, and internally consistent claim. Regardless of the virtues of these students, if they pursue their demands, it will damage the legacy of the revolution and its touched political program, which is bad for our national law. I want to explain is that the perception of these students is cynical.','other',2030.3,2046.8),(1313,51,6379,6380,'And open composition points to generic reasons why people are suspicious of politicians, which I think are true, but I think there are some more specific reasons why people are likely to be particularly skeptical of students. Crucially, I think youth and radicalism is something that is viewed more fondly without politics than within, which is just to say that people understand things like youth and radicalism as virtues in many instances, but they also understand, for instance, that they were far more soridid in their youth, that their views change over time, that young people are still developing and do not always have broad ideological views,','other',2046.8,2077.3),(1314,51,6381,6383,'which I think is, in general, pretty bad in terms of young people getting elected, in terms of them attacking older people\'s votes. But I would additionally note that it poses a barrier to collaboration with older people in politics. Like, I think it is quite likely that even probably pretty good politicians who are older, who are more experienced, are pretty skeptical of these young people, making it far more difficult for them to achieve their aims.','other',2077.3,2097.2),(1315,51,6384,6384,'The second thing I would note is that the specific demands, not all of them, but some of them, made by these young people are both unachievable and undesirable.','other',2097.2,2104),(1316,51,6385,6387,'And I think this is probably a better explanation of the problems of these young people than open government, who I think assumes that they\'ll do things that are particularly corrupt and particularly immoral. But we\'re just going to explain that if they pursue the political program outlined in their 9-point plan, that is bad. What is their political program?','other',2104,2120.8),(1317,51,6388,6391,'A couple of things. Firstly, they want to fire a tract of police, commissioners and superintendents who are involved in student casualties. Secondly, they want to fire a tract of vice chancellors of universities all across Bangladesh. And thirdly, they want to remove security personnel at large from university campuses in Bangladesh.','other',2120.8,2136.7),(1318,51,6392,6392,'The first thing I\'m going to do here is explain why those political positions are likely inflexible, and then I will explain why they are bad.','other',2136.7,2142.4),(1319,51,6393,6393,'The importance of explaining why they\'re inflexible is it shows why you would sort of, no thank you, will not be strategic and adopt particular positions in order to avoid this backlash that I\'ll explain later.','other',2142.4,2152.1),(1320,51,6394,6394,'Why are they inflexible?','other',2152.1,2153.2),(1321,51,6395,6397,'Well firstly, I would note that these things are highly public, highly published, they\'re in the 9-point plans, which I think makes them relatively difficult to walk back, because they are a stated ideological commitment of these young people. But secondly, I think that they likely believe them, both ideologically, but also incredibly emotionally, because fundamentally these sorts of demands represent revenge on the kinds of people whose students viewed as being particularly punitive or particularly immoral. That, I think, additionally means that this is likely an expectation of their peers in the movement, making it socially very difficult to avoid.','other',2153.2,2182.6),(1322,51,6398,6399,'Additionally though, I would note that the most radical young people are likely to be at the forefront of politics. Those are the people with potentially the most charisma, those are the people with the strongest belief that pushes them into political roles relative to more moderate young people.','other',2182.6,2194.9),(1323,51,6400,6400,'Finally, I would note that they probably have a pretty rational desire to disempower political enemies like vice chancellors, like police, but I\'m going to explain now why that is probably a very bad thing.','other',2194.9,2204.5),(1324,51,6401,6402,'So, young people enter politics with a list of demands about disempowering people like vice chancellors, like police. This, I think, is highly unachievable.','other',2204.5,2212.4),(1325,51,6403,6405,'I think that this is a set of people who are obviously incredibly empowered in society, they have access to incredibly important institutions like universities, like police, and they are important, like obviously it is important to have police in Bangladesh, and that means I think that they are particularly powerful enemies. And this provides to you, I think, a far more specific reason why I think that who\'s all more moderately institutional resistance is inevitable, because the set of people specifically who these young people have upset are the ones who their political programs irritate, who are the ones who their political programs continue to provoke. But I would also note that these are numerous enemies, like we\'re talking not just about powerful people, but about like hundreds, probably thousands of police.','other',2212.4,2253.4),(1326,51,6406,6408,'These are people who are going to be motivated against these students. It also alienates you from potential political allies, because it\'s pretty unlikely that, for instance, the BNP is particularly keen on like mass firing security from university campuses. I think a lot of adults are probably pretty like, they think security is a good thing to have at university in some level.','other',2253.4,2273.6),(1327,51,6409,6409,'I\'ll take you in a second.','other',2273.6,2274.5),(1328,51,6410,6411,'All of that, I think, means that young people are likely to suffer in terms of getting traction, in achieving their political goals. They\'re likely to be ineffective, and that, I think, is something which is far more likely to lead to problems with youth engagement described by both opening teams.','other',2274.5,2289),(1329,51,6412,6412,'I\'ll take that opinion now.','other',2289,2290.4),(1330,51,6413,6415,'If the impact is a coup, then there\'s still a coup. But if they attempt to pursue an anti-dressalist policy, if the impact is a coup, and just they lose political support, they lose the next election, they will never find a new policy. I already explained why they\'re likely to be euphristic, why they\'re likely to be strident and inflexible.','poi',2290.4,2303.5),(1331,51,6416,6418,'But I also just explained why there are a series of less-high things about a coup that are still bad. Things like institutions refusing to cooperate with you, things like potential political allies refusing alliances with you. The next thing I want to explain is that formal politics uniquely leads to division and confiding, which is bad.','other',2303.5,2319.8),(1332,51,6419,6420,'And I don\'t know, I think this is actually kind of conceded by everything Oxford does, who says, well, it\'s fine if they become a bit corrupt because they can just condemn their peers. Formal politics is more divisive than civil society, firstly, because you are required to take far more specific political positions, which opens up far more ground to disagree with each other, to have petty differences.','other',2319.8,2336.4),(1333,51,6421,6423,'Secondly, politics is competitive and sorry-some. You\'re likely running against each other in things like preselections, and it\'s the kind of personalities that are trying to gain notoriety, trying to gain media coverage. Thirdly, I regret that these are likely people with reputations as firebrands.','other',2336.4,2350.3),(1334,51,6424,6427,'They want to prove it by holding those around them accountable, by being particularly patriotic in discussions. Why is this division so bad? It\'s the quickest route to absolutely destroy the credibility of this student movement. You\'re required to, one, incredibly public spat to make people distrust these leaders, to make them, you know, to be more sceptical of them.','other',2350.3,2367),(1335,51,6428,6429,'Secondly, it splits the movement all the way down because the peers, the people who follow these leaders, are likely to want to pick sides. And thirdly, it obviously just has the opportunity cost to becoming a far more unifying civil society leader.','other',2367,2377.8),(1336,51,6430,6432,'I\'m going to weigh civil society here because I think opening government is a bit vague about what civil society figures can actually achieve. Why is civil society important? Firstly, it\'s far more accessible.','other',2377.8,2386.5),(1337,51,6433,6435,'Voting opposition tells you it\'s incredibly hard to vote in elections, there are an enormous number of barriers. But things like social media, things like accessing media, are incredibly inclusive and allow people to participate in politics far more easily. It\'s important to be there.','other',2386.5,2398),(1338,51,6436,6442,'But secondly, civil society is more resilient. It is harder to cut down on civil society figures because they\'re perceived as less than equal because it\'s opposed by international actors. Which means it\'s important to seize your notoriety to be in these civil society figures. A place where it\'s important to have powerful individuals, weakening politics is probably already covered by individuals being motivated to vote, caring about politics in a historically unique way. I\'m going to just read the speech so I can get my bag. Or if anything, I can come back in time to read it. What if I get my bag? Never mind.','other',2398,2443.2),(1339,53,6547,6548,'Now I\'d like to give the government whip to follow up government venture. First, I\'ll explain why closing the government wins the debate, and secondly, why the Opposition bench loses.','other',2989.1,3008.5),(1340,53,6549,6551,'Firstly, why did we win the debate? I think we made kind of two contributions. The first is just explaining in substantial detail why these student leaders would be both bad at politics, but also, perhaps more importantly, good at civil society, and impacting why that is a society and politics changing force.','other',3008.5,3026),(1341,53,6552,6554,'And the second, I think, is just outlining that most of these specific demands of the anti-discrimination student movement, in their highly publicised 9. 0 plan, published after closing the scene up, are bad. And of course, as this team might respond, some of those demands are like, for instance, asking for compensation for families who have protested to a hill. That\'s fine.','other',3026,3046.3),(1342,53,6555,6559,'But those kind of demands are not sensationalist, nor are they radical. They\'re extremely unlikely to be the focus of campaigns, of opposition, of media, of the things that they are held to by political opponents, and that are indeed the spotlight for the media at the moment. Many of those demands are incredibly bad. Things like firing huge tracks of police who are related to the university. or whose jurisdiction is the universities where protest was suppressed, even if those police were not directly involved, like they didn\'t kill a person, they just have jurisdiction over those universities, no security at the universities, firing fuse attacks at VCs, and national boundary alarming the uni-quake.','other',3046.3,3081.1),(1343,53,6560,6565,'And I think those policies are bad in a few respects. Firstly, I don\'t suppose many of them on face value on their grounds alone. For instance, I don\'t think you should ban student movements of most political parties until they pass a minimum and extremely high threshold. I think that is probably something we should be conservative about, not restricting free speech. I think police who are often underpaid, who are not personally responsible for violence against students, who happen to be part of a systemically corrupt institution, but don\'t have much choice in that, should not be fired from their jobs. I think those are immoral demands and we should not stand for them.','other',3081.1,3111.1),(1344,53,6566,6570,'Secondly, those demands are literally impossible to fulfil. As Ellie points out to No Response, without powerful enemies in the form of the whole police force, this is incredibly scary. This doesn\'t work, which at very worst for us, which is an enormous opportunity cost of resources, a failure to get your policies passed. But also I think it\'s incredibly embarrassing and delegitimising for these student protests and for all the civil society organisations and movements that we\'ve heard that they\'re attached to, when they can do good work out of politics. Maybe, for instance, you want to go into politics later, you can\'t because you have an initial track record of doing terrible things and failing to pass through a policy.','other',3111.1,3145.1),(1345,53,6571,6573,'The response we get to this from opening opposition in a POI is, firstly, well if there\'s a risk of coup, then you moderate. I think Ellie pre-empts this in quite a lot of detail. We explain people aren\'t rational, that it\'s very unlikely that there\'s a risk of a coup on their side, and you\'re not thinking about the future to that extent.','other',3145.1,3159.8),(1346,53,6574,6575,'But it\'s incredibly embarrassing and maybe, for instance, brings backlash or political violence in the short term to backtrack on your established demands. And importantly, even if some people are rational in this way, the most prominent leaders of that movement will not be.','other',3159.8,3172.8),(1347,53,6576,6577,'The second plan they made is, well if you don\'t moderate, you\'re going to lose the election. But firstly, I don\'t know if that harms the movement of anti-authoritarian student protesters.','other',3172.8,3181.8),(1348,53,6578,6581,'Regardless, even if it\'s true that you lose the election by having these demands, it still hurts that movement. It hurts, for instance, initial movements by students, any student in Bangladesh against corruption, against authoritarianism in the future. They are targeted that brush. It\'s really idiotic for opposition parties to media outlets to pick up on.','other',3181.8,3200.1),(1349,53,6582,6590,'But secondly, maybe at their very best, this plan hurts OG, because we explain our why involved in civil society. Successfully put politics later, for instance. And you could run more successfully put politics later, if you\'re not targeted this brush. If, for instance, you have demands that are bad, that can\'t operate in civil society, probably still do with. And in politics, in representing this constituency in parliament, you cause huge amounts of chaos. You shift the Overton window. You mean that people discredit other, more sensible left-wing policies, like restrictions on police powers. You mean that there\'s gridlock in parliament. All of that is terrible.','other',3200.1,3238.7),(1350,53,6591,6593,'Why does this then get us above opening government? I take a fairly generic criticism of young left revolutionaries on the basis of their radicalism, but also the tempting vices of politics like corruption. But the problem with this is, firstly, claims in terms of entitlement to corruption, while true, I think seem relatively symmetric, given the features that this team themselves point out in terms of the political system in Bangladesh at large.','other',3238.7,3260.1),(1351,53,6594,6595,'But secondly, radicalism, of course, and our opening text, hand you back, but can also be put, right, at deeply radical movements, like, for instance, anti-caste movements or feminism, are both good ideologically, but are deeply attractive to people, even though they ask for very large changes to the status quo. We explain that the changes we\'re asking for are bad, which means they\'re both unpopular, but also undesirable.','other',3260.1,3279.2),(1352,53,6596,6597,'But finally, we impact the alternative. Civil society, not just politics, which makes society and makes government better. We win.','other',3279.2,3286.1),(1353,53,6598,6598,'Let\'s now talk about opening opposition.','other',3286.1,3287.7),(1354,53,6599,6602,'Opening opposition, I think, is dangerously pessimistic in their vision of the status quo. They say that the NDP will have no political competition without students to push back. The reason for this is because it\'s prohibitively hard for the youth to vote, the BNP receives money from lobbyists, they\'ll always get young people on side, students don\'t win. This is dangerous because it\'s a self-defeating argument for opening opposition.','other',3287.7,3308.8),(1355,53,6603,6605,'If the BNP is so powerful, then students collecting and testing elections will fail regardless. And their one attempt to bridge this gap is to say, well, people care a lot about youth and revolution now, we have to take that opportunity. That isn\'t sufficient for a few reasons.','other',3308.8,3321.9),(1356,53,6606,6608,'Firstly, it could lose that momentum, right? Like, if you present yourself as political critics, part of civil society, it is likely that anti-authoritarian momentum lost. But secondly, if opening opposition is right, there is an attitude of our type in supporting this specific political party, especially because their power is in opposing the AL, not in creating and enjoicing their own political platform, which I know requires many different skills and virtues.','other',3321.9,3346.1),(1357,53,6609,6611,'Finally, they say the BNP has a textile contract, which is bad. One, this is self-correcting. Yes, lobbying is powerful, but votes matter for the individual people whose wages are going down.','other',3346.1,3354.3),(1358,53,6612,6612,'There are other competing businesses that don\'t like cronyistic contracts. Contracts expire.','other',3354.3,3358.2),(1359,53,6613,6615,'Or secondly, if the textile industry is so cemented, and that means that other parties who are established also need money, also take that money. Finally, let\'s provide closing opposition. Firstly, they say the young people in politics will help the future political system in terms of the changes to the constitution.','other',3358.2,3372.7),(1360,53,6616,6617,'One, it\'s unlikely that those young people are elected or listened to if they are elected, for the reasons of youth. They don\'t have the agile and institutional capacity that this team describes as necessary.','other',3372.7,3381.2),(1361,53,6618,6618,'That is overpowering.','other',3381.2,3382.3),(1362,53,6619,6622,'Secondly, Yunus has decreased corruption massively, and it\'s likely that his oversight as the caretaker government will make sure that the special assembly is good, that it\'s not done corruptly, and so on. But finally, they say that students have united lots of different parts of society. That\'s true with regards to taking down the AL. It\'s not true with regards to rewriting the constitution, where there are hugely different interests, if you are the military, as opposed to if you are students.','other',3382.3,3405.2),(1363,53,6623,6624,'They say next that the BNP will fail. Firstly, nice of your opening, because they are guaranteed success no matter what.','other',3405.2,3410.5),(1364,53,6625,6626,'But secondly, the barriers they put up are transitory. They say the leader is bad and you get a new one.','other',3410.5,3414.8),(1365,53,6627,6627,'Again, Yunus has massively decreased corruption in Bangladesh.','other',3414.8,3417.6),(1366,53,6628,6628,'They say your relationship with India is bad.','other',3417.6,3419.6),(1367,53,6629,6630,'I\'ll flip that. It\'s way worse, because India hated the student protesters, set on with police to target them and bring them down.','other',3419.6,3425.6),(1368,53,6631,6632,'This ruins your relationship with India, or rather hurts it, because obviously India doesn\'t like the student protesters. It doesn\'t like it when the special assembly supports them and has them in their midst.','other',3425.6,3434.5),(1369,53,6633,6633,'We didn\'t speak very much for their speech, and we now welcome the opposition to close off the debate.','other',3434.5,3493.8),(1370,64,2194,2195,'The US is the main medium of oil. Main medium, I agree.','other',642.2,776.9),(1371,64,2196,2200,'They talk about that they have British Central Bank members, two-thirds of the parliament need to approve an internal audit. They claim that this would prevent any discrepancies in the parliament, which I think is fairly untrue because of the fact that corruption is so top-down. Corruption is so top-down. For example, in 2015, their president was actually jailed for embezzlement. Because of the fact that corruption is so top-down, even if you have two-thirds of the parliament, you will be unable to have the level of independence that the US Fed has, which will be explained in the monetary policy.','other',776.9,807.4),(1372,64,2201,2207,'Moving on to what else they say. In their model, they talk about they\'re going to peg to the US dollar over time. First of all, this is directly against the model. You can\'t peg to another currency because it\'s, by definition, a pegged independent. You can do whatever model you want, you cannot peg it because peg is what\'s happening right now. You need to make it an independent currency. Peg, no. Get rid of that.','other',807.4,829.3),(1373,64,2208,2209,'On to our points of framing. We have three points of framing.','other',829.4,833.8),(1374,64,2210,2221,'Firstly, is that the US dollar is a global reserve currency. I think we\'re mostly in agreement on this. I have three main reasons. We have global dependence. Everyone depends on the dollar. We have two real world trends. The dollar is really strong and dominates other currencies. And three, the USD is behind every other currency in the world, whether you like it or not. And four, it\'s historically present and tolerant. The dollar has gone from ups and downs to always coming back. So I think we can agree that the USD is the backbone of global finance. I don\'t think there\'s much to speak here.','other',833.8,857.5),(1375,64,2222,2229,'Second point of framing is what Panama looks like in the status quo. Here, I think we have a lot of disagreements. So Panama looks like four things in the status quo. One is Panama is a high income country, one of two high income countries in the Latin American region. The other one is Mexico, which is also dollarized, significantly up from its neighbors. Two is it is service based, which focuses on offshore banking, tourism, and the Panama Canal. Three is Panama has a special relationship with the US because 40% of the trade passes through the canal. And four is a small country, both geographically and populated.','other',857.5,887.6),(1376,64,2230,2231,'This characterization actually rebuts their argument on diversity and diversifying the economy and experts. And I\'ll show you why.','other',887.6,896.1),(1377,64,2232,2234,'So what do they tell you on their side? They tell you that why strong currency harms Panama. Because Panamanian experts become more expensive and Panama wants to manufacture less.','other',896.1,904.2),(1378,64,2235,2238,'Let me tell you why this doesn\'t work. So first of all, three responses. One, Panama\'s economy is service based, reliant on tourism and the Panama Canal. Devaluing the currency really hurts their main interest.','other',904.2,914.3),(1379,64,2239,2245,'Second is, it\'s a very small economy, both population and geographic wise. Which means the raw material access they have is really small. Even with a weaker currency. Panama would not be able to be a manufacturing based economy. They created it as if Panama was going to grow into this manufacturing powerhouse like China, which is simply untrue. There is no way they\'ll transition to a manufacturing economy while maintaining its high income status. And obviously that\'s a bad thing.','other',914.3,939.7),(1380,64,2246,2250,'And then three, even if our goal as Panama was to have a better manufacturing sector, which it\'s not. Even if it was. We need FDIs in order to diversify those sectors. Which I will show you in my first substantive as to why we use FDIs and investor companies. So that removes the diversity. Moving on.','other',939.8,959.5),(1381,64,2251,2257,'And the third point of hearing is a burden that I would like to push on the side of opposition. Because we know what to do. When you introduce a new currency, you need to show that the public will trust that new currency. Because if you introduce a new currency and it\'s not used by the citizens, that is pointless. You have to prove that the public will trust their new currency instead of just hoarding the US dollar and ignoring the competition. Our opposition has the fiat to introduce a new currency. They do not have the fiat to say everyone is going to be forced to use it.','other',959.5,988.4),(1382,64,2258,2260,'Moving on to argumentation then. So if your characterization is that Panama has a small economy, that only breathes into our analysis of how the US won\'t care about the Panama economy, thereby increasing the necessity of an independent monetary policy. I\'ll address that when I talk about Iranian security.','other',988.4,1005.6),(1383,64,2261,2264,'So starting my first substantive. On better economic stability. The thesis of this argument is that Panama\'s use of the US dollar is superior for economic growth and stability. Panama\'s unique success in the region can be attributed directly to the US dollar.','other',1005.6,1017.5),(1384,64,2265,2272,'Why is the US dollar better for Panama? Three reasons. First is ultra-low inflation and built-in stability. Panama\'s inflation rate is just 0. 7%, among the lowest in Latin America. The US dollar is a globally stable currency not subject to rapid devaluation. Crucially, Panama cannot simply print more money, which opposes automatic fiscal discipline. This matters because inflation is a direct harm to the working class, eroding wages, savings, and purchasing power. Stable prices are what protects real income.','other',1017.5,1045.3),(1385,64,2273,2277,'Do as I do. investment confidence in economic growth. The dollar is entrusted global currencies. That trust extends to PAN, which has attracted SDI with nearly 80% of its GDP. Investors know that the dollar is a safe bet, giving them larger incentive to invest as they don\'t face risks from currency fluctuations, inflation, and economic risks.','other',1045.3,1063.2),(1386,64,2278,2280,'So they say that FDIs are less likely, and they\'re arguing too, because of high prices. I think it\'s a lot more likely that capital flight occurs when I see a transition, because it\'s a very volatile moment when they\'re transitioning, and when they transition, I see it\'s a new currency, not trusted by anyone. I, as an investor, will now want to pull out, instead of an economy which uses the US dollar, which is globally trusted, which I think they pretty much would agree with.','other',1063.2,1088.5),(1387,64,2281,2288,'And three, easier and cheaper trade. How about imports? Most of this, because I told you, it\'s not a manufacturing company. From food to electronics, because it uses the US dollar, it can pay for imports directly. A local currency would require, a local currency would require, first, exchange local money with the US. If demand for a new currency is low, the exchange rate would be unfavorable, making imports more expensive. These extra costs are passed on to consumers as higher prices. Using the US dollar removes this inefficiency, keeping prices stable and essential goods affordable, which is very good for the working class of the US. Point.','other',1088.5,1120.2),(1388,64,2289,2293,'Why a new currency would be dangerous. Three reasons. One is investors like expensive debt. A national currency with no tracker could only inspire global trust. Investors would pull out or demand massive risk premiums. This will be important when I talk about emergency relief.','other',1120.2,1134.9),(1389,64,2294,2298,'Two is inflation risk reform of the swan commerce. Countries like Venezuela and Argentina fail to maintain and manage their own currencies, resulting in hyperinflation and eventual dollarization. Panama wants to monitor infrastructure to avoid a similar outcome. A new currency, even if they try and maintain stability, will never match the dollar\'s stability. Three is cost of trade and inflation spike.','other',1134.9,1154.7),(1390,64,2299,2302,'Panama was still in the US dollar\'s string of four. Without sufficient reserves, it would resort to printing money, causing devaluation. This pushes import costs higher, which drives inflation, weakens the currency even more, and this is a continuous spiral. And once that spiral begins, it\'s nearly impossible to stop.','other',1154.7,1168.9),(1391,64,2303,2305,'In fact, these are pretty intuitive. With a new currency, we get hyperinflation and lose the stability that we have in the status quo. We lose investor confidence and thus FDIs as a bonus, and we spoil the working class by decreasing their purchasing power with a new currency.','other',1168.9,1183),(1392,64,2306,2311,'Secondly, on the idea of crisis relief. Let me just, there are two types of crisis relief. There are one type which affects both Panama and the US, in which case we face the same problem. They claim that, no, it\'s different, but really, because we have the same currency and a very sling to economy, because 40% of the trade goes to Panama now. We have the same response. Either we need a stronger currency, in which case we raise our lower interest rates, or we need a weaker currency, in which case we raise our lower interest rates, depending on what we need.','other',1183,1210.2),(1393,64,2312,2319,'So that\'s out of the way. Secondly, the thing about it is that the price is unique to Panama. So there are a couple of reasons why we need short-term relief over them. When we need to have more investment coming, so we get cheap loans. In their case, we don\'t get cheap loans. We are more likely to get them. And two, we can\'t panic-fringe money, which leads to hyperinflation. In their case, we can just panic-fringe money, which they\'re so keen on, which completely screwed over Zimbabwe\'s economy.','other',1210.2,1232.3),(1394,64,2320,2321,'And you can see that. In our case, we prevent an economic crisis, and we have done so much better, directly in the product of the loans.','other',1232.3,1239.4),(1395,66,2420,2427,'Starting in three, two, one. Panama. If you\'ve been in Panama for a while, it\'s likely that you\'ve come across this coin. This coin is the Panamanian Balboa, and it\'s the coin system that Panama uses as a medium of exchange. If you notice on the Panamanian Balboa, there\'s a picture of a man called Vasco de Luna\'s Balboa, and he was a Spanish conquistador who came to Panama in the 1600s to take over towns, pillage people, rape women, and kill children. Yet, Panama uses it as the basis of the Panamanian Balboa. Why? Because their independent identity isn\'t held within a single symbolic gesture. Their independence is held within the strategic choice they made in 1904 to pivot towards the US dollar because it posed strategic advantage to them in 1904, and still does because not much has changed with Panama. Antitoposition\'s ideal world of whether Panama can actually pivot to an export-based economy doesn\'t exist because it\'s a dystopian dream that wouldn\'t exist in a real-life scenario.','other',1842.5,1959.5),(1396,66,2428,2428,'Let\'s start with framework.','other',1959.5,1960.9),(1397,66,2429,2433,'Firstly, they claim to have an independent central bank or that they wish to. Or that they wish to exert an independent central bank. We tell you that this is not possible as of status quo. Even if they do have the FIAT, it\'s likely to fall in the future because Panama doesn\'t have the, because Panama lacks the infrastructure to actually uphold a central bank. So given that they do have the FIAT to actually hold a central bank, we don\'t think it would run very well and stable in the long term.','other',1960.9,1983),(1398,66,2434,2434,'More on framework.','other',1983,1983.7),(1399,66,2435,2437,'They talk about local and international members of the bank that would actually run the bank. So now we\'re going to invite people from other countries with different incentives to actually run a country that we actually wish to operate and benefit of Panama. So that\'s an inherent contradiction on their side in terms of framework.','other',1983.7,1997.3),(1400,66,2438,2441,'They also talk about a two-third parliamentary bank majority to actually impose any decision. We tell you that this is also unrealistic because they want parliament to be taking part in everyday central bank decisions, which is just unlikely and doesn\'t happen with most countries. And they also talk, it\'s also hypocritical considering they want a fast system, yet they want parliament to sit down every time the bank, the central bank has to make a decision regarding monetary policy and fiscal policy. It\'s not possible to have a fast system, yet always have to convince two-thirds of parliament to agree upon a monetary or fiscal decision.','other',1997.3,2029.1),(1401,66,2442,2444,'They also talk about this idea of a one to 2% float, which is essentially a very fake way to say they\'re going to peg to the US dollar, which again doesn\'t fulfill the requirements of the motion. The motion asks them to have independent currency, even if they would, to remove this peg after five years, we think would fall apart because again, Panama doesn\'t have the infrastructure to support it. And Panama as of today is facing very unfortunate circumstances of a largely corrupt government.','other',2029.1,2050.5),(1402,66,2445,2448,'Moving on to prop one, they talk about stronger USD earning exports. The thing is, Panama isn\'t export-based. We\'ve told you that, they\'ve told you that. Panama is mostly service-based and mostly transport-based.','other',2050.5,2063.7),(1403,66,2449,2455,'They have the Panama Canal. And that\'s the largest single individual contributor to their economy. So their idea to pivot to an export-based economy, I\'ll get to on later, but as of now, it\'s not true that Panama\'s export-based. Also, secondly, they talk about investors in the Panama Canal, and they want to use the Panama Canal as leverage, as they say, because it\'s a very strategic asset. We actually agree with that. We think that earning the Panama Canal is a very strategic asset, and that we will continue to be a strategic asset, which is why we don\'t think we should risk that strategic asset by switching to a flimsy currency and trying to make it more profitable. risk our strategic assets in the Panama Canal.','other',2063.7,2098.2),(1404,66,2456,2460,'We don\'t think that\'s a good move, because people actually, the US, 40% of US trade actually passes through the Panama Canal. And we don\'t think that there\'s going to be very positive US backlash once we switch currencies. We\'ve seen in the past, we\'ve seen in the past few weeks with the US invading Iran for small incentives. We think that switching currencies is a direct indicator to the US, say, hey, come take the canal that you\'ve literally built for us, because now we have incentive to use that as leverage against you. So we don\'t think the US is going to respond to that very positively.','other',2098.2,2126.5),(1405,66,2461,2464,'They also say that they\'re going to create more jobs because of a manufacturing and export-based economy. This, again, is an inherent contradiction, because they want to reduce imports, and they say that they want less imports. And they also agree to the point my first speaker made, when he said that Panama currently doesn\'t have the natural resources to support an export-based economy, so they will actually have to import the raw materials, which would, again, increase the import-based economy. So that\'s an inherent contradiction on their side, and should be taken into consideration with this motion.','other',2126.5,2150.9),(1406,66,2465,2468,'Firstly, next, they also talk about how control and monitoring policy is important for crisis management. We tell you this is a blatant lie, because for 100 years, Panama has been able to manage crisis with its fiscal policy. In the 2008 global recession, Panama did significantly better than any other Latin American country. In fact, the next year, it came in with a 7% GDP growth, something no other Latin American country had seen, and we attribute that to the US dollar.','other',2150.9,2172.7),(1407,66,2469,2471,'We don\'t think that that would have been possible with this new influence of currencies that they instead proposed. Moving on, they also talk about fiscal solutions accumulating debt. This, again, is not true of Yankee, and this fiscal policy is just a lever for the government to control spending and taxes.','other',2172.7,2185.6),(1408,66,2472,2478,'So the government\'s been doing this for a long time, for 100 years, since 1904. It\'s worked perfectly. We don\'t see incentive to change this. We don\'t see the need to change this. If they can show to us where Panama has actually done bad in a crisis, where people have been left starving, where people have been left homeless, then we agree that there\'s incentive for a change, but Panama\'s been doing fine. Why do we have to idealize this version of monetary policy? Because Panama\'s been fine without it, and Panama\'s seen a 6% average GDP growth, and we think that the little growth that they would see on their side isn\'t enough incentive for us to take this risky maneuver.','other',2185.6,2216.6),(1409,66,2479,2483,'Moving on to a substantive theory. Firstly, the premise of this argument being we have better monetary policy on our side. Firstly, because Panama\'s use of the US dollar isn\'t about economic convenience. It\'s also about wisely avoiding the risks of managing monetary policy at home. Unlike fiscal decisions, which are visible and accountable, monetary decisions are technically, are technical, opaque, and highly vulnerable to abuse, which, again, is very likely in Panama because Panama is severely corrupt, and they haven\'t shown us how they\'re actually going to deal with the corrupt nature of Panama itself.','other',2216.6,2244.3),(1410,66,2484,2488,'The only thing that they\'ve said is they\'re going to bring people from outside to work in these banks, which, again, we think is contradictory because they\'re going to have their own incentives. And then we have the US monetary policy is being better for Panama because we have the US Fed, the strongest monetary institution in the world, is doing our work for us. Why change that? Why risk it? That\'s what we push for on our side.','other',2244.3,2265.4),(1411,66,2489,2490,'We also talk about urban crisis management and how that has successfully helped Panama navigate through COVID-19, through the 2008 depression. We haven\'t heard any examples from their side of the house, which is why we think this argument should be disregarded on their side of the house.','other',2265.4,2279.1),(1412,66,2491,2496,'We also talk about why independent monetary policy would be actively dangerous. Two points on this first. Exclusive harms. Corruption in monetary policy is hidden and devastating. So unlike fiscal policy, actually, where you can track budgets, investigate spending, and hold politicians accountable, that\'s not possible with monetary policy. A government can\'t track which politicians are putting pressure on which banker to actually print more money and cause inflation to fill their own pockets. This is possible with fiscal policy.','other',2279.1,2302.9),(1413,66,2497,2502,'Panama\'s been doing it for 100 years. Every other example of a Latin American country that\'s trying to implement this has failed. We\'ve given you examples like El Salvador. We\'ve told you about Argentina. We told you about Mexico. All these countries that are actually trying to impose things like this have failed because they don\'t have the infrastructure to support it.','other',2302.9,2317.8),(1414,66,2503,2508,'Moving on, we also talk about no infrastructure or expertise. Panama has never had to manage a full monetary system. It lacks a credible independent central bank. It lacks deep reserves to stabilize its currency. And it also has global trust in its economy of print. Building all this from scratch would take decades if it ever succeeds at all.','other',2317.8,2334.6),(1415,66,2509,2511,'Yet, why is this about monetary policy and non-fiscal policy? Some might say that Panama handles fiscal policy like taxes and spending and it hasn\'t collapsed, but that\'s exactly the point. Fiscal policy is more accountable, as I mentioned before.','other',2334.6,2346.2),(1416,66,2512,2516,'Moving on to the impact, Dean, what\'s at stake? Panama has remained stable while its neighbors like Nicaragua and Venezuela suffered economic collapse. That\'s not an accident. It\'s because Panama took one of the most dangerous levers of power, monetary control, out of local hands and gave it to a system that works creating national currency that reverses that. So, moving on to substantive three, which is stability and adaptation meets resilience in crisis.','other',2346.2,2366.3),(1417,66,2517,2518,'Now, we\'ve heard a lot about stability and resilience in crisis from side proposition, but we think that\'s the most blatant lie that\'s been told in today\'s debate because if anything to do with crisis, it simply just lies on our side of the house. Panama has independent dollar generation from the canal and it\'s 80% service-based economy that they actually accept.','other',2366.7,2384.2),(1418,66,2519,2520,'So, Panama organically earns the US dollar. So, the thesis of this being that we don\'t care that they think that with a new independent currency, Panama would actually be able to adapt to crisis more.','other',2384.2,2396.8),(1419,66,2521,2524,'We tell you that this is not possible at all because this new currency will be flimsy because of reasons we\'ve mentioned. We tell you that US dollar is the global reserve. We tell you that Panama earns the US dollar organically and we tell you that\'s the best way to manage crisis because it has the reserves. I\'m gonna let you go for a hundred weeks before we run into any more issues.','other',2396.8,2413.9),(1420,66,2525,2526,'Do you want it? No, we can\'t. Go ahead. Let\'s go.','other',2414.8,2417.7),(1421,68,2607,2614,'We hear a lot about diversity, but we don\'t hear what it actually looks like. Tourism, 8%, Panama Canal, 6%, banking, 12%, and exports, 11%. The rest is miscellaneous. What I\'m trying to say is, they say diversity has a pretty big push, but we don\'t actually hear why it\'s not already diverse enough. It\'s not like Saudi where it\'s only oil. We have other things. It\'s just mainly service-based. We\'re playing to our strengths, never being eligible.','other',3033.4,3099.5),(1422,68,2615,2617,'Two key things here. Firstly, on the framework, and second, on two key clashes. Firstly, on economic growth, and second, on stability and economic crisis.','other',3099.5,3108.3),(1423,68,2618,2619,'So, let\'s start with their framework. Why is their model so extremely problematic? Five reasons.','other',3108.3,3115.2),(1424,68,2620,2624,'One, they tell you about a fluctuating bank. Does anybody in this room know how problematic a fluctuating bank is? You know how much capital flight is there when you have a fluctuating bank. It\'s like saying, this economy is not working, so we\'re going to do this over five years, and then we\'ll get to a stage where we\'re good. In those five years, every investor is running because they know the current system is not working.','other',3115.2,3140),(1425,68,2625,2627,'Second, we tell you very clearly. .. that Panama lacks the expertise to be able to do this. They have no track record compared to the US, which is the best monetary policy maker in the world.','other',3140,3152),(1426,68,2628,2629,'And three, we tell you corruption is top down. We tell you self-interest is their main priority, and they bring up checks and balances, but we tell you these external checks and balances are not good enough because it\'s top down.','other',3152,3164.8),(1427,68,2630,2643,'The way they counter this is, four, they tell you corporations and all these people, you know what, the problem is that these corporations also prioritize self-interest. And they tell you, hey, even though they\'re prioritizing self-interest, they care about the economy because that also benefits their self-interest. Let me tell you something. The thing is, an economy could be falling. Everybody could be falling. But some people can benefit from it. We\'ve seen it everywhere. And these corporates and these corrupt people, top down, will benefit. Whether or not the poor people suffer or not, they don\'t care about this. What they care about is their self-interest. Are you gonna bring up corporates and JP Morgan and show why they care about the average person? When we very clearly tell you they can benefit even though the economy\'s failing because when they bring money, they can use it to their advantage. They can be very strategic in how they invest. They can use currency rates and be able to use that to solve the penny in currency and benefit from it.','other',3164.8,3230.1),(1428,68,2644,2645,'This is not a good check and balance to have. Moving on to the key questions, but before that, I\'ll take a point.','other',3230.1,3237.1),(1429,68,2646,2646,'How will a JP Morgan operating through a paramount economy be able to benefit from hyperinflation, which causes a crisis that means that your life saving doesn\'t mean anything at all?','poi',3237.1,3246.6),(1430,68,2647,2650,'I very clearly told you that what they can do is they can take advantage of the interest rates. Knowing that the economy\'s going to collapse, they can very clearly sell all of their penny in currency and use that to their advantage because they\'re saying they will have trust. It means the penny in currency will be at a certain point and they can start selling all of it and benefit from that. I think that\'s pretty intuitive.','other',3246.6,3269.8),(1431,68,2651,2652,'Two questions then. First, you have economic growth.','other',3269.8,3271.7),(1432,68,2653,2655,'Let me flesh that out. What do we hear from them? They tell you that economic growth is better on their side because high rates of BSE hurts the sectors.','other',3271.7,3280.5),(1433,68,2656,2662,'A few responses to that. One, we tell you about the nature of Panama and its economy. We tell you it\'s service based and we see no issue with it. We tell you that it\'s diverse enough and we also tell you that we\'re playing to our strengths and we see nothing wrong with it. But two, we tell you that devaluing actually hurts these main sectors. So what they\'re trying to say is, hey, this is what we\'re nationally very good at, but let\'s hurt that and let\'s improve these weaker things that never have the potential to grow. Four, we tell you that this is not good enough in the first place.','other',3280.5,3315.6),(1434,68,2663,2667,'But three, we tell you even if you assume that the manufacturing can grow, the investor confidence we have is significantly more important than anything they can break off, which they lack with a new currency. But also four, we tell you the U.S.A. gives you such good leverage. When you have that high rate, when you deal with other countries, it actually helps you because you\'re the one growing. They present this problem of the U.S.A.','other',3315.6,3338.8),(1435,68,2668,2673,'growing as problematic to Panama. But guess who has the currency that is growing? Panama, because that\'s the currency we deal in. So all of our reserves are in the U.S.A. and you trade with other countries where our rate is higher. It means we\'re benefiting day by day.','other',3338.8,3353.7),(1436,68,2674,2677,'Take it from a Tanzanian, my currency from the day I was born was a thousand shillings to one dollar. Now it\'s 2,800. You could have a dollar and you could have doubled in Tanzania. Panama is better to take and that is clearly very true.','other',3353.7,3368),(1437,68,2678,2681,'But two, we tell you about the idea of exports and imports. Again, I repeat to you, we would rather play to our strengths and tell you that our strengths are diverse enough in nature. We tell you we have tourism, we tell you we have panhandling, we tell you we have banking. Just because we\'re service concentrated doesn\'t mean that we\'re not diverse.','other',3368,3385.3),(1438,68,2682,2684,'But two, we tell you that it\'s very unlikely for Panama to have a strong manufacturing base. This is for three reasons. One, it\'s a low GDP.','other',3385.3,3394.4),(1439,68,2685,2690,'Yes, I\'ve heard of a POI. It\'s an unfair comparison to the United States of America which has such a strong GDP. We tell you it\'s not an example game, but this is the structural reason we\'re giving you. Their GDP is not strong enough because of the population size. But two, we tell you we don\'t even have the natural resources. You literally lack the ability.','other',3394.4,3414.8),(1440,68,2691,2696,'In terms of growth, number three, we tell you that there\'s no wage stagnation. They tell you, hey, with the U.S. aid, it\'s only the job benefiting. We tell you that the workers also get paid in U.S. aid, meaning that there\'s no wage stagnation. What they can buy and what they get is pretty symmetric.','other',3414.8,3433.2),(1441,68,2697,2701,'What do we tell you then on economic growth? We face no reputation to this. We tell you that the U.S. aid actually helps them in terms of investor confidence, but it also gives them a high range of leverage and it allows easier and cheaper trade. To this, we receive absolutely nothing.','other',3433.2,3448.5),(1442,68,2702,2706,'We also bring up that a new currency is particularly bad for three reasons. We tell you that it doesn\'t have stability because of how small it is. Check South American currencies for reference. Two, we tell you that there\'s no public trust or investor trust. And three, we tell you we have costlier trades with the higher exchange rates.','other',3448.5,3467.1),(1443,68,2707,2711,'On win then, even if you\'re diversifying money hurting the economy, who\'s winning? But two, we tell you it\'s totally fine to not be diversifying. Even take extreme examples of Saudi who are so reliant on oil. They\'re doing totally fine. And if we have an economic life plan, we\'re totally fine with that and we don\'t see the problem with it.','other',3467.1,3486.9),(1444,68,2712,2714,'In the worst case. Second flashpoint, which is a bit smaller, but on monetary policy. Their theme is on crisis and they tell you U.S.','other',3486.9,3493.7),(1445,68,2715,2720,'and Panama are different interests. Four key responses. One, we tell you the monetary policy set by U.S. is going to affect both U.S. and Panama symmetrically. It means that they\'re not acting out for the best interest of Panama, but in acting in their best interest, it\'s helping Panama.','other',3493.7,3508.7),(1446,68,2721,2725,'But two, if crisis comes in two ways. One, it\'s a global crisis and it affects both of them. Then their reaction, which is lowering money, is the same. It\'s not like fiscal policy where you have to be significantly flexible. If you\'re in a crisis, you can just lower interest rates and it\'s going to work symmetrically.','other',3508.7,3527.1),(1447,68,2726,2728,'But three, that all tells you that you don\'t have to care about Panama for us to be doing well. But four, we tell you even when it\'s different, fiscal policy is better. Lastly then, on fiscal policy and why it\'s better.','other',3527.1,3539.7),(1448,68,2729,2730,'That\'s the question we tell you. And why better deals with the economic crisis.','other',3539.7,3545.2),(1449,68,2731,2732,'One, they tell you fiscal policy is slow, but it\'s fast. In terms of crisis, Panama mobilized $15 million like that.','other',3545.2,3551),(1450,68,2733,2734,'Two, just look at historical precedent. With COVID-19, we dealt with it so well, Panama was one of the best countries in dealing with it.','other',3551,3561),(1451,68,2735,2736,'But three, scale of impact, when corruption is that bad, then we would much rather have fiscal policy than monetary policy in our hands, which cannot be accounted. Never been accountable.','other',3561,3570.3),(1452,69,2739,2740,'We\'ll do it. We\'ll find a pearl in the ocean and throw it back in the hopes of a diamond.','other',3573.7,3610.7),(1453,69,2741,2743,'We are one of two high-income countries in the Latin America region, second only to Mexico. And guess what guarantees both of those countries use? The US dollar.','other',3610.7,3618.6),(1454,69,2744,2745,'They claim long-term benefits on their side of the house and they are happy to concede the short-term. But the problem is, when you have so many short-term benefits that it prevents the long-term to happen, which is what is happening with the currency.','other',3618.9,3634.1),(1455,69,2746,2748,'We have three main issues in this debate. We have one, who does monetary policy better, the US Fed or Panama? We have two, economic growth is still early, and then three, the impact of the crisis.','other',3634.1,3642.6),(1456,69,2749,2750,'Let\'s start with monetary policy. So we very clearly told you on our side that we have top-down corruption, from the Pellet all the way down.','other',3642.9,3650.3),(1457,69,2751,2753,'They told you the truth there is a problem. The problem is when corruption is so top-down, especially in developing countries like Panama, Tanzania, and Senata, the parliament is majorly controlled by the government. So their checks and balances are infected in that truth there is a problem.','other',3650.3,3662.8),(1458,69,2754,2756,'Another thing to talk about is general checks and balances like the IMF. So if you\'re going to allow the IMF to control your central bank, then all your claims on crisis become symmetric because now you don\'t control your monetary policy either, the IMF controls it. So either or, you can choose.','other',3662.8,3676.9),(1459,69,2757,2764,'And we gave you two structural reasons as to why monetary policy, giving it to the enemies, is significantly worse than having them have fiscal policy, which have not really been done. We talk about monetary policy is so much harder to see and be accountable for. Because of the fact that you can audit a budget and you can\'t audit an interest rate. Because you can track the budget and see, oh, this money did not go where it\'s supposed to. I can clearly see, oh, there\'s corruption here. How am I able to see, oh, this interest rate isn\'t what it\'s supposed to be? Oh, this value isn\'t what it\'s supposed to be? It\'s so much difficult, which is why it incentivizes corruption, because it\'s so easy to hack.','other',3676.9,3708.4),(1460,69,2765,2769,'And secondly, even if that\'s not true, scale of impact is significantly worse if you mess up monetary policy, which I think they can see too. If you mess up monetary policy, it\'s a big deal. If you mess up fiscal policy, okay, $1,000 can\'t do it. You mess up monetary policy, you\'ve messed up the economy for like, structurally, for the next four years. Next 100 years, actually.','other',3708.4,3723.9),(1461,69,2770,2770,'The second thing on growth and stability.','other',3723.9,3726.6),(1462,69,2771,2773,'Okay, we told you two things in our first argument on growth and stability. We talked about the low inflation rate, which I think they agreed with, 0. 7, which is ridiculously low in all of Latin America.','other',3726.8,3737.6),(1463,69,2774,2775,'And secondly, we talked about the issue of investor confidence in FDA. We very clearly told you how we have capital flight in our world, and I think they can see it.','other',3737.6,3745.2),(1464,69,2776,2779,'In the short term, capital flight will occur. And the problem is, you need that capital to diversify the economy as they really want. And that\'s something I\'ve not really dealt with. So in order to diversify, I don\'t know where that their money is coming from.','other',3745.2,3755.5),(1465,69,2780,2791,'It\'s just gonna, maybe they\'ll print it. And if they do print it, it\'s gonna lead to higher inflation again. So double bind here. Let me just, and again, let me just deal with this expert-based thing. First of all, we\'re not expert-based. We don\'t need to be expert-based. They said we don\'t need raw materials to be expert-based. We talked, we gave you the natural resources thing. If we truly don\'t need raw materials, then we\'re gonna import all those things. Then again, our import costs will be really high because we don\'t have the U.S. to trade it. So that\'s again, a contradiction on their side.','other',3755.5,3781.2),(1466,69,2792,2797,'So we don\'t want devaluation because of strong currency definitely. I don\'t know what we don\'t have growth. So even if I take them at their best, where I say, okay, we have big stagnation, the growth doesn\'t happen. Because of the fact that inflation is so low, at worst, because inflation and wage stagnation are equal, purchasing power is important for income class cases. And that is our worst case scenario. On their case, hyperinflation is a big risk on their side, which is definitely better than the class crisis.','other',3781.2,3803.7),(1467,69,2798,2798,'Now we\'re gonna go to a major thing that we talked about, which is crisis.','other',3803.8,3807.5),(1468,69,2799,2801,'So first of all, we talked about the crisis that affects wealth. I think this is pretty clear, because you just raise or lower interest rates. And because we have the same currency and our economies are there, it\'s pretty clear.','other',3807.5,3816.8),(1469,69,2802,2805,'The more important thing is that you need to plan. So three things here. First of all, we need investment confidence to get cheap loans. Without investment confidence, and I\'ve already shown that you can lose investment confidence, we get expensive loans and we get debt tax.','other',3816.8,3827.8),(1470,69,2806,2808,'Either we don\'t get the loans because they refuse to give it to us, or we get really expensive loans and suffer debt crisis. Like they claim we have 100,000 and they actually have 12,000. Second thing is you can prevent people from living with hyperinflation.','other',3827.8,3838.4),(1471,69,2809,2809,'Like Zimbabwe did.','other',3838.4,3838.9),(1472,69,2810,2813,'When they face a crisis, they panic rents, and they face hyperinflation and just mess up the economy completely. And then three, fiscal policy is significantly faster. It\'s pretty clear in this transitional period, they\'re likely to cause an economic crisis in their attempt to mitigate a hypothetical future crisis. And that is why we\'ve created money.','other',3838.9,3852.9),(1473,63,2095,2097,'Two pieces on framing. First, what does a status quo look like? Currently, the USD is used as the main medium of transaction with the Valvo uptake to the USD, and there is no central bank that can control the monetary policy.','other',146.6,177.3),(1474,63,2098,2099,'Second, what\'s our policy? First, we will create an independent central bank.','other',177.8,182.1),(1475,63,2100,2103,'Our model is similar to the Chilean model, which has largely been successful. We will search for central bank leadership, both locally and internationally, requiring previous central banking experience. A two-thirds majority of parliament will be required to appoint or remove members. All of this will ensure that the bank is politically independent.','other',182.4,199.8),(1476,63,2104,2106,'Lastly, we will establish internal audit units. They will review decisions, as well as publish monthly units and quarterly reports to annual IMF reviews. This will create transparency, accountability, and credibility.','other',200.4,211.6),(1477,63,2107,2110,'Second, we will keep the Valvo as the official currency and phase out the exit of the dollar over five years. Initially, we will keep it fluctuating only up to 2% of the dollar. Each quarter, we widen this fluctuation band by 1%, giving markets time to adjust. After year five, the value of the Valvo will fluctuate greatly.','other',212.2,229.1),(1478,63,2111,2113,'Third, we phase out the legal tender of the dollar in those five years. Initially, it will continue alongside the Valvo, while we require all government transactions to be made in the Valvo, giving people time to get used to the new currency and learn to trust it. After year five, the dollar will lose its legal tender, but dollar accounts will remain legal for international trade.','other',229.1,248.8),(1479,63,2114,2115,'During those five years, the government will build up its foreign reserves to equal 100% of money in circulation. This allows Panama to sell US dollars and buy Valvo as to stabilize the currency, effectively increasing demand.','other',248.8,261.2),(1480,63,2116,2116,'Moving on to our first argument about economic growth led by increased international revenue.','other',261.2,266.4),(1481,63,2117,2120,'First, on why the USD rate is so high. The dollar is used by many as the world\'s reserve currency. Countries around the world hold literally trillions in US dollar reserves, increasing demand at its value. Second, why a farmstead flow of foreign money into Panama.','other',266.4,281),(1482,63,2121,2124,'A strong USD makes Panamanian exports more expensive, hurting its competitiveness against countries with lower currencies, such as Mexico or Vietnam. It also discourages foreign investment and tourism as businesses face higher costs. Third, on why foreign influx of money is crucial for growth. Exports make up half of Panama\'s overall economy.','other',281,301.2),(1483,63,2125,2129,'Tourism and services account for 80% of its GDP, increasing those who dramatically increase their income. Crucially, Panama is an ideal place for investors. Its canal access reduces transportation costs drastically, it has a high rate of unemployment population that makes cheap labor potential, and when the US tries to divert its imports from China, it is looking for new locations for manufacturing. The only reason Panama\'s economy has been stagnant so far is the overpriced currency, making them lose to competitors. The implication is that our policy doesn\'t only help self-existing exports, but attract MNCs to create new ones, kicking forward the manufacturing industry that is only 5% of the economy right now.','other',301.2,340.8),(1484,63,2130,2132,'Finally, on the impacts. Firstly, growth translates into increased revenue from tax collection. This allows the public sector to grow and provide people with better welfare, education, and police.','other',340.8,351.1),(1485,63,2133,2136,'It allows the government to pay its crippling debt. It creates a fiscal solution for economic crisis. Secondly, it diversifies the economy. This diversification is crucial.','other',351.1,361.3),(1486,63,2137,2143,'It makes Panama more self-sufficient and less reliant on imports in the long run, thus making it less vulnerable to tariffs and economic pressure from the US. It prevents economic shocks that impact one industry to drag down the entire economy. It creates more diverse job options, with manufacturing jobs being more accessible to the poor and underprivileged. It creates a workers\' cycle. Revenue from manufacturing exports can be reinvested into machinery and R&D that improves manufacturing productivity and creates even more growth. You can\'t improve the productivity of services with technology, so on top there\'s a ceiling to growth. Moving on then to our second argument about crisis.','other',361.7,397.9),(1487,63,2144,2145,'First, we think that the control of monetary policy is crucial for response to economic crisis. During COVID, Panama\'s income debt increased from 40% of GDP to 63%, and its economy shrank 18%.','other',397.9,411.6),(1488,63,2146,2146,'This is nearly double of what other countries with monetary policy experience, all because Panama only had fiscal tools.','other',411.6,418.7),(1489,63,2147,2148,'So why is that bad? Firstly, legislative processes decide fiscal policies too slowly.','other',418.7,424),(1490,63,2149,2153,'A bill needs to be written, opened for public comment, debated in parliament, amended and voted on. This process takes months. This means at best, the crisis drags longer. At worst, by the time you pass a bill, it is too late to solve it. On our side, the central bank can respond promptly with monetary stimulus by lowering interest rates.','other',424,443.4),(1491,63,2154,2155,'Secondly, fiscal solutions achieve link debt. When your only way to create stimulus is to increase spending in a time when there is less productivity and revenue, your only option is to borrow money.','other',443.4,455.5),(1492,63,2156,2157,'With monetary independence, you can create stimulus without a net crisis. Via interest rate reforms, money creation or quantitative easing.','other',455.5,462.4),(1493,63,2158,2163,'Thirdly, fiscal solutions are too politicized. Because fiscal solutions need to be passed in parliament, political incentives bias them. This looks like adoption of irresponsible and populistic solutions. This means no solutions to inflationary crisis as fiscally-poor in the market can only happen through cutting government spending, which is extremely unpopular. On our side, the politically independent central bank can design a responsible solution that will reduce the public pressure on government. Second, on why the Fed won\'t save Taiwan.','other',462.7,493),(1494,63,2164,2166,'Firstly, the Fed will only resolve an economic crisis that makes the US diverse. An economic crisis in Taiwan doesn\'t always mean an economic crisis in the US. Since the US has a much more diverse and wealthy economy, they are less likely to be hit with a concerning crisis.','other',493,506.9),(1495,63,2167,2168,'Secondly, even when a crisis does hit both countries, the Fed will intervene in ways that only benefit the US, not Taiwan. Firstly, because American interests favor prioritizing price stability at the expense of growth and unemployment.','other',506.9,519.9),(1496,63,2169,2172,'The American economy is high-tech and refinery-based. It buys raw materials and then refines them into high-quality products. Which means that when prices increase, it doesn\'t only increase American cost of living, but also cost of production, resulting in business collapse and job loss. In contrast, more developing economies are not as vulnerable to price increases.','other',519.9,538.9),(1497,63,2173,2174,'They are much more vulnerable to stagnation, as it makes them struggle to keep their underdeveloped industries afloat. This is why in the current economic crisis, the Fed has been very reluctant to reduce interest rates.','other',538.9,550.3),(1498,63,2175,2176,'While in countries like India, the central bank has already lowered interest rates several times. Panama\'s economy is much more similar to India\'s than the US, yet its response to crisis is tied to the Fed.','other',550.3,561.3),(1499,63,2177,2180,'Secondly, the Fed also tailors its policy to correspond with Congress fiscal policies, not with Panama\'s. When Congress issues large stimulus packages, like during COVID, the Fed is less likely to cut interest rates because recession risks decrease, and excess stimulus could cause inflation. But the Fed\'s US fiscal stimulus packages don\'t go to Panama\'s Indians, so the recession crisis persists. Finally, on how the harms of crisis can get even worse as a result.','other',561.3,588.3),(1500,63,2181,2181,'When crisis strikes, businesses close, people lose jobs, etc.','other',588.3,591.6),(1501,63,2182,2184,'Delayed aid cannot undo permanent damage. You cannot reconstruct a business that shut down in a stimulus package that arrived too late. You cannot reform a poor person who couldn\'t pay their mortgage with an aid package that arrived too late.','other',591.6,605),(1502,63,2185,2188,'Crisis is straggling longer. People who lost jobs in initial shocks spend less and therefore money circulation lowers further. Investors pull out when they see no response to the crisis and expect it to last. Even after the crisis, you suffer long-term consequences.','other',605,618.4),(1503,63,2189,2191,'Then, you have to pay for years and years with interest. The heightened risk of crisis deters investment and increases interest rate on loans. Even default is not an impossible outcome.','other',618.8,629.2),(1504,63,2192,2193,'It literally happens to everyone who already needs to process a debt crisis resulting from dollarization. Today, we reclaim what 120 years of dollar dependence stole, the economic prosperity of Panama. Thank you.','other',629.2,642.2),(1505,65,2323,2325,'This is a question of the US, because it is a proposition that we will literally end the period. It is shockable by its use of the US dollar to prevent it from diversifying, enriching a small elite while ensuring that the country remains one of the most unequal. Its link to the US dollar means that it lacks monetary independence to protect citizens from recurring crises, and leaves it at risk of a crippling financial crisis.','other',1241.5,1367.6),(1506,65,2326,2327,'Two simple questions. First, I\'m gonna deal with growth, and second, on stability, and possibly on extending third-party later in my speech.','other',1367.6,1375.1),(1507,65,2328,2329,'Firstly, on growth. The main mechanism I would like to, the main line of mechanism we get from their judge is about increased income costs.','other',1375.1,1382.8),(1508,65,2330,2331,'We would argue two things in response. One, we\'re going to argue that we would prioritize exports over imports, and second, I would argue that the increase in import costs will be marginal at best.','other',1382.8,1392.6),(1509,65,2332,2337,'Firstly, why wouldn\'t we argue the prioritization of exports is important? A few mechanisms. One, the increase in revenue is higher than the increase in cost because of the way manufacturing works. You buy cheaper, raw material, refine them, and then sell expensive, refined products. The final product to export is just much more expensive than the materials they import for, so the currency rate markup, which is the same for both, pays a more negative cost. Second, exports increase income, and imports increase the cost of living.','other',1392.6,1419.7),(1510,65,2338,2340,'Those cancel out. What doesn\'t cancel out is growth, which is the only lever that can make wages out in prices by increasing productivity in the economy. This is only enabled through exports.','other',1419.7,1429),(1511,65,2341,2342,'Third, on job creation, which is enabled only through exports, which is logically higher to the affordability of any imported goods. If goods are cheap but people are unemployed, they still can\'t afford them.','other',1429,1440.2),(1512,65,2343,2345,'Fourth, because expensive imports are outwards to short-term development. Because when market growth and diversification manifest, we will have more local production self-sufficiency. Import reliance will then solve itself over time, stagnation in knowledge.','other',1440.2,1452.3),(1513,65,2346,2348,'Fifth, Panama is surrounded by a lot of cheap exporters, as these make the majority of Latin American countries. It\'s not short for cheap import options. It is much more threatened by export competition.','other',1452.3,1463.6),(1514,65,2349,2349,'Furthermore, I would argue that there is a bunch of mitigatory mechanisms against expensive imports.','other',1463.9,1469.2),(1515,65,2350,2354,'First and most importantly, the vast majority of Panama\'s imports are just not necessities. The country is largely self-reliant on food, producing enough heat, fruits, and other stuff. Basic goods are only around 1. 8% of all of Panama\'s imports. Import prices then don\'t impact cost of living as much as they impact the cost of luxuries for the well-fed elites.','other',1469.2,1488.9),(1516,65,2355,2357,'Second, many of Panama\'s products, many products that Panama imports, could be produced domestically. Guatemala, for instance, has seen its electronic manufacturing sector growing dramatically. The core difference between Panama and Guatemala is not population size or skill, but that the overvalued dollars means that it is far too cheap to import these goods, and there is no incentive to draw up these sectors and do it internally, nor for foreign-income investors to use Panama as a base of goods to export from.','other',1488.9,1514.5),(1517,65,2358,2363,'Third, companies use a different pricing system, accounting for different purchasing power in different countries. This means that prices of imports in the U.S. can become unaffordable with the federal law. By laws of supply and demand, companies will adjust prices down accordingly. This is why, for example, a Big Mac costs less in India than it does in the U.S. This largely deals with the third mechanism in the first argument about import-import costs.','other',1514.5,1535.8),(1518,65,2364,2364,'Crucially, I would also argue their two responses against our first argument in proving growth rates are sufficient.','other',1535.8,1541.8),(1519,65,2365,2367,'Their first response argues that due to the service-based nature of the economy, the devaluation harms the current industry. I would just ask them to explain how this happens. Clearly, an increase in economic growth in the U.S.','other',1541.8,1555),(1520,65,2368,2369,'and the currency devaluation doesn\'t impact the cost of internal services in Panama. Furthermore, I would argue that diversification just tends to be a more important impact in this debate.','other',1555.3,1564.6),(1521,65,2370,2375,'Panama, the Panama Canal, offers great short-term opportunities, but if not exploited for more domestic growth than it is now, it is not a sustainable source of revenue in the long term. For example, alternatives to the Panama Canal in the northward shipping passage through the Arctic, which has only been viable in the last few years due to the Hawaii\'s caps melting, or in the last year itself, the number of ships passing through the Arctic Ocean and the Bering Sea has tripled compared to previous years. There\'s a lot of other proposals, for example, about Chinook or O\'ahua and the Pan-Canada Canal there as well. All of this means that the Panama Canal is losing and will continue to lose its competitive advantage. It is lethal for the future of the Hawaii without diversification. If we don\'t take advantage of our opportunity to be able to attract businesses or to manufacture domestically in Panama, and we hedge our risks against this by replacing canal revenues over time, like revenues from manufacturing exports and creating domestic growth less reliant on international trade, Panama is doomed for failure.','other',1564.6,1616.4),(1522,65,2376,2377,'Their second response against our lack of argumentation argues that there is low raw material in Panama, which means we can\'t manufacture. Why then do we argue diversification is possible?','other',1616.4,1625.9),(1523,65,2378,2378,'On an intuitive basis, you don\'t need natural resources or a giant population to create a manufacturing center.','other',1625.9,1632.7),(1524,65,2379,2380,'Look at Taiwan or Singapore. Both have few resources and a small population, yet a small, strong manufacturing plant.','other',1632.7,1638.7),(1525,65,2381,2382,'Panama is strategically located. If you set up a company in Panama, you are close to the canals and shipping main.','other',1638.7,1645.5),(1526,65,2383,2385,'This keeps transport costs low. There\'s a shoot-and-shoot advantage. The US is trying to cut or reduce Chinese manufacturing, and Panama is among the best-placed countries to benefit.','other',1645.5,1653.6),(1527,65,2386,2387,'It is not far from the US as Vietnam. It has untapped labor markets, et cetera.','other',1654,1657.7),(1528,65,2388,2388,'But all of this, and the weaker currency, means exporting is easier as well.','other',1657.7,1662.3),(1529,65,2389,2392,'All of this means it\'s really possible for diversification to occur. All of this creates the first clash on growth. Second clash, on price and system. Their first argument argues, and there are two mechanisms behind this, argues low inflation and stability occurs as a result of the USD, and also investor confidence occurs.','other',1662.4,1681.1),(1530,65,2393,2396,'In response to this, I\'m going to prove largely a few things. Firstly, I\'m just going to prove that the central bank will be done well. Crucially, this will then mitigate against the vast majority of their analysis that argues, for example, elements like corruption or mismanagement of finances. Firstly, a lot of our model already mitigates the risk of corruptibility, as it prevents the government from doing things like impacting more broad board members without a broad consensus, et cetera.','other',1681.1,1703.7),(1531,65,2397,2400,'Second, other institutions can hold the central bank accountable. For example, the IMF will hold the central bank accountable, or also the strong financial sector in account, including corporations like, by banks like JPMorgan, also have incentive to pressure the government to avoid meddling in the central bank. Thirdly, corrupt governments are stupid, and no meddling with the voters\' bank, and no meddling with the central bank risks rapid inflation and demotion for their credit score, which voters will punish them for. Crucially, Hamill, these voters know of these crises, look, they know Argentina, for example, and therefore have high incentive to hold the government accountable in any case.','other',1703.7,1737.5),(1532,65,2401,2401,'Jack, go ahead.','poi',1737.5,1738.3),(1533,65,2402,2402,'What incentive does JPMorgan Private Equity Firm have with Panama Central Bank?','poi',1738.3,1742.4),(1534,65,2403,2404,'They literally operate in the country. If the currency in that country is screwed, then obviously they\'re also screwed.','other',1742.4,1750.1),(1535,65,2405,2405,'The second thing I would like to do in response to the first argument, though, is also just prove the stability of the currency.','other',1750.1,1757.7),(1536,65,2406,2408,'Hamill, there are four reasons as to why the currency will be stable. One, for the vast majority of their firms to materialize, they don\'t just need to prove devaluation, which we concede and welcome, but uncontrollable, rapid devaluation. This is unlikely for all of the obvious reasons, like central bank intervention happening.','other',1757.8,1773.9),(1537,65,2409,2411,'Secondly, we obviously preempted a lot of this from the brink. For example, our foreign reserves enabled fast response, and despite what they\'re claiming, it\'s within our fiat to argue that we will slowly reduce the peg. Thirdly, Panama has a good track record of financial responsibility.','other',1773.9,1787.2),(1538,65,2412,2413,'Like after COVID, it maintained fairly low wealth, fairly low debt, established a sovereign wealth, and et cetera. And fourthly, in the immediate term, like 6% of the global trade goes to the Panama Canal.','other',1787.8,1799),(1539,65,2414,2415,'It\'s a lot of transactions in the above wall, contributing both its stability and investor promise. Furthermore, Panama\'s importance also means that international actors have incentives to help ensure a smooth transition and avoid monetary crises, which means, for example, institutions like the IMF or nations have also incentives to help out here.','other',1799,1815.2),(1540,65,2416,2416,'All of this in response to their lack of orientation on investor confidence and stability.','other',1815.2,1819),(1541,65,2417,2418,'Finally, I also just want to quickly deal with their absurd assertion that the US, that economic crises can be solved in their world. Clearly, even if the US and Panama are both impacted by a crisis, we prove from our second argument that the method by which they would like to deal with the crisis is different, which is why their second response is going to work better.','other',1819,1840.5),(1542,65,2419,2419,'Oh, I\'m gonna do it, you can\'t be quicker.','other',1840.7,1842.5),(1543,67,2527,2530,'First thing, because I think when Argentina implemented a similar policy, I mean, because I think the current US administration speaks of the self-reliance of other states rather than free riding off the US, meaning it is presumably going to be reacting in a much more positive way than they would characterize in so far as it\'s reason for a more self-reliant policy. Third of all, I think the current administration rather hates the current effect management. Therefore, I think this reads into this current justification as to why the Fed is going back. But even if you don\'t take this panel, even all of your reasons are true.','other',2417.7,2610.9),(1544,67,2531,2533,'This isn\'t the pivoting point because if you look at the status quo, the current Trump administration has already asserted to take the control militarily of the Panama Canal. It\'s unclear to me why this one specific policy is going to be a pivoting point for their case. Static. First things first, let\'s have an implementation.','other',2610.9,2627.7),(1545,67,2534,2535,'I think there are one line of response that we need to follow. There\'s a corruption in the status quo and that just might be the result of a bad way of consent for that.','other',2627.7,2637.5),(1546,67,2536,2539,'There are two ways in which not to do this. First, in terms of the policy. I want to note that all of our policies just effectively mitigated your claim. Even if the government is in fact corrupt, all of our arguments about things like external checks and balances existing were able to prove to you that your policy wouldn\'t land on a claim that way.','other',2637.5,2654.1),(1547,67,2540,2543,'But even if your thing does, I\'ll still engage in procedural supports. First, even if those governments are corrupt, we provide you selfish incentives of the current government to just have the economy that is just much more stable than our center of the house. We talked about how central bankers do have a reputational incentive to care about the people on the ground. And notably, as per your own mechanism that helps stability is important, people are likely to hold these individuals accountable.','other',2654.1,2678.1),(1548,67,2544,2545,'You just literally don\'t want them to have the type of inflation you have in the first place. Secondly, because corrupt governments aren\'t stupid, meddling in the central bank risks rapid inflation, which would obviously affect your money that you have in your pocket, which is the reason why I think you have selfish incentives as well.','other',2678.1,2694.2),(1549,67,2546,2547,'But even if you don\'t, if you ask our panel, we\'ve already proved to you why international activists like the IMF and JP Morgan will be in parallel. Their first response is that international technocrats have no incentives in parallel.','other',2694.2,2704.2),(1550,67,2548,2551,'One, I think IMF obviously does, because in the UN, the literal mandate of the IMF is to care about the stability of the developing country. But secondly, in terms of the JP Morgan and international institutions, they too have an incentive to do so. Hence, so far, they have businesses operating within Panama and in instances where there\'s currency volatility, there was like, as per your own mechanism, you obviously wouldn\'t want that, right? Which is the reason why we\'re likely to hold these governments accountable with the foreign side of the house.','other',2704.2,2733.5),(1551,67,2552,2555,'But even if you don\'t take that, and even if you buy into the thought that Panama is corrupt, that isn\'t becoming the reason why central bank will be corrupt. Because this assumes the most basic version of corruption and does not realize country can be corrupt in some ways, but not in others. The US is in fact corrupt, if you think about it, in the level of insider trade within the Congress. But that does not follow that Fed is corrupt, which is the reason why our current policy specifically ensures the incorruptibility of central bank, which is the reason why you just cannot throw away a lot of reasons as to why corruption is taking this debate already.','other',2733.5,2765.2),(1552,67,2556,2559,'The final thing I wanna do to deal with it under this implementation claim is your idea that you have to get two-thirds parliament approval every time. No, that wasn\'t our policy. Our policy was to only appoint and remove members and get consensus within it. But literally, it\'s not about every single policy that\'s just part of the debate.','other',2765.2,2782.7),(1553,67,2560,2568,'First things first, that\'s charitable economic growth. I wanna note strategically, this was a very charitable argument to begin with. Because we say devaluation in a currency is a consensus in a debate. It is their reason as to why we have more imports that are expensive. It is our reason as to why we have more cheaper exports. And here\'s where Seiji\'s analysis would be particularly important. Because what we told you from Seiji\'s speech was that in instances where Panama is currently relying on the service sector, it is critical for Panama to diversify its economy, it is critical for Panama to increase its revenue through the exports. And here is how the lack of responses to that and the immediate extreme, like the direct weighing mechanism as to why you want to bifurcate imports over the other. The final thing I wanna point out is that they just mischaracterize what the status quo of Panama looks like.','other',2782.7,2834.2),(1554,67,2569,2571,'Because what we have pointed out is that even though Panama Canal is giving you sufficient amount of revenue in status quo, you cannot rely on them in the future. Which is the reason why you must diversify economy. Which is the reason why you must access cheaper exports in order to just grow its jobs, in order to grow the manufacturing sector that is currently unreliable.','other',2834.2,2852.7),(1555,67,2572,2575,'Then they say diversification is impossible. Here is the process. One, on an employment level, you do not need natural resources or a giant population to create the manufacturing sector. Look at Taiwan, look at Singapore, both have few resources and a small population to get a strong manufacturing sector.','other',2852.7,2869.2),(1556,67,2576,2578,'Secondly, Panama is strategically located. If you set up a company in Panama, you are both the channels in the shipping lane. That keeps transport costs low and has a huge advantage in the use of our set of channels.','other',2869.2,2880.8),(1557,67,2579,2580,'Third of all, the US has spiked the cut where we use Chinese manufacturing in supply chains. Panama is also the best place for countries to benefit, which is the reason why it is likely to have the capacity to diversify our set in-house.','other',2880.8,2893.3),(1558,67,2581,2582,'Before I move on to experience the problems of the individual land. Which of these people do I prefer, unfair or comparative, in a political economy?','other',2893.3,2900.1),(1559,67,2583,2585,'Okay, are we giving you magnificent reasons? And furthermore, I don\'t think you can say, okay, who\'s out of the league? Now let\'s just experience the people that you think they think of their world.','other',2900.1,2913),(1560,67,2586,2588,'The first thing they may disavow is torture capital flow out of Panama. I think the first thing I want to point out is that all of our reasons and stability allow you to be anarchist. But secondly, the incentives we make make it to maintain your operations in Panama still exist with its panels and developed financial sector.','other',2913,2926.5),(1561,67,2589,2590,'It\'s the reason why it\'s all through the US capital is like spiked, vertical, but made. Even if it\'s true, only torture farm do you have comfortable in this debate, which is the reason why we are more than happy to just prioritize longer farm longer benefit.','other',2926.5,2940.4),(1562,67,2591,2593,'Last, last step in terms of stability. The first thing I want to explain is to explain why response to crisis is more important than the number of crisis that exist and they say is more powerful. Four main mechanisms.','other',2940.4,2953.6),(1563,67,2594,2596,'Firstly, some economic crisis are inevitable even in the strongest parties. This means a hard to inevitable response that is certain to manifest in some instances. In contrast, currency inflated out of control is speculative.','other',2953.6,2966.1),(1564,67,2597,2598,'It is a force of nature that has to happen in our sector and house. And our expectation being observed that their thing was about inflation happening, but they automatically assume that there\'s going to be a hyperinflation of our world, but all of their arguments only stand in terms of the most extreme spectrum, meaning that their argument is speculative.','other',2966.1,2985.1),(1565,67,2599,2604,'Secondly, our analysis of the response to crisis is logically prior to the markets of currency volatility. If we are able to properly and effectively respond in time, even inflation, we can just nip it in the bud. Thirdly, volatility risks are imperative. Opposition needs to recognize the risk that they allow on their world. The risk of over-reliance on the service industry, the risk of the Federal Reserve\'s policy causing a crisis in Panama, the risk of defaulting on debt, and the risk of fiscal policy fueled inflation. If anything, we think the risk is higher in their world as it is asymmetric on both sides of the house.','other',2985.1,3018.9),(1566,67,2605,2606,'It\'s our ability to respond to the crisis far better and far sooner in the house, which is the reason why the monetary policy, which we can flexibly use with Panama\'s situation, was critical to win a civil league class as well. We win with the metric as well.','other',3018.9,3033.4),(1567,70,2814,2816,'We come to the close of this great debate about the proposition of the ZEIT. I\'m gonna give it to you. And then we\'ll turn.','other',3852.9,3862.8),(1568,70,2817,2819,'We talked about how other institutions like the Central Bank, other institutions like the IMF will hold the Central Bank. Obviously IMF has a lot of money to go back to its incentive there. It also came with like a fear of capacity to get loans in the future, which means Panama also has incentive.','other',3862.8,3967.6),(1569,70,2820,2823,'In South Africa, we also talked about how like corrupt governments just aren\'t stupid. Not only will voters punish them, and Panama\'s race is inherently very, very close in who gets to, and the fragmented parliament also needs this rotation of power between parties, et cetera. All of this for the Central Bank will be done fairly well. Second, we also proved that currency will be somewhat stable.','other',3967.6,3985.5),(1570,70,2824,2830,'For there are terms to materialize, they obviously diverted us through rapid uncontrollable devaluation. We concede devaluation to a certain extent anyway. We preempted the rapid and extreme nature of this from Ukraine by arguing that foreign reserves, for example, can be used to buy Valvoa with USD. We also talked about the fairly good track record as they concede that Panama has a financial management. And we talked about the nature of Panama and its importance in a global economy, meaning international actors of incentive to help them. But even if they were to come in in order to prove hyper-devaluation, we proved that Panama, like every policy change, obviously has its risks. They can\'t just claim speculative risks and then claim de-dollarization is not worth it.','other',3985.5,4024),(1571,70,2831,2832,'There are other existing risks you must weigh this claim against. For example, the risk of an economy that relies on one canal in a single sector industry or a monetary crisis that Panama has no tools to resolve or defaulting on debt.','other',4024,4034.3),(1572,70,2833,2835,'Compared to these risks on hyper-devaluation, the way is, one, these harms have materialized before and are resurging to materialize again, as some form of economic crisis always happens once a decade. Second, these harms last longer as these are structural risks as opposed to these short-term priorities coming with Valvoa that are eventually obviously stabilized. And thirdly, these harms can be fixed, at least not without independent monetary policy, as opposed to inflation, which can be moderated.','other',4034.3,4059.2),(1573,70,2836,2837,'At worst, the response to the crisis is even more important than stability. There were three main mechanisms from the ZUJI to prove this to you.','other',4059.2,4067.2),(1574,70,2838,2840,'We proved response to crisis, despite what they said from our first speech, by proving that US monetary policy, even if it is done, it doesn\'t align with what Panama needs. For example, the US prioritizing stability over growth and unemployment, or US policy being tailored to obviously US fiscal policy, which is different from Panama. Furthermore, there was just no response to two mechanisms from our first layer under this argument that proves that fiscal policy actually leads to, for example, the accumulation of debt or the politicization of fiscal policy and the bad fiscal policy.','other',4067.2,4095.3),(1575,70,2841,2842,'Second question on growth. Firstly, and most importantly, this question is actually fairly important, because despite what they claim, Panama is not doing fine and won\'t be fine in the long term.','other',4095.3,4107.8),(1576,70,2843,2845,'The real unemployment rate in Panama is 30%. Alternatives to the Panama being formed. The majority of its economy is based on an untaxable service sector that is informal.','other',4107.8,4115.4),(1577,70,2846,2850,'We also talked about how when we diversify, Panama can become more self-sufficient, which prevents it, which saves it against, for example, potential US tariffs, or it prevents economic shocks that impact one industry, which drags out the entire economy. It also leads to more diverse job options that allows for, for example, the less educated and more impoverished individuals to actually get a job, which is a big problem in the inequal industry. And we proved that manufacturing industries has more potentiality for growth, while service industries cannot innovate for growth. Crucially, we would argue that they, this dispels their silver bullet on empirical ground. With that out of the way, we would also argue that it\'s actually fairly easy to diversify, and it\'s possible to diversify because of Panama.','other',4115.4,4155.8),(1578,70,2851,2854,'They claim the lack of natural resources prevents Panama from diversifying. But obviously, manufacturing doesn\'t necessitate you create the natural resources in the country. You import resources, you make the product, and then you export the product. The low GDP response also doesn\'t do it.','other',4155.8,4172.7),(1579,70,2855,2856,'Historically, Singapore was poor before they were able to create a manufacturing industry. We would argue that it is possible to diversify.','other',4172.7,4179.2),(1580,70,2857,2857,'This is an important impact, and we win the debate here.','other',4179.2,4182),(1581,70,2858,2860,'Have a careful vote. Thank you. Thank you very much for coming. Once again, thank you very much for a great talk.','other',4182,4188.6),(1582,56,1507,1508,'This is a follow-up, the lesson is well-understanded, and we can deal with the sets of problems that they talk about with other forms of direct regulation. If they want to say, just stay this week, it\'s not enforcing that direct regulation, those are the same weak states who will rightfully, incredibly badly, not to do the sets of things that they want to do, and instead subvert democracy to make things worse.','other',475.2,492.5),(1583,56,1509,1511,'Two arguments promote the opposition. First of all, this massively frees down investment and mergers and acquisitions, both of which I would. Secondly, I\'d like to see a worse, less easy, and ultimately more harmful stance to aim for.','other',492.6,502.3),(1584,56,1512,1512,'Let\'s start with why this frees down investment.','other',502.5,504.3),(1585,56,1513,1515,'The first thing to say is that it massively broadens the scope of what can be used. So the first premise here is why does this make mergers way? Firstly, this massively broadens the scope of what can be used to reject the merger.','other',504.6,514.9),(1586,56,1516,1518,'Secondly, that scope becomes nearly boundless, but it\'s non-economic harms, the nearly boundless categorization. And it\'s incredibly easy, even if there are codifications, to argue for further expansion of it, because you can say, well, the combination of this right and this right implies this further right, this implied right, natural laws that we ought to care about. So it\'s incredibly easy to go further.','other',515.2,534.4),(1587,56,1519,1520,'Thirdly, these are much more interpretable, because they\'re a lot more about value trade-offs, which also means that you raise the uncertainty, because judge-specific risk is more specific, it is higher, because particular judges are going to interpret this in many more different ways, whereas judges at the moment, when it\'s a less subjective stand, are likely to have more correlated views of how it works. And it also shows the willingness of courts and of legislators to continue to expand antitrust legislation into the future, which means that you\'re incredibly likely to have people think that mergers are less likely.','other',534.4,565.2),(1588,56,1521,1522,'Why is this a problem? First, because you get less mergers, and secondly, because you get less initial investment.','other',565.5,570.4),(1589,56,1523,1523,'Let\'s start with mergers.','other',570.7,571.6),(1590,56,1524,1525,'Obviously, when it\'s harder to get a merger approved, you\'re less likely to attempt that merger, because a high level of investment that has to go into setting up a merger and finding a merger partner, you have to do things like due diligence, set it up, that is an incredibly high cost, it won\'t do, or you might want to put it on, like, clearly you want to do it sometime, but on the margin you\'re less likely to do it, at the point where it\'s less likely to get through.','other',571.8,590),(1591,56,1526,1531,'And we think that mergers are on net good, because they lead to larger scale. It means you can operate more efficiently, because you get economies of scale. It makes firms more durable to idiosyncratic market shocks, so they\'re less likely to go down, leaving consumers without goods and workers without jobs. It means you can reach more consumers more easily, so you have more scale. It means you can invest more on net, you can do more innovation. It means that profitable parts of the business can cross-subsidise non-profitable parts of those businesses, though that means that these mergers are on net good.','other',590.1,617.1),(1592,56,1532,1535,'And I\'ll note that those mergers are likely to be pro-competitive, for the reason that, first of all, that under the consumer well-being standard, they wouldn\'t be let through, if they were not pro-competitive. This team says we just might not notice the fact that a merger had happened. In almost every jurisdiction around the world, and in a few months in Australia as well, there\'s mandatory notification of the regulator when you reach a certain threshold. They\'re not going to ignore that from happening.','other',617.3,639.6),(1593,56,1536,1540,'But secondly, I would argue that mergers are on net pro-competitive. And the reason for that is that without mergers, you often get one or two large firms who rise to the top, and then no one else is ever able to compete with them, or grow to compete with them. Because if it\'s theoretical competition, but I\'m 80% of the market and you\'re 2% of the market, you\'re never going to have the kind of economies of scale to be a reasonable competitor. But when all the smaller firms can amalgamate, they can then become reasonable competition. So actually, mergers are on net pro-competitive.','other',639.9,664.1),(1594,56,1541,1545,'I\'ll also note that there\'s now an incentive for larger firms not to provide business-to-business goods that allow a fixing of those externalities that would allow smaller firms to merge. So to take an example of this, Google letting you use their login on their website is really important for you being able to ameliorate privacy concerns. But if Google knew that it would allow the small players in the market to amalgamate, because there might be a concern otherwise that they would have too much data and there would be privacy concerns around it, they\'re unlikely to let those small players use that Google login. So they don\'t want to provide goods that allow the amelioration of those externalities, because they know that the amelioration of those externalities makes it easier for there to be competition with them. So now we get more competition.','other',664.1,704),(1595,56,1546,1552,'I\'ll also note that this now means that competition law becomes less popular on their side. First of all, which is now making decisions about things that aren\'t universally agreed on. Secondly, it\'s associated with the left, because under the status quo, the right can be pro-market, but can say, oh, this is necessary for the market to function efficiently. Whereas now, you\'re saying we should trade off market efficiency for other outcomes, which makes it far less popular. It can also be framed as a form of corruption, because you\'re using it as a political weapon. And you\'re now likely to have lots of people who lose their jobs and can make the argument that my job is to trade off for fucking squirrels. That makes competition law less popular, which means you\'re less likely to be forced in the first instance, if they\'re worried about competition.','other',704,740.5),(1596,56,1553,1556,'We now get less investment into really important things. And if you don\'t do the initial investment, if you\'re worried you might not be able to merge or acquiesce, first, you often do the initial investment because you want to get bought out in the end. And you\'re more likely to be able to make your business bigger if along the way you can purchase out other firms. So the lack of ability to emerge, it means that my initial investment is worth less, which means I\'m less likely to do that initial investment, yes.','other',740.5,763.5),(1597,56,1557,1558,'The issue is when these firms become too big to fill, you\'re unable to have these environmental and privacy regulations in the future, because a fine is a drop in the bucket for these large companies. You need to have this broader regulation.','poi',763.5,774.9),(1598,56,1559,1563,'Then have larger fines. Like there are incredibly large fines that have been put against huge companies in the order of billions of dollars, where you just ban and you send people to jail. Like the regulation doesn\'t always work. On-net regulation is followed. Come on, most debater I\'ve seen in the world.','other',774.9,789.6),(1599,56,1564,1566,'Second argument, why is this a worse standard to aim for? Because when you\'re optimizing for their standard and more things, you can less optimize for our standard of economic things. I\'m here to explain why the economics is more important.','other',789.6,799.4),(1600,56,1567,1572,'First of all, it\'s easier to be able to optimize for. This is many more steps removed from the company. So you do the chain of causation is less certain. That makes all of the harms less speculative. It\'s also more dependent on exogenous outcomes that they can\'t control. So it\'s much harder to optimize for.','other',799.4,812.9),(1601,56,1573,1575,'And it\'s a value distinction, right? We don\'t want, it\'s trivially good that cheaper prices and better quality is better for everyone. But this is about a series of value trade-offs which makes it incredibly difficult to optimize for.','other',812.9,822.9),(1602,56,1576,1579,'But even if we could optimize for both equally, we would say this is a more important standard because economic conditions are the most important thing to most people around the world living close to the property line. Because this matters to everyone, not just people who particularly care about pricing. It matters to consumers, it matters to firms and their growth and their workers when they can get cheaper input goods. And we can probably predict it much better on our side when we have things like market modeling.','other',822.9,844),(1603,56,1580,1584,'We have lots of super analogous examples. We also get far better, I\'ll also note, we can on our side regulate out the set of things that they want to talk about. Because we can set a baseline. We can say you can\'t do these bad things for instance dumping, but you can\'t set a baseline for competition law because you\'re worried about, for instance, breaking out supply. You can\'t set a baseline for how much innovation you have to do.','other',844,864.2),(1604,56,1585,1587,'And the next thing to say is that consumer law actually feeds into other things. The consumer standard feeds into other things because when goods are cheaper, consumers can more optimize for themselves what kind of privacy they want because they can discriminate between goods far easier when the cost of doing so is less. Because economic development allows you to develop things that end up in the future contributing to those externalities.','other',864.2,884.6),(1605,56,1588,1591,'We also get less corruption on our side because when they talk about democracy, I don\'t think it\'s a good idea that the government is able to make decisions about who can and cannot speak. I think that the government shouldn\'t be making that decision. I think that in the worst case when democracy is actually under threat, that is incredibly likely to get weaponized in order to throw down your political enemies. That is a huge harm.','other',884.6,904.6),(1606,58,1675,1677,'I\'ll get into the specific claims made by open government. The most obvious question you need to ask in this debate is, is trading on business growth, or these goals, environmental, privacy, and democratic protections actually work? This is central to whether you should empower these organizations to, against democracy, through the will of judges, decide to do so.','other',1402.3,1516.1),(1607,58,1678,1678,'I\'m going to turn it over to the back.','other',1516.1,1517.6),(1608,58,1679,1682,'Firstly, there\'s little to no proof from open government of how mergers and acquisitions actually result in these harms. There is a very, very small number of even examples of how this would occur. That just suggests to you that, at best, I think this happens to a very small extent, even if closing government later is a proof that I don\'t think we\'ll be able to prove it. That\'s a good thing.','other',1517.6,1534),(1609,58,1683,1685,'Secondly, there\'s also no proof of why causing these harms necessarily comes from a lack of competition, which is to say that a tech firm buying a data company can significantly harm the privacy of its users and of consumers in general, whilst being in a very competitive market. And that\'s important, because it dramatically expands the scope of these regulatory bodies to consider, often what is only mildly anti-competitive behavior, to be something that is worth being shut down. So they\'re now acting beyond the scope of what they were previously acting for.','other',1534,1559.8),(1610,58,1686,1687,'For the rest of this week, then I would now intend to simply have proven both of those things to be triable and probably to assume a close government approval later. Why is it not worth trading our business approach for these goals?','other',1559.8,1568.9),(1611,58,1688,1694,'Firstly, as we explained, these harms are not universally agreed to be harms in the first place. Not all people believe that harms of the environment are actually harm. The idea that animals and trees actually matter as beings is something that takes the least, and I believe, but a lot of people don\'t believe it. Maybe that\'s bad, but it\'s true of a lot of people. Even things like privacy, for example. A lot of people want companies to have more data so that they can offer them better services. Comparatively, higher costs are every election, year on year, agreed to be a huge concern for consumers that everyone doesn\'t want, because it objectively makes your life more expensive and more difficult.','other',1568.9,1599.2),(1612,58,1695,1698,'Secondly, even when they are agreed to be harm, the trade-off is much more ambiguous than with consumer issues. Environmentalism, for example, is often a controversial issue. It\'s something that affects a small group of people, the people who live in that area that is being polluted, at the benefit of everyone else\'s able access to the goods that it provides. Whereas consumer protections affect everyone, because everyone bears that cost from the outside optionality of the products.','other',1599.2,1623.2),(1613,58,1699,1701,'And thirdly, business growth is very important. It gives people access to jobs, but because it prevents businesses from increasing by acquisition or emergence of products, because those two businesses, it creates more efficiency, which economy growth is substantially better than, so it makes companies larger, therefore more resilient to economic shocks. So the business growth should value as something that is important to people\'s lives as a moral concern, just like the moral concerns of the environmentalists are important.','other',1623.2,1654.3),(1614,58,1702,1703,'The other thing that I want to prove here is that Joseph\'s book, Urban Decisiveness, overly valuing these kinds of protections, like protections of the environment and democracy, over business concerns. Why is that true?','other',1654.3,1664.6),(1615,58,1704,1707,'Firstly, because they\'re appointed by politicians, and some politicians care a lot about business concerns, but others care a lot about the environment and about privacy and about those issues. For example, many left-wing governments value those things as being above and far more important than other things. Socialist governments, for example, value those things at the entire expense of economic growth. These people will select, deliberately, judges that are most likely to be appointed when they engage in corporate politics, which is popular, but are common for cities.','other',1664.6,1690.3),(1616,58,1708,1712,'Those judges are unaccountable once appointed. They often cannot be removed because of separation between legislature and the courts, to mean that when they make bad decisions, or judges that have proven to have a record of overly leaning towards environmentalism cannot be reviewed. And that level of judicial activism is common around the world. For example, judges in New Zealand are extremely judicially active, as well as judges in South America. This is a very common problem.','other',1690.3,1711.5),(1617,58,1713,1714,'But really, they don\'t have a very narrow view. They think only about the law, and whether it\'s great for specific criteria in that matter, because if they\'ve proven a level of harm to the environment, they likely are overly cautious.','other',1711.5,1720.2),(1618,58,1715,1717,'On the way, what you should believe is, firstly, this trade-off is unlikely to be worth it in theory or in practice. But secondly, and arguably more importantly than that, this is clearly something that\'s better handled by governments and by the mob. Because if the trade-off is ambiguous, and it\'s unclear whether it\'s worth it, you put it in the hands of people that are accountable to the public, rather than unelected judges who have no conclusion on how to mandate to do so.','other',1720.2,1740.2),(1619,58,1718,1718,'Let\'s just respond specifically to the claims made by this team.','other',1740.2,1742.8),(1620,58,1719,1720,'Their first claim is that this will lead to more data being released on the level of environmental and privacy harm that occur. So, preemptively release that data.','other',1742.8,1749.1),(1621,58,1721,1724,'Firstly, there\'s no reason to believe they\'d preemptively release that data, right? They might store that data so they can later use it in court. But it\'s unclear why they would make that data public unless you mandate them to. Secondly, it\'s deeply possible they hide that data because if you have to prove that they were going to cause some harm to the environment, cause some harm to privacy, they can also just never store that data so that if they were in court, you wouldn\'t be able to prove the merger or argument decision was going to lead to that harm.','other',1749.1,1770.2),(1622,58,1725,1727,'Thirdly, collecting that data is often a bit expensive. It slows down the business because you have to do a lot more record keeping and keeping track of that data which means you do actually cause a trade-off to be a positive event, which in many cases isn\'t worth it. You don\'t need to do that because the merger or argument decision was fine in the first place.','other',1770.2,1783.3),(1623,58,1728,1729,'And finally, we just think there are other better ways for this. You can directly regulate this and systematically collect data if you believe this is absolutely necessary.','other',1783.3,1788.9),(1624,58,1730,1730,'Next, it says this will lead to more political will for M&As to be controlled and blocked.','other',1788.9,1793),(1625,58,1731,1736,'This isn\'t the easiest rule in the world, but it\'s still untrue. Firstly, these are much more ambiguous now. DPM explicitly says that issues like harm to the environment or harm to privacy or harm to democracy occur over very long periods of time and are things that consumers are unable to detect and therefore must be in the hands of the FCC or other organizations. That is the concession that now calls government roles best to stand by. If you believe that\'s true, you must believe that consumers are unaware of why regulators are making these decisions or at the very least were skeptical of them. Which means they see regulators blocking things that would create jobs, maintain business stability, and support growth at the expense of something they have not realised yet.','other',1793,1829.3),(1626,58,1737,1742,'Comparatively, other things are much more clear which is why those sensible levels of regulation get more support on our side. But secondly, it\'s not politically unpalatable to look specifically to right-wing parties. These are the obvious things that skew towards left. Issues like privacy, democracy, environmentalism, other left-wing issues, to which specifically right-wing parties are opposed to. The impact of this is corporate leadership is less popular in general to fund regulators and it\'s specifically popular for right-wing parties to remove their funding. That\'s important because when they are right, they\'re less funded. Yes?','other',1829.3,1854.7),(1627,58,1743,1744,'Your take seems entirely inconsistent. The vast majority of people care primarily about economics and politicians probably appoint people who don\'t go crazy far left-wing and only care about environmental issues while your own characterisation is beneficially appointed.','poi',1854.7,1866.7),(1628,58,1745,1749,'Well, the problem with that is there are different people in the world, right? Some people prefer those issues, others don\'t. We have left-wing parties and right-wing parties. Our intention is when left-wing parties are in power, which obviously happens a lot around the world, they appoint politicians that cause a series of harms. And if you believe that a right-wing politician is appointing right-wing judges, you should believe that judges will basically never implement your policy if they don\'t support that level of.','other',1866.7,1889.1),(1629,58,1750,1752,'.. I don\'t think that really works. The impact here is when regulators are less funded, they both do their job less, they do less for regulation. The other thing that works is that they select worse on the regulation instead of worse for judging other.','other',1889.1,1900.1),(1630,58,1753,1758,'The final thing I want to point out in this case is that a large amount of corruption will likely occur as a result of this. This occurs at the individual level where the regulators are captured by governments because it becomes substantially more in their interest to do some cheap political goals as well as those smaller financial goals. But also at the government levels where you get certain firms wanting to buy out politicians so they appoint judges that will be more conservative in certain directions. This is bad for competition, it\'s extremely corrupt. It decreases people\'s trust in the government. It\'s obviously extremely harmful. Very powerful.','other',1900.1,1925.9),(1631,60,1845,1847,'Two things in the speech. Firstly, I want to vertically explain why larger companies are more important economically. Secondly, I\'m gonna talk more about why regulation is better and more technically host and why it\'s important to have skilled market companies that should abide by regulation and also follow its social order in the first place.','other',2430.1,2504),(1632,60,1848,1849,'So first on economics, I want to begin by explaining why the blocking versions of that position are gonna be overused on government side of the house. I know this is a less contentious work, but I also want to respond to the closing government POI about why certain judges are also gonna care about economics and err on the side of caution in this instance.','other',2504,2519.2),(1633,60,1850,1851,'I can see a reason judges aren\'t gonna care to some extent about economic interest. It\'s because oftentimes in terms of the way the government is gonna appoint judges, there are disconflicted interests.','other',2519.2,2526.6),(1634,60,1852,1856,'And the premise of this argument is that the government has incentives to want to win the case that they bring to court. This is A, for PR incentives, you want to look more successful, you don\'t want to look weak and think of failure and you\'re wasting resources by pursuing cases against companies that are ultimately gonna fail. But B, you have financial interest as the state as well, because often the state receives the settlement money from cases that they win. And this is money that can be used, for example, like to fund other services for the people which improves their democratic popularity. And you can get incentives to win these cases.','other',2526.6,2554.3),(1635,60,1857,1859,'What is the implication of this? The implication here is that it means that state has incentives to appoint judges with similar ideological interests as them in terms of conceptualizing what the social good is. So when they bring cases to the FTC, the judges that exist there are likely to actually approve the cases and block the purchase and acquisitions, which also unlocks a meaningful amount of the OG case on the margins of what is gonna be done in an overly large amount of cases in response to closing governments.','other',2554.3,2577),(1636,60,1860,1864,'What are the actual harms of this? The first thing I\'m gonna do is explain why economies of scale is actually important for lowering costs. O of O\'s suggests that in a single line, that economies of scale, this means we have efficiency, this lowers legal prices. These are just words, so I\'m not gonna actually explain what economies of scale means or actually does. I want to walk through why economies of scale actually meaningfully lowers costs.','other',2577,2597.2),(1637,60,1865,1872,'So the premise here is that capital is a fixed cost. So when companies want to produce, they often have to invest in fixed capital, which takes a substantial amount of money. The difference now is they don\'t have to duplicate capital to capital across firms. So you save a significant amount of cost but you can increase production. You\'re not linearly trusting about the capital that you can use. But furthermore, you can also optimize for the best resources different companies have. So if company A has very good and efficient manufacturing capital, the company B has more efficient resources for innovation in other areas. You can use the best resources from each company and combine them so you have better capital allocation and you can actually have a company that is working more efficiently because the technology that it\'s accessing to is better and more optimized as well.','other',2597.2,2636.9),(1638,60,1873,1875,'Secondly, I want to explain why we also get better innovation because again, O of O fails to explain why larger companies are actually needed to get more innovation. It could be that, besides bigger goods and more research because they total money in three ways the same. To explain why having it all consolidated in one company is uniquely important.','other',2636.9,2654.3),(1639,60,1876,1878,'So I think that firstly, similar to the economy of the steel argument, research often has upfront costs where you have to invest and R and T and most critically, R is often entirely speculative and risky, i.e. you can put a lot of money in research into documents but all this is just a go to waste if it produces nothing in the end. Most companies have a larger pool of capital to be willing to invest in the first place which you uniquely get when companies merge together.','other',2654.3,2674.5),(1640,60,1879,1881,'Secondly, the importance of innovation is you get more talent and knowledge sharing across companies. The most talented workers and innovators in research across two different companies can come together and share knowledge and resources and talk about ideas but also, for example, company secrets can be shared with one another and industry research as well allows these two firms to acclimate their knowledge and to engage in innovation in this way. But thirdly, it\'s important for innovation because larger firms can also borrow at lower interest rates because they are often perceived as more stable and perceived as less risky to go under because they have more capital to work with.','other',2674.5,2705.6),(1641,60,1882,1885,'This is important because if you\'re perceived as less restrictive and more safe, you can borrow at lower interest rates because interest rates are often seen as insulation against the risk of default. So you\'re seen as being less risk, but less open to default. You have less need to insulate against this risk in the first place so you can borrow at lower interest rates. Similarly, for example, bond prices are higher so it\'s easier to raise money and yields are lower because you\'re perceived as safer as well.','other',2705.6,2726),(1642,60,1886,1887,'Finally, the last economic thing I want to add that is completely distinct from opening governments is you also similarly have less incentives to want to expand as a company as well because this also applies to things like trust busting and breaking up companies. I think CG also goes into their characterization.','other',2726,2739.8),(1643,60,1888,1889,'And the reason this creates an incentive to not want to expand as a company is because oftentimes you\'re uniquely afraid of, for example, being busted because of all the reasons given about why this could be overused. And the reason that the risk of this, of being broken up as a company, outweighs your other financial incentives to want to expand is this.','other',2739.8,2758),(1644,60,1890,1894,'Similarly, expansion carries some risks when you break into unknown markets. You can fail and you don\'t adapt to the markets. All of your money can go to waste. So this becomes a unique incentive point to not want to undertake the process of trying to expand into other markets and wait for a moment. If you think you\'re gonna be broken up in the process, even when you expand, the profits are no longer worth the risk when you actually have increasing threats of being broken up as a company.','other',2758,2781.2),(1645,60,1895,1896,'This is a deeply harm-centered balance we need to look at going after this. And this is because oftentimes the most significant businesses need to be expanded to are emerging markets that typically don\'t have businesses so you neglect a significant part of this world that currently does not have access to meaningful goods and services opening.','other',2781.2,2794.1),(1646,60,1897,1899,'Companies can grow in the absence of M&As but M&As is a mode of growth often coming massively off of thousands of dollars, dips in productivity, the process of aligning business activities, and other forms that you don\'t come to recognize. But businesses often can\'t grow with M&As because you need one of them. The reason you want to pursue a merger and acquisition in the first place is to have the necessary resources to grow in the markets.','poi',2794.1,2813.6),(1647,60,1900,1901,'It\'s not what Citi says where you have this economy of scale. If you\'re limited to this economy of scale, you\'re not gonna have a merger and acquisition.','other',2813.6,2819.9),(1648,60,1902,1903,'Why does this provide a trade-off at regulation? So, I want to first begin by explaining why scale is actually an important way of aligning, of social good.','other',2819.9,2826.8),(1649,60,1904,1910,'And this is because it\'s often not comparable to a bunch of aligning social goods. For example, polluting less means you have less profit. You have to spend money on lawyers to make sure you\'re aligning with regulation in the first place. You can\'t produce this efficiently. This is generally not comparable. Therefore, you only have incentives to want to try and do things that, for example, care about the environment. If you have enough money to do so, then you can try to do well.','other',2826.8,2846),(1650,60,1911,1919,'Why do businesses have incentives to want to follow regulation but also responsibly closing off their card markets? So, I think, firstly, citizens themselves have a growing demand in, for example, many white-ruled democratic countries. For example, they want to have no protests or boycott companies with bad environment or social practices. These protests, even if they don\'t directly harm company profits, don\'t create enough unrest that deters investment as well, which means that the business stability is also bad, even if the boycotts themselves are very effective. I think shareholders have many other incentives that care about the SQA as well, often perceived as more sustainable. So, for example, oil is becoming increasingly volatile, making it more sustainable in the long term. Climate is also bad for shareholders, but even if a company can survive, it often harms, for example, the amount of equity or dividend a shareholder can have. And you have separate incentives when they care to some extent about regulation, which proves that companies self-regulate, even if all of the incentives that CHG outlines about why imposing regulation is very hard is true. I want to explain finally why this plays out with regulation.','other',2846,2901),(1651,60,1920,1921,'So, I think the reason this regulation stopped existing on their sticky posts is that voters start to become complacent because they see that the antitrust is insufficient, and this means they have less single-issue voters on the idea of trying to impose regulations in the first place, which means they have less fraud regulations, which means that these regulations can apply to all companies. This is the more important impact of this problem.','other',2901,2917.9),(1652,60,1922,1922,'So, all these reasons for voting. Good.','other',2917.9,2919.1),(1653,62,1988,1992,'Consumer saving money on a smartphone means they spend on other goods, creating jobs for low income workers who wouldn\'t have jobs otherwise and wouldn\'t have the money to access healthcare. Technological innovation is more than that. Companies engaging in technological innovation has been the driver of economic growth for the past century and economic growth brings living standards. It gives societies the resources and tax revenue they need to invest in clean technologies. A lot of these companies are investing in clean technologies.','other',3447.4,3543.3),(1654,62,1993,1995,'Anti-trust enforcement in order to help the environment has often been a block of consolidation in the green energy sector on the basis that potentially this helps the green energy sector develop or this prevents oil companies from making up patents. But oil companies investing in the green energy sector is critical to ensure solar panels get developed in order to fight climate change. These are the large impacts and these are the concentrated impacts.','other',3543.3,3564.1),(1655,62,1996,1997,'Here\'s the problem with arguments about health, life and death and environmental harms. Their mechanism for anti-trust helping is literally anti-trust will punish these companies which calls them really not do this.','other',3564.1,3575.6),(1656,62,1998,2000,'We have another mechanism of punishing companies this way, fines. Regulations work in the specific context that Manny wants to weigh because lies are arguments oh you don\'t get that with technology, don\'t apply to that, wait till anti-trust regulates firms engaging in things like environmental damage which is deterring them by using anti-trust to break them up. This form of deterrence is better accomplished through targeted regulation as Liz explains.','other',3575.6,3596.7),(1657,62,2001,2001,'Now I\'m going to address their specific arguments on why this is primarily about big tech and what harms they do in tech markets.','other',3596.7,3603),(1658,62,2002,2003,'The first one they rate is more innovation. They say there is more revenue for smaller tech companies because they won\'t be killed by a large company.','other',3603,3609),(1659,62,2004,2005,'Flip this argument. Small tech depends on big tech.','other',3609,3611.8),(1660,62,2006,2006,'There are a number of reasons why anti-trust enforcement that blocks mergers and acquisitions that tech companies engage in ensures small tech companies can\'t develop and can\'t innovate.','other',3611.8,3619.8),(1661,62,2007,2013,'Reason one, big tech can potentially buy up small tech firms. Small tech companies struggle to compete precisely because of the network effects that buying makes. If you start a social media company, investors are going to be terrified of investing in you. Why? Because a social media company needs lots of users but users are attracted by other users already being present on the platform. This triggers investors to want to invest in a small tech company even if it creates a social media company that has a critical new innovation that people want to access. But investors will invest in a small tech company if they realize that this new innovation is also something a big social media platform that has users can use. For example, Facebook can buy WhatsApp and that led to the initial investment in WhatsApp that allowed for a social media company that was a messaging platform.','other',3619.8,3663.1),(1662,62,2014,2015,'Or even if the company\'s media platform itself doesn\'t get used, the innovations that it creates can be leased out to big tech firms because big tech firms want the innovation from them. And this is also why they want to simply block out these firms or prevent them from existing or run them into the ground using lawsuits.','other',3663.1,3678.3),(1663,62,2016,2020,'Because if Ryan is right, that small tech firms have some advantages. For example, they don\'t suffer from diseconomies of scale. Big tech firms want to use those advantages for their own consumer markets. And the way they use those advantages is by giving them money to lease technology from them, which is why they don\'t just run these companies into the ground. This means small companies have some advantages, but these advantages depend on getting investment and this investment depends on the existence of large tech corporations.','other',3678.3,3703.9),(1664,62,2021,2022,'This is why the greatest political advocates for small tech in the US, people like Mark Andresen and VC\'s like Andresen Horowitz, oppose bank trust enforcement on big tech companies. Their second mechanism is democracy.','other',3703.9,3714.9),(1665,62,2023,2023,'Their claim is that aggregation allows you access to data and this poses a threat to democracy.','other',3714.9,3719.5),(1666,62,2024,2024,'First argument here, I want to reflect this argument.','other',3719.5,3721.8),(1667,62,2025,2025,'There is a greater risk of social media companies trying to influence politics on their side of the house for two structural reasons.','other',3721.8,3727.8),(1668,62,2026,2033,'One, currently social media companies face substantial amounts of PR pressure to not interfere in democracy. This is particularly in large firms like Facebook. Facebook lost substantial amounts and breached public relations scandal as a direct result of their contribution to the residential genocide and the Cambridge Analytica scandal. See, Mark Zuckerberg being grilled in Congress and activist investors dropping Facebook stock. Facebook stock dropped sharply after the 2016 election. This creates substantial pressure on Facebook to try to seem politically neutral and project political neutrality. However, this calculus changes for Facebook in a world where they believe antitrust regulators are more likely to be after them because then you want to influence governments to prevent over-aggressive regulation of your industries, meaning you preemptively act before antitrust enforcers can get to you to do things like use user data. But second, they don\'t actually explain why antitrust enforcement works in this case.','other',3727.8,3779.8),(1669,62,2033,2036,'But second, they don\'t actually explain why antitrust enforcement works in this case. Because suppose you break Facebook up into a bunch of different social media companies, their own network effects mechanism proves that a lot of the users will still stay on Facebook. So you break Facebook up into multiple companies, a bunch of users go to the other platforms, they realize other users aren\'t following that link on these platforms, they go back onto Facebook. Flip number two, the rise of newer and smaller tech companies in response to Facebook antitrust likely increases political influence. Why?','other',3775.7,3802.9),(1670,62,2036,2040,'Flip number two, the rise of newer and smaller tech companies in response to Facebook antitrust likely increases political influence. Why? Because smaller tech companies, in order to survive, the ones that do survive in the end of an antitrust lawsuit are the ones who are likely to attract a lot of users. So for example, the only one which will survive when you split Facebook and WhatsApp up is WhatsApp with key product users and a bunch of users who use both WhatsApp and Facebook. Here\'s a distinction however, when a bunch of users use more WhatsApp and Facebook but they have different owners or new owners who face less public scrutiny because they are smaller companies once they are broken up, you\'re more likely to have owners who want to use the social media platform to push a political agenda. This is why X, which is a social media platform much smaller than any of the platforms they described, is the one that was actually politicized in the 2016 election.','other',3795.5,3839.9),(1671,62,2041,2041,'No, thank you.','other',3839.9,3841),(1672,62,2042,2042,'This means that regulation of tech doesn\'t actually work at accomplishing the objectives they want.','other',3841.1,3845),(1673,62,2043,2045,'However, it does fucking hurt innovation. And here I want to explain why other industries are also meaningfully affected by this standard. Because their argument is basically, ah, these industries aren\'t really concentrated so they have less of an influence so it\'s unlikely that regulators will get them for that argument.','other',3845,3859.5),(1674,62,2046,2050,'Even if you prove that more M&As are good, this debate is also about regulations of general business conduct. Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, and the regulations of general business conduct one, stop mergers and acquisitions. Two, split companies apart. Three, punish behavior that attempts to get firms to expand or become larger or cover a larger number of industries or diversify in these sectors. So that\'s the reason why Manny\'s blog about M&As being there doesn\'t affect our case because other kinds of business conduct are also affected.','other',3859.5,3884.3),(1675,62,2051,2053,'Why is it likely that other industries are also overwhelmingly affected? One, if it\'s a less concentrated industry, and the M&As are just more likely to be affected by democracy because it means a single large company has some potential capacity to lobby while other firms don\'t have it and therefore the large company uses lobbying in order to influence other firms. Second, if it\'s a less concentrated industry, you\'re more able to fight a lack of trust case.','other',3884.3,3904.2),(1676,62,2054,2055,'Regulators work with a lack of trust case, give them access to settlements, which is why they try to get M&As. And third, many of the specific impacts are irrelevant then but environmental protection standards are meaningfully affected because it eases the use of the standard against M&As in the end.','other',3904.2,3916.9),(1677,62,2056,2062,'In this context, I want to be crystal clear. You get way too much of this because open competition justification is that you might get some copies of judges, you might get some conservative judges. Here\'s the problem. As value points are unpopularity might limit that. This is why Bill\'s extension comes in. There is a conflict of interest. The government appoints judges but it gets money by winning anti-trust cases through anti-trust settlements and this conflict of interest results in over-policing.','other',3916.9,3940.6),(1678,62,2063,2065,'And this is where the impacts come in. We explain the argument on economies of scale. A larger impact than cross-subsidization because cross-subsidization might lead to lower prices in one bloated, unproductive industry while economies of scale lead to lower prices for every single consumer group and it\'s also the law of companies to expand the world.','other',3940.6,3956.7),(1679,55,1440,1442,'This is a debate about courts and justices and judges. So judges will interpret this as a sit and preside over certain appeals over mergers and acquisitions after regulators have regulated and companies sue if they think that this is unfair. Secondly, I think this framing is important because regulators will know that this is a legal doctrine that judges and courts will adopt, so therefore the way we approach regulated mergers and acquisitions will keep a broader scope in mind.','other',5.8,31),(1680,55,1443,1444,'For instance, they know they can get away with being able to complain that this merger acquisition has failed to conduct some environmental information surveys on how this merger acquisition would deplete energy resources or for instance, pollute in the local environment. This is important because our first argument is that the consumer welfare declines on the consumer welfare doctrines in the SAS rule are too narrow.','other',31.1,52),(1681,55,1445,1448,'The first thing I want to talk about here is that we\'re going to be able to essentially create companies to self-regulate before they go into the merger and acquisition process. So here\'s the through line, right? They will perceive that regulators will regulate against them if they do not give out information on things like the way they influence them like that. So therefore, they will preemptively have this information on hand in order to prevent things like like getting sued and stuff like that.','other',52.3,77.1),(1682,55,1449,1453,'The reason why this is important and the reason why they\'ll do this is threefold. Firstly, investors are wary, so they\'re going to be scared if this is too risky because of the media attention that will happen if they get sued for instance, other considerations. Secondly, legal fees are incredibly expensive to have lawyers and things like that. Thirdly, if you fail the first process in the district courts, you have to appeal again and appeal again and appeal again. These appeal phrases take a long time for you as well.','other',77.3,103.3),(1683,55,1454,1455,'So as a company, you want to make sure that you cover all your cases. Fourthly, you are acquiring another firm.','other',103.5,110),(1684,55,1456,1456,'So there\'s likely that that other firm might get cold feet if they feel as though you have not prepared enough for this process as well.','other',110.2,116.9),(1685,55,1457,1461,'The importance for this is because we reduce information asymmetry. So here\'s how I want to contextualize this. If a company knows that they will have the merchant acquisition locked because they have not provided information on pollution, what they will do is they\'ll go and hire consultants to make sure they conduct stuff like an environmental impact statement that reveals the total GHG emissions that will result from this merchant acquisition. The reason why we think this is fine is because if you have a locked capital, this means you have to have a locked capital in order to acquire another firm. So this is going to be calculated in your decision-calculating process.','other',117.3,151.8),(1686,55,1462,1465,'The reason why this information is good, firstly, I think it\'s good for the public to be aware of this information. That\'s to say, if you live in these communities that are going to be near these factories, near these data centers, your energy prices might increase if they come in. It\'s good for you to do things like have a long campaign or local campaigning for your local politicians to create ordinances against these type of companies coming in. But also secondly, I think it\'s really good for investors too.','other',152,176.4),(1687,55,1466,1467,'You might want to invest in this company after the merchant acquisition to know the type of things that they\'re getting into. Revealing all this information to the public is good for the company and for the market as a whole.','other',176.5,187),(1688,55,1468,1468,'The second argument I want to talk about is that we have more political will on our side of the house.','other',188,192.4),(1689,55,1469,1470,'I want to note that regulators do want to regulate. Because on their side of the house, they might think that regulators are going to do this in a bad way.','other',192.8,199.3),(1690,55,1471,1474,'But firstly, the reason why they do so in a bad way in the status quo is because they have too narrow a stick to adjudicate mergers and acquisitions. If it is the case that you can only block an M&A if they reduce the competition and prices, it is a lot easier for a company to budge the numbers, to hire people to say actually no, the competitor prices are going to decrease. But because we\'ve seen a proliferation of M&As in the hundreds, like 450 in the past year, the Department of Justice and the FTC currently do not have enough resources to investigate all of this. That\'s to say it is easy for M&As to go unnoticed.','other',199.5,232.5),(1691,55,1475,1478,'Secondly, they also have a repeal preference for doing this because for instance, they want to get promoted, they want to get more funding from Congress, they want to get more media attention on their agency so that they\'re able to do this. And thirdly, because there\'s bipartisan support. That\'s why for instance, a lot of people like Lisa Tan, because Lisa Tan is really harsh against tech, that\'s why we have things like conservatives, right? And for instance, because of this bipartisan support, we\'ve seen a lot of interest in this as a whole.','other',232.8,255.9),(1692,55,1479,1479,'The reason why this independently is not enough for them to block more M&As is because using this as a legal document is too low.','other',256.1,263.7),(1693,55,1480,1481,'The second argument I want to, the third argument I want to posit this week is that on the comparative, it\'s far worse. On the comparative, it is far worse to get environmental accountability and privacy accountability on their side.','other',263.7,275.7),(1694,55,1482,1485,'The first thing behind this is because things like privacy laws and environmental laws lag behind industry. So for instance, laws regarding the protection of consumer, like a welfare on data and AI, oftentimes have to catch up to the technology that\'s emerging in the South. Things like the environment, like EPA and stuff like that, constantly get repealed in the South. Like for instance, the Halliburton loophole, which allows the crafting companies to not have to dispose of wastewater properly is something that was repealed by corporate interest.','other',276,304.9),(1695,55,1486,1486,'I\'m going to prove to you why these type of regulators are not susceptible to these flaws, but before I continue, I\'ll take a few questions.','other',305.2,311.5),(1696,55,1487,1489,'Just still kind of explain why you think you have to solve any of these problems. Like why does it help with the environment? Why does it help with privacy?','poi',311.6,317.1),(1697,55,1490,1493,'Why does it help with any of these things? So the thing that I mentioned is that it\'s a preemptive thing, right? So this is proactive. So before the M&A already occurs, they have to prove that, they have to use data studies and stuff like that to prove that there\'s going to be less environmental harm and stuff like that.','other',317.3,333.8),(1698,55,1494,1495,'So this happens before the M&A happens. And then I\'m going to prove to you why you now give courts more ability to have legal prosecution on R7,000.','other',333.9,343),(1699,55,1496,1500,'All right, so what happens on R7,000? What we think happens is when regulators, I don\'t know, when regulators, like in the deal chain and stuff like that, like regulating M&As, they\'re not susceptible to acts and stuff like that. They get to create their own doctrine when they\'re trying to do this. So when these regulators get sued, if other companies think that this is weak, what happens is this goes through the court process and this creates cases for future court decisions to be based on them. So for instance, if a utility company gets sued, then the facts of that case gets to be applied to future courts and things like that.','other',343.6,375.3),(1700,55,1501,1502,'The reason why this is important is because these doctrines actually give future courts legal basis to determine this information. The third argument I want to posit, sorry, the impact of this is that, firstly, what happens is we get less M&As on R7,000 because the barriers to that is much, much higher.','other',375.6,389.9),(1701,55,1503,1505,'So therefore, you have to ensure that the information you put out there is strong enough. Third argument, I\'ll close. Thank you very much for that speech.','other',390,401.1),(1702,55,1506,1506,'Now I\'d like to welcome the leader of the opposition, here.','other',401.4,404.4),(1703,57,1593,1593,'Okay, can everyone hear me if I speak like this?','other',914.8,949.4),(1704,57,1594,1596,'Two questions in this speech. First, on which side is regulation more likely to be successful? And second, the side that provides the most impactful regulation.','other',949.4,973),(1705,57,1597,1599,'On success, OO makes a number of claims here. The first thing they say is that this makes mergers and acquisitions more difficult because it relies on a more nebulous legal standard and more nebulous people using force of law. First, I want to point out that this doesn\'t interact with the material you get from Victor\'s hypothetical claims, why M&As are likely to be harmful in the first place, and in that list, which side is it?','other',973,995.8),(1706,57,1600,1601,'in the symmetric sizes of this content. I will explain why they\'re particularly harmful, why these harms are incredibly pernicious to individuals on the ground.','other',995.8,1002.5),(1707,57,1602,1606,'But secondly, I just don\'t think this is true, right? Like I think many a time, the legal system is able to establish legal standards for things like harm to the environment, harm to privacy, for example, are very easy to prove because we have a broad sense of what kind of data we don\'t want to be in the public domain, or the kinds of data that we don\'t want to be in the hands of corporates. Crucially, I would flip this, because the test for increased prices, for example, is vastly more speculative. This is the aim of margins. Often it\'s obvious where there\'s a violation of privacy, and with the average of increased scrutiny on privacy, due to things, for example, like artificial intelligence, like abortion legislation, and so on, this is likely going to improve over time.','other',1002.5,1037.9),(1708,57,1607,1610,'With prices, what\'s different is, it is difficult for you to prove that the margin with which you reduce or increase prices is a margin that is likely to be consequential. Is a $1 increase enough? Is a $2 increase enough? That\'s what they are.','other',1037.9,1050.4),(1709,57,1611,1612,'They then say that a reduction in M&As is harmful in two ways. First, because on their side, in the absence of M&As, companies operate on a larger scale.','other',1050.4,1057.6),(1710,57,1613,1617,'I think this is funny, because already the companies that are able to engage in a version of that decision in the first place are profoundly powerful companies. They have incredible influence over political systems, they are incredibly dependent, they make the economy incredibly dependent on them. Imagine then, given the nature of companies that are engaged in these M&As, what happens if they consolidate and become much more competitive? It just supercharges their arms, if you care about the picture. They then say that these companies become less vulnerable to shocks.','other',1057.6,1082.5),(1711,57,1618,1620,'I think this is only true for horizontal mergers, that is, cross-industry mergers. But I think in the majority of cases of this debate, this likely deals with vertical mergers, because this is where arms to antitrust are most consequential. Over the entire production line, this is an incredible bridge to antitrust standards and antitrust expectations.','other',1082.5,1100.1),(1712,57,1621,1622,'Lastly, I want you to note that often, the standard necessary to prove a harm under the consumer welfare standard is often very difficult to acquire at the point at which this regulation is happening. There are two reasons for this.','other',1100.1,1112.7),(1713,57,1623,1626,'First, businesses are often able to do things like price scout, particularly at the moment of the merger or the acquisition. They keep their prices very low at the beginning of their ownership, and then the fruits of the market, then they drive these prices up. This means often, you\'re not able to prove an immediate harm in court because the harms associated with monopolies are the harms that emerge over time. This is important because this debate likely happens in Western countries that rely on apostrophic legal systems, legal systems where it\'s impossible to preemptively litigate, or rather, very difficult to preemptively litigate because courts are very focused on proving a degree of harm to consumers that they will never be able to prove.','other',1112.7,1149),(1714,57,1627,1628,'Secondly, though, businesses in court are often unable to make claims with respect to economies of scale. That make it easier for them to claim that they will actually have the financial powers to be able to do things like lower prices, to increase quality, and so on.','other',1149,1161.4),(1715,57,1629,1632,'The weighing of this is as follows. Both sides must come to the agreement that the success of regulation is important. Companies hold extraordinary levels of power and influence new places where this debate is likely to exist. When there\'s an incentive to cap this power and influence as far as possible, we think we do that best.','other',1161.4,1177.1),(1716,57,1633,1633,'Before the second question on impact of regulation takes.','other',1177.1,1179.8),(1717,57,1634,1637,'Should we clarify, do you agree that there will be a substantial increase in blocking of commercial acquisitions on both sides? We think there will be a reasonable amount of mergers and acquisitions on both sides. We think this debate starts from the premise that a finite number of mergers have been flagged. This is simply a question of, on both sides, we\'re mitigating for the same M&As, which one is more likely to succeed?','poi',1179.8,1199.3),(1718,57,1638,1639,'Which side gets more impact from regulation? Victor tells me that on our side, we make companies preemptively regulate in such a way, in a way that they are unlikely to do in the world of job acquisition.','other',1199.3,1208.8),(1719,57,1640,1641,'Boho says that economic conditions are often the most important thing to consumers. But I want to be perfectly clear on a number of levels here.','other',1208.8,1215.7),(1720,57,1642,1644,'First, often consumers within the market are able to regulate all things that affect their economic conditions most directly. Consumers can see for themselves when prices have gone up. Consumers can see for themselves when quantity has gone down.','other',1215.7,1228),(1721,57,1645,1650,'This is different for harms to democracy, for harms to the environment. Because these are harms that often happen over a protracted period of time. Democratic backsliding doesn\'t happen overnight. That is over a long period of time. These are harms that are very difficult for the immediate consumer to see for themselves. This is precisely why we think the technocrats in the FTC and the DOJ need to be the ones to preemptively point out these harms and try to prevent them.','other',1228,1249.5),(1722,57,1651,1655,'But secondly, I think there\'s a logical priority here. Because obviously, economic conditions for the immediate consumer are reliant on things like the existence of a functional democracy. In the absence of democracy, you don\'t get the kinds of investments that we think are necessary to create jobs and create the value for individuals. The absence of a clean and healthy environment, you don\'t have, for example, a labor force that\'s equipped to go out into the work force. You don\'t have, for example, the land that\'s necessary to the vast majority of economic activity in many parts of the world.','other',1249.5,1276.3),(1723,57,1656,1658,'I want to weigh this in two ways. One, the places where this debate likely exists are places where harms to non-economic outcomes are under new scrutiny in a number of ways. Places like the US and the EU, where right-wing populists have gained power, where climate justice movements are on the rise, and where privacy due to things like the wars for the rights of abortion and the war on the regulation of AI, connects these struggles in such a way as to raise political capital to this regulation.','other',1276.3,1301.7),(1724,57,1659,1661,'I think raising this capital is important because, to be comparative, big companies are often able to market themselves as doing incredible things for the market. That is, before AT&T and T-Mobile attempted to engage in a merger in 2011, they flooded the market with advertisements about how much the cost of accessing, for example, the internet would go down, and how incredible this would be for the immediate consumer. Often, we think it is necessary for each of the groups to connect pre-existing movements for regulation in such a way as to raise political capital for regulation of these members, and for these businesses and their activities.','other',1301.7,1332.8),(1725,57,1662,1668,'But secondly, the harms associated with privacy, with democracy, and with the environment often disproportionately affect vulnerable groups. Environmental harms, for example, ruin the lives and livelihoods of individuals on the ground. Indigenous people, for example, are disproportionately affected in the world in which we are, the environment. Harms to privacy disproportionately affects women, who in many instances have very limited access to the right to the strike. In the first place, they\'re often likely to be disastrous to have their lives ruined by a violation of the strike. Harms to democracy are often dangerous for middle class, because you take away the political capital that they often use as a check against the economic capital of the ruling class. This means, most importantly, that we need these kinds of harms should be prioritized with that they want to most approximate to the immediate consumer.','other',1332.8,1374.9),(1726,57,1669,1671,'This has two consequences. One, we need these other kinds of harms that lead to consumers actually raising political capital for these mergers and for these acquisitions. But secondly, we need to fulfill an obligation to prioritize the most vulnerable individuals in a way that you would never be able to do on their side, in a way that makes us so incredibly proud to propose. Thank you.','other',1374.9,1392.2),(1727,59,1762,1763,'The problem with the top half is the problem of characterizing the types of cases where the consumer welfare to the citizen welfare standard. The answer from closing government is relatively straightforward.','other',1936.9,1995.7),(1728,59,1764,1765,'The clearest example where this debate meaningfully differs is the example of large tech corporations like Google, Microsoft, AI, et cetera. Why does this sort of a debate realistically occurs? Three reasons.','other',1995.7,2006.2),(1729,59,1766,1766,'First, large tech companies are naturally highly consolidated in the network effects.','other',2006.3,2011.2),(1730,59,1767,1768,'For example, there\'s often a consolidation of large people or large numbers of people onto a small number of social media platforms, which means that these industries often are ones that are large. But more importantly, the reason the consumer welfare standard cannot apply to these platforms, even though they are particularly large and consolidated, is because the vast majority of services provided by large tech companies are either free to use or very low cost.','other',2011.2,2032.4),(1731,59,1769,1771,'For example, no one has to pay money to Google search. No one has to pay money to use open AI free services. And the consequence is the consumer welfare standard is completely unable to address consolidation in large tech companies.','other',2032.4,2043.5),(1732,59,1772,1774,'The other reason the consumer welfare standard does not work in the context of large tech corporations is because the perception, especially amongst the non-tech world, is that large tech companies are enormously innovative and job-creative. So most people think of Google or Microsoft or Amazon as highly innovative, which means it\'s very difficult for a judge to argue we should break up Google or spin off open AI from Microsoft, because the perception is there is not a lack of innovation in the strategy as well. I want to be very clear on why this framing is important.','other',2043.5,2069.8),(1733,59,1775,1777,'Open air opposition runs a very scary, doomsday case about what happens when judges start blocking M&As left, right, and center. But no, that\'s not what\'s going to happen under this debate. Why? Because on both sides of the house, you will only block mergers and acquisitions in cases where the increased competition would pose a meaningful threat to the environment, security, to data, et cetera.','other',2069.8,2090.6),(1734,59,1778,1780,'The problem is that most other industries are not highly concentrated, which means that the merger between two relatively small automobile manufacturing companies has a relatively limited effect on the environment or a relatively limited effect on privacy. Whereas the much more important part of the debate is the context of tech companies, which is why, for example, Lena Kahn, it\'s not Lisa Kahn, by the way, OG, is so in favor of our policy, because she wants increased capacity to go after large tech companies. The other reason this is important is it answers opening opposition\'s challenge.','other',2090.8,2118.4),(1735,59,1781,1782,'Oh, there\'s a fair play. Why can\'t we just regulate these companies?','other',2118.4,2121.7),(1736,59,1783,1783,'Why do we need antitrust?','other',2121.7,2122.6),(1737,59,1784,1785,'This is the verdict Jose Gaviria-Philip puts us over OG. You cannot regulate big tech companies for three reasons.','other',2122.6,2128.5),(1738,59,1786,1790,'Reason number one, governments are structurally unable to regulate tech. This is because the vast majority of politicians are old, they\'re boomers. They often don\'t understand how tech companies work. Like, have you all seen that hearing where they literally ask Facebook what the hell an algorithm was or what a phone looks like? The problem is, given that you need power and money and connections to be in politics, the people tasked with regulating big tech are often incompetent.','other',2128.5,2150.3),(1739,59,1791,1796,'Secondly, the pace of advancements in AI is far faster than the pace of advancements in legislatures. Now, to throw open the government\'s credit, this is a one-liner idea, but here\'s the real reason why. The legislative process is slow, because it involves forced trading. We have had multiple discussions between multiple parties. By contrast, tech advancement happens rapidly, because it\'s naturally self-fulfilling. When one company, for example, develops a better AI algorithm, another company incorporates that algorithm into its products, and advances its own rate of advancement.','other',2150.3,2177.1),(1740,59,1797,1800,'Which means you can never legislate as fast as the tech sector advances. Reason number three, governments do not regulate tech because of fears of regulatory arbitrage. The reason the United States will never strictly regulate AI is for fear that if you tightly regulate AI, AI companies will flow elsewhere, for example, to less regulated districts. This answers the problem that opening up a government does not solve.','other',2177.1,2197.4),(1741,59,1801,1802,'Why is antitrust specifically necessary? It\'s because regulation is insufficient.','other',2197.4,2201.4),(1742,59,1803,1806,'The other reason this framing is important is because it frames out opening opposition to concerns about mergers and acquisitions. Why is that? Because these industries are already highly concentrated. So the additional marginal benefit of further economies of scale, as well as the limited.','other',2201.4,2214.9),(1743,59,1807,1809,'Like Jenny had questioned, these are trillion-dollar companies. Why do you need even more mergers and acquisitions to get the economies of scale opening opposition is talking about? Therefore, this is mostly a debate about utilizing the citizen welfare standard to enforce antitrust legislation against companies like Microsoft, like Facebook.','other',2214.9,2231.4),(1744,59,1810,1814,'What does this look like in practice? To be clear, I just want to be very clear about this before someone comments on it. Our argument is not that we will break up all big tech companies ever in the world and they\'re all going to spin the wheels. Our claim, more sensibly, is that there is more vulnerability on our side than you do things like spin off open AI from Microsoft, that you, for example, sever WhatsApp from Facebook. And even if you don\'t split companies off, you are more likely to impose antitrust penalties or fine money on companies in cases where they could engage in anti-competitive behaviors.','other',2231.8,2257.6),(1745,59,1815,1815,'Why is this valuable?','other',2257.6,2259.2),(1746,59,1816,1817,'Three reasons for closing government. Reason number one, large tech companies constitute an existential threat to democracy.','other',2259.2,2265.7),(1747,59,1818,1823,'The platform of Facebook literally has more users than the entire Catholic Church. And this is a problem for two reasons. Reason number one, large tech companies have enormous access to data. The reason this is bad is not necessarily just because of privacy. It\'s because when you have data from many different platforms, for example, Facebook has your data from WhatsApp, but also from its social media platform, you can then increase the effectiveness of algorithms to increasingly manipulate people\'s political preferences to run more targeted advertisements, CFP or Danalytica. There are strong political incentives to do this because large tech corporations do stand to benefit from influencing political outcomes, especially in developing countries where the regulatory system is relatively weak.','other',2265.7,2305.1),(1748,59,1824,1827,'But secondly, it often means that these companies control what information people are exposed to. They\'ll say, ah, there\'s competition between different social media companies, but there\'s an oligopoly at play. A small number of social media firms and a small number of platforms, which means that the algorithms you are exposed to shape your political views. This means under our side of the house, even if we don\'t break up social media, we are more likely to have threats of antitrust penalties enforced against these companies that incentivize them to do things like moderate their algorithms to ensure they produce less sensationalist, hateful content, CFP, Bernie\'s, Genocide, and Myanmar literally being provoked by hate algorithms on Facebook.','other',2305.1,2340),(1749,59,1828,1829,'The big tech companies are enormously productive, which really explains all their politics and analogies, because better technology grows unpredictably in things that help the environment, like energy technology through cross-subsidizing. But there\'s an unproven claim here.','other',2340,2354.2),(1750,59,1830,1831,'Why do you need the extra large economies of scale? We\'ll flip this.','other',2354.2,2357.9),(1751,59,1832,1836,'There\'s huge diseconomies of scale the larger and larger a company is. For example, there are huge communication inefficiencies across the vast corporation. You are more efficient under our side of the house. Because just to be clear, if you spin OpenAI off from Microsoft, OpenAI is still a multi-hundred billion dollar company. The benefit is you do not have the enormous inefficiencies that come from operating a vast company.','other',2357.9,2376.7),(1752,59,1837,1838,'Reason number two, we are more likely to get innovation under our side of the house. Why is that?','other',2376.7,2381.2),(1753,59,1839,1840,'First, you\'re more likely to lend to smaller companies when the perception amongst investors is that smaller tech companies now have a viable chance because they will not immediately be killed or sued into the ground by a large company like Tesla, which often engaged in patents evergreening and patents beginning to crush competition. There is now a credible threat that large companies might be broken up, which incentivizes increased capital flows to smaller firms.','other',2381.2,2402.8),(1754,59,1841,1842,'Reason number two, smaller companies are more able to access the crucial sources that are needed for innovation, like data, like power sources, like electricity, like rare earth elements, which are currently monopolized by large tech companies. If small tech companies cannot get their hands on the data, they\'ll never build the groundbreaking AI they can use antitrust. Proposed.','other',2402.8,2422.3),(1755,61,1926,1928,'This debate is not a debate about is antitrust good or is it bad, as for both the OO and CO case, which is the biggest problem on their case. Because their event is our standard results in more enforcement than that fact. Our intention beats this, because we explain to you why this doesn\'t actually apply to the vast majority of cases that both options want to talk about.','other',2928.3,3028.6),(1756,61,1929,1930,'It is largely a debate about how we want to regulate and how we want to curb the growth and anti-competitive expansion of large tech companies. That immediately minimizes all of the impacts that both teams want to talk about.','other',3028.6,3041.1),(1757,61,1931,1933,'And more importantly, I don\'t think it\'s very fair for the way we characterize our inspection. Our argument isn\'t just about threatening democracy, it\'s also about innovation. We give you three independent reasons why we get more innovation on side C and G.','other',3041.1,3054.1),(1758,61,1934,1936,'First, through smaller tech companies even growing the first. This economy for smaller firms. It\'s critically important and crucial for startup companies to make sure the product can even get off the ground.','other',3054.1,3082.1),(1759,61,1937,1938,'Two questions I\'ll ask. First question, innovation, economy of scale, yada yada.','other',3082.1,3096.7),(1760,61,1939,1941,'Second thing, how does regulation happen to who you employ? What are these large companies worth? So as long as I prove either one is true, the argument is out.','other',3096.8,3109.6),(1761,61,1942,1942,'The first is M&A\'s don\'t actually significantly reduce or this debate isn\'t about that, so the impact is minimal.','other',3109.6,3115),(1762,61,1943,1943,'The second way is that large companies are just not good, so we\'re fine even if M&A\'s don\'t count.','other',3115,3120.8),(1763,61,1944,1949,'Method one, the vast majority of mergers, outside of all the reasons Ryan has already given as to why they\'re not relevant in this round, that happen are small scale mergers increasingly. If you read reports from places like Bazaar or companies like Bank of America, you realize that most mergers across the world, including Europe, are small cap mergers. Because why? One, you tend to be able to increase your deal flow more. And two, often they\'re easier to negotiate within a shorter amount of time, because often, guess what, mergers and acquisitions are really negotiation heavy and costly because often companies can\'t agree on the same price. And third, it often requires lower costs in general. What this means is that it\'s just inherently untrue that the majority of mergers right now are large cap and often get so large that you have these concerns in the very first place.','other',3120.8,3165.1),(1764,61,1950,1951,'This isn\'t a knife of fortune, by the way. And like, these OG friends that M&A goes down, Bo, Taz, and Crew with, argue that the scale isn\'t nearly as large as either team wants to say.','other',3165.1,3174.9),(1765,61,1952,1954,'And also, in most cases, in their case, they\'re very best. This is not about whether an acquisition problem, so they have a fear from investors of uncertainty. But the large companies implicated in the anti-dependent mergers, think companies like Walmart, like Google, like Meta, are making these investments regardless.','other',3174.9,3197.6),(1766,61,1955,1959,'This is Google already having ways in Gemini. This is Microsoft going on their side. The problem is that because the companies out there are able to have lots of upfront investment, they can borrow at lower rates. The two sides of the money, the end outcome, people is small. This intensive with a group of people versus very diffused benefits, the CGBB reports out of the way.','other',3197.6,3274.1),(1767,61,1960,1963,'So yes, 30,000 people in China will die in cancer villages in Lijiang because the apple factories have done toxic chemistry in there for year