ndrick and to Drake. You\'re not just in a tunnel vision of just listening to one single person. And secondly, these people are gonna see that these bad things that happened, that these artists were like domestic abusers, that actually, they\'re actually really rude, or they\'re actually sexually assaulted people and children, which means that these people or fans are gonna keep these people accountable because they have friends or family who watch movies or can empathize with these people, even if they support their music, they\'re not gonna support the artists, which means they\'re gonna keep them accountable.','other',1989.8,2036.7),(1813,82,3849,3851,'But B, I wanna say that even if we don\'t win on fans keeping these people accountable, notice that this didn\'t tackle our argument because we didn\'t hinge our argument on fans. We hinged it on record labels and producers not actually supporting these artists. Why these labels are not gonna support the artists even if fans continue to do so?','other',2037,2054.3),(1814,82,3852,3854,'Because first, you as a record label or as a producer want other artists, sign up other artists, or you want other important actors to be in your movie, which means that if it\'s found out that someone is a domestic abuser, some other artists are not gonna want to work with this person, which means if you sign up that artist, you\'re gonna lose a large amount of other artists which are good and which are not gonna work. to come under your record label.','other',2054.4,2077.4),(1815,82,3855,3857,'B, bands from other bands, for example in record labels, are gonna backlash onto you because if someone from another, if someone from some band is problematic and that record label signed up that band and another band, people are gonna backlash onto the band and did nothing wrong because they\'re under the record label, which means they\'re gonna go out of the record label because the record label supports someone who is problematic, which means record labels is gonna lose other, not potential, but people they have right now. Record label right now.','other',2077.5,2109.6),(1816,82,3858,3860,'And three, if someone made something problematic, they don\'t know if more evidence is going to resurface later, that\'s gonna prove that this person was actually much worse and fans are going to be mad at this person later because this comes in waves. Even if, just like Henrik Lamar, now doesn\'t have a lot of backlash, what knows what is going to resurface in a month and people are gonna be mad at him again. Record label doesn\'t want to actually risk it, which means that our impact still stand because the record labels is gonna be the ones that is not gonna give you promotion, is not going to actually give you any media coverage, is the ones that\'s not gonna give you any money, not gonna give you to actually produce some songs, which means that all the impacts are still manifest on our side,','other',2109.9,2152.1),(1817,82,3861,3861,'which means on our side, we have actually the accountability to, we have the accountability of celebrities.','other',2152.2,2158.4),(1818,82,3862,3866,'Third point that it is, why is it unique that we do it online and we cannot do it in private conversations? I\'m gonna say that first, they cannot claim that it\'s going to be, first thing, they claim that it\'s going to be on the internet, that you\'re just gonna post this on the internet but not engage with it because you have to engage. Your teams are going to have to make some statement and people are going to start talking about it, which means they cannot cross over the bench and say that we\'re gonna get the benefits that our side is actually talking about. Secondly, I wanna tell you there\'s no incentive for you to call on someone if you\'re not gonna have a monetary benefit out of it. Why would you want to put yourself out in the open, talk about someone being violent, actually risk yourself being in a risky situation that those fans are gonna backlash onto you, that you\'re gonna have less traffic labels when you call on someone if you will not do this because you\'ll get no monetary benefit out of it, which means that don\'t see how they can manifest any of their benefits about this actually being resurfacing.','other',2158.8,2210.9),(1819,82,3867,3867,'Please, I can take it one time.','poi',2211.2,2212.4),(1820,82,3868,3869,'Sending evidence to the label also incites the fear that that growing public will lose you fans. This accountability is still symmetric because the fear mechanism still works even if it\'s not publicly said.','poi',2213,2224.7),(1821,82,3870,3871,'No, because like I told you that if no one has incentive for you to call you out, you\'re afraid you\'re gonna be called out as well. I don\'t understand how you still have the incentive since you know that other people will not call you out because you have some dirt on them, which means you have your freedom to do whatever they want because you have something on them.','other',2225.9,2244.5),(1822,82,3872,3875,'And thirdly, I wanna say that victims won\'t want to call you out even if they feel like you\'re bad because they\'re afraid that you\'re the only one. They\'re afraid they will lose against a big artist that has a lot of money. Just look at that Me Too movement. The women, even if they were actually harassed, did not come out until other women came and they knew they were not the only ones, which means only on our side we get the benefits of actually this evidence resurfacing when these people have monetary incentive to actually tell something about it.','other',2244.7,2270.6),(1823,82,3876,3876,'Which means on our side we have the principle of having more right to information and we have all the benefits out of it.','other',2270.7,2276.5),(1824,82,3877,3877,'Second class then about collateral damage.','other',2276.5,2278.3),(1825,82,3878,3885,'I wanna say that first, I want to talk to us how this impacts the family members. A, mitigation. They\'re already a family member of a celebrity. They\'re already harassed online. Not only are they going to be harassed if they\'re in a public feud, they\'re always someone who hates a certain celebrity, which means you\'re always gonna hate him no matter if you have this beef. B, you\'re not gonna be able to access all the online forums that are actually hating you. You have your kids who are going to protect you, which means that those people are gonna actually be able to gain access to all of these comments. And C, you\'re gonna have no physical harm because you\'re gonna be in these gated communities. I don\'t think you\'re gonna actually have harm to actually outweigh the victims that are gonna be less victims on our side if we have people seeing that this is not good behavior. Secondly, offense.','other',2278.3,2325.6),(1826,82,3886,3889,'I wanna say that A, most people don\'t go online to talk about this, but B, if you picked up some fights online, you\'re probably in like 30 more fights online, some more of these harassments is not gonna be so impactful to you. But I wanna say that if you support a problematic person, we want to actually trade off the harms on that person because if you support a problematic person, you\'re probably going to be encouraged by their behavior. You\'re gonna be the one who is going to become a maximum user. We\'re going to support a person like that, wanna trade off that we don\'t want these people actually being in their lives.','other',2325.8,2356.3),(1827,82,3890,3894,'And then again, just a few other points about normalizing violence. I don\'t see how this is unique to their side. People in schools, kids in schools, always think that someone is bullying someone that\'s violent. We have violent movies online. I don\'t see how you have unique benefits of just like this and we want to outweigh this by victims that we have if we don\'t have the comfortable things.','other',2356.6,2376),(1828,82,3895,3895,'So please, of course.','other',2376.1,2377),(1829,84,3990,3991,'Great talking to minors isn\'t something that\'s illegal to do. The only way we find out about that and get justice is through accountability on our part of the house.','other',3030.6,3040.1),(1830,84,3992,3998,'Two main questions in my speech from the core position, one is debate, first on accountability, secondly on collateral damage. But before that, what is this debate really about? Notice it\'s not about your mama jokes. It\'s about actual bad things artists did. For example, talking to minors, beating their wives, and so on. Why? Because these are the most impactful cases that we clearly recognize from our first. Why through escalation, these conflicts lead to these sort of reputational harms being called out and put to light.','other',3040.3,3072.5),(1831,84,3999,4001,'First, that\'s on accountability. Notice we tell you, when we get a unique way to get this information out and punish these people. What does the opposition say?','other',3072.9,3083.3),(1832,84,4002,4009,'First, they tell you that this isn\'t unique. Then I want to make it as why it\'s a unique way to get accountability. Because propositions compared to accountability is just court. Why is court bad and why doesn\'t work in these ways? Firstly, because sometimes these things aren\'t illegal but are morally punishable. Drake talking to minors, where he\'s able to get punished in court, but this is an action we want to deter other artists from doing, and that\'s why we need to find it out. They have no comparison to court. on that point.','other',3083.6,3113.4),(1833,84,4010,4015,'Secondly, we tell you that the courts just don\'t work on these artists. Why? Because these are extremely rich people. They have hush money. Insofar as they beat their wife, they rape someone, they sexually harass someone, they will just offer them millions of dollars to just stay silent. This is something that happens in the status quo. Yes, if someone like, I don\'t know, murdered my grandpa, I would want them in prison, but if that same murderer offered me 5 million dollars, I would keep shush because that\'s just money that\'s good for me and that\'s money that I can use to get better things in my life.','other',3113.8,3144.3),(1834,84,4016,4016,'But also, they just have better lawyers that just very often get them out of prison even if they don\'t offer hush money.','other',3144.5,3150.7),(1835,84,4017,4023,'Why do we then get a unique way to get information? Well, notice it must come from popular artists. Otherwise, people just won\'t listen to them. Why? Because they have good PR teams and they have a cult-like following just as proposition mechanizes from you. Notice I also explained why it can\'t come from government. So, the only way to get this information, to get accountability is to give other artists incentives to call each other out. We provide the most obvious incentive and that\'s profit, right?','other',3151,3177.9),(1836,84,4024,4029,'Like, insofar as you call out someone by making a song that\'s going to be insanely popular, you just get yourself money. Notice why is this profit incentive necessary? Why they can\'t just post on Twitter, oh Drake is a pedophile? Because they just won\'t care otherwise. Like, they necessarily aren\'t that close friends. They likely just don\'t care about the fact that he talked to minors and so on.','other',3178.2,3203.8),(1837,84,4030,4035,'But B, you always face a risk of harms happening to you, right? Because someone can always retaliate. If Kajit just posted, oh Drake is a pedophile, there is a huge risk of Drake just retaliating and then they both go into a lose-lose scenario. Yes, this risk exists in the state of scope. But A, they can justify it because yes, you risk losing some fans and someone retaliating, but you also get a lot of money in return. That\'s a profitable incentive for you, incentive that doesn\'t exist on their side of the house.','other',3203.9,3230.9),(1838,84,4036,4039,'But B, at the point, this only happens at the point when you are deep in the beef, right? Like, that\'s good to this point. And now you don\'t want to back out on Arsenal House because you just risk losing the beef and seeming weak. That\'s why even if it\'s risky, you still do it on Arsenal House, but on their side of the house, you don\'t do it.','other',3231.2,3249.8),(1839,84,4040,4042,'Notice I just proved to you why we have a unique way to get accountability on Arsenal House. They cannot just cope this by saying, oh, we get government\'s help. I\'ll take that to your right.','other',3250,3259.2),(1840,84,4043,4043,'The incentive is symmetric, not just because there\'s competitive incentive, but also not reporting crimes oftentimes just implicates you in the crime as well.','poi',3259.5,3267),(1841,84,4044,4049,'No, it doesn\'t implicate. It\'s very hard to prove that Kajit knew about these things. But also, there\'s no profit incentive on their side of the house. You just go into a lose-lose scenario with both of you. You have no positive impact on the one, on the artist reporting the crimes. Then they tell you, oh, but this call-out culture just doesn\'t work because these people have a cult-like foaming.','other',3267.7,3286.4),(1842,84,4050,4053,'A, I want you to notice this is largely untrue, right? Because no one is a die-hard Drake fan. You\'re just a die-hard rap fan, which means you\'ll be listening to both Kajit and Drake. But B, this is largely unresponsive to our materials because our accountability isn\'t hinged on these people losing their listeners, but rather it\'s being dropped by their record labels.','other',3286.8,3304.1),(1843,84,4054,4056,'And notice this still happens. So why does the record label drop you? A, they want to sign other artists that don\'t want to cooperate with the one that\'s being implicated in these sort of harms.','other',3304.7,3315.6),(1844,84,4057,4060,'So the record label must choose. They like to choose the majority. But B, you have the fans from other artists that don\'t have cult-like followings toward the original ones also backlash against the record label, don\'t want to cooperate with them, don\'t want to purchase their products, their music and so on. If they don\'t drop the artist, this is why it happens.','other',3315.8,3334.6),(1845,84,4061,4066,'We see this in status quo where Adidas literally dropped Kanye West even though he still has a ton of one month listeners. Then they tell you D or C, okay, but sometimes you lie. Why you don\'t lie? Because you want to show evidence. Insofar as the other fans have cult-like following towards the artist and you want to convince them that he\'s doing this sort of harm and a bad thing, you show active evidence for it because otherwise you won\'t be able to convince anyone. This way false accusations are largely unpopular.','other',3334.8,3366.4),(1846,84,4067,4069,'Lastly they tell you oh, but we can\'t punish them as people. A, we can\'t because I just proved to you that white courts don\'t work. This means that the only other possibility is to punish them as people.','other',3366.7,3379.3),(1847,84,4070,4074,'But B, we have a right to information. Here they tell you oh, but no, these people have a right to privacy. No, insofar as you don\'t give someone any information, their life is significantly harmed. You owe them to give that information. For example, if I have an STI, I owe it to someone to tell them I have an STI before having sex with them because this will largely impact their lives.','other',3379.6,3403.9),(1848,84,4075,4079,'I want a way of why this is the most important point insofar as we prove it. A, on justice. This is the only way to give these people justice. This is like a core principle in society. Insofar as these people did these bad things, they did these crimes, we ought to punish them at any expense possible.','other',3404.1,3420.3),(1849,84,4080,4086,'I want to weight off especially this with collateral damage. I don\'t care if fans suffer or families suffer in the process. I care more about justice. Why? Because we do this in the status quo every single time. Insofar as a man supports his family working two jobs, but he rapes someone, I will still put him in jail because he did a bad thing. Even though his family will suffer, they won\'t be able to pay rent that month, they won\'t be able to feed themselves. I don\'t care how rapists should be put in jail and should be punished.','other',3420.7,3448.7),(1850,84,4087,4091,'But B, on practicality. We get the biggest practical harm avoided in this debate. Artists just become much more careful on the outside of the house. They just avoid doing these things. This means just less people get sexually harassed and so on.','other',3449.1,3464.2),(1851,84,4092,4092,'Second clash then on collateral damage.','other',3464.4,3465.9),(1852,84,4093,4103,'Opposition has two points here. A, on families. This rarely happens. Why? Because proposition frames this debate about big actors. Notice they have PR teams and managers. These teams won\'t allow the artist to attack the families or just call out someone for being gay because this is seen as a weak, as a low blow. Especially because the other artists will call you out. Like why are you attacking my family instead of me? Are you weak? You have nothing to prove against me. This way you attack the person directly.','other',3465.9,3492.9),(1853,84,4104,4105,'But B, this can also backfire, right? Because your fans won\'t support you insofar as just attack innocent families.','other',3493.2,3498.1),(1854,84,4106,4112,'We saw this happen every time with Eminem\'s daughter. Whenever someone attacked her, they lost massive popularity. But also there\'s little impact here on the country. So why? Why? Because they are detached from fans. These are rich people who go to private schools. They live in gated neighborhoods. Nothing can really happen to them.','other',3498.4,3515.1),(1855,84,4115,4115,'So for all these reasons, please.','other',3525.7,3527.4),(1856,85,4118,4119,'I think that Japan cannot win this debate by trying to outweigh some more bullying in schools, by thousands of lives of people who have been constantly under the oppression of domestic abusers, of people who are afraid to come out because they were sexually assaulted. I cannot see how this can be a winning case for the side of opposition, so I just want to do a little weighing and say why it would clearly win this debate.','other',3567.3,3589.7),(1857,85,4120,4120,'First, let\'s talk about accountability of artists.','other',3590.1,3592),(1858,85,4121,4127,'One, why is private not enough? We told you that Japan never proved that victims would have an incentive to come out and engage in a lawsuit. We told you they would be afraid someone would dig up the dirt on them if they engage. They\'re afraid they will lose because they\'re going up against a large artist and they will lose money and time and face a lengthy lawsuit. We all debated a law case one day ago. We\'re aware how these cases can be dragged out. People don\'t want to engage with them.','other',3592.4,3615.5),(1859,85,4128,4128,'We told you that only if people were going to come out, if they have a monetary incentive, and they will only have this monetary incentive if they can actually make music, make art out of it, have Twitter and gain more following, that can only happen on our side, that people will actually come out.','other',3615.7,3627.7),(1860,85,4129,4132,'We told you that B, even if they have the incentive, we told you that it is not likely that this is going to happen because we have some large artists, we have large PR groups that can actually twist this. Because if the conflict is in pop culture and all around you, you cannot escape it. But if it\'s doing in a private room, you can actually escape it because PR teams can just hide it and make something else, make you release another music so you can actually hide it. You cannot say they were able to keep these artists accountable on their side.','other',3628,3657.3),(1861,85,4133,4133,'Secondly, under the accountability, is it likely for these things to be false and that you\'re lying about these things?','other',3657.6,3663),(1862,85,4134,4137,'We told you that why would you risk backlash? So there are other people digging up your dirt. You see lame and losing fans if you\'re found out. You can just reveal things that they have actually done because every artist, they\'re rich, they\'ve been surrounded with drugs, they\'ve been surrounded with other rich people, they likely have done something, you\'re likely to call out those things rather than risk your whole career.','other',3663.2,3684.8),(1863,85,4138,4141,'Thirdly, why are we the only ones that actually get the accountability? We told you that labels will not want to sign you up because they\'re afraid of backlash. Because no, labels hire more than just one artist under their label. So even if they prove they have the fans, the conflict following of this one person, they\'re still going to support the label.','other',3685.2,3702.3),(1864,85,4142,4144,'Labels still want to have other artists or directors want to have other producers or other actors under them. They cannot just rely on one conflict base. Which means that we are the ones that actually get the backlash of these people actually removing funding to these artists and actually giving them backlash they deserve by not giving them the media space, by having them less money, letting them progress their career as they want to.','other',3702.7,3727.3),(1865,85,4145,4147,'So I just want to outweigh, say why does this outweigh any of the collateral damage they\'re trying to push onto us? Because first, they\'re trying to push onto us families who say that they\'re rich, they have PR teams that actually shield them from these kinds of stuff and they\'re already a family member of a celebrity, this is not going to be so much greater impact on them. Secondly, on fans, we told you that if you engage with these kinds of content, you\'re likely to engage with other kinds of content which can actually bring you backlash not so impactful and we want to flip this, if you\'re not accountable, we\'re okay that you actually get some backlash because we don\'t want you to become like those who you support.','other',3727.5,3765),(1866,85,4148,4149,'And thirdly, on artists, we said that even if some artists are wrongfully accused because they\'re rich, because they have good PR teams, they\'re not going to be the ones who suffer a lot because of this. So I just want to weigh why, I want to say that this is like a trade-off we\'re willing to make.','other',3765,3778.5),(1867,85,4150,4150,'So why do we weigh?','other',3778.7,3779.6),(1868,85,4151,4154,'Because of the intensity of our impact, because if we prove to you that we have the accountability on these people, we say that artists are going to be deterred from doing stuff like this, they cannot just hide behind their money and just continue being domestic abusers, continue being pedophiles and having so much victims under them just because they\'re rich, we deter these people because they are afraid they\'re going to get called out and B, we have less other people who are going to be encouraged by these kinds of behavior because they see their favorite artists going away and not getting punished because of all of these things, which means that they are less likely to actually continue with this violence. Because these are victims of domestic abuse, they\'re not like kids being bullied in schools, please oppose. I\'d like to invite the opposition to the floor.','other',3779.7,3826.5),(1869,85,4155,4155,'I\'d like to invite the opposition to the floor.','other',3828.8,3832.8),(1870,79,3511,3517,'Am I audible at this point? Yes. Thank you. When in a rap battle, people often say, your mama this, your mama that. People laugh, people around you also make fun of you. In the pop world, this is a joke. This is entertainment. But for many, like me, it wasn\'t a joke. It was a destruction of my dignity.','other',138.6,162),(1871,79,3518,3521,'We\'re proud to stand on proposition. I\'ll talk about three things in my speech. Firstly, on unjustful say. Secondly, on ingraining bad ideas.','other',162.5,168.4),(1872,79,3522,3524,'Before that, I want to talk about framing. Firstly, on frame, what does the prominence of this look like? We think this is largely the massive popularity of increase, often the intentional way to gain attention for many artists.','other',168.5,179.3),(1873,79,3525,3533,'Secondly, what does the counterfactual look like? We think it can be resolved by two things. Firstly, on private conflict. But secondly, we think reasonable discussions in public will happen. But note here that things like violence and physical harm is unlikely on their side of the house. This is for two reasons. Firstly, on regulation and laws, we think people are likely to follow these things. But secondly, on private protection, like things like bodyguards also exist for many artists. We think things like fighting each other in dark places is unlikely to happen.','other',179.7,207.5),(1874,79,3534,3537,'Third, an important thing on small artists. We think this context is only about big actors. This is because small artists instigating conflict against bigger actors is never going to gain attention in the first place. We think this debate is about big actors.','other',207.8,220.7),(1875,79,3538,3538,'With that being said, I want to move on to my first argument on why this leads to unjustful hate as a result of associations.','other',221.1,226.3),(1876,79,3539,3539,'Three layers to this argument.','other',226.8,227.9),(1877,79,3540,3545,'First layer, on why does this create hate? There are two reasons why this is true. A, direct harm. People like family members are harmed. Kendrick Lamar literally made a 10 minute song talking about the mother, father, and son of Drake and literally making fun of those people. Also, people like fans are often harmed in these conflicts directly.','other',228.3,246.4),(1878,79,3546,3551,'Often, artists call out the other fans as being uncool or lame to gain attention and to gain leverage over other fans. B, even without things like association, we think that simply being involved with that person or associated with that person actively creates harm. Like fans, you listening to Drake or you having Drake in your profile bio often means that people make fun of you for listening to Drake. But also, family and friends are also harmed. You being part of their life, like you being the mother of Drake, often creates harm. But also, people living in local communities are also made fun of because artists often mention these places in their songs to make fun of them.','other',246.8,281.5),(1879,79,3552,3554,'Which means that fans who are also involved in that area are often harmed. Second layer on why this is uniquely bad. Two things. Firstly, on scale and intensity is far greater in our context.','other',282.3,293.8),(1880,79,3555,3560,'Because artists actively allow this and instigate it to happen. This doesn\'t normally happen in things like rivalry between artists because artists don\'t mention this in the first place. Artists saying it intentionally and pushing for it is a unique tipping point. It creates a unique validation for fans to act upon this and to create active harm. Crucially, artists cannot control how their fans act. So at the point in which when this happens, this creates immense harm.','other',294.1,317.8),(1881,79,3561,3563,'Secondly, on sense of direct attack when both sides criticize, fans feel that they need to defend themselves. If not, when they don\'t retaliate against other sides, it creates a perception that their side is weak and losing. The impact of this is threefold.','other',317.8,330.6),(1882,79,3564,3568,'Firstly, on mental harm. This leads to things like bullying in places like schools or even in universities. If you get called losers, if you are fans of Drake, this has happened many times after the Drake and Kendrick conflict. Also, we think things like social media hate and death threats often happens to fans as well who are following Drake or even to Drake himself as well. These things are anonymous and cannot be regulated.','other',331,354.7),(1883,79,3569,3570,'Also, things like, an example of this, Sabrina Carpenter fans being set death threats by Olivia Rodrigo fans for supporting Homewrecker. Second, on physical harm.','other',355.3,364.8),(1884,79,3571,3573,'Things like doxxing have happened in the past. Eminem\'s daughter being stalked by fans who hate Eminem has happened. But note that all of these are extreme because people idolize their celebrities.','other',365.1,375),(1885,79,3574,3574,'Third, impact on principle.','other',375.5,377.1),(1886,79,3575,3577,'Consumers who did nothing wrong and just simply listened to their music are harmed as well. This is a principle harm because they never opted into this or created this harm for themselves. They never agreed upon this.','poi',377.7,389.1),(1887,79,3578,3584,'Before I go on to my second argument on ingraining bad ideals in daily life, I\'ll take that deal on. If your friend supports a problematic person like Jay-Z who was a domestic abuser, isn\'t it good that you know that your friend did this? You can call them out and actually talk about it and say that this behavior doesn\'t happen again. Like I talked about, many of the promises are fake. This culture often creates things as lying and just simply making fun of the opposition for the culture, for the fame, for the clout. This is something that cannot happen on your side of the house. This is not an active accountability measure that exists on your side of the house.','other',389.3,420.9),(1888,79,3585,3587,'With that being said, I want to talk about ingraining bad ideals in daily life. A characterization of this is that things like rap battles on YouTube, Kendrick Lamar beef, making fun of other artists are something that are popularized, largely consumed, and perceived as cruel. With that being said, first layer on why this is likely to happen.','other',421.2,438.1),(1889,79,3588,3593,'Three reasons under this. Firstly, on increasing instigation of conflict. So you feel that you get attention when you do these things in daily life. For students, if you do a rap battle in a school cafeteria, there are many people watching you. If you make a good line or something, you get clout. There\'s a huge incentive for you to do this and start a conflict in the first place.','other',438.8,457.1),(1890,79,3594,3598,'But secondly, the way conflict happens is much worse on their side of the house. Because the nature of rap battles or things like that is a very problematic way. You\'re allowed to do things like lie or sacrifice accuracy for the fact that you made fun of other people. Or you\'re allowed to make fun of family members or things like that. Something that doesn\'t happen in normal conflicts.','other',457.4,481.6),(1891,79,3599,3601,'But it simply just lowers the burden to make fun of other people. Things like bullying, you have a rational idea that these things are bad. But the fact that this is created as entertainment and something that can be done, and is something that is done by popular artists, creates a reason for young people and children to often do this as well. C.','other',481.9,500.1),(1892,79,3602,3606,'The way we resolve conflict is also worse on their side of the house. Because you\'re unlikely to actually resolve the conflict once it happens. Artists who do the same thing, who create these conflicts, never resolve these conflicts. And the reason for this is that if they back out, they\'re perceived as weak and they have lost the conflict. And people on the ground who consume these types of media are likely to perceive this as well and are likely to follow this narrative and never resolve the conflict themselves.','other',500.3,525.1),(1893,79,3607,3611,'Why is pop culture unique in creating conflict? I know opposition is going to come up here and tell us that teachers are going to tell them to follow morals or not do these things in the first place. The reason why this is not true is for a few reasons. Firstly, you see pop culture to be cooler and modern than teachers and adults. The people who are telling you to not bully or things like that are people who are much older, who are less cool than people like Hendrik Delmar that you\'re listening to on social media and on Instagram.','other',525.9,554.1),(1894,79,3612,3616,'Secondly, it\'s harder to realize that it\'s bad in the first place. Things like bullying, like literally hitting someone or kicking someone, you know these things are bad. But the fact is that these things are created as pure entertainment or as a joke. This makes it easier for people to actually do this in the first place. C, it\'s more accessible.','other',554.6,573.9),(1895,79,3617,3620,'Most people watch these content on social media. Also, most people are not malevolent individuals. So they\'re not gang affiliated and don\'t know like punching other people or stuff like that. But normal people are allowed to do these things because they consume things like normal music.','other',574.4,588.9),(1896,79,3621,3624,'What is the impact of this argument? Mental harm is you face immense mental harm when you\'re made fun of. But crucially, it\'s really hard to opt out on their side of the house or like regulate this on their own. This is true for two reasons.','other',590,603.6),(1897,79,3625,3628,'Firstly, it\'s perceived as cool and as a joke. As I told you, if you try opting out of this scenario, you\'re probably going to be perceived as someone that is uncool or like someone that is boring who is unwilling to participate in rap battles. This creates an incentive for you to stay in the conflict. Secondly, it\'s popular, right?','other',604,621.3),(1898,79,3629,3631,'Artists constantly create new views and new content to allow this to happen. Which means that insofar as you want to be trendy and be trend, like stay on the trend in school, you\'re unlikely to leave this and opt out yourself. For all those reasons, we\'re very proud of this Danhok proposition.','other',621.5,636.4),(1899,79,3632,3632,'I\'d like to thank the first speaker of the proposition for his refined speech in the length of 8 minutes and 12 seconds.','other',642.1,646.5),(1900,81,3732,3733,'At the top, I\'m going to prove one thing that will largely win us this debate, I\'m going to prove that the following about these artists is oftentimes extremely cult-like in nature, it makes it hard for opt-out and accountability to occur. Crucially, I\'m going to use this light of mechanism to respond to the vast majority of their assertive reconstruction, or rebuttal against their material, and it\'ll deal with some of their material as well.','other',1265.7,1286),(1901,81,3734,3737,'Why is this likely to be cult-like? A few mechanisms. One, oftentimes, there\'s just massive self-glorification that occurs in media, for example, the fact that you portray yourself as always being correct, or the fact that you, or the fact that you have immense amount of money to, for example, form media personalities online that allow you to look good. Secondly, because competition between artists oftentimes fosters more attachment to the artist.','other',1286.4,1308.3),(1902,81,3738,3739,'This necessarily oftentimes occurs before the actual cult celebrity conflict occurs, on a simple level, on a daily basis, such competition is likely to form cult-like following. Thirdly, oftentimes, individuals\' media consumption directly forms individual identity.','other',1308.6,1323.5),(1903,81,3740,3741,'For example, the type of music I consume oftentimes forms the type of person I am, or the personalities I have, and by extension, the people I support. Fourthly, because it\'s just on a very simple level, it\'s just very easy to like these people, right?','other',1324,1336.7),(1904,81,3742,3743,'They have cultivated media personalities online, they have tweets that go through PR personnel. Crucially, this means they make it very easy to like these people.','other',1336.7,1345.3),(1905,81,3744,3744,'All of this then proves the cult-like following nature of these artists.','other',1345.8,1349.2),(1906,81,3745,3746,'Crucially, this then responds to a lot of their material, for example, about how collective counseling makes opt-out easier, because the cult-like nature of such following makes collective cancellation impossible because there\'s a collective action problem, where because you support them and people around you support them, it\'s very hard for you to actively voice out against them. This responds to a lot of their material.','other',1349.6,1369.4),(1907,81,3747,3748,'Let\'s look at the rebuttal that came against our first argument, for example. Against our first argument, there were four main responses from their bench.','other',1370,1377.1),(1908,81,3749,3752,'The first idea is that the family can take a stance against the person, and therefore, they will not face this backlash. At the top, obviously, just because you then disavow your kid doesn\'t mean that you don\'t face harm. Like the fact that you put this person on the planet, the fact that you supported them in their past life, in the past few years, for example, are still grounds for you to hate them. But also, secondly, it\'s just not likely that family disavows their kid just after one incident, especially when it\'s extremely unclear if it\'s true and it\'s just a big media, it\'s just a big celebrity fight and beef on social media, for example.','other',1377.3,1411.1),(1909,81,3753,3756,'Their second response then would argue that even if this doesn\'t happen, it\'s okay because the family actually enabled this person. Crucially, on a principle-embarked level, this is obviously false. These people, no matter whether or not the person who they gave birth to is the murderer, for example, that doesn\'t give you then grounds to persecute the family who gave birth to this person. But also on a very intuitive and practical level, I don\'t think this is oftentimes true because these people didn\'t create the practical harm for which they, in the court of public jury, are being persecuted for.','other',1411.5,1441.8),(1910,81,3757,3758,'Thirdly, their third response argues that artists won\'t do a lot of these things, especially when it\'s not true because it\'s not popular to do. But all of the mechanism for cult-like following proves that it is not only impossible for you to hold these people accountable, but actually is actively popular to do because these cult-like followers think you are correct and therefore would pick up any information that seems to be correct or seems to be in favor of the artist they support.','other',1442.4,1465.8),(1911,81,3759,3762,'Their final response is that the PR team won\'t let you is wrong. Obviously, they mechanized all of the popular incentives for the PR team to do this for attention. They try to then get rid of our impact by claiming a lot of stuff about why you can do this symmetrically elsewhere, but we proved to you that the cult-like nature of such following extremely amplifies the scale of impact and you simply cannot opt out of this just by removing this from your bio. Obviously, hate persists afterwards.','other',1466.2,1489.3),(1912,81,3763,3766,'In response to our second argument, they would argue that this is easy to spot and therefore suddenly all of these impacts don\'t happen. The problem with the type of conflict that they instigate, however, is that even if it is easy to spot, it is very hard to punish for because it is impossible to determine whether this is pernicious and malicious in nature or a simple joke between friends. Rap battles can both manifest in a friendly manner where your friends jokingly do roast battle and in a harmful manner in cases of such bullying. So punishments like getting expelled are just very hard to do because it\'s very easy for the person to justify as a joke.','other',1490.3,1524),(1913,81,3767,3772,'Finally, let me respond to their first argument by proving two things. One, a mutiproof call-out can symmetrically be done in our world, and secondly, a mutiproof call-out is better done in our world. Firstly, why can call-out be symmetrically done in our world? Three mechanisms. First, oftentimes there are very legal avenues to the most pernicious scale of harms. For example, if you have evidence that an individual is a child molester, for example, it is much more important that you take the legal avenue out of this rather than you do on social media. Secondly, simple call-out mechanisms that don\'t lead to celebrity conflict is still okay because if the party doesn\'t engage back in the conflict, then celebrity conflict doesn\'t manifest, which means if Kendrick still had evidence that Drake was a child molester, it\'s still fine for him to post that as long as he doesn\'t then engage into a rap battle or a roast battle or social media posts that amplify the scale of conflict.','other',1524.5,1574.6),(1914,81,3773,3775,'Call-out is symmetrically possible in our world. Thirdly, you can still do things like tip the manager or the label under which this person is under, and crucially, these labels have incentive to maintain some amount of reputation, which means there\'s still incentive to care in these situations, especially when the harms are as extreme as they pointed out to be. So we would argue that call-out can just symmetrically be done in both worlds.','other',1575.1,1596.1),(1915,81,3776,3779,'The second level of response, though, is to argue that call-out is actually better done in our world for two reasons. One, the type of call-outs that celebrities in opposition world will result to is public call-outs, which oftentimes is not adequate in holding individuals accountable. Drake still has somewhat of a following despite, for example, the actions that he\'s being called out for. Crucially, then, we would argue it\'s better that the call-out be done through the legal avenues or through tipping the manager and label which actively find them rather than on simple social media, and crucially, it\'s unlikely that both happen at the same time.','other',1596.4,1632.4),(1916,81,3780,3780,'But also secondly, when there\'s an absence of celebrity conflict, the call-outs that are important are more likely to get attention in our world because it is more important, it is more obvious, and it is more easy for sensationalization and people to catch on to. Go ahead.','other',1632.6,1645.5),(1917,81,3781,3781,'If there\'s actual evidence, then it\'s more likely when all of the evidence actually comes to the surface to have a stronger case even if you want to sue him in court.','poi',1646,1653.1),(1918,81,3782,3784,'My problem is how is a rap battle evidence of child molestation, right? Just because I say Drake did something doesn\'t mean I\'m now posting evidence about it. Kendrick never proved his allegations against Drake.','other',1653.1,1664.8),(1919,81,3785,3790,'Third extending argument, then, on artists. I\'m gonna prove three things. One, worse conflict. Two, the simple harm that manifests from conflict. And three, oftentimes the unjust type of conflict, the unjust harm that occurs. Essentially, this is three claims in one argument.','other',1665.3,1678.9),(1920,81,3791,3794,'First, why is worse conflict likely? One, because conflict is more likely to heat up in the public sphere. Legal avenues or simple call-outs are less likely to manifest harm as it is a public sphere that cult-like call-outs oftentimes amplifies. But also secondly, because posting on social media, then, empowers, justifies, and makes it easier for harmful practical actions to be taken.','other',1679.9,1699.5),(1921,81,3795,3798,'When your fans support your call-out, you are more likely to take harmful action. The second claim, then, on the simple harm of conflict. Public beef necessarily incurs harms that do not manifest in, for example, legal avenues. For example, things like career downfall, profit downfall, death threats, bullying, doxxing, and stalking to the artists themselves.','other',1699.8,1717.3),(1922,81,3799,3801,'Crucially, all of this is not just important because it\'s a practical harm, but the third thing I\'m gonna prove. We would argue that oftentimes this type of harm is unjust. I\'m gonna prove firstly why oftentimes the accusations in beef is false.','other',1717.8,1729.3),(1923,81,3802,3802,'One, because on the incentive level, obviously you have incentive to win and to garner attention, and accountability doesn\'t apply in these situations.','other',1729.3,1736),(1924,83,3897,3898,'I invite the third proposition speaker to conclude the proposition substantive case. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Is this full on claim? Yup.','other',2385.7,2459.3),(1925,83,3899,3899,'I\'ll start in the next five seconds.','other',2459.7,2461.8),(1926,83,3900,3903,'If you look at the mech dump at opposition, all of their analysis and accountability is basically contingent on our counterfactual where accountability is minuscule. But if you look at their line of analysis, L even concedes that the accountability measures exist in our world. When you talk about how family and their harms to family oftentimes are not gonna manifest in our city, the house because the accountability measure. It\'s unclear to me what that accountability measure does not exist in favor of our argument to begin with.','other',2467,2496.8),(1927,83,3904,3907,'So the first thing I wanna prove to you why counterfactual is not a fact as you pointed out in our first speaker. And I wanna note all of the analysis that we provided you in our second speaker is just unresponding to the fact that we have legal avenues. The fact that we can still call out because if you look at the motion, this motion is specifically about the prominence even without the prominence and the active incentive of those media and labels to just make the content about beefing. I think you can just call out specific worst forms of action for example, cheating that they have talked about in your example, all of which means that I think all of the harms are symmetric.','other',2497,2531.5),(1928,83,3908,3911,'If anything though, we think that the call-outs are better done in our world. Firstly, because people would use the legal avenues more in our world because the fundamental assumption in their case is that there\'s only one reason which you can hold one individual accountable, i.e. the celebrity stupid culture. But in the absence of this culture, the only mechanism is legal avenue and we think this is more effective insofar as we can use things like police officers which can oftentimes get the evidence that is actually sufficient for their case.','other',2531.9,2559.3),(1929,83,3912,3913,'But secondly, I think the attention is zero-sum. In the absence of the celebrity culture, that\'s when legal avenues actually arise which is the reason why our counterfactual is this far better.','other',2559.5,2569.5),(1930,83,3914,3914,'Three themes in this speech.','other',2569.8,2570.8),(1931,83,3915,3915,'First, an artist.','other',2571.1,2571.9),(1932,83,3916,3921,'Second, an external hate that the fans face. And thirdly, a conflict. Before that, why not? Okay, yeah, legal avenues don\'t work because these are rich people who can just have rich lawyers who find loopholes and never actually be blamed for these things. Note that every celebrity is so, so rarely actually like put in prison because of the things they do. Note how unintuitive that theme is.','other',2572.3,2592.3),(1933,83,3922,3922,'Even rich individuals cannot just go against the state.','other',2592.4,2595.5),(1934,83,3923,3925,'I think there\'s a sufficient number of accountability measures insofar as everyone has the voting power. I think politicians care more about electability. Even if you don\'t buy that, I\'ll engage you that as well.','other',2595.8,2606.9),(1935,83,3926,3929,'First things first, let\'s talk about the artist\'s claim. I think their one line of analysis is to say, oh, that clash from the public is important and that\'s when I think the labels care about profit and that this is the only way in which you can do so. The first and obvious response to point out is that public accountability measures is symmetric on both sides of the house. Even if you don\'t have the culture that they have talked about, I think you can still say they have the evidence on our side of the house and just fear monger them that these things might go out in the public, which is the reason why these things still happen on our side of the house.','other',2607.2,2641.7),(1936,83,3930,3933,'Secondly and more importantly, this just discredits all of the analysis we provided you from our second speaker on how the most of the individuals that these labels care about are the cult-like followers. Why is it that these individuals care more about the cult-like followers than the general public? The reason for that is oftentimes the profit comes from the most proximate fans in the first place, which is the reason why these are the individuals who are likely to go on and just share your beliefs. These are the individuals who are more likely to actually buy tickets for you, which is the reason why the accountability measures, which is the one-liner magic bullet for their side of the house, is unlikely to happen, which is the reason why the premise of your case just falls apart.','other',2642,2682.3),(1937,83,3934,3936,'The second thing they say is that you won\'t lie on their will because the person will just, you know, fight against the will and then will still lie. The first thing I want to point out is that it\'s just not contingent on you lying or not. Whether or not you lie doesn\'t really matter to the point at which they can lie independent of your action, which is the reason why oftentimes individuals would likely have a collective action problem where they have to operate on the worst-case scenario, which is why oftentimes a lot of the individuals often resort to lying, and therefore our claim and false accusation still stands at the structure of the debate.','other',2682.6,2717.1),(1938,83,3937,3938,'But even if you take all of them, I think these only have two impacts at first opposition. If we were able to just disprove each of them, I think we still win the debate.','other',2717.2,2726.9),(1939,83,3939,3944,'The first impact is on the principle. They say that this creates a proportionate punishment. One, they never prove lies is properly proportionate. All of our material preempts this when we talk about false accusation happening. One, I think there\'s an incentive on the label to create false accusation to begin with. I think this is a good form of entertainment to just talk about a subjective and speculative thing, all of which means that oftentimes this disproportionate punishment flip in their impact.','other',2727.1,2753.4),(1940,83,3945,3948,'The second impact they give is fans\' right to know. One, this is still contingent on false accusation. Secondly, I think there\'s basic levels of privacy that even artists have, which is the reason why this is a more proximate right, even hierarchy of right. Second question, an external hate that fans receive.','other',2753.7,2772.2),(1941,83,3949,3949,'The first thing they say is that hate will happen anyway.','other',2772.5,2775.1),(1942,83,3950,3953,'One, they just never really respond to the analysis and uniqueness we provided you with first. The difference is the scale and the intensity of harm that exists on both sides of the house. And secondly, I think it\'s just much more on self-defense. Even if there\'s a competition of popularity on both sides, these popularities are not as zero-sum as the fact that you are just literally fighting against each other and you are going to lose the conflict in instances you don\'t fight back, which is why they just miss the entire nuance.','other',2775.1,2802.7),(1943,83,3954,3956,'The second claim they make is that large harms won\'t happen due to accountability measures. Seishi already explains that the lines are oftentimes gray. But secondly, it\'s just much impossible to hold the fans accountable on the sheer number of the fans that exist and the fact that they\'re anonymous on social media, which is the reason why fans have the capacity to just send slurs through our fans to begin with.','other',2802.7,2824.7),(1944,83,3957,3959,'But last thing they say is that family can just take a stance and do things like that. In instances where they support, the harm is fine. One, they never prove why this is proportionate again, because oftentimes just raising your child does not really mean that you can get a death threat from your followers to begin with, all of which is the reason why I think their argumentation just really doesn\'t make sense, which is why I think our first argument still stands at this juncture of the debate.','other',2824.9,2851.8),(1945,83,3960,3960,'What was their positive material then?','other',2852.1,2853.9),(1946,83,3961,3962,'I think their one liner from Saken was that other victims can become more empowered to speak out. Firstly, I want to point out at the top that the types of call-outs and beef are not really empowering.','other',2854.2,2864.1),(1947,83,3963,3966,'I, things like yo mama, or things like, oh, you raped a minor, isn\'t a really big thing that a victim wants. Secondly, I\'ll flip this. This is just demean for the victims insofar as this looks like these issues that they really care about are things that are light on the public. They want illegal avenues that you need to exist in our side of the house.','other',2864.4,2882.8),(1948,83,3967,3968,'And I would also argue that the things are just emphasized on both sides. It\'s not really an important argument.','other',2883,2887.8),(1949,83,3969,3970,'On Wavve, fans are far more important than artists for two reasons. First, on the number of individuals.','other',2888.2,2893.7),(1950,83,3971,3973,'There\'s no moral distinction on whether you care about artists or fans, which is why you must resort to number. Secondly, on the scale of impact. Do not underestimate the harm we provided you from first.','other',2894.1,2904.5),(1951,83,3974,3974,'It was about death threats.','other',2904.8,2905.7),(1952,83,3975,3979,'It was about the bullying that happens in your schools. And when fans are not used to the harms on a daily basis, that\'s when these things like death threats are just much worse and much more big than what artists go through. Finally, let\'s talk about the conflict resolution. I think the one minor response that came from their world was to say, hate is symmetric because the fans support them on both sides of the house. I\'ve already preempted this in two ways, that there\'s a delta.','other',2905.9,2930.3),(1953,83,3980,3985,'The second thing is that there\'s no physical harm. Again, this is an underestimation of all of our impact because it wasn\'t just about physical harm, yes. It was about your roasting. It was about your slurs. It was about the ways in which you cannot resonate and you cannot just resolve the conflict. And it was about you resorting to more pernicious and toxic forms of harm when all of your one minor response doesn\'t work, which is a reason why I think the lack of response was damning for their case.','other',2930.7,2953.9),(1954,83,3986,3986,'On all metrics, opposition clearly takes vote Japan.','other',2954.1,2957.3),(1955,86,4157,4161,'The first reason was that we force individuals to choose better, more accountable and effective call-out mechanisms. The point is that public accountability is oftentimes useless. Drake is still rich, still employed at his label and still popular despite his actions and despite the public accountability that came. We would argue that the types of accountability methods that we incentivize are just better. But also secondly, on zero-sum attention, because actual call-out is actually empowered in our world rather than crowded out by random hate and media frenzy, we would argue that we do call-outs better.','other',3874.5,3906.6),(1956,86,4162,4166,'Their mechanism for doing call-outs better is largely on the level of incentive, i.e. that there\'s higher incentive to call-out in their world due to the profit incentive. From POI, we pointed out that the incentive is largely symmetric. One, oftentimes there\'s still just competitive incentive to out-compete other people in the same market that you are in, but also secondly, that not pointing out or not reporting oftentimes implicates you. Maybe this is not criminally, although oftentimes it is if you look at, for example, these parties, but also just on the level of public court, we would argue that it still implicates you in a lot of ways.','other',3907,3937.7),(1957,86,4167,4168,'We would argue then that the incentive to call-out is symmetric. On the actual whether we can do call-outs symmetrically in our world level, we would argue that this washes out at worst for three reasons.','other',3937.8,3947.5),(1958,86,4169,4171,'One, we told you that legal avenues exist, and though there\'s a mirage of responses that come from their world about how there\'s a lot of non-immoral, non-illegal immoral things, the actual worst-case harms are always going to still be illegal. We are still going to be able to persecute, and even if victims don\'t come out, we can still anonymously report things, etc. We would also pose that a second and third alternative would still work.','other',3948,3967.8),(1959,86,4172,4174,'Our second alternative about how we can still call-out on social media without having to instigate conflict and create a rap battle about it, and this would still create accountability. And we told you that we could still go to the label, and this still creates the same fear of this information going public without the information actually having to go public, and they will still then, according to their mechanism, repair. Second question, on innocent individuals.','other',3968.1,3989.8),(1960,86,4175,4175,'The first thing is innocent celebrities, and we would argue that innocent celebrities getting called out is a fairly large problem, and their level of response on the incentive is to argue that you don\'t want the other side to attack you, and the other cult-like members to attack you for the lack of evidence.','other',3990.2,4004.5),(1961,86,4176,4178,'The problem is not only do a lot of these celebrities never have to and never do publish their evidence, but also not be mechanized as why you are more likely to care about your own cult-like following than you would the other side. On the level of capacity, they would argue that PR teams don\'t allow this, but not only is their profit incentive to allow this, but you can create plausible deniability in your gray areas by doing things like what Kendrick does by saying he\'s probably a minor and not actually a minor. This impact is important.','other',4004.8,4028.9),(1962,86,4179,4179,'Even if these people are rich, and even if these family members are used to it, we would argue that rich people can still face harm.','other',4029.2,4034.8),(1963,86,4180,4181,'Suicide rate among celebrities is higher than the average job, and you\'re not really used to it. You\'re a family member.','other',4034.9,4040.3),(1964,86,4182,4183,'Families rarely get attacked. You don\'t know Taylor Swift\'s mom, but if Taylor Swift was in a rap battle, the mom would get attacked, and fans don\'t have any of these mechanisms anyway.','other',4040.4,4048.3),(1965,86,4184,4185,'So fans, then, are they even innocent? Because they would argue that there\'s no moral distinction between a murderer and the family of a murderer.','other',4048.5,4054.7),(1966,86,4186,4189,'The problem is, there is obviously not only a moral distinction between active harm and not, but also sometimes you just don\'t know about this until it goes public, and you\'re still able to get attacked for it. You can\'t punish people for not being constantly online. The harm is fairly real as well, and they would like to say doxxing is not possible from a distance. That\'s literally just what doxxing is, but just on a principle level as well, we mechanize why this is principally wrong.','other',4055.1,4078.6),(1967,86,4190,4190,'Third and final thing in the speech, though.','other',4079.1,4080.5),(1968,86,4191,4192,'For the average person, we would argue that the types of conflict that they promote are harmful, not just because you instigate more conflict, but the way by which that conflict occurs, or the fact that you will never end these conflicts. We posed three reasons why this was unique to no response from their side.','other',4080.7,4097.1),(1969,86,4193,4195,'We told you about the coolness of this. We told you how hard it is to realize it\'s bad when it\'s glorified and made as entertainment rather than portrayed as harmful and delight. We talked about the accessibility by which you get this type of information.','other',4097.3,4109.2),(1970,86,4196,4198,'We would argue this is a massive mental harm, especially because it\'s very hard to opt out of these types of conflicts, because when other people instigate it, it seems like you\'re weak for opting out. We would argue this is a real harm that Mitsuki weighed from his whip as well. For these reasons, we propose.','other',4109.6,4122.2),(1971,86,4199,4199,'I\'d like to thank the proposition by our speaker, who has defined peace and length of four minutes and 40 seconds, and all those who played around.','other',4127.7,4132.5),(1972,96,4850,4852,'The underground Church is nothing short of mere symbolic resistance. What lies underneath is the destruction of the followers\' beliefs, their lives, their communities, their freedoms. So proud to stand in this high proposition today.','other',549.7,616.6),(1973,96,4853,4854,'Two arguments I\'ll be forwarding in my speech. Firstly, on why we prevent the extinction of the underground Church, and secondly, why we moderate the Chinese patriotic Church.','other',617.1,625.4),(1974,96,4855,4857,'But before that, I have two important clarifications to make on context. Firstly, recognizing the underground Church would violate the ongoing 2018 China-Vatican Agreement, which was renewed last year to extend to 2028. This means that Pope must defend a world in which you have an active and unilateral reversal of this diplomatic status quo by the Holy See, which would be a provocative statement.','other',625.7,648.1),(1975,96,4858,4862,'But secondly, the Catholic Church itself can be seen as increasingly Americanized. Pope Leo was elected as the first Pope from the U.S. this May, and this decision was publicly embraced by almost all American leaders, including Trump himself. So the timing of this shift is likely to be perceived as a Western-led challenge against the CCP\'s sovereignty and its control, particularly since it would attract widespread attention on Western media. This perception would reinforce powerful anti-Western narratives within China, but citizens have been told since they were born that they face a century of humiliation from foreign meddling.','other',648.5,685.7),(1976,96,4863,4865,'With that, I would like to go into my first argument on why the recognition of the underground Church leads to its extinction. Note that this cuts down an entire premise of that entire principle argument, because note that that principle argument was contingent on the practical case, that once you recognize the underground Church, that they will be able to access its religious freedom, that they will be able to reparate and restore the underground Church to what it was before. But we\'ll be cutting this premise by proving to you that the underground Church will be even more oppressed than before.','other',686.1,713.9),(1977,96,4866,4867,'With that said, what is the current treatment of the underground Church? Unlike what the Pope tells you, we think that currently, Beijing both factually and logically does tolerate a small underground Catholic Church, and we will mechanize this to you.','other',714.2,727),(1978,96,4868,4869,'Firstly, because it has hidden operations, the majority of followers are socially isolated and operate completely underground, so underground members are careful to do very little to spread their message in fear of facing even the smallest of persecutions. But B, because the underground Church is a drinking threat, new believers tend to join the patriotic Church because it has higher visibility, so the underground Church is very, very limited in expansion.','other',727.4,753.3),(1979,96,4870,4871,'So we are not claiming that the underground Church faces no persecution, but we do think that it is currently very limited. For instance, there is no reason for the CCP to surveil or attack a weak and powerless institution that is declining in power and even less incentive to do so once their own Church gets formal recognition, so the underground Church will start losing followers on our side of the house as well.','other',753.8,776.9),(1980,96,4872,4872,'With that said, there are three reasons why this incentive actually flicks on the opposition side, and this is for three reasons.','other',777.1,785.3),(1981,96,4873,4876,'Firstly, because there is a loss of legitimacy of the patriotic Church. The real threat to the CCP would be a powerful Church whose existence undermines the CCP\'s authority and legitimacy, and prop creates this threat for two reasons. A, because Beijing will be concerned that the underground Church will grow in number, since their recognition signals to Chinese Catholics that true religious authority lies outside of the state-sanctioned Church. But B, because further, if the underground Church gains traction, any shifts towards anti-CCP messaging would pose a serious, serious threat.','other',785.3,821.5),(1982,96,4877,4879,'Note, even if the underground Church itself does not formally denounce or collate the CCP, grassroots threats still remain. A single rogue bishop that feels empowered and morally righteous after gaining recognition from the Vatican could cite the Bible to implicitly or explicitly attack the CCP\'s actions, which would trigger a lot of dissent. Thus, the CCP would have to take further action to prevent the growth of a Church that they simply cannot control in the future.','other',821.9,849.3),(1983,96,4880,4885,'The second reason is that there is a sense of betrayal against the whole BC. When possible, Beijing would rather compromise because crackdowns do carry costs. However, now that this compromise has failed, they have to crack down. Look at their actions in Hong Kong after the 2019 mass protest. Before 2019, they were actually willing to offer Hong Kong concessions to pro-democracy advocates and to offer them voting rights. But after the 2019 protest, Beijing started cracking down because they saw that compromise as emboldening the opposition.','other',849.7,881.1),(1984,96,4886,4892,'Here, in the context of this motion, the CCP didn\'t compromise with the Vatican back in 2018. It allowed the underground Church to discreetly practice their beliefs and allowed the Pope to veto their bishops\' elections. This wasn\'t an easy decision for Xi Jinping, who is a strong-arm leader and does not want to give authority to the stand-off actors. So on proposition side, and the point at which you actively recognise the underground Church, their compromise is rewarded by the Vatican literally spitting in their faces. It would be seen as a group that cannot be trusted. Compromise is weakness. They will crack down, not believing that the Vatican will not go further to support the underground Church.','other',881.7,919),(1985,96,4893,4893,'No, thank you.','other',919.2,919.8),(1986,96,4894,4898,'Onto my third reason then, on why there\'s long-term reputational risk. Even beyond this specific case, the long-term implications of this retraction will trigger the CCP to respond. In cases like this, where the compromise has been undermined by the other side, we think that the CCP, not falsely responding, actually sends a message to other parties that you can betray the CCP and actually get away with it. So this perceived weakness might invite more challenges against the CCP by other religious minorities within China. So this is a legitimate threat to the CCP\'s authority.','other',919.8,952.2),(1987,96,4899,4904,'With that said, let\'s be clear. What are the impacts of these crackdowns? Millions of underground Christians can face mass arrests, forced disappearances, or be forced to go into re-education camps, which are efforts of China\'s darkest challenges. This is currently not happening in the status quo. They can even face torture and be treated like the Uyghurs or the Tibetan Buddhists that they literally talked about. This also means that the religious freedom of the underground church would be limited, which literally cuts away the promise of that last argument.','other',952.8,981.4),(1988,96,4905,4906,'With that then, onto my second argument on why the Vatican can moderate the Patriotic Church. We will prove that once the Holy See recognizes the Patriotic Church, it will be able to exert influence over the Patriotic Church\'s ideology and that to moderate it.','other',981.8,993.9),(1989,96,4907,4912,'This is in four ways. Firstly, through direct leverage. The CCP will want to maintain people\'s support, fearing that its followers will grow skeptical of the CCP\'s Church and choose their loyalty to God over their loyalty to the state. Further, it wants to achieve more common goals beyond just recognition, such as unifying both churches, as agreed in the 2018 agreement. So the CCP needs the Vatican on our side. That\'s trust and communication between those two actors to maintain this leverage.','other',994.1,1022.5),(1990,96,4913,4915,'Secondly, through the bishop selection. Based on the 2018 agreement between the Vatican and the CCP, the Pope can veto bishop nominees that undermine Catholic beliefs. So this is a certain power on our side that will be cut once we take all of that action from their side of the house.','other',1022.5,1037),(1991,96,4916,4918,'Thirdly, even without direct control of the Vatican. nominations. Cardinals do care about getting into the Pope\'s good graces because they\'ve grown up being told that the Vatican is important and that they want to climb up the hierarchy to become archbishops. The Pope thus has leveraged other bishops directly, but only once they are recognized.','other',1037.3,1056.8),(1992,96,4919,4920,'Fourthly, we think that the patriotic Church\'s dogma will change due to increased Pope exposure to counterbalancing their governments. We think that more bishops from the Vatican will visit China and that for privileged lair to their followers, that what they\'re being taught by the CCP is not necessarily true.','other',1056.8,1072),(1993,96,4921,4924,'On proposition, the CCP will be further incentivized to turn the patriotic Church into a glorified center for the spread of propaganda. Vatican\'s direct challenge to the CCP authority means that even the slightest resemblance to the Vatican cannot be tolerated. So the CCP will strengthen censorship of Vatican narratives and surveillance over its own believers. We moderate the open Church and the Pope makes it worse.','other',1072,1095.1),(1994,98,5021,5021,'I\'m going to cover two clashes, first on the principle and second on the practical for the underground church.','other',1766.7,1771.4),(1995,98,5022,5023,'But before that, I kind of want to clarify what the characterization of the CCPA is. Because from the first, they seem to characterize the CCPA as this inherent moral bad that is always bad in all instances, and all members of the CCPA are constantly at their own harm.','other',1771.7,1784),(1996,98,5024,5027,'Firstly, sure, the CCPA does spread some propaganda, like the entire media and school system around it. But it also does protect the spiritual and emotional lives of entire communities. And that is much more to do with the role it plays. For example, for our mother in a rural city, she may work long hours and is under constant pressure, but will find solace in religion.','other',1784.4,1802.6),(1997,98,5028,5029,'But secondly, we argue that the church to some extent can be moderated, recruited through the continuation of existing mechanisms like involvement in bishop nominations, creating new leverage, and counterbalancing narratives that followers will be exposed to even independent of that leverage. Given that, even if we get on both sides, the CCPA will hold some power over its followers, it\'s better that we moderate them.','other',1802.9,1822.1),(1998,98,5030,5032,'Thirdly, we argue that moderation is actually more valuable because weakening the open church is not the same as making it better or worse. By the contrast, when the CCPA feels under threat, its incentive is to hold its followers on a tighter leash. This means more indoctrination, this means more surveillance, this means more threat against it, this means more harsh punishment for its dissenters.','other',1822.5,1841.5),(1999,98,5033,5038,'With that clarified, on a principle question. The general response to the principle, even if you don\'t take any of the smaller responses I\'m about to give is this. That on a level of logical priority, for any principle to matter to people, you need these people to be alive. Remember, this is not an after-motion, we must debate from a universal perspective. Universally, protecting life and liberty is much more important than symbolic gestures and religious purity. Subjectively, the Catholic Church may have prior obligations, but this is irrelevant from a universal standpoint.','other',1841.5,1869.1),(2000,98,5039,5040,'Responding to their first idea on the principle, then, as to how this is the only entity who can do things and allow for things like salvation, which creates a unique responsibility to the people of the underground church. At the top, obviously, this is contingent on our first argument on an access to the underground church, but being charitable, two or three levels of response.','other',1869.8,1886.7),(2001,98,5041,5048,'One, it is wrong that the OBC recognition is the tipping point for a salvation and all of the other benefits from manifesting in the first place. And the intuitional plot is this. Many people are not in a situation to leave the church. Many people, for example, are busy on their Sundays. Many people are in non-religious families that don\'t allow them to go to church. Many people live in situations where a church is incredibly available. These people still get access to salvation and all of the other benefits of Catholicism, regardless of access to the church. And in the same manner, we would argue that the underground church still has some amount of access to salvation.','other',1887.3,1916),(2002,98,5049,5051,'But also, secondly, we would argue that extending salvation to all people is not necessarily a responsibility that the Vatican has on a principle level. Because by this logic, it would be principally wrong, for example, that the Vatican doesn\'t create churches and rule Japan just because there are some Christians there. Maybe these people can get access to salvation, but this then doesn\'t create a responsibility for the Vatican to have.','other',1916.4,1938.3),(2003,98,5052,5054,'But finally, they don\'t even fulfill their own responsibility. Because recognition never promotes that there is proper and safe access to such institutions. So this is still practically contingent at worst.','other',1938.6,1948.8),(2004,98,5055,5056,'Responding to their second principle, on condoning the harmful CCP ideas and tactics. Obviously, at the top, supporting the open church is not the same as supporting the CCP itself.','other',1948.8,1958.4),(2005,98,5057,5059,'Yes, it is an institution of the state and under some degree of state control. But by that logic, for example, supporting humanitarian aid in Gaza is analogous to supporting Hamas because it is facilitated through them. If our only way to help victims of the regime is through regime control operation, that does not make it support of the regime.','other',1958.8,1976.8),(2006,98,5060,5065,'But also, there are a bunch of other responsibilities we can give here. For example, should we even oppose peace talks with Putin because he is evil? Or Hamas and Netanyahu, even if we can achieve a ceasefire and end the genocide? We cooperate with their moral actors to protect innocents all the time. It is insufficient for a prop to scream that the CCP is evil. They must then prove collaborating with them is more evil than allowing underground Catholics to be tortured or killed.','other',1976.8,2000.8),(2007,98,5066,5072,'Second, the premise of this case is that the patriotic church remains evil after receiving recognition. The status quo has already produced results. Since 2018, 10 dioceses have received jointly approved bishops. Underground and patriotic clergy are cooperating in some areas. There are signs of gradual convergence and not collapse, which we improve further on our side. Walking away from this progress is not a principled standard. It is reckless evaluation.','other',2001.2,2022.8),(2008,98,5073,5078,'But finally, the evaluation is already a moral actor. I genuinely don\'t understand what you got here. It is already constituting moral acts because it has institutionalized the moral acts. We constantly hear, for example, about child sexual abuse in the Vatican. It is the largest history of prosecution of gay people and religious minorities and women. Even if just supporting Ismael Pope cares about people, should Catholics, for example, detricate before they forward a Filipino president who massacred people, the Vatican is already immoral.','other',2023.1,2048.5),(2009,98,5079,5084,'Responding to finally the third principle, on historical reparation, then. For a reparation principle, they must prove active and intentional harm and equal reparational product. Not only did the Vatican not necessarily kill the people of the Underground Church, which doesn\'t create the necessity for a reparation principle, but we\'d argue this is not even the way to give it back because you aren\'t restoring it in historical fact. You are recognizing the Underground Church, which creates harm to the people. So your whole point is good or bad tactics, since the people from the Sextian Church are the most important stakeholders and should protect them, rather than the people from the Underground Church are going to have servers to operate them. using these from China\'s resolution.','other',2049,2081.5),(2010,98,5085,5086,'Our first argument protects the people of the underground church. Before I do extension, because I don\'t, before I do it all, I don\'t want to run out of time, so here\'s the extended third argument.','other',2082.1,2090.1),(2011,98,5087,5090,'Maintaining the Holy See\'s unique diplomatic anchorism. The framing is simple. We are not just talking about the Catholic Church as a religious authority, but the Holy See, a sovereign state recognized by international law. Uniquely, unlike most states, it is not seen as a political threat or ideological enemy, but perceived as a neutral and principled moral actor.','other',2090.5,2106.2),(2012,98,5091,5093,'Vatican\'s moral diplomacy has historically been to do things about mediating ceasefires or securing protection for oppressed religious minorities, or influencing global opinion on issues like migration, poverty, and peace. This is a highly influential role, as Vatican\'s statement leads to massive public opinion shifts in Catholic-majority countries, shaping government decisions on things like aid or defunding war. Beyond politics, grassroots Catholic communities can be driven to donate or support humanitarian causes as well.','other',2106.6,2128.2),(2013,98,5094,5095,'Prop Shatter is a diplomatic model that relies on persistent engagement to achieve slow but steady progress globally. There are three reasons why.','other',2128.6,2134.9),(2014,98,5096,5098,'One, fractures the church. Pope Francis\' efforts to deepen ties with China were initially considered controversial, but Catholics in the global church community warned that the Pope\'s commitment to preventing polarization and positive reconciliation. Props for clopping inconsistency undermines the church\'s unity and pastoral authorities for its followers.','other',2135.3,2151.3),(2015,98,5099,5100,'Second, it promotes the Vatican\'s reputation as a consistent diplomatic partner. As Sirogo explained, the sudden revoking of an existing deal signals to other states that the Vatican is an unreliable and even more potentially politically charged power.','other',2151.7,2162.5),(2016,98,5101,5102,'But thirdly, it confirms our virginity and it\'s clear that religious diplomacy is a trap. Authoritarian regimes force the conscience away from them and that repression is safer than cooperation of them to endorse issues that result in a collapse.','other',2162.9,2173.1),(2017,98,5103,5106,'This is important for two reasons. One, the OVC maintains formal diplomatic relations even with non-Western authoritarian powers including China, Cuba, Iran, and parts of Africa. During the 18th CCP Vatican, we located the Vatican and entered into China\'s religious structure for the first time in 70 years since the Communist Revolution. Maintaining this trust and communication channel is the only way to allow the Catholic Church to influence and offer basic religious protection for its most vulnerable followers and authoritarian regimes.','other',2173.4,2197.1),(2018,98,5107,5109,'But also secondly, the OVC is one of the most important global actors that engage in tractor diplomacy about relationship building and changing perceptions for a slow transition to decent unity. The Vatican\'s diplomatic trust is a rare and fragile asset. It doesn\'t usually deal with threats, but it opens doors to the one-off scenario.','other',2197.3,2212.7),(2019,98,5110,5112,'When you throw away that trust, you don\'t just lose China. You lose influence in every regime watching this debate. We protect that trust and that global will.','other',2213,2220.5),(2020,98,5113,5117,'Finally, because I have some time left, I want to defend our first argument against the two responses. The first response we made is, CCP is under a lot of scrutiny, and et cetera, et cetera, et cetera. We pointed out this hasn\'t worked in the past. They say the tipping point is Vatican help. But all the same, Vatican has historically called out, for example, humanitarian right crises in China before, and none of that was left to the international scrutiny necessary to undercut any of this.','other',2221.4,2243.1),(2021,98,5118,5121,'Furthermore, we argue that the CCP has already adapted and is willing to risk some backlash over risking their global power. Then they claim crackdowns are already bad. But the harsher Trump claims crackdowns already are, the harder it makes their own burden to prevent and stop them on their side. At least an hour would be to prevent them from making it worse.','other',2243.3,2259.3),(2022,98,5122,5122,'For these reasons, first I\'ll be having three questions, which is, which side does the Vatican fulfill its duty?','other',2259.6,2396.4),(2023,100,5193,5194,'Fulfilled, if you can be a good, sufficiently dealing with the premises, lack of responsibility, and shared propitiation. I want to talk about two things.','other',3035,3058.1),(2024,100,5195,5196,'First, that the principle to do in underground church. Lastly, I\'m explaining what you need is a purifying principle for you.','other',3058.4,3068.6),(2025,100,5197,5199,'First things first, let\'s talk about the principle. I want to know in strategy, if the propitiation principle must have been inherent to the reputation of an underground church. Because if the conclusion of their principle is that we should care, we just literally care through our practical argument.','other',3068.8,3083.1),(2026,100,5200,5202,'I want to do two things on this though. Firstly, I want to bring back this egist call out that duty is irrelevant. Secondly, that this duty doesn\'t exist.','other',3083.5,3091.8),(2027,100,5203,5207,'Firstly, that this duty is relevancy. Because I want to point out that this motion is not an actor motion to begin with. It is a third party motion to begin with from a neutral perspective. And therefore, there is no moral distinction between a duty that this Vatican has and this duty to protect other individuals of all the life and death of individuals underground. It\'s unclear to me why this subjective duty that they have pointed out is important to begin with.','other',3092,3115.1),(2028,100,5208,5210,'But secondly, in terms of why it doesn\'t exist, they have multitude of principles. Let\'s take a look. engaged in one by one.','other',3115.3,3120.7),(2029,100,5211,5214,'The first thing they say is that recognition is necessary for salvation. Why? This is a literal assertion. There are hundreds of thousands of underground churches on the ground and that I want to first point out that I think a lot of the popes do tell them what the God thinks and individuals in fact individualistically connect with God. I don\'t agree with me why recognition is that important.','other',3120.8,3140.8),(2030,100,5215,5216,'Secondly, in terms of historical reparation, I want to point out that this is just entirely happening in contingent. Thirdly, in terms of emotional solace, one, we are proudly valuing human lives over human feelings.','other',3141.2,3153.1),(2031,100,5217,5220,'This case simply isn\'t just an impact on the debate. Two, emotional solace is contingent on the underground Catholics being able to access their religion, which they say they cannot on their own. Three, this is symmetric in emotional solace that open church followers get in bars instead of in house because notably there is no moral distinction between those individuals in underground church and those individuals in the CCPA. On their world, CCPA used recognition, which is the reason why I think they lose on this as well.','other',3153.3,3180.7),(2032,100,5221,5225,'The final thing is that you\'ll be complicit in the emotional church. Even if you don\'t buy these three methods that we provided you with your second speaker, I want to point out, one, there\'s a difference in supporting CCPA and CCP, but two, this is contingent on CCPA being inherently bad. We provided you our second argument as to how you can literally improve the CCPA through leverage the days it exists. The reason why the leverage is unique in our world was literally three mechanisms that it still unresponded to. That the Holy See\'s recognition asks for legitimacy, that it decreases the resource burden for the CCP, that there\'s a diplomatic benefit, and the CCP just gains the benefit uniquely in our world because the Vatican now supports CCP uniquely in our sense of the arts.','other',3180.9,3224.7),(2033,100,5226,5227,'The final thing you see is that China has broken the agreement. No, literally since 2015, the 10 appointed ones have broken the road, not from the CCP, which is why they\'re just blind in this debate.','other',3224.9,3235.4),(2034,100,5228,5232,'Finally, I\'ll be very practical. Why not? Why is the benefit of the government-sanctioned church exclusive to your side of the house? Why don\'t we have this whole thing as a pain outside the house? Why wouldn\'t this happen? I don\'t want to forget what benefit they\'re talking about, but if you want a proper little crackdown, let\'s talk about it right now.','other',3235.8,3250.8),(2035,100,5233,5235,'I want to note, don\'t underestimate the impact we\'ve proved to you. Because we\'ve proven in our world that on their side of the house, churches will be desegregated and their followers will be detained. If they cannot safely access their church or have no church at all, then they cannot practice this religion, which is pre-requisite for their principles and function.','other',3251.2,3268.3),(2036,100,5236,5243,'But on terms of their own, you\'re just taking risks all the time. I want to weigh that principle below this practical example for a few reasons. Firstly, because we\'ve proved the extent of persecution they face now is much smaller than the type of persecution that exists in their world. Insofar, the capacity for the CDP to crack down exists symmetrically on both sides of the house. The difference on both sides is in incentive. We explained what incentive magnifies in their world. Secondly, because the majority of them didn\'t even meaningfully consent to this risk, children following their parents to church, wives following their husbands, all of the things are risks that they never consented to. I think they\'re intuition-pumped and not aware of the principle being enforced on their stand.','other',3268.6,3308.4),(2037,100,5244,5246,'Two sub-themes under this clash. First, on the clarification of what the status quo looks like. Secondly, on whether backlash works.','other',3308.7,3314.5),(2038,100,5247,5253,'Firstly, on status quo, because Proposition 1\'s tell you that the status quo is already bad and people are taking risks. I don\'t point out that this is just not true, but even if this is true, this is strategically bad for the proposition. First, because the harsher the proposition claims crackdowns already are, the harder it makes their own burden to prove that the Vatican can stop them from their world. Secondly, even if crackdowns are as egregious as they say, this is not comparative. And one thing that they need to say is true is the fact that the undergrounds are already existing in the status quo and the CPP has allowed it to exist. What we have claimed to you is that on their world, CPP will literally crack down on them just like mid-extinct. Now then, let\'s chat about why crackdowns won\'t happen anymore when there\'s new ones.','other',3314.7,3357.4),(2039,100,5254,5256,'There were two main challenges. The first claim they made is that the Vatican can now use its leverage to protect its underground followers at an independent opinion. The first thing I want to point out is that whatever leverage Vatican has, it is lost after they lose trust of the CPP by recognizing the underground church and once the CPP has nothing left to lose, in order to try and appease the Vatican, the CPP needs to believe that it is a trustworthy partner that they can rely on and allow them the authority to run the world,','other',3357.7,3392.7),(2040,100,5257,5257,'not already have the uncondensed and open church because they would have to say in the underground church is only legitimacy and therefore all of which means to you that the CPP will not listen to the Vatican on their side of the house, the leverage that they say is important only works for our side of the house.','other',3392.7,3408.1),(2041,100,5258,5261,'Finally, let\'s talk about their claim of backlash. Note, if this was possible, they would presumably also listen to the demands towards Uyghurs and further on. But the second thing I want to point out is that things like countries sanctioning them presumably is unlikely to happen because people and the countries are presumably relying on the Chinese to do it for us. I think China is the number one exporter in the world.','other',3408.4,3428),(2042,100,5262,5269,'But let\'s say, let\'s say their best game happens, that you get attention and there\'s going to be some benefits set towards it. The first thing I want to point out is that literally no one cares, right? Currently, we all know that the Uyghurs are suffering, but we obviously prioritize our self-interest over others because it doesn\'t directly impact you. But secondly, this is short term. There\'s so much information in the status quo, it\'s unclear to me why this one single information will help you. Third of all, there\'s a time lag problem, right? There\'s a time in which this attention materializes into support. I think what CTP\'s response does is to crack down now before they have help come in.','other',3428.2,3461.6),(2043,100,5270,5272,'But the final thing I want to point out, independent of any other clashing debate, is our third argument that still hasn\'t been responded to, which is what we\'ve proved to you is the unique diplomatic influence that the whole thing has outside of China, in countries like Cuba, in countries like Iran, where Western countries that fail to actually provide support for the most vulnerable individuals on the ground, I think we create the largest impact in the debate through our third argument. We are able to provide unique help through the leverage that the whole thing has.','other',3461.8,3490.6),(2044,100,5273,5274,'The lack of support from the third opposition was dying under case. On the scale of impact, we went into a clash.','other',3490.9,3496.9),(2045,101,5275,5277,'Recognition is not a moral gesture, it is a risk. To endorse the underground truth is to invite repression, not revival. It sacrifices believers for symbolism, when opposition reproducts people in ways that actually matter.','other',3586.6,3598.9),(2046,101,5278,5280,'At the top, an independent path to victory. Unrespondent is our third argument that proves that the maintenance of the OPC\'s unique diplomatic influence not only impacts people in China, but globally. This was a fairly important argument.','other',3599.4,3611.8),(2047,101,5281,5282,'The impact of this argument proved that in every regime, authoritarian or not, the Vatican\'s unique standing as they can see in the global diplomacy sphere allows for the Vatican to influence countries in ways that no other nation can. That its standing in international law as a sovereign state with mutuality and with moral mindset in mind allows for its power to be heard around the world.','other',3612.2,3635),(2048,101,5283,5285,'The weighing for this argument was simple. One, on the level of scale, the number of people and the situations that these people are in, and therefore by extension, the way by which we save these people extends beyond China and to every authoritarian state in the world. On the number of people but also the intensity of impact that we save them from, this is an important argument.','other',3635.2,3654.3),(2049,101,5286,5287,'Secondly, in the worst case, even if this is speculative in terms of saving vulnerable people, both sides have vulnerable people that the Holy See can potentially save but cannot 100% certainly save. So at least this shouldn\'t be a reason to vote in favour of opposition.','other',3655.4,3668.6),(2050,101,5288,5289,'But finally, on the level of certainty, because just the fact that you change the decision, you change and alter the decision to go against the previous Pope Francis\' decision and the norm from the last decade fractures the Church regardless of whether the consequences of this decision is good or bad for China. So this is true on the level of certainty.','other',3668.6,3687.2),(2051,101,5290,5291,'We would argue this independently wins us this debate, not just because they didn\'t engage with it but also because this is an important impact, a surging impact, on one that opposition had to deal with. Their lack of response alone is damning.','other',3687.2,3699.9),(2052,101,5292,5295,'This is the most important thing I wanted to do in this speech. The second most important thing I wanted to do in this speech is to weigh off the principle in this debate. From decades before the majority of the work, and my book extends this work, we proved that all of these principles are practically contingent. This is true on three levels.','other',3699.9,3715.7),(2053,101,5296,5299,'On a very intuitive level, access to the Church is the prerequisite for all of their principles manifesting. Reparation doesn\'t matter if you can\'t accrue any of the benefits of reparation. Protected access to salvation doesn\'t matter if you cannot access the Church that offers that salvation in the first place. So we would argue that we win on the level of practical, obviously, due to the lack of response, and this wins us on the level of principle.','other',3715.7,3738.3),(2054,101,5300,5303,'But also secondly, their principle is just flawed in a lot of ways. For example, their argument that we all protect the access to salvation never makes it clear why the regulation from the Vatican is the only unique way by which an individual can access salvation. We responded in three levels with their second level on the principle on condoning the CCP, and we proved that none of the criteria for reparation is ever met under their principle line. So, on the practical level, and which we\'ve proven is almost more important.','other',3738.3,3764.7),(2055,101,5304,5304,'Firstly, on the underground church.','other',3764.7,3766.7),(2056,101,5305,5311,'Their incentive responses bore down to, well, we can get international security, but if the underground church would have been safe already, the reason why it hasn\'t worked is because international scrutiny fails. And the mechanism and the tipping wood that they would prove is Vatican says it, so it must be suddenly extremely influential. But the Vatican says a lot of things that never actually get, that never actually happen. It\'s concluding, I guess, it\'s China. We, for example, the Vatican calling out the concentration camps in rural China has never manifested in these concentration camps being reversed. So the methodology of international scrutiny doesn\'t work. The only way is to decrease oppression, which we do through recognition of the CCPA.','other',3766.7,3804.6),(2057,101,5312,5315,'Finally, on the CCPA, they claim that the benefits of the CCPA are not mutually exclusive and can be accessed in their world. The problem with this one-line response is not only from the intro from my deputy, we prove that it actually gets worse in their world, but they are the ones themselves who say the CCPA is bad, and we provide the CCK with a unique methodology to rectify its harmful practices. Because Vatican recognition is the only methodology by which moderation of the patriotic church can ever be achieved. No other diplomatic method has worked.','other',3804.6,3834.7),(2058,101,5316,5316,'We must recognize the CCPA, both for the underground church, for the CCPA, and for every other country in the world. I\'ll close.','other',3834.7,3841.6),(2059,95,4773,4776,'Panel, the Holy See signed an agreement with an ultra-orthodox state that persecutes religious minorities. This agreement guaranteed that a point of issue is not an incitement to regime and close your eyes to the persecution of their Catholic brothers and sisters. This agreement was signed in 1933 with the Nazi Germany. We should not make the same mistakes and we should learn from the past.','other',58.5,76.9),(2060,95,4777,4781,'So how did you propose? What I\'ll do in this speech. First of all, I\'ll explain why I made this argument. I\'m trying to recognize the University of Chicago Church in China and my failure to do so makes it completely authoritarian persecution. Before I make those arguments, a few pieces of setup.','other',77.2,91.2),(2061,95,4782,4785,'What was the cause of neutrality for the last years? History is not tied to neutrality in the face of opposition. When the Vatican signed an agreement with the Nazi regime in hopes to secure religious freedom, instead it lent authority to tyranny. With the same state being held now repeatedly against it in China.','other',91.6,109.4),(2062,95,4786,4790,'Since 2018, the Vatican entered a secret agreement with the Chinese Communist Party. It handed the right to a point of issue to a government that subdues churches, jails, prisons, and places a gruesomely close focus of their present Xi Jinping. That agreement has led to no real gains. Underdog Catholics remain persecuted. State churches preach the law to the party, not to God.','other',109.4,129.6),(2063,95,4791,4799,'And what has the Vatican done? We must understand the intentional localization of religion by the Chinese Communist Party and Xi Jinping. Since early 2000s, and especially under Xi, the regime has launched a systematic policy called socialization. A campaign to conserve all religious tools of the state, strip the form and influence, and align with the Communist ideology. What does this mean in practice? It means, for example, religious texts are removed to reflect party values. Images of Jesus are being replaced by proteins. Servants are monitored, punished, and imprisoned if they resist. And so on and so forth.','other',130.1,160.8),(2064,95,4800,4800,'This Communist regime, also the same regime that imprisons over 1 million people Muslims in re-education camps, vandalizes the beaten Buddhist practice, and persecutes fellow non-followers to the point of forced war on their hearts.','other',160.8,172.8),(2065,95,4801,4805,'Furthermore, the state church is not an independent religious institution. It is an extension of state repression. The Vatican in 1957 by-law was drawn and by the party to fracture the Catholic Church and co-opt its spiritual influence. So when the Vatican recognizes the state church, it is not protecting faith. It is endorsing a lie, a fabricated church built to silence dissent and legitimize dictatorship.','other',173.8,195.9),(2066,95,4806,4806,'This debate asks, will the Holy See stand with the machinery of tyrants that rewrites the gospel and buries the martyrs in silence, or with those who, like the Christian pentecostal, chose faith over fear, Christ over Caesar?','other',196.4,209.8),(2067,95,4807,4810,'So, moving on to our first argument, on how the Holy See has a moral duty to its followers. The first reason why. The Holy See has a duty to the Catholics as the one who inherits the world\'s religious spirit, founder of the Catholic Church. Therefore, being the only institution capable of providing a legitimate synodality to all the members of the clergy, with the pope being the direct representative of God\'s will of martyrdom.','other',210.4,233.9),(2068,95,4811,4814,'That means that a Catholic can direct the Holy See to be able to exercise their faith, a core of their identities. So, a canonical canonist can only be baptized, for example, if the atheist is a willful and a member of the clergy, or the sacred writes false news, and that false canon never attains the artist, for example, and all the others need the Vatican to be able to exercise their religion. Therefore, the false autonomy and capability of self-automization, of fulfilling their metaphysical needs, of achieving salvation and avoiding eternal punishment in the afterlife, is only possible if the Holy See, through its actions of representatives, recognizes and endorses their faith. That creates a strong moral obligation of the Holy See, since it is the only entity responsible and capable of enabling their multidecimal autonomy to the Christians. Second reason.','other',233.9,282),(2069,95,4815,4819,'Condoning and actively recognizing an oppressive regime such as the Chinese regime, even with the aim of minimizing its harms, is tenuous and helpful for two main reasons. Eighth. To recognize it is to grant legitimacy to the regime and consequently to its actions. It is a symbolic act of empowerment and support for a blood-same state. There is no way to separate these actions. Supporting one facet of the regime is supported with success and consolidation, which means being completely disenfranchised.','other',282.3,309.1),(2070,95,4820,4823,'If you make any kind of agreement, this means you\'re giving some kind of legitimacy and giving legitimacy to all actions, but only to one party. Second. The recognition also implies approval of the moral morality of the regime\'s actions and its underlying values. It is the establishment of certain tactics as officially accepted, such as attacks on freedom of expression by the government and attacks on freedom of religion, the destruction of human dignity through torture, rape, imprisonment, authoritarianism, and disregard for the ability to do good as well as being a civil court. This means policy supporting and making agreements with the state\'s sanctioned church directly in reply with actions of this government.','other',309.5,350.6),(2071,95,4824,4825,'They say this is okay because we want to protect our people, but by this they say it\'s okay to kill and persecute millions of Christians throughout this dissent. Before I move on to take a point.','other',350.6,359.7),(2072,95,4826,4826,'So if your argument is that CCP is evil, do you think that all states have a moral obligation to recognize Taiwan at the expense of the Taiwan?','poi',360,367.6),(2073,95,4827,4829,'I don\'t believe that the CCP recognizes Taiwan at the expense of China. The CCP is well protected and moreover, the Holy See has directed to Christians. You can\'t compare this to any other religion or any other kind of population in the world because Christians is the only religion that has this centrality, that has this duty to fight for them and this duty with its followers and to respect their past and to fight for their legacy.','other',367.6,398.4),(2074,95,4830,4833,'The third reason on this argument is the historical reparation. The obvious instinct when something is wrong is to try to restore the morals to the state it was in before the heart occurred. This applies regardless of whether you were directly responsible. This means that this duty of reparation is especially compelling when it was your responsibility to prevent the heart, in this case the Holy See.','other',399.1,420.4),(2075,95,4834,4836,'In this case, there is a duty long rejected by the Holy See towards faithful in the underground churches, a duty of recognition and fulfillment of its part in the reciprocal spiritual contract of the church with its followers. Therefore, reparation to the poor must be given in a form equivalent to the entry. The only way to remedy for the Holy See to return things to how they should have been in the past is with the faithful of the underground churches being recognized and legitimized under the spiritual authority.','other',420.4,445.5),(2076,95,4837,4840,'Now, how are these principles being heard? Firstly, the Holy See is recognized as being very responsible for the deaths of thousands of its followers and the official representative of the church granted this regime, which is authority. Through the eyes of underbound Catholics, the Holy See is not merely abandoning them through mission. It is actively endorsing their character, torture, and killing.','other',446.4,471.9),(2077,95,4841,4844,'Secondly, on our side of the House, the Holy See ceases its policy of turning a blind eye to the suffering of its believers and instead recognizes their devotion to faith. It sends the same symbolic message as God\'s representative to those who remain loyal at the cost of their own lives, those who remain loyal to the dogmas of Rome, refusing to yield to the distorted virtue of Catholicism propagated by the CCP. There is comfort and a sense of fulfillment for the faithful of the underground churches on our side, when they are finally recognized and legitimized by the Pope, the closest voice of God on earth. In all their acts of faith in the modern world, this honors and brings to light all those who were killed in the past and, showing their love, forgotten.','other',471.9,508.9),(2078,95,4845,4847,'And when the opposition is found, worse than not being recognized, the underground faithful are necessarily framed as the illegitimate group acting against what is now declared to be the true presence of the Church in China, the state\'s sanctioned churches. This is more than just support for the faith, and more than anything else, because for the faithful, their life on earth holds minimal importance compared to an eternity of fulfillment or suffering in the afterlife. The exercise of the Holy See\'s duties to enable them to live out their faith is more important than varying lives.','other',509.2,535.4),(2079,95,4848,4848,'This does the preferable, even if the message will result in greater repression.','other',535.7,544.4),(2080,95,4849,4849,'For all these reasons, there is a place so proud to propose this one.','other',545.2,548.9),(2081,97,4925,4928,'The problem with opposition is that they never acknowledge how the Czechs hold themselves closely. They face the union as something magical, as something where China absolutely follows, and China will absolutely comply with the idea of unifying all the churches. Of course, it obviously won\'t be easy. Who never went to church and who constantly are fighting against the Chinese Church and prefer to die than to join their church to continue living?','other',1097.6,1208),(2082,97,4929,4931,'The problem is that China has already broken the agreement multiple times, appointing bishops who value the people of the Vatican, and appointing bishops who never spoke low to the Vatican, to the