ing international backlash. Because notice how many questions we have all around the world. Look at the United States, where Christianity is so, so strong, where the President is Christian, where the Bipartisan movement is Christian. Look at Europe, where conservatism is rising with Christian movements, with Catholic movements. Notice how this is only, notice how this is likely to generate a huge backlash for China because all of their main, all of their main traders, trade partners are part of the Catholic Church. And they\'re likely to retaliate because they also feel that their friends, their sisters, and their brother are doing it.','other',1308.2,1340.8),(2088,97,4953,4957,'But notice I\'m also a consumer. Because people are also going to see this. It takes one camera with one bad photo of someone suffering, of Catholics suffering in China for people all around the world to notice suffering, but they\'re putting it away. Why is this so? Because people, because there are people who are willing to die for their faith.','other',1341,1359.2),(2089,97,4958,4961,'Because in China, other people in the other Churches have been willing to die for their faith for a long, long time. We think all around the world there are also a lot of very strong, very faithful Christians. And we think these people are also very likely to boycott China as to what they can do. For example, a farmer in Brazil, because he\'s very, very faithful to his Christian Catholic faith, he\'s likely to stop maybe buying stuff from China, even if it costs more, because he understands that the cost is worth saving or boycotting his brother\'s non-compliance to the people who have actively coming to his faith, or actively coming to his faith.','other',1359.3,1392.2),(2090,97,4962,4965,'But even if not all of that is true, even if it\'s all false, even if you don\'t buy all this analysis, notice how often the comparative effect would not be worse on our Southern house. Because we think that persecution cannot get any worse on our Southern house. Because notice how the Southern government Church on their Southern house is already subject to arrests, appearances, surveillance, torture, secret trials, propaganda campaigns, and positive censorship on all positions on the Southern house. This means that the baseline is already extremely high.','other',1394,1417.5),(2091,97,4966,4969,'And for China, there\'s very little room left to increase the pressure. Because now they\'re planning to extremely disperse the world more than before it is, and generate more black prostitutes. And once it is dispersed, the Vatican backtracks back to the Golden Law, where it could be noticed how now, on our side, the Vatican is actively supporting these people, they\'re not exploiting them, they\'re just testing them and putting them to the test. And this is why the Vatican is trying to make the most of the situation, which reignites public doctrine and law abiding, and human rights movement globally.','other',1417.9,1445),(2092,97,4970,4970,'So the political commission would function as a moral megaphone, and a diplomatic tool, not a provocation.','other',1445.1,1450.2),(2093,97,4971,4978,'On the point of my point of contention, we think this is extremely simple, and that\'s why we\'re not letting it happen. Because China has already violated the human rights movement. Because China will never see anything that it can say. Because they made reason as to why China continues to persecute and drop churches, and because they want to be the one who is most authoritative. This is why there are no more Christians who describe their churches as set pictures of cheating feet. Because they do not want the Vatican to be more powerful. So we do not understand why they would ever comply with Vatican agreement with Vatican agreement, as they do not seem to be susceptible. I don\'t understand why their house would be better.','other',1450.5,1481.3),(2094,97,4979,4984,'On our side, I think we should be very clear on three principles. One, are they on duty? They\'re faithful. Because they are faithful. Because only the Vatican can enable the salvation of the Christians, of the Catholics. And these people who have fought for years and years and years, to continue to be faithful to the Vatican, deserve their recognition, deserve to know that they will be as soon as they have a single day death. Secondly, because there is a policy for recognition.','other',1481.5,1501.8),(2095,97,4985,4989,'Because a symbolic endorsement, anything that\'s symbolic already means a lot. It empowers the oppressed. It empowers China to say, Vatican is on the right, other countries can\'t retaliate, because the policy is giving them the moral compass. They have no response to how legitimizing the CCP church also validates their control over the faith, also tells other people about the CCP. And lastly, because I think the principle of historical justice, because over the years, people have died over their faith in China.','other',1502.2,1532.1),(2096,97,4990,4991,'We can work to restore it to the community of the right. This is a duty that only the police hold, and only in our side of the house is preserved.','other',1532.2,1538),(2097,97,4992,4996,'China has things like concentration camps all over their country that international scrutiny has already found. The CCP has never responded to such scrutiny. Why would it work here? Because we have Vatican help, we have holy-seeing missions. Because it means that all around the world we have Christians and Catholics actively trying to back us, trying to stay seated at the Vatican\'s helping.','other',1540,1559.5),(2098,97,4997,5000,'Because Vatican is the biggest moral compass for all Christians around the world. In Vatican, the holy-seeing themselves are recognizing the CCP, so that no one else around the world has the authority to question the CCP\'s scrutiny. Because Vatican, at the highest level, is already giving the CCP the authority, the legitimizing of the CCP. However, on our side, we have the Vatican saying that the CCP is wrong, that they are the one that are legitimizing the underground churches, that they have much more to ask for, because Christians will always stand with the holy-seeing, will always stand with God.','other',1559.5,1593.9),(2099,97,5001,5006,'All that argument is how visibility saves, and how recognition is a real protection for the persecuted, the house of the unhoused by Christ. The good news is that authoritarian regimes thrive on silence. The Chinese government can oppress underground Catholics precisely because no one talks about them on their social media. That\'s why they don\'t recognize them, because they think the CCP is right. But when the holy-seeing can actually be on the ground, it brings global attention. And in that, pressure, accountability, and actual protection that never exists on their social media.','other',1594.3,1623.1),(2100,97,5007,5009,'Because right now, Vatican agreements with the Chinese state obscures reality. It gives the illusion that there\'s a religious freedom, that the state-taking church represents the Catholicism in China. This illusion is exactly what prevents journalism, human rights groups, and foreign governments from focusing on the actual persecution happening underground, happening in suburbs, or no one can see.','other',1623.5,1641.9),(2101,97,5010,5014,'When the Vatican said publicly that it would recognize the underground church, that illusion went through the roof. In one, it legitimized the underground church as real, not illegal, not marginal to the Catholic. In two, it empowered NGOs to track and defend the rights of its believers. In three, it forced international media to report abuses because they now invoke a recognized right of global religion. In four, it creates new diplomatic consequences, involving majority nations face pressure from its citizens to respond to religious persecution.','other',1642.1,1666.7),(2102,97,5015,5019,'The authoritarian states fear exposure more than moral condemnation, because no one can actually know their trade deals towards an international state is at risk. For example, after the Nobel Prize was awarded to Liu Xie, the Chinese government couldn\'t treat him like any other president, because he became a symbol. Because his name was spoken in parliaments, in protests, and in newspapers. Because the visibility didn\'t save his life, but it changed the rules of the Indian government. The same applies here.','other',1667.4,1686.8),(2103,97,5020,5020,'I\'m proud of this.','other',1687.1,1687.5),(2104,99,5123,5124,'And then the second is, which side supports or dissists? And then third, which side actually protects all these followers of the church?','other',2396.8,2406.2),(2105,99,5125,5128,'So first I would like to state that, first going off to which side actually fulfills the duty of the Vatican. So first, the Vatican has a duty towards its followers, just like the state has a duty to its citizens. We already state this in the statements. Second of all, is that we have a sort of reparation that is a duty of the Vatican.','other',2406.6,2425.2),(2106,99,5129,5133,'And why is that? First, we already explained that an underground church kept loyal to Rome and kept loyal to the Pope and to the actual Catholic teachings for its entire existence, even during many, many decades of intense persecution where their side was being murdered and arrested by a dictatorial regime. And so we see that these people are actually devoted and are actually available to give up their lives in order to maintain their faith. And so, and also another thing is that Rome actually brought this religion and imposed that religion on them. So this is why we have this duty to protect these citizens of the Vatican state.','other',2425.4,2464.8),(2107,99,5134,5135,'And also we have, and also recognizing the state church is basically betraying the devoted followers that kept loyal to the church for many years. And it\'s also betraying the Holy See itself.','other',2465.1,2478.4),(2108,99,5136,5140,'It\'s betraying their own teachings because they are supporting a church that doesn\'t actually follow any of their own teachings and is actually state controlled and changes completely what they stand for. And also another thing is that by recognizing the underground church, we finally help out the vulnerable. And also, their case about diplomacy, even if this doesn\'t actually help with the Holy See diplomacy, which we don\'t think actually is the case, it\'s still not as important as protecting its own citizens. Even if it doesn\'t actually help the diplomacy of the Holy See, the Holy See has their primordial duty of protecting their own followers and helping their own followers. And this is what\'s most important.','other',2478.6,2525.1),(2109,99,5141,5145,'And also diplomacy with the CCP, we already tried this, we already tried this agreement. And China broke this agreement multiple times. This diplomacy is a failure. And we already seen that we need to take action and not just this basic diplomacy. And also, and so now moving on to which side actually supports the CCP.','other',2525.5,2547.5),(2110,99,5146,5149,'So of course we both agree that the CCP is bad and evil. Both sides have stated that they have internment camps. Both sides have stated that they have media control and other mediums of control that actively hurt the population and hurt the most, those are the most vulnerable, which are the followers of the underground church. We already agreed that they are a bad stakeholder in this debate.','other',2547.9,2570.2),(2111,99,5150,5154,'But in their side, opposition supports the church that doesn\'t uphold the Catholic values and also validates the regime. The state-sensitive church is basically an organ of the states. It\'s an organ of the state that the state\'s CCP can control and can influence the citizens. On our side, we\'re replacing the church that actually uphold Catholic values and that actually uphold all these values that they want to uphold. They are protecting and they\'re validating an organ of the Chinese dictatorial party.','other',2571.2,2603.1),(2112,99,5155,5157,'And also another thing is that they say that the CPCA isn\'t a socially sanctioned church. It\'s important for the people that follow it and how this is helpful, but we don\'t care. There are not important stakeholders here.','other',2603.6,2617.8),(2113,99,5158,5159,'The people that follow the state-sanctioned church are not important in this debate because they don\'t rely on the Pope and they rely solely on the state and they\'re not actually important to this debate. We are talking about the ones who are actually vulnerable because the people that follow the state-sanctioned church are the real ones that have been persecuted, the ones that have been persecuted are the ones in the underground church and those are the ones that are actually important for the debate and those are the ones that will actually be impacted by what happens in this debate.','other',2617.9,2645.1),(2114,99,5160,5162,'And also, they were saying that breaking this contract is wrong, but boo-hoo. China did it many, many times and many clergy members were approved without the Pope actually approving it and without the Pope actually helping this decision. And so, they have done it many times and the Vatican has been doing it to protect them.','other',2646.1,2668.1),(2115,99,5163,5166,'And also, another thing is that this perceived weakness will make other parties react against the CCP. So, while the Vatican showed some defiance against the CCP, this would make other parties react, but isn\'t this a good thing? Isn\'t this what would drive social change against the dictatorial fascist party in China? We see that in our side, we can actually drive change instead of just validating what is going on in China.','other',2668.3,2694.8),(2116,99,5167,5171,'And also, before moving on, I\'ll take a look at the why. Yeah, you keep saying that the CCP keeps violating the 2018 agreement. They\'ve never done this since 2018. All 10 people who were elected from the CCP have been approved by the OVC. Can you cite one example where this doesn\'t happen?','poi',2695.3,2713.5),(2117,99,5172,5175,'So, we are actually appalled by this statement because we have stated in our own cases throughout the bench what examples there are and what actually happened for us for this contract to even be broken. And we already stated how the CCP is, of course, will never hurt the, help the underground church and how they have no consensus to even help the underground church. And so, the Vatican is the only stakeholder that can actually protect them. But also, moving on to which side protects the church the most, the followers of the church the most.','other',2714.5,2749.4),(2118,99,5176,5177,'So, first, their argument is actually about the extinction of the underground church and how they\'re important and how this persecution on our side of the house would increase. But we already explained how this increase in persecution is highly unlikely because, first, there\'s a lot of domestic, there\'s also domesticated very much, a lot of international instability right now.','other',2750,2775.1),(2119,99,5178,5180,'CCP does not want more of it. And many of China\'s trade partners are, of course, of Catholic descent, of Catholic culture, at least in general, or of Christian culture. And we already explained that just one person filming and showcasing to the world what China is doing to these Christians and what is happening in China, given this transparency, and the Holy See recognizing this church as the true church of China, this would mean that all Catholics in the world would be appalled and would be actually impacted.','other',2775.2,2807.8),(2120,99,5181,5183,'And so, for example, trade wars, sanctions, could actually hurt China. And even the people that are trading with Chinese companies, even the farmer in Brazil wouldn\'t sell the soybeans that they farm to Chinese companies because they don\'t want to reconciliate with a country that is hurting their own members of the same church. People have empathy towards people of the same faith, and this is a fact.','other',2808.1,2833.2),(2121,99,5184,5187,'And this is why we also know that backlash is highly unlikely. And so, in persecution, we see that we don\'t have this persecution. And second, we have transparency, as Maria explained. The Holy See\'s recognition is crucial for protection because they hold China accountable, and it protects much more than their side.','other',2833.2,2850),(2122,99,5188,5190,'And so, a little bit of weight here. So, with their best case scenario, we still have more religious freedom on our side, we still have less persecution on our side, and we win on the pragmatic, but we also win with the principle of Vatican fulfilling its duty, which they don\'t actually engage in. That\'s why I\'m so proud to propose this.','other',2850.3,2867.4),(2123,99,5191,5192,'I\'m proud to propose this. I\'m proud to propose this.','other',2867.8,3034.9),(2124,102,5317,5322,'I want to show you this top slide. The opposition ultimately dismisses our principle as always symbolic, but the agreement they hold so dearly is ultimately always symbolic. Because in 2022, the Chinese government unilaterally moved Bishop John Fenn to leave the CCC not to prove my point. This violated their view, because it was not proven by the political system. However, the Vatican really issued a no-discretion, no sanctions, no consequences, just silence. And that\'s what exposes the reality of an opposition that defies the unconventionalist ideal of China, that is the world today is about, that our principle is so important, and certainly unparalleled.','other',3841.6,3931.1),(2125,102,5323,5329,'First, why is our principle so important? Because this debate is not only about pragmatism. Because religion is not only about pragmatism. Because Christianity is not only about pragmatism. It\'s about moral legitimacy, spiritual truth, and institutional responsibility. Things that are only fulfilled, burdens that are only fulfilled by the Holy See and our civil health. People, vulnerable people, who are only protected on our side of the house.','other',3931.1,3951.2),(2126,102,5330,5333,'What are the principles we bring to that? One, that there\'s a moral duty for the faithful, for the ones that the Holy See owes a virtue of loyalty to those who have fought tirelessly to profess the true doctrine. Secondly, that there\'s an obligation to enable salvation, because the Holy See has only fought in the human record with the destruction of religious institutions and clergy, and thereby enabled the sacraments necessary for the eternal salvation that all Catholics fight for. And lastly, that there\'s also a historical reparation that is needed on an event that is now a very unconstitutional because it is technically relevant, it\'s demanding symbolic corruption, a measure that the opposition never does.','other',3951.2,3984.2),(2127,102,5334,5338,'Why is this so important? Because Catholicism is also highly symbolic. Because people have lost family members, because people have lost family members to the nation. They\'re willing to die for their faith. And this just shows how much their faith means value to them.','other',3984.2,3999.2),(2128,102,5339,5343,'Because the Holy See recognition shows support for everything they have ever fought for. Because they\'ve validated their efforts and told them that they\'re not a people. Because virtue of legitimacy is clergy, because clergy is not only just a thing from the Holy See, it means that someone can now be baptized in the Roman churches because they don\'t have access to an ordinary name birth, which means that the sacraments and rites hold no validity in their circumstance. Because Catholics depend on the Holy See to be able to act in a certain way, which is more powerful than the others. Because the only way Catholics can achieve true salvation is through the recognition.','other',3999.2,4030),(2129,102,5344,5347,'Because it\'s fundamentally wrong to actively recognize the symbolic empowerment of someone who tortured, who persecuted, and who killed those who are only trying to show what they have to portray their faith. We think the principles of clergy are two-dimensional. We think one is constitutionally correct and morally correct for the Holy See to do this. We think it is their duty to build this constantly fought, continuous and uncontinuous fight.','other',4030,4052.4),(2130,102,5348,5350,'And secondly, simple recognition is already enough for these people. They have never thought for their faith, their faith, which we value and we honor in our counseling. But secondly, on a paradigm, because they have this table that they do not.','other',4052.4,4064.8),(2131,102,5351,5356,'They tell us two things. One, backlash. And secondly, cooperation. Cooperation is very deeply mitigated, just by the example I tell you. The CCP will never cooperate with the Vatican. Because for an authoritarian regime, cooperation means surrender. Because an authoritarian regime always wants to be the ones with more power.','other',4064.8,4080.6),(2132,102,5357,5363,'Look at the example. They already betrayed these human wants. They already betrayed the truth in multiple times. This is one of the main, multiple examples. We think it is likely that this will happen again. We see no incentives for the CCP to combine the underground churches with their church. But they actively torture people from the underground church.','other',4080.6,4095.4),(2133,102,5364,5367,'And then on backlash, we\'re telling them that one, it\'s unlikely to happen because it increases domestic instability. Secondly, there is international backlash. And lastly, the persecution could never get so bad in our center of power. Or else the backlash will only increase.','other',4095.4,4107.9),(2134,102,5368,5376,'And we tell them, and they have this wish, that we\'re going to increase more credibility on their side. But notice how the Vatican on their side is not neutral. They are actively supporting the Christians. And this is only decreasing more credibility on the CCP. Because it\'s not like other authoritarian regimes. They can also persecute people who are very faithful. But on their side, the Vatican is not going to do anything. So, the backlash on their side is self-defeating. We tell them that visibility is crucial.','other',4107.9,4135.3),(2135,102,5377,5380,'We tell them that this alliance is unproper. That it empowers NGOs. That it forces international media to increase diplomatic access. We tell them that this is a layer of protection that\'s kind of holy.','other',4135.3,4144.6),(2136,102,5381,5381,'We tell them that they are only on their own side.','other',4144.6,4147.4),(2137,88,4278,4279,'I\'m going to hand over to our speaker for the speech and I will open the debate for the opposition. Hello, friends. Today I\'m going to be talking to a couple of people from Brussels, from their first speaker, and then going to our main point.','other',521.5,562.1),(2138,88,4280,4280,'First, I just wanted to say to our first speaker, when they talked about, when they gave us this narrative that NATO, that European countries should leave NATO because this just leads to Russia and Russian aggression, and that NATO, that they speak, that Russia is demonizing NATO and are just scared of NATO and that\'s why they\'re attacking it.','other',562.1,579.3),(2139,88,4281,4290,'But, panel, this is inherently wrong. We\'re telling you Russia didn\'t attack Ukraine because they were scared of NATO. Russia didn\'t attack Ukraine because they were scared of NATO. They just attacked Ukraine because they wanted a land in Ukraine. They didn\'t care, they weren\'t scared, they wanted a land. Because, in the end, Russia was so afraid of NATO and if they just attacked because they\'re scared, then they would just not attack the first place because of the fact that they were there. If they attacked Ukraine, for example, or if they attacked a NATO country, they know that NATO is going to respond. So, if they were so scared of NATO, they wouldn\'t attack them in the first place. They\'re always holding on to the fact that Russia, all they care about is getting more land. Putin attacked Ukraine because he wanted more land, not because he was scared of NATO.','other',579.5,618.4),(2140,88,4291,4291,'Secondly, they also tell us that the US won\'t be NATO until the very end.','other',618.9,626.1),(2141,88,4292,4302,'At the beginning, the US would probably still stay NATO. But this is also just inherently wrong. So, don\'t be told what the US actually wants from NATO. They don\'t care about the money. The US has so much money, they don\'t want a lot from NATO. What they care about is getting intelligence from NATO. What they care about is getting access to bases. And these are the sort of things that are going to go away at the very beginning when NATO splits up. Their money might still be there until the end, but that\'s not what the US cares about. So, the US is actually set up to leave quite early. And they\'ll stay there because what they actually care about will be gone from quite early.','other',626.6,651),(2142,88,4303,4311,'Now, going to some pieces of data from our side in order to make this matter. So, firstly, this looks like a complete removal of all European countries from NATO. This looks like there\'s stopping of sharing of intelligence between NATO countries. There\'s stopping of base sharing. All the countries, all the troops from different countries in different NATO countries will be pulled back to their original countries. This is going to be extremely expensive because logistically you\'re going to have to move away a lot of different people from different bases. All these people are going to have to get new jobs, they\'re going to have to train themselves in a new way because they\'re being moved. Logistically, also the process of separating all the systems, deciding who gets what when you\'re splitting up. It\'s going to take a lot of effort and money.','other',651.5,700.1),(2143,88,4312,4314,'Now, going to my first slide, I\'m going to talk about how this is actually better protection for all NATO countries. Both from the outside and also inside and minimizing conflicts inside NATO. Now, I\'m just talking about how this actually protects NATO countries from non-NATO countries.','other',700.6,712.4),(2144,88,4315,4318,'Firstly, when we look at NATO\'s collective forces, which are a much stronger force than each individual country, they present a much bigger force and it basically scares away the enemy and stops the attack. A lot of people do not want to attack NATO. The first thing to do is to know that if they attack NATO countries, all the other NATO countries are there. They have so many resources, they have so much intelligence that they will probably not stand a huge chance because all the other countries are forced to actually intervene and help the country or defend the country that has been attacked.','other',712.6,746.3),(2145,88,4319,4324,'The second thing you\'ll see is that if a country gets attacked, there\'s just a lot more intelligence. All the NATO countries, through their intelligence, they most probably can predict the attack much easier. They have so many more resources figuring out how the attacks are going to be. The element of surprise is just way less and they\'ll be able to deal with these attacks much easier. Because of all the intelligence, Trump will be able to pool all the different resources that NATO has. So yes, they\'ll be able to pool all these resources.','other',746.8,775.5),(2146,88,4325,4331,'Now why is it impacted? We see that the fact that if we catch 12 people from attacking both, if NATO catches 12 countries from attacking NATO countries in a second, if we catch 12 countries from attacking other countries, this of course stops wars from happening in the first place because if people won\'t even attack other countries, which of course is a big lie by the way. They could have attacked Ukraine decades ago. The reason why it happened now is because Ukraine tried to rejoin NATO. NATO is expanding and that scares Russia. Obviously it hurts Russia. But in the end, Putin still only attacked Ukraine because he was flat right.','other',775.5,811.3),(2147,88,4332,4340,'We see that Putin slowly attacked Ukraine. Putin slowly attacked Ukraine because he was flat right. He didn\'t attack them in the sense that he said, I just want to, because he\'s so scared of NATO that he has to attack them. No he didn\'t because he actually was flat right. And to a certain extent, he is scared. When Ukraine is part of NATO, he\'s so scared. And there\'s a reason why Putin actually attacked Ukraine and other European countries that are inside NATO. Because they\'re already there. At that point, Putin can tell you that he\'s actually scared to a certain extent because he\'s actually stopped him, as I said before, he\'s stopped him from attacking those countries because he knows all the NATO countries will have to come to their defense.','other',811.5,860.4),(2148,88,4341,4344,'So that actually makes no more point about the fact that Ukraine was part of NATO and they were already, but he wouldn\'t have attacked. But he wanted to land. That\'s why he attacked. He only was able to do that because they were part of NATO, which will defeat all other European countries if they split NATO up.','other',861,877.1),(2149,88,4345,4350,'Now, only kind of saying this after a conversation with this kind of situation within European countries. Firstly, when we see that most European countries are in NATO, we see that most of them therefore feel protected for each other. They know that their allies work with all of them and they feel protected with each other. They have quite low incentive to attack each other because they also depend on resources that they get from each other. They have more intelligence that they get from other countries when they depend on the resources. So there\'s no sense in attacking other countries.','other',877.5,908.3),(2150,88,4351,4359,'You\'ll see that any conflicts that arise within European countries can be much easier to govern because they\'re all part of NATO. There\'s so many more diplomatic processes that are in NATO. People that are appointed inside NATO to actually help mitigate the conflicts that do actually happen in NATO countries. Therefore, it leads to less military action, but there\'s at least some diplomatic action. We see this for example now with what Macron did. He basically showed Trump the middle finger by recognizing class-division of the country. This was so diplomatic action he was able to take. He just doesn\'t take military action, which is the thing we want to avoid because that\'s the thing that will cause fires. And apparently, to live in the most important thing, we should save as many lives as possible.','other',908.6,946.5),(2151,88,4360,4366,'So when countries in Europe are all part of NATO, they don\'t really have a reason to attack. If they were to split up, we can see for example that in India, a landlocked country inside of Europe would not have a naval base anymore. It would also not have resources that other countries have. And now they actually have a reason to attack other European countries. Now they have a reason to go and try to get their hands on the resources. Why they get those resources? Because they don\'t have this, because they don\'t have that protection, or they don\'t have to, they actually have a reason to do this, they have an incentive to do this, and this will just cause even more conflict, right?','other',950,982),(2152,88,4367,4369,'And why we don\'t want conflicts in South Korea, right? All that conflicts leads to, all that military conflict, sorry, we don\'t want military conflicts, all that military conflict leads to is a loss of lives, and it costs an extreme amount of money, right? Military, war is always expensive, war is always expensive, and we, we of course want to save lives, but we also want to make sure that we can save as much money by volunteering our military, that was very helpful, thank you.','other',982.2,1005.9),(2153,88,4370,4370,'Thank you very much.','other',1006.2,1006.5),(2154,90,4445,4447,'We may reject this as a question that\'s neither more than a speech, and that would appear to be a very good proposition to be put at the center of the debate. Firstly, I would broadly respond to their argument on Russian incentives and why they truly have to win. I would make it crystal clear whether their argumentation is deeply flawed.','other',1541,1603.2),(2155,90,4448,4450,'Firstly, the other response on why Ukraine was not attacked decades ago, and it was attacked in the second half of the century. Many decades ago, the USSR was still established. If you look at the number of countries, if you look at, because the USSR, let\'s say, ended in the, close to the late 20th century, there have not been many years where Russia has time to independently build a infrastructure, have capabilities to start a full assault.','other',1603.2,1628.2),(2156,90,4451,4452,'But then even if we say that, okay, they could have done it before, the critical argumentation is that, just because people were doing the right thing for the first 20 years does not mean that suddenly we can expect them to be, we cannot expect complete moral and parliamentary improvement later on. But we see that how it would be due to the annexation of Crimea and due to the fact that, due to the fact that of the limited, let\'s say, American or NATO response to the Russian aggression, we see why Ukraine were completely justified in seeking a patchwork to NATO because they recognized the defense mechanisms they have.','other',1628.2,1661),(2157,90,4453,4453,'And this is to say that if Russia were to attack, for example, all the other things, the annexation of the, Ukraine was already part of NATO in 2014, the annexation was led to direct military confrontation by the US and would have led to Russia not being able to have a full frontal scale assault to this extent in Ukraine.','other',1661,1677.6),(2158,90,4454,4455,'But even if we buy that short, on the big chart, even if we say that Russia only attacked Ukraine because they were afraid of it, and even if we say they were not significantly deterred by the, by currently, if you look at it, hundreds of thousands of Russians killed by American-supplied drones, even if they were deterred by that, we see that now it\'s very, very unlikely that any future conflicts will happen in the coming years because Russia is so drained by the current conflict in Ukraine that they\'re unable to start another conflict with, for example, all the other NATO countries because they don\'t, despite that they don\'t have the capacity and they don\'t have the incentive considering how badly this military adventure failed, right?','other',1677.6,1716.4),(2159,90,4456,4458,'Because they recognize, oh, we attack a NATO-adjacent country, which is a country wanting to go to NATO, and we got so screwed over that such a relatively small country who\'s not under nuclear power gets so much funding from NATO that we were unable, that thousands of people were dying, but we get sanctioned so much that countries are not buying our goods, our gas is not flowing to other countries, we have lost so much of our geopolitical dominance, you know what? NATO is still being led at that point.','other',1716.4,1742.1),(2160,90,4459,4461,'This is completely moving on by, so the best, very, very best case, this is equal. But then secondly, we see that because, because we see such a significant response on a non-NATO country, we argue the response will be significantly more if they attack a NATO member because of these treaties, because they have written legitimately that their support is expected, which means that, for example, another annexation, let\'s say they want to attack the Czech Republic, these things would have a much stronger response and then would deter Russian aggression, which is why NATO as a force itself is a force of deterrence and we see how that does happen.','other',1742.1,1776.6),(2161,90,4462,4463,'The same reason why Russia attacked Ukraine and not other NATO countries, right? Sure, part of it is geographical, but the reason they attacked Ukraine at this time is they can also be of urgency because they recognize, oh, if Ukraine is gonna join NATO, it means it\'s gonna be harder for us to annex the whole of Ukraine because they\'re gonna have more significant resources, this is why they\'re back now.','other',1776.6,1797.8),(2162,90,4464,4466,'But secondly, in terms of resources and looking at things, because they argue that NATO is uniquely military, because they are such a strong military-formed force, you argue that it is a significant group, which means that the European economic incentive, the European groups, is not a significant group. Also, the individual treaty with Germany and Italy and Germany and Czech Republic, because they\'re not a collective of information, because they don\'t have shared satellites, because they don\'t have a basis, as a collective on an individual basis, when you sum that up, it\'s not gonna have the power of a collective to work together, because it\'s a collective, it\'s a representation, not of the sum of individual forces, but because of the shared resources, because of, let\'s say, ideal purchase conditions for weapons, they\'re gonna be a more significant force directly.','other',1797.8,1837.6),(2163,90,4467,4467,'But then, note that because, and people know, I\'m gonna call this out, most of the analysis set up on setting up basically NATO, but just within Europe, they\'re like, oh well, they can set up new agreements, they can set up new military agreements, so any benefits you get from that are just gonna be marginalized on their side because they\'re not gonna have the framework in NATO, they don\'t have the experience,','other',1837.6,1855.4),(2164,90,4468,4469,'they need to set up all these new systems, they need to first set up new legislation, they need to hire more people, and then ask them, let\'s say the US, and now I wonder why the US is sending this voter to say, firstly because the US is consensual to say, first of all, it\'s very unlikely Trump will actually vote, Trump is a lot of all, no five, he said he would attack Greenland, he said he would make Canada a different state, he said he would make Mexico a different world, he\'s a very popular guy, he likes to abuse his white and populist voters, apparently he has no incentive to convince NATO to do this, because the intelligence users, because they want the soft control in Europe, because the US wants to have their influence in Europe,','other',1855.4,1892.1),(2165,90,4470,4470,'because they want to be able to access the bases, they want to access the hell out of Europe, and access the satellites, because of this quite simple geographical location, the US will continue to have that center, and after the worst case, in two years we\'re gonna get a new president anyway, so if you don\'t have a whole speculative case on that, the US maybe might leave NATO','other',1892.1,1909.5),(2166,90,4471,4471,'because they\'re not getting paid, you see, the US has always been the more prominent military spender, the US always stayed in, quite simply because of the intelligence and geographical size, but secondly in terms of Europe unprepared, you see how Europe has recently increased military spending because of you, you see how Europe has increased, you have even Ryan McCaul\'s talk about how military spending has happened, which means they\'re gonna be relatively more prepared,','other',1909.5,1932.6),(2167,90,4472,4472,'but then in the case that the US does leave, that doesn\'t reflect as a European country, without the US, then NATO will still be better, then they\'re from prior parties, they\'re setting a completely new European-ish NATO, which is completely speculative, they\'re willing to say how this will look like, what is this, but it will still be better because the frameworks exist, the agreements exist, so basically NATO without the US is still gonna be better than basically NATO just for Europe without the added military presence in the sentence.','other',1932.6,1956.3),(2168,90,4473,4473,'So they\'re all benefits, they\'re completely marginalized.','other',1956.3,1958.8),(2169,90,4474,4478,'But then thirdly, on their case about the opportunity cost in China, because as the motion suggests, this set up an establishing independent military presence, that means if you set the establishment, rightfully means increased spending because they don\'t have the access to, let\'s say, the NATO\'s internet because they don\'t have access to the infrastructure. So initially, we say, okay, this is actually a meeting, but we\'re not our side. Because first, it\'s expensive. Because first, it\'s a transition, right? Because you have to hire new people because you don\'t have access to shared infrastructure to say anything.','other',1958.8,1986.5),(2170,90,4479,4484,'Secondly, because secondly, this is about, right? Because first, now it says it has no share, that means you need, let\'s say, more spies, more hackers, and now this is looking more R&D for like ships, more R&D for missiles because let\'s say, because there\'s not a NATO, you have to spend more because you don\'t have that trade agreement with military because now people don\'t want to send weapons because now you\'ve got a structuring ally, right? And also because now people are more afraid of war because they\'re individual entities. They want to spend more money to do this. They don\'t have the huge amounts of military reports coming in to swoop them in case of an invasion. This is about the increased military spending on R&D.','other',1986.5,2019.2),(2171,90,4485,4491,'So why is this bad for something? We\'ve got all the arguments, right? But also, come to the point, all the arguments, that means they cannot spend on education, they cannot spend on this, and specifically, we don\'t argue that, and you know, the European structures are strained because we see how populist parties like the AFD are able to exploit people\'s borders, people\'s inaccessible education and this, but also have political arguments. Why this is bad is because you have this war base because they\'re gonna increase the local narrative and try to get more constructs. That looks like propaganda, saying come support our country, military is good, we want more things. That means that people signing up to work for right-wingers, because they\'re being pro-propaganda, they\'re not doing the local positions. It means that we\'re in these unethical positions, and this is, we believe, is principally wrong to support a narrative to push weapons.','other',2019.2,2066.7),(2172,90,4492,4493,'But also, specifically, generally, we see this can lead to the rise of nationalism within countries because the methods in which these countries are gonna propagate the military against is gonna be through language, support your country, we are the Swiss people, join our military, we want to be strong, case the Russians, so ask the House, we have no doubt, we have increased spending, like they said, on the wrong things. More nationalism and more threats to European countries.','other',2066.7,2091),(2173,90,4494,4494,'Thank you, never been prouder to propose.','other',2091,2094.5),(2174,92,4584,4587,'In the NATO, the countries there, they have internal accountability. That is why, say, you are taking account of what\'s with each other. That is to mean that if there\'s a war happening, all the people who are trying to help each other out, all the vulnerable will be taking account. They won\'t be attacked as an independent country.','other',2686.8,2935.9),(2175,92,4588,4597,'Yes, sure, maybe some of the strong economic sense or military sense of the country, in the EU, they just go out from the NATO, they might have the abilities to sustain themselves. But there\'s nothing. There\'s still some of the small countries. They just simply don\'t have the capacity. For example, they don\'t have enough population of the troops. They don\'t have enough money. They have their own economic problem. They have too many deficits. They need too many. They just don\'t have money to allocate to the military.','other',2936.2,2967.6),(2176,92,4598,4601,'If you say, okay, some countries, they don\'t need so much money like Spain. The thing is, they actually need money too. Why? Because they need the quality of the military force. Or else they will just expose themselves, expose their vulnerability towards the white crowd.','other',2967.6,2982.8),(2177,92,4602,4602,'Now, before moving on to the deterrent, I will tell you a lie.','other',2982.8,2987.5),(2178,92,4603,4606,'You say NATO is so much better than the EU. What\'s the difference between NATO and the EU Mutual Defense Treaty? Is it US? I will talk about what\'s the difference between EU and US, addressing my third point, which is the intelligence element of the military.','other',2987.5,3002.9),(2179,92,4607,4607,'I\'ll address your point later.','other',3003,3004),(2180,92,4608,4617,'But before that, I will talk about the deterrent\'s point. The deterrent\'s point, the only response we hear from them is that this is symmetrical. But they didn\'t provide with any clear mechanism of this. In our second part, the deterrent is plausible. It\'s so feasible because we tell that people in collaborative effort, we have this kind of internal agreement. That is to say that when you have already a collaboration between each other, among the group, you won\'t just have started a war. Because you are in a mutual accountability. So that means that our extent is bigger. Our extent is bigger. And that\'s a good thing.','other',3004,3045.4),(2181,92,4618,4622,'And also without, for example, in NATO, against all types of war, like Russia, the only kind of country they mentioned as a big power, for example. They\'re also a big part of the US, there\'s China, like this kind of country. They won\'t just attack NATO because there\'s so many countries within NATO. They have military force. So in terms of the extent, we\'re still winning.','other',3045.4,3067.6),(2182,92,4623,4629,'Okay, moving on to intelligence over military sharing. Their point is EU also have it. What they tell us in the framing is that EU is only about economic focus, mainly. And it\'s in a passive. That means that, actually it contributes to themselves, is that they have less capacity because they don\'t even address like a very direct military forces. Okay, even though there\'s some kind of military association with it, but it\'s not as big as NATO. NATO is so much bigger and with the extent, we create something of bigger stability.','other',3067.6,3101.5),(2183,92,4630,4638,'And we tell you that actually during this transition period, we need a lot of economics. We have a lot of expenses. These expenses include, okay, you have to hire people, you have to establish places. You have to maybe, like if your country don\'t even have like a military-based system, you have to hire this tons of troops and people. And maybe like it will like to turn to their normal life. And then maybe like a family, they\'re living on their own business. They have to force to go to military because their country need more military. So their whole life will be like destroyed eventually. That\'s like a very big impact.','other',3101.5,3145.1),(2184,92,4639,4646,'But before that, I will move on to like very minor things. What\'s more in reality? Okay, we do have some yellow pieces. And the only reason also we hear is that, okay, most countries, they don\'t need to spend a lot of money. But this is just false assumptions. Because actually a lot of countries, they need it. They\'re just, they just want it and need it. Because for example, they can be like, there\'s like, wait.','other',3145.1,3168.3),(2185,92,4647,4656,'For example, and also they tell us, okay, why they don\'t use so much money? Because geographically, they are far from Russia. But the thing is, it\'s not, this response is not very complete. Because first thing, that does not mean they have no threat. It can also be threat geographically. For example, okay, because they\'re individually, the German probably will attack like Spain because they have some kind of big cooling drug. And also Russia might attack by like, by some drones. Or they will have some secret initiation because they\'re like independent. So they will still have so many threats. And anywhere else.','other',3168.3,3204.9),(2186,92,4657,4658,'Before I move to our piece of evidence, I\'ll take it. So if you can see that deterrence is effective and couldn\'t pierce NATO, NATO expanding on your side becomes a reason why everyone\'s on the attack now.','poi',3204.9,3215.5),(2187,92,4659,4659,'Sorry, I didn\'t hear you, please don\'t talk.','other',3215.5,3217.6),(2188,92,4660,4660,'Yeah, so NATO expanding on your side of the house, if you say deterrence is effective and they couldn\'t pierce NATO, that becomes a reason why Putin wants to attack countries right now.','poi',3217.9,3226.6),(2189,92,4661,4669,'Why does Putin want to attack NATO? The thing is, okay, the reason why NATO created is because they want to like stable and military forces. And okay, the reason why NATO might expand is because they want to protect country. They don\'t want to just create like a meeting between each other. Maybe Putin have some problem. But if NATO provide like a stable ground, then the country will be protected. And because we\'re safe, like we think like some political negotiation is so much like less impact that we actually save lives and we provide sustainable trope things. And also individual countries, they need to spend a lot more money due to like independence, due to exposed to the weaknesses on geographically and politically. And also I want to point out that because they lack addressing our impact on propaganda and nationalism, and for all the reasons.','other',3226.6,3281.2),(2190,93,4670,4675,'.. Honestly, I realized something, particularly because of how much we agree. Firstly, we agree that Putin was an irrational actor. But when we presented it as justification for them launching the assault on a different country, that was out of the way. Say no, Putin was only considered by NATO. Later they brought up, no, he\'s a rational deal at other countries regardless of their funding. Slightly contradictory, but we do want to emphasize that Putin does not have a plan.','other',3281.2,3348),(2191,93,4676,4680,'They do not have the capacity. They are underfunded. They don\'t, they\'re focused heavily on the Ukraine war because Ukraine has been so heavily funded by NATO, which is why we think that any argument on their case, which they argue that, okay, Putin\'s gonna start spontaneously starting other wars, like just the other wars, which he had perhaps more capacity to do so for the past few years, when he had more money, means less spending in Ukraine. Ours would have spontaneously happened. US would not like any of that because quite simply, because he can\'t afford it, because too much time is spent on the country that is being supported by the human force that is NATO.','other',3348,3378.4),(2192,93,4681,4683,'But secondly, in terms of NATO, they would agree on spending. But they initially framed that NATO is unique quite simply because it is a military president, and because it\'s military, unlike the EU, you\'ve read something new, which means that it has its unique military benefits. As it turns, that we both agree that the European Council does not act to the point where they go later to say, oh, they can set up new agreements, but they don\'t specify why this would be superior to the only established NATO, which is why we shouldn\'t buy any of that.','other',3378.4,3403.6),(2193,93,4684,4685,'But it\'s specifically because NATO has these military capabilities, because these are such a powerful force, because NATO is US plus Europe with all these other countries, it is quite right as an opposite of these things. It is very easy for Russia to calculate that yes, they will lose this war because this is such a huge force.','other',3403.6,3418.6),(2194,93,4686,4689,'But then specifically, but then moving on to why this actually does occur, first analyzing now for exchange, the Ukrainian incentive, the reason Ukraine wanted to do that as part of our third speaker was that because NATO is its protection force, because they can use, because this is a gateway to avoid said Russian aggression. As you analyze multiple times throughout our speech, that NATO is a more powerful force because Russia did suffer due to NATO aggression, because Russia is struggling because of NATO interference. They quite frankly don\'t have additional incentive or capacity to do so, whilst they have been able to truly recognize why this would happen. Much of their argument is back to say, oh yes, Putin is Russian, once they can be casual as a nation.','other',3418.6,3459.7),(2195,93,4690,4690,'But secondly, on the case they actually brought was on America giving NATO, they said that, okay, you know what, America might give NATO and this is bad, but they did not say why the framework of why just the Europeans and NATO is gonna be comparatively worse than the European agreement.','other',3459.8,3475.2),(2196,93,4691,4691,'They already gave you a reason why the NATO framework, because it is uniquely military, even with just Europeans, is gonna be superior to the European things that they develop, which means that the benefits on our side of the house, if you have Europe, Europe only NATO, it being completely charitable, I\'m not gonna go through why America might still stay, but why in Europe, this framework that is already established,','other',3475.2,3495.4),(2197,93,4692,4692,'completely outclasses their argument of they might set up a new system which might see the military, which on that side, it just basically makes a new NATO, so minus for a few, where there\'s a chance the US just says, let me have the benefit.','other',3495.4,3508.2),(2198,93,4693,4697,'And then thirdly, on the case that does have, you know, this opportunity cost. Now I\'m gonna do something, now I don\'t have the, we gave you tangible reasons why the country would need to spend more. First, initially, because we need to separate the systems, they can set their new move on the basis of the initial, which is to stay very clear, completely outclassed. But then secondly, because the NATO is a more powerful military force than the European ones, they can adopt it into their infrastructure. They can use their satellites, they can use their naval base, everything, set up things.','other',3508.2,3532.3),(2199,93,4698,4700,'We need to plan more ways for them to spend more, rather than some countries going to spend more, they\'re not justified. Unfortunately, for our countries, do want to spend militaries, anyways, they haven\'t since we saw, but we gave you time to reason. So in terms of mechanization for me, thus we win.','other',3532.3,3544.8),(2200,93,4701,4703,'But now, interestingly, I\'m gonna weigh against my own case. Here we could be completely charitable, our case on deterrence, because war is such a severe impact, because war, could literally lead to the death of the Indians. Because it was Ukraine that was in fact, and not Sweden, not the countries that are established, you know, weighing my case against my case, I want to argue that, if we learn, weigh exclusively on deterrence of war, we should still vote for us, because this is a very significant thing now.','other',3544.8,3570.1),(2201,93,4704,4705,'Without war, we could simply have more economic benefit, but more people dying, not less geopolitical tensions, etc. Well, in this level of power what we get is maybe less war, because food didn\'t arrive. Thank you.','other',3570.2,3581.6),(2202,87,4204,4205,'NATO is an organisation that was established to defend against Russia, but it is precisely because of this that Russia is so threatened in the status quo and is necessitated to act out in aggressive ways. We aim to restore true peace on cyber-opposition today, so proud to propose.','other',4.2,22.1),(2203,87,4206,4207,'I have two arguments to make from cyber-opposition today. Firstly, on why we get less Russian aggression, and secondly, on why US reliance is bad.','other',22.6,30.2),(2204,87,4208,4212,'But before that, I have three pieces on framing. Firstly, what does gradually withdrawing from NATO look like? We would obviously do this in a fairly smart manner. Firstly, we think that we\'d set up necessary alliances before leaving. But secondly, we think that the primary funded nations of NATO will likely be the last to leave NATO so that it remains functional in some sort of manner before eventually closing.','other',30.2,54.4),(2205,87,4213,4214,'A second piece of framing. We think that we still have the EU mutual defence treaty under our side of the house.','other',54.4,61.7),(2206,87,4215,4218,'So how is this different from NATO, which opposition side we use to defend? We think that it is different in mainly three ways. Firstly, we think the structural purpose is basically different. We think that NATO is basically a military agreement, and on the other hand, we think that the EU is a purely economic agreement, which means that there is less active military and it is only relevant for self-defence mechanisms.','other',62,93.6),(2207,87,4219,4222,'But secondly, we think that the degree of interaction or the degree of military intervention from NATO and EU is basically different. We think that NATO has active military intervention, whereas EU is mainly incredibly passive. But thirdly, we think that the frequency of military operation is also different. We think that for NATO, there is a daily operation and daily training that happens on the ground, whereas for EU, there is basically none unless conflict is actively instigated.','other',94.1,123.8),(2208,87,4223,4225,'But third piece of framing then. We think that these countries that fall out of NATO will still be able to defend themselves in the long term. And this is the implication of all of this framing.','other',123.8,133.1),(2209,87,4226,4227,'A, because of the EU mutual defence treaty, but B, because of the individual agreements that are formed between European countries. We think that countries like Germany still have a huge incentive to defend their borders against any aggression from Russia, which is why they likely have tried to set up buffer zones in different countries and therefore militarise other countries and stuff like that.','other',133.5,153.1),(2210,87,4228,4232,'But lastly, we think that they also have the capacity to defend against other countries right now. We think that in the status quo, the reason why European nations are not spending enough money on the military is because there is a collective action problem. Because they think that the US, through NATO, will defend them. Because they know that the nature of NATO is very interventionist. We think that these funds that are not being used on their military in the status quo will be unlocked once they actually go outside of NATO and join the EU, in which they can actually now spend their money on their military.','other',153.1,183.2),(2211,87,4233,4234,'I\'ll then go into my first argument on why we get less Russian aggression. The thesis of this argument is to state that NATO is incredibly, incredibly threatening, which is why in the status quo continues, we think that Russia will be even more and more aggressive.','other',184.2,197.9),(2212,87,4235,4240,'We think that this is basically three reasons as to why NATO instigates so much aggression in Russia. A. Because of the offensive nature of NATO. Obviously, as I told you in training, they do things like active military training. It says that it\'s in the defence pact, but it really isn\'t when you see all of its PUS operations in Africa, all of its PUS operations in Yugoslavia. So why does this instigate a Russian conflict then? We think that Russia will believe that they will be attacked anyway because NATO is such an active military threat, which basically means that if they\'re going to attack them anyway, might as well attack them first so that you can have an upper hand in the conflict and actually be the one negotiating where the conflict takes place and stuff like that.','other',197.9,242.2),(2213,87,4241,4243,'In the comparative, we think that the EU is a purely economic alliance. So even if countries do go into the EU defence pact, we think that Russia, firstly, will think that there\'s no reason to attack them to begin with because they won\'t be attacked unless you attack them first. But secondly, the fact that these countries made an active transition and made an active decision to actually transition to a less aggressive treaty means that Russia will view these countries much more favourably and then instigate much less conflict.','other',242.5,272.4),(2214,87,4244,4250,'But B, we think that another reason why NATO is so threatening is because of ideological tensions that it has with Russia. NATO was literally instigated and established to be anti-Russia. We think that Putin has demonised NATO to a large extent over the course of history. And from the eyes of Putin, NATO is imperialistic, it is expansionist, it is against everything that Russia stands for. We think that it is because of these reasons that if NATO does attack or if NATO continues to exist, Russia is necessitated to be more violent against them. Because if it backs down against NATO, there will be basically an ideological loss and stuff like that. They need to maintain their legitimacy, which is why they will be more aggressive against NATO.','other',272.4,321.5),(2215,87,4251,4255,'In the comparative, we think that the EU is much more of an economic alliance as we told you. It is never anti-Russia. We think that Russians have never demonised the EU before, historically speaking, which is why they are much less likely to be aggressive and stuff like that. We think the impact of this one is simple. We think that Russia will be much more willing to negotiate with the EU when the EU does something a little bit aggressive because they do not think it is an ideological loss to negotiate with them.','other',321.9,351.6),(2216,87,4256,4261,'We think that the third mechanism under this is that there is a perception of power imbalance. And that is to say, we think that NATO is expanding literally by the minute, with Ukraine wanting to join NATO and Georgia trying to join NATO. So if Russia were to attack NATO, it is only now in which they would be able to gain a strategic advantage over NATO, because this is when NATO is the weakest. In the comparative, we think that the EU, the last country that joined the EU was literally in 2004, which was Croatia. And we do not think that the EU will expand a lot in the future, which is why Russia will not be as panicked as to aggressively attack the EU as it will be to Russia. All of these prove that Russia will be much more aggressive towards NATO and why we were able to defend that on our side of the house.','other',351.6,398.6),(2217,87,4262,4263,'Our second argument is on why we think that the US reliance on these countries\' debt to NATO is very, very bad. So firstly, we think that the US is very, very likely to leave NATO or defund NATO in the very near future.','other',398.6,413.2),(2218,87,4264,4268,'And this is because there is a growing anti-NATO narrative within the United States, because it literally says the United States no geopolitical funds and funds so much into NATO but is literally getting nothing back in return. And that is the reason why they are likely to leave. We think that in the comparative, the US won\'t immediately leave on our side of the house when countries start leaving NATO. And this is because the US doesn\'t have to spend as much resources on NATO anymore when more countries are leaving, right? So the implication of this is that in the process of countries gradually leaving NATO, we don\'t think that the US will get up and leave and will be able to maintain some scope of geopolitical stability in the short term.','other',413.5,453.4),(2219,87,4269,4274,'Why is the US ultimately leaving in the long term very, very bad? We think that this is bad because countries will be able to defend themselves immediately. And it is impossible for these countries to be able to develop that with individual militaries in time for two reasons. A, because it is unpredictable when the US is likely to leave. We think that the policies of Trump are very, very volatile. We think that the really tight electoral nature of the US means that you never know when which political parties are going to be voted in, which is why it is impossible to preemptively develop your military in time.','other',453.4,490.2),(2220,87,4275,4276,'But B, it also just takes a lot of time to develop your own military. All of this to prove that when countries remain in NATO, they face really short term risks that occur of the US leaving.','other',490.5,501.5),(2221,87,4277,4277,'For these reasons, sir, I\'m proud to stand on this side of the opposition today. Thank you.','other',501.8,505),(2222,89,4372,4376,'The question we asked from first is fairly simple, why is the U.S. still in NATO, and why were they, why did they originally join NATO? Well the original reason was fairly obvious, it was expansion of soft power in Europe, well our second argument for you, that the incentive for the U.S. to remain in NATO It was decreasing day by day. The more and more, for example, the US creates treaties with the countries in Europe to get intel, which is their mechanism for US staying in NATO, and the more and more, US becoming less reliant on Europe for their defense, we would argue that the US is likely to lead.','other',1085.5,1118.8),(2223,89,4377,4379,'And in such a context that we live in, the deterrence factor that opposition would argue, not only need to resist, and our second argument directly has their line of analysis in the first argument, but furthermore, that there\'s an instigating harm in the short term of extreme weakness in European nations at the point in which the US leaves before the countries are able to expand their militaries. Crucially, our second argument was then fairly simple. Rather than allowing for Trump to dictate when NATO falls, we should be the one to dictate it, because we can then structure our militaries in a better way.','other',1119.1,1154.1),(2224,89,4380,4383,'But before I get to that, I wanna first deal with the best case case-care to wonder prediction from their report. This is a situation in which the US is still in NATO, and the US still funds NATO to some extent, which side then still better protects countries. Our claim from first was this, that the NATO and its structure and its family, in every way of all, it directly aggresses Russia. Crucially, even if they\'re able to prove mechanisms like deterrence and intelligence, as long as we\'re able to synthesize these to some extent, the active aggression we prove in this argument is the tipping point for action in the period.','other',1154.6,1188.3),(2225,89,4384,4386,'Your response here is simple. They argue that Russia doesn\'t attack because of fear, it is simply about land and expansion of their horizons. The problem is, as my POI pointed out, then this would have happened decades ago, and would have happened to a more significant extent.','other',1188.8,1206.1),(2226,89,4387,4392,'The reason why Ukraine was attacked is because they attempted to join NATO. The reason why these countries are under attack right now, and the reason why countries around, for example, Ukraine, and most U.S.S. nations have to be scared is because they\'re leaning towards the West. Crucially, obviously, Russia isn\'t showing on land. The land in Ukraine is not that unique to Russia, considering how massive geographically Russia is. Obviously, it\'s not about the land.','other',1206.5,1232.1),(2227,89,4393,4393,'But secondly, even if this response were to stand, it doesn\'t engage to our analysis, because even if this were true, and that Ukraine was attacked because of land, our mechanisms prove that this type of attack is still likely, regardless of what uniquely happened in Ukraine. Go ahead. Yes?','other',1232.5,1248.9),(2228,89,4394,4396,'You said U.S.S. nations are not as scared as they\'re leaning towards the West. So are you saying that we\'re bottom of the world, but Ukraine is not a leader team to have connection with the West?','poi',1249.2,1257.7),(2229,89,4397,4401,'No, no, no, no. They can be left leaning, but the way they should, they can be Western. The way they should be Western is not to aggress Russia by attempting to join NATO. We could argue that NATO as a function is wrong. If you want it to be Western, you can do it, but the way you ought to do it is don\'t aggress Russia by doing it through NATO.','other',1258.5,1275),(2230,89,4402,4407,'Let\'s respond to their analysis. Their analysis proves two things. First, on the turrets, and second, on intelligence sharing, both of which argue that they protect countries better. Crucially, at the top, I would note that our line of analysis actually supersedes the vast majority of their analysis, because all of their analysis are only about current NATO nations. Our analysis also subsequently proves possible NATO nations are likely to get attacked to preemptively prevent these countries from being able to join NATO as happened in Ukraine. So even if they\'re able to be both deterrents and intelligence, non-NATO nations, who are prospective NATO nations, still face harm.','other',1275.6,1311),(2231,89,4408,4411,'Let\'s actually engage them. Against deterrents, two main levels of response. One, our argument that deterrence is, to some extent, at the very least symmetric in both worlds, because deterrence is not necessarily about, because deterrence need not be extreme advantage against Russia, it just needs to be sufficient, and we would argue that EU mutual defense treaty and defense treaties that countries have with one another is to some extent symmetric. Crucially, as long as we prove symmetric, we can take this deterrence class, because then, it all uniquely proves a tipping point for deterrence no longer being a factor because of things like aggression.','other',1311.2,1344),(2232,89,4412,4413,'In response to intelligence, then, the argument largely argues about how you can preemptively nullify attacks. Obviously, this can symmetrically be done in the EU, where countries can still share information with each other, and we would argue that other countries are still likely, for example, to create treaties around this, because they have, obviously, the incentive to nullify attacks.','other',1344.5,1360.9),(2233,89,4414,4420,'Furthermore, I would secondly argue that you oftentimes just don\'t know what these attacks are. Nobody predicted that Ukraine would attack. If they were to claim that intelligence knew of it, they\'re wrong. Third, yeah, sure, actually go ahead. No, it\'s not exactly true, but it was because Ukraine\'s lawful and legal, therefore, they were able to create this attack, and therefore, whistleblowers were able to do it. Yeah, but no other country knew about it, either. Like, no European nation and no current NATO nation knew about it, either.','other',1361.2,1387.2),(2234,89,4421,4423,'Okay, this is assuming that the best case for the US remains. Our second argument proved that the US is likely to leave, and their response actually doesn\'t deal with this. Crucially, this argument is subsequent to their analysis, not just because it\'s the more realistic situation, but also on the scale of harm.','other',1387.2,1402.4),(2235,89,4424,4424,'Final thing in my speech, then, third argument.','other',1402.8,1404.8),(2236,89,4425,4432,'We would argue that, at worst, this war thing is just, in general, the unlikely impact on this debate. We don\'t think this is a very likely thing, on that, either meant. We would argue that the NATO as a structure that forces nations to spend vast amounts of their GDP on the military itself is a negative harm, and an important one. Third argument, on forced GDP spending being bad. Firstly, what is the problem right now? The problem right now is that NATO, in its constitution, fundamentally demands all nations to spend what was originally a few percent, which is now 5% due to Trump terrorists, of their GDP on the military. Crucially, the reason why this is really bad is because it takes a lot of money away from other things that are more important to these countries. The important analysis here is this.','other',1405.1,1449.8),(2237,89,4433,4437,'A lot of countries don\'t need 5% GDP spending for their military to function in the way of deterrence and action. Japan doesn\'t spend 5%, and neither do a majority of these European nations who currently are in NATO. For example, if you look at, for example, Italy, or Greece, or Portugal, or Spain, who are just so geographically distant from Russia that invasions against them are highly unlikely, 5% GDP spending, which is a massive portion of their income, billions of dollars, is not important. The impact is fairly simple. These tax dollars can be used not just to reduce tax rates to allow for more economic circulation to occur, but also, for example, reinvestment into more important things for these countries, like education in Italy, or welfare, or research and manufacturing sector in Portugal, to allow for economic development to happen and for the quality of life in these countries to improve.','other',1449.8,1496.2),(2238,89,4438,4440,'This argument alone shouldn\'t win us this debate, because this is the most certain impact on this debate. 5% is being spent, and 5% will be spent, and this alone is a harm year on year. Even if you would argue that war is a speculative harm in this debate, this is the certain harm in this debate, and one that we should care about.','other',1496.2,1514.3),(2239,89,4441,4444,'Crucially, then, while all of this analysis proves not only do we win on the likelihood of war occurring, but also we would argue that we have largely synthesized all of our mechanisms, and we prove a certain harm in this debate. Final thing in my speech, then. At worst, even if we don\'t prove a certain harm, we would argue that, at worst, we would argue that all of the talking about deterrence and intelligence are just as symmetric as if we already proved that. For these reasons, propose.','other',1514.3,1541),(2240,91,4495,4499,'Speaking for the next speech, and now for my other paper, I\'m going to read my next paper. Applying a paper at this point, the outcomes are the next. At the club, I\'ll just knock their second argument out and talk about three themes. First, the notion of oppression. Second, flexibility, and lastly, the use of violence.','other',2094.5,2202.3),(2241,91,4500,4501,'First things first, let\'s talk about their second argument. Their argument that came from Elwood was to say that in instances where we have NATO, where you uniquely achieve the collectivization and nationalism that exists within Europe, in the counterfactual world of our world, somehow European countries will start fighting against each other.','other',2202.3,2219.2),(2242,91,4502,4508,'First, I think it\'s worth pointing out the symmetric incentive that exists in both sides of the House. He wants to have the unity to begin with, because all of their analysis was contingent on NATO being the only unifying force. One, EU exists. Two, your symmetric incentive to have peace on both sides of the House. One, because I think wars often have resource-intensive, you obviously don\'t want to send soldiers on the ground. Two, because I think it is politically costly to act if you don\'t initiate the conflict to begin with. There are people like opposition parties holding you accountable, you need two-thirds majority from the Congress in order to initiate the conflict in most countries, which is why their argument is to slow down.','other',2219.2,2255.2),(2243,91,4509,4512,'First question on Russian oppression. Opposition says that NATO creates deterrence. Proposition says that it escalates Russia. How do we break the deadlock?','other',2255.2,2263.8),(2244,91,4513,4517,'One, we flip their deterrence mechanism against their world, because they mischaracterize the status quo that opposition needs to defend in their side of the House. Because our third argument was about that NATO keeps expanding the status quo on their side of the House. Because if you look at the real world, look at Sweden, which has joined NATO in 2023. Look at Ukraine, which is trying to join NATO, and many other countries who are currently fearing of the Russian oppression that exists in their world. But thank you.','other',2263.8,2290.3),(2245,91,4518,4521,'And they say Russia fears NATO. If that was the case, then that is analysis as to why Russia fears NATO in the future, and presumably their incentive analysis that they have posted about how Russia wants land, about how Russia has an ideological incentive to want to invade, which is true, might be true. But like they act against their side of the House, which is the reason why they likely attack on like, you know, if deterrence exists. But the second thing I want to point out on the level of deadlock breaker is the existence of alternatives.','other',2290.3,2319.1),(2246,91,4522,4527,'Because I want to look at the incentives debate. I would argue that Russia cares about its own dignity and its own defense. So absent NATO, they have the incentive to negotiate with the EU, which are, say, the House. Why is this likely? The reason for that is that war is costly, and Russia themselves are currently getting harmed by this economic sanction that is stemming from the Russian-Ukraine war that\'s happening underground. Many of the economists say that Russian economy is oftentimes going to degrade in the future, and yada, yada, yada.','other',2319.1,2347.9),(2247,91,4528,4533,'Why is it that on their side of the House, the incentive to attack overwhelmingly outweighs the incentive not to do so that exists in their world? The reason for that is, could be statistical, is a strong banner here where his legitimacy is built upon the image of a stronger Russia that exists in their world. But here\'s where the lack of analysis and response that came from their second speaker is particularly harmful for their case. Because what we outlined to you from first speaker is that currently, Putin has literally demonized NATO that exists in their side of the House, which is the reason why when you have the EU, when you have the lack of NATO, when the country is gradually up out of NATO, and when it is no longer a thing, that\'s when Putin can resort to more peaceful things that are more easier, and that is something that is much less costly to our side of the House. Why do you think that the incentive to invade, if it exists, was not an inherent one, it is something that Putin needs to resort to in instances where NATO is actively threatening to Russia, which is a nuanced case that effectively raises the gun.','other',2347.9,2412),(2248,91,4534,4537,'Opposition says, oh, if you cannot win, you will attack. One, this is predicated upon the assumption that you can perfectly calculate whether you are a winner or not, you can win, right? But obviously, from the lens of the many of you, even war experts, just cannot win, and you just cannot calculate which side will win. Like, initially, Russia would, it was said that the EU, Russia would surrender in like one year, but now, there\'s like three years, and the war is just dragging on, which is the reason why it\'s just much more speculative.','other',2412,2441.9),(2249,91,4538,4540,'But secondly, I wanna note the irrationality of Putin to begin with. The reason why Putin is an irrational actor is because there\'s oftentimes, this is a big theater that is surrounded by guest pen, which is why oftentimes, through decision-making calculus, his favorite force within the existing terror world. Before I move on to the second part of the lecture, I\'d like to meet you either in the Ukraine war to end or go to Paris for the other lecture, and then, remain in capacity to address other nations or to go into the fact that I\'ve spent so much in the Ukraine war.','other',2441.9,2469.7),(2250,91,4541,4543,'Look how vulnerable, and just look how vulnerable the Ukraine is, right? We don\'t have to prove that the Ukraine war will end. What we need to prove is that the conflict will get actively worse in the terror world, and even without the capacity, I just proved to you the irrationality and the incentive that Russia to initiate the conflict if it is a threat exists.','other',2469.7,2488.2),(2251,91,4544,4546,'Second question on flexibility. Our political system is small, but many of the countries have other priorities. This NATO, of course, is even the smallest country to spend your money on your military.','other',2488.2,2498.8),(2252,91,4547,4549,'The first response we provide to you is that establishing on your own military in the counter-factual is expensive. One, this is symmetric, because NATO already requires countries to invest 5% of their money in military. On the other hand, Japan even only spends 3% of military, of their money, into your military, which illustrates the problem.','other',2498.8,2517.6),(2253,91,4550,4552,'But secondly, I would also note that most countries just don\'t need that much amount of military spending to begin with. Look at Spain, look at Italy, look at many other countries that are being relatively impoverished with Europe, but are geographically far away from Russia. We are talking about these countries and the ways in which they can spend money in other places that they actually need.','other',2517.7,2537.1),(2254,91,4553,4558,'Third of all, if you wanted to talk about the smaller countries that are geographically closer to Russia, I would argue that countries that are rich, like Germany, still have an incentive to engage in a passive treaty that is defense mutual treaty. And the reason for that is that they want these countries to stay on your side and not fall on the side of Russia, because they want a cushion between those two countries. The reason why this is not a pension is because this is a much more passive thing. I.e. , we\'ve pointed out in your framing that I think this is not much more passive, and you don\'t engage in things like defense, you know, like a mutual defense act. Like all the time, you don\'t have things like, you know, like military operation that is happening on our side of the house. That is the difference.','other',2537.1,2577.4),(2255,91,4559,4564,'On Wayne, three things. One, uncertainty. Our second speaker already talked about how this is the most certain thing happening today. Even their side of the house have argued that their work is speculative. But secondly, it is the largest part or many of these nations that just won\'t get the cap on either side of the house. It is a big deal for you to be able to invest 1% of your GDP into your education, into your jobs, into the manufacturing sector that you actually need to. But third of all, in terms of Delta, I think it\'s created some largest Delta in the nation.','other',2577.4,2605.4),(2256,91,4565,4567,'Last thing, I want to talk about the US alliance. It was based in the US whole state. But if you look at their analysis, it just doesn\'t make sense.','other',2605.4,2612.4),(2257,91,4568,4571,'The person that served us the most just only applies to Trump. One, I would argue that this wasn\'t just about Trump. It was an increased sentiment that currently exists within the US population. You have to prioritize yourself because they are tired of being the global leader in status quo, where there\'s a re-energization of people who thought that they are being ignored.','other',2612.4,2632),(2258,91,4572,4575,'Secondly, they say that Trump has its metric incentive to gain self-power. One, because I think self-power is the best metric. You have EU-US economic trade on both sides of the house. And the fact that the EU-US is a global actor that still has a lot of power, this proves to you.','other',2632,2647.7),(2259,91,4576,4578,'In terms of the intelligence, though, one, individual agreements still aren\'t enough. Two, I think Europe is an incentive to just propagate your intelligence and share it with the US on both sides of the house. In terms of the basis, though, I think the basis is not contingent on NATO existence in the beginning, which means that this is largely symmetric on both sides of the house.','other',2647.7,2667.9),(2260,91,4579,4582,'Finally, they say that Europe is increasing its funding for Trump finance. I don\'t think this is a good thing. What we think, to the person who I pointed out, is that this is a good thing in instances where they need. But I don\'t think this is a good thing for universally the European countries to have money, although our second argument still stands.','other',2667.9,2684),(2261,91,4583,4583,'For all those reasons, proposition takes its place.','other',2684,2686.5),(2262,94,4706,4707,'I\'m gonna spend 15 seconds just getting rid of a weird principle claim. Obviously, not only is military conscription just happening all the time, which is a continuation of the status quo, it is all practically contingent on deterrence and aggression, so I\'ll just prove that there, and that\'s still going to get rid of the principle.','other',3581.6,3653.8),(2263,94,4708,4712,'OK, three things. First, US will leave. Will the US leave on a position? Our claim was there\'s a growing anti-NATO sentiment. Maybe Trump amplifies this, maybe he doesn\'t, but all of this still means that US will leave.','other',3653.8,3665.2),(2264,94,4713,4717,'They have three reasons to claim that US will leave. First is on soft power. Natsuki\'s response is to argue that soft power already exists. NATO was founded to prevent Russia from sucking up post-USSR nations. This can\'t happen anymore because these countries have westernized their relies and they have too many economic ties.','other',3665.2,3681),(2265,94,4718,4722,'Their second claim was intelligence theory. Now, do you claim that this is symmetric? It\'s still the agreements and the ASEAN consent. If we share these, intelligence is symmetric. Then, in response to bases, Natsuki argues not only is this not a current provision in NATO anyway, and if countries are doing this voluntarily right now, they would just do it later afterwards anyway.','other',3681,3698.8),(2266,94,4723,4724,'Their way of never engaging is going to be this. Crucially, this means countries will leave the US in opposition.','other',3698.9,3704.3),(2267,94,4725,4729,'What was the implication of all of this? I\'m sorry, countries would leave NATO in opposition. What was the implication of all of this? One, it undercuts all of their claims on deterrence. Because NATO is heavily relied on the US, 20-something percent of their funding comes from the US, all of the deterrence factors that they would claim are significantly higher when the US leaves.','other',3704.3,3721.4),(2268,94,4730,4735,'What is the second? Our second argument, it brings a massive short-term weakness of NATO the moment the US leaves. The way OPF tried to deal with this is to argue that the US would collapse symmetrically in the Argo once nations start gradually leaving. Firstly, at worst, this response still doesn\'t dispute the first implication on screwing all of their deterrence mechanisms. But also secondly, the US won\'t leave because obviously now in our world, we have appeased to their desires of wanting the European countries to be more militarily dependent. All of this proves that the US will leave harsh.','other',3721.4,3753.6),(2269,94,4736,4739,'Secondly, on deterrence versus aggression, there were two ways we broke the deadlock in favor of aggression. One, if they say deterrence is effective and Putin fears NATO, the NATO expanding in opposition becomes a reason why Russia wants to attack now, i.e. increase expansion into Ukraine. But also secondly, because alternatives to war is uniquely available when NATO doesn\'t exist.','other',3753.6,3772.7),(2270,94,4740,4741,'Putin has demonized NATO and has to attack. Much like the Swiss, which I find very ironic, we would argue that aggression is going to be harmful and deterrence is better, exactly what the Swiss do.','other',3772.7,3783.6),(2271,94,4742,4744,'Third, final clash then, on the search and response. We are playing this fairly simple. 5% of GDP is big.','other',3783.6,3789.1),(2272,94,4745,4750,'The all-responsible argument against this is that the transition is extremely costly. There are two problems with this response. One, the transition fee doesn\'t apply for countries who don\'t need to increase their military spending. Obviously, this doesn\'t engage to our argument because this argument is only about nations who don\'t need the current amount of military and can decrease it. Clearly, Germany is never going to engage this way. The second response is to argue that it doesn\'t cost the transition money either, because from our first speaker and all the way onwards, we talked about a fairly easy alternative to the EU that exists for the vast majority of the countries and for military treaties, this country exists.','other',3789.1,3824.2),(2273,94,4751,4755,'We would argue this argument is quite important. Spain being able to invest 1% of their GDP into social welfare to haul up with their aging population. Or Greece being able to use more of their capital to subsidize the manufacturing sector so they can get out of the debt crisis. Resource is zero-sum. Much of these countries, 5% is too high.','other',3824.2,3840.7),(2274,94,4756,4757,'Finally, the conclusion of my speech is this. Even if we lose on anything, at worst, right?','other',3840.7,3846.1),(2275,94,4758,4762,'Worst is not the likely and most important impact of this debate. That means that we win on the surge in arms. But far more recently, even if you have to debate on the level of war, not only did we prove that aggression is the likely way that Russia invades rather than, sorry, active aggression, rather than is more harmful and redistributes deterrence, but also secondly, that we proved that the US is likely to do, which not only undercuts all of the mechanisms and implications that they have, but also then strengthens our second argument, proving to you that there\'s a massive shortfall and weakness in NATO the moment the US leaves, which leaves NATO alone. And this means it\'s part of the standard. Thank you.','other',3846.1,3880.8),(2276,72,2925,2925,'Can I be heard at this point?','other',611.5,612.8),(2277,72,2926,2927,'In courtrooms across the world, scientific evidence is being ignored by judges who lack the capacity to understand it, and corporate money rewrites the roles that present us. We end this auction of justice by letting those who know the science, not those who know the judge, interpret our laws.','other',619.6,636.3),(2278,72,2928,2928,'We\'re very proud of those.','other',636.6,637.5),(2279,72,2929,2931,'I want to talk about two arguments in my speech. Firstly, on why agencies set better standards. Secondly, on corporate capture.','other',637.8,643.5),(2280,72,2932,2932,'But first, I want to make a few responses and talk about the setup.','other',643.8,646.2),(2281,72,2933,2934,'First, I want to talk about the second argument on how agencies are bad incentives. I\'m going to clash directly with their first argument on why judges have better incentives in my argument, so I\'ll talk about the second argument here.','other',646.2,657.1),(2282,72,2935,2935,'They say that they have bad incentives, they can do things like neglecting heads of agencies.','other',657.3,660.8),(2283,72,2936,2940,'But I have a few responses against this. Firstly, I would say that agencies are held to the standards of administrative law. This means that they are required to file RIAs, which exist in many developed countries in the world, which must demonstrate things like empirical data to factor decisions. You have to consult with impact stakeholders, you have to consider alternatives and justify rejecting them. These are the kind of accountability measures that exist for many agencies in the status quo.','other',661.2,684.6),(2284,72,2941,2941,'I really don\'t understand how their side will suddenly leave lobbyists with bad incentives by corporations.','other',684.8,692.4),(2285,72,2942,2942,'No, thank you.','other',692.6,693),(2286,72,2943,2943,'I\'m going to weigh this later in my speech as well.','other',693.7,695.7),(2287,72,2944,2944,'Let\'s move on to my setup.','other',696.1,697.1),(2288,72,2945,2945,'The model we defend, much like the precedent set in Chevron, is as follows.','other',697.4,700.9),(2289,72,2946,2951,'Firstly, we would only defer to interpretations in agencies\' formal public documents, ensuring transparency. Secondly, we would only defer to agencies when statutes are ambiguous. Thirdly, the interpretation must be a reasonable reading of the law. This means agencies are still limited in their powers, what the legislators intended. Fourthly, just like unambiguous statutes are up for judicial review, so is the interpretation of an ambiguous law. Meaning, if an interpretation makes a law violating the constitution, human rights, international law, or administrative law, it can be struck down by the Supreme Court.','other',700.9,731.3),(2290,72,2952,2952,'First argument then, on better or worse.','other',731.7,733.1),(2291,72,2953,2954,'There are two layers to this argument. Firstly, on the quality of decision, and secondly, on preventing unpredictability.','other',733.6,738.7),(2292,72,2955,2955,'First layer then, on quality of decisions.','other',739.6,741.5),(2293,72,2956,2956,'Why do agencies have the unique capacity in terms of the expertise and tools required to shape interpretations?','other',742,747.8),(2294,72,2957,2960,'I have a few mechanisms here. Firstly, on specialized knowledge. Judges might get expert opinions, but they lack the specialized knowledge to understand what experts say and weigh into the decisions. Unlike propositions, no expert can explain complex economics or epidemiology to a person without even a first degree in econ or medicine.','other',748.3,767.5),(2295,72,2961,2962,'On the flip, agencies regularly employ the best experts in their field for decision-making decisions. The Federal Reserve has the world\'s best economists, CDC has the world\'s leading medical scientists.','other',768,779.1),(2296,72,2963,2963,'Secondly, on dedicated time.','other',779.6,781),(2297,72,2964,2966,'This is where we think there\'s a huge difference. In the overworked and understaffed judiciary, time crushes nuance when judges handle like different cases daily, then revisit each case months later after forgetting crucial details. This is what happens in courts.','other',781,794.4),(2298,72,2967,2967,'But in contrast, government agencies are solely responsible for implementing laws in their field, allowing them to dedicate thousands of hours by a large team to work towards the best policies using data analysis and talking with stakeholders looking at comparable different policies in different countries.','other',794.6,809.5),(2299,72,2968,2968,'What are the impacts of this?','other',809.8,810.9),(2300,72,2969,2969,'Panel, having the best data-driven statutory interpretation is nothing short of a matter of life and death.','other',810.9,816.9),(2301,72,2970,2972,'In the lead-up to Raman Khandel\'s collapse in Bangladesh, factory inspectors identified cracks in urban evacuation. But when factory owners challenged what constituted immediate danger in the court, judges who never studied structural engineering couldn\'t evaluate the technical evidence. 1,134 died the next day.','other',817.3,837.1),(2302,72,2973,2974,'Technical experts saw death. Legal experts saw ambiguity.','other',837.1,841.1),(2303,72,2975,2977,'When the Philippines tried regulating prescription drugs, courts had to interpret what constituted essential medicines. Judges ruled that branded drugs couldn\'t be substituted with generics for safety reasons, but health experts knew the generics were identical. So medicine prices stayed 300% higher than neighboring Thailand, pricing out millions of Filipinos.','other',841.7,861.6),(2304,72,2978,2978,'Judges couldn\'t evaluate the pharmaceutical evidence showing bioinfluence.','other',861.6,865.4),(2305,72,2979,2979,'When human lives are on the line, we\'re proud to stand on the side of expertise over ignorance.','other',865.4,870.7),(2306,72,2980,2980,'I want to talk about the second layer in my argument, on preventing uncertainty.','other',870.7,873.9),(2307,72,2981,2981,'The mechanism for this is on preemptive versus reactive setting of standards.','other',873.9,879.2),(2308,72,2982,2983,'Judges only interpret laws after disputes have occurred, which leaves stakeholders uncertain about their rights and duties. What\'s even worse, under a COPS rule, even companies that fully follow agency rule can still be sued if one viable.','other',879.2,892.2),(2309,72,2984,2985,'Agency sets standards in advance on the other hand. This gives market actors certainty about the regulatory framework they have to work with.','other',892.2,899),(2310,72,2986,2986,'The impact of this layer is twofold.','other',899,900.6),(2311,72,2987,2987,'Firstly, principally, this is wrong because relevant parties are punished for breaking rules they couldn\'t foresee.','other',900.6,905.7),(2312,72,2988,2989,'But secondly, on a practical level, the uncertainty around the rules makes laws harder to enforce. This not only raises litigation costs in general, but becomes an additional barrier for small businesses that cannot afford these costs, creating a disproportionate chilling effect on their operations.','other',905.7,921),(2313,72,2990,2990,'Before I move on to my second argument, I\'ll take the floor.','other',921,924.1),(2314,72,2991,2993,'Trump\'s DOJ and DOD uses vague interpretations of how they legislate to unjustly deport people. Courts cannot rule against this because they have to refer to agencies. What have your administrative law standards done to stop this?','poi',924.1,936.7),(2315,72,2994,2995,'Yeah, I think I gave you a bunch of mechanisms on why there are accountable measures against agencies exist in the status quo, on how they have to release probable measures, and they have to talk to different stakeholders. I\'m really not sure why that is only possible under a set of COPS.','other',936.7,950),(2316,72,2996,2996,'But if you want to engage in this, I\'m going to talk about corporate capture and things like lobbying exists more on your side of the house in my second argument.','other',950,956.4),(2317,72,2997,2997,'Big corporations regularly abuse legal procedures and set precedents that benefit them at the expense of ordinary people.','other',956.4,962.8),(2318,72,2998,2998,'This looks like courts allowing pollution under vaguely defined safe levels and let industries associations be EPA, harming the health of local communities.','other',962.8,970.4),(2319,72,2999,2999,'This looks like courts letting insurance deny life-saving treatment by narrowly confining medical necessity and reclining the state of California.','other',970.4,977.6),(2320,72,3000,3000,'These bad rulings then become precedent for all future cases.','other',977.6,981.1),(2321,72,3001,3001,'Why is it easier for corporations to influence courts than agencies?','other',981.1,985.1),(2322,72,3002,3004,'Four mechanisms here. Firstly, the process of persuasion. In courts, one judge is lawyer, often in many cases in private chambers and decides alone, whereas in agencies, multiple experts must agree, transparent records, oversight, and public pressure exists.','other',985.1,1000.2),(2323,72,3005,3006,'Secondly, on exhausting the opposition. Like corporations use the economic asymmetry between them and the plaintiffs to file endless appeals and challenges, knowing that the weaker party has less capacity to weather through the trials and will eventually drop out. their cases.','other',1000.2,1013.4),(2324,72,3007,3007,'In contrast, you can\'t just exhaust a politician or an agent, you must actually successfully persuade them.','other',1013.4,1018.9),(2325,72,3008,3010,'Thirdly, on judge shopping. Corporations often exploit the system through judge shopping to get favorable legal rulings. They file lawsuits and drop them when getting an unfavorable judge, only to file them again as many times as needed until being assigned a favorable one.','other',1018.9,1033.1),(2326,72,3011,3012,'In contrast, when lobbying politicians or bureaucrats, you can\'t just choose which people you need to convince. You must persuade whoever is in power and persuade a large group of people to get your way.','other',1033.1,1043),(2327,72,3013,3014,'Fourth, on forum shopping. In many regulatory fields that involve transnational litigation like IP, privacy, and tech, MNCs even get to choose the jurisdiction that they file a suit in, allowing them to be judged under the most favoritism regimes.','other',1043,1055.6),(2328,72,3015,3015,'In contrast, with lobbying an agency, they must convince the agency their own jurisdiction, which significantly limits their ability to evade liability.','other',1055.6,1063.2),(2329,72,3016,3016,'Panel, even if you don\'t buy this claim, assume both courts and government agencies engaged in lobbying, we\'d argue that lobbying courts is comparatively more harmful than lobbying agencies.','other',1063.2,1073),(2330,72,3017,3019,'This is because even the lobbied politicians and government agencies are directly subject to public support in the election. This is the most proximate and existential consideration for politicians and the agencies controlled by them. If the public doesn\'t support them and don\'t vote them into office, lobbying just doesn\'t matter.','other',1073,1088.8),(2331,72,3020,3021,'This isn\'t true for courts that are perceived and preferred to be independent, they\'re not democratically elected, and their terms are much more long-term, so we don\'t want them to be swayed by the whims of politics. This illusion of impartiality uniquely allows room for particularly pernicious forms of corporate actor that disproportionately favor large and powerful actors, even at the expense of the interests of people on the ground.','other',1088.8,1109.3),(2332,72,3022,3022,'Justice must not be an option for very powerful police.','other',1109.3,1112.4),(2333,74,3110,3113,'Statutes do not speak for themselves. And when they\'re evolved, when words fall short, someone must read the end of them. The question is, who? How could have you believed that judges, distant from facts and detached from the field, should override the very experts who live and breathe the law\'s application every day?','other',1807.1,1828.8),(2334,74,3114,3114,'But interpretation without expertise is not justice. It\'s guesswork. Two clashes.','other',1828.8,1833.4),(2335,74,3115,3117,'First, an outcome. Second, a manipulation. Finally, an extent in third-party.','other',1833.4,1837.9),(2336,74,3118,3118,'First, on outcomes.','other',1837.9,1839.6),(2337,74,3119,3120,'The main challenge we get, I guess, to our idea is agencies have the incentive to use their capacity in bad ways. Why then would we argue agencies have the incentive to use their capacity in good ways?','other',1839.6,1849.6),(2338,74,3121,3123,'One, agencies to some extent are held to the standards of administrative law. This means they\'re required to file things like regulatory impact assessments or RFII-RIIIs, which must demonstrate empirical data to back their decisions, have consulted with impacted stakeholders, and have a considerable alternative than justifying rejecting them. It also means government agents are criminally liable for breach of their statutory obligation, which require them to be impartial and punish themselves for decisions they\'re impartial about, refuse to accept any form of bribe, avoid using said information because of any past occupations or future occupations, and be held to a freeze period after their exit from office when they cannot work in their native industries.','other',1849.6,1885),(2339,74,3124,3124,'Secondly, agencies are accountable to the government and by proxy to the voters.','other',1885,1889.4),(2340,74,3125,3125,'If an agency does a bad job resulting in bad outcomes, it\'s to create public backlash that politicians will punish the responsible for.','other',1889.4,1895.5),(2341,74,3126,3127,'They say agencies have low visibility. The problem is there\'s massive incentive for opposition parties to capitalize on agencies being bad and actively politicize, which crucially creates more visibility when agencies are being bad, even if this is not regularly a thing.','other',1895.5,1908.9),(2342,74,3128,3130,'Thirdly, people who work in government agencies just tend to genuinely care about doing the best job possible. These are experts who chose a public sector job over a private sector job because they care. These are people who have decades of experience behind them, who learned to look up and admire the best in their field and want to become like them.','other',1908.9,1927.4),(2343,74,3131,3131,'But finally, if their interpretations are made in a way that\'s unfair or unjust, lacking due process and cannot be justified, in these cases alone, obviously, courts can also intervene in our world.','other',1927.4,1938.1),(2344,74,3132,3132,'I would further argue that even if you and all of us talk about government abuse of power, this is dealt with there.','other',1938.1,1944.7),(2345,74,3133,3133,'Yeah, go ahead.','other',1944.7,1945.3),(2346,74,3134,3136,'In debates, the judge is an informal average voter. They don\'t have to be specialized in the economy to judge a job because they are smart enough to navigate it. As we as debaters are expected to convey our concepts and effects clearly, is that not similar?','poi',1945.3,1956.9),(2347,74,3137,3138,'I don\'t know if you\'ve ever done an economics debate before, but I would say you\'re like no debater unless they\'re studying economics. It\'s that good at making the correct economic arguments and can evaluate this very well.','other',1956.9,1968.7),(2348,74,3139,3139,'Our first argument proves all of the specialized knowledge that is necessary, but furthermore, even if specialized knowledge to some extent is able to be done by these lawyers and by these judges, we talk about the lack of time and the time crunch that causes such harms to manifest.','other',1968.7,1981.8),(2349,74,3140,3140,'Let\'s talk about their three mechanisms to attempt to prove judges\' capacity to navigate technical fields as a sub-way.','other',1981.8,1988.3),(2350,74,3141,3141,'Their first idea is courts are specialized.','other',1988.3,1990),(2351,74,3142,3143,'The problem is, oftentimes, courts just aren\'t specialized in specific areas. There\'s no environment court that you can yield to for environmental lawmaking.','other',1990,1997.6),(2352,74,3144,3145,'But also, secondly, this is just oftentimes less true the higher up in the court ladder you go. There\'s no environmental Supreme Court and there\'s no housing Supreme Court that we can yield to when we\'re making statutory interpretation.','other',1997.6,2007.7),(2353,74,3146,3146,'Their second response about bringing in experts are arguments already presented to the U.S.','other',2007.7,2011.7),(2354,74,3147,3150,'So their final idea is about the impacts of the interpretation can easily be visualized. I don\'t understand if you\'ve seen law before, but it\'s really hard to visualize some of these things. Even if we were to, for example, debate on what the standard for what a vehicle that should be regulated is, on environmental laws, it\'s unclear to what extent this harms the environment and it\'s very hard to visualize this. But also secondly, judges to that extent can only visualize the specific case in front of them, they cannot therefore visualize the precedence that they set or the future extent of harm that may manifest as a result of this, so interpretation being visualizable is just wrong.','other',2011.7,2043.7),(2355,74,3151,3151,'There are two sub-ideas that come in their depth, and you can get so much more to better outcomes that I won\'t be able to provide you with more.','other',2043.7,2049.8),(2356,74,3152,3152,'The first idea is about predictability.','other',2049.8,2051.2),(2357,74,3153,3154,'The main response here is to note the crucial difference between predictability and stagnation. The law is evolving, and this is a good thing, you are happily standing behind laws changing in accordance with changing values, circumstances, new information.','other',2051.2,2062.6),(2358,74,3155,3158,'What it is not is unpredictable. When agencies change their protocols, they make this public. Everyone knows about the change and it only applies prospectively, not retrospectively. Further, they usually give a notice before they give the change to give markets time to apply and adjust gradually.','other',2062.6,2076.1),(2359,74,3159,3161,'Obviously they have incentive to do this. In contrast, when statutory interpretation is decided in court, this actually creates actual unpredictability. There is no prior timeline, the standard is left unknown until it is decided in court after a dispute has already occurred, and once a verdict is given, it applies instantly with no transition or adjustment period.','other',2076.1,2092.9),(2360,74,3162,3162,'The other idea that may implicate two better decisions that they argue is about how holistic understanding can only be done by judges.','other',2092.9,2100.2),(2361,74,3163,3164,'Firstly, I will flip this. Judges are biased towards the case they are looking at, agencies are interpreting statutes without a specific case in mind, which allows for more holistic understanding.','other',2100.2,2109),(2362,74,3165,3167,'Secondly, this holistic understanding is sometimes just bad and compromises the specific context of the law at hand. Judges use broad legal concepts and precedents for unrelated theory. They apply the same definition of necessary to a grand legal law that they use for a tax law, but these words mean different things in different contexts.','other',2109,2125.3),(2363,74,3168,3170,'Finally, agencies also understand and consider different viewpoints. They have advisors from various fields, work in collaboration with other agencies, and hear claims from various stakeholders. They in fact have much more time to collect all of these different perspectives than courts do, which engages further claiming about holistic understanding.','other',2125.3,2142.1),(2364,74,3171,3171,'Second question, on manipulation by big actors.','other',2142.1,2145.9),(2365,74,3172,3172,'Firstly, I want to begin with the assertive claim from their bench that this is not factual reality.','other',2145.9,2150.5),(2366,74,3173,3177,'In International Shoe Corporation v. Washington, the USSC expanded the concept of personal jurisdiction to allow court to hear disputes over defendants who had only minimum contracts with its jurisdiction. In Philip Morris v. Uruguay, PMI instructed its ownership so that the Uruguay subsidiary became held by a Swiss entity, enabling access to arbitration under the Swiss courts to circumnavigate courts and challenge Uruguay\'s tobacco packaging and anti-smoking regulations. Form-shopping is a real problem.','other',2150.5,2177.6),(2367,74,3178,3178,'The other and better response you get is this idea of regulation, but if the court wants to regulate against it, I would like to see them succeed where many governments have failed.','other',2177.6,2185.7),(2368,74,3179,3179,'Thirdly, corporations can easily, for example, change their headquarters or subsidiary regulation, etc.','other',2185.7,2190.7),(2369,74,3180,3180,'Finally, in the last one minute and a half of my speech, an extending third argument.','other',2190.9,2196.2),(2370,74,3181,3181,'Third argument, agencies have an interpretation that is closer to what the legislator\'s intent is, what we should principally strive for when interpreting the law.','other',2196.2,2205.4),(2371,74,3182,3182,'First argument, why agencies\' interpretation is closer to the legislator\'s intent. Three mechanisms.','other',2205.4,2210.3),(2372,74,3183,3184,'One, the government appoints agents with a deliberate intent to execute its broader ideology. In contrast, judges in courts are nominated by an independent committee, making their understanding of the law divorced from what the legislator intended.','other',2210.3,2222.4),(2373,74,3185,3187,'Second, agencies work alongside the government through the legislative process and regularly meet and consult with government departments. In contrast, judges grow from the academy and private sector into courts that are passing through parliament. They lack familiarity with lawmakers and with the process of lawmaking, making them ignorant to the considerations behind choosing specific terms.','other',2222.4,2240.4),(2374,74,3188,3190,'Third, agencies are accountable to the government. They rely on effort, budget, authorities, promotions, and promotions. In contrast, judges have little interest in the legislator\'s intent as they are nominated for life and their careers are immune to government-specific decisions.','other',2240.4,2251.8),(2375,74,3191,3191,'A very good feature of judicial overview, but one that is bad for statutory interpretation.','other',2251.8,2256.1),(2376,74,3192,3192,'Second and more importantly then, why does this mean that we must principally reject this motion? Hand up.','other',2256.1,2262.5),(2377,74,3193,3194,'The conclusion of the analysis is simple. Agencies\' interpretations are what the legislator actually intended, and judges are therefore bound to them just like they are to the law.','other',2262.5,2271),(2378,74,3195,3197,'This is when it can depend on the practical laws because the justice system isn\'t designed to make the best laws. That is not its mandate. The public voted in the people whose mandate it is, and these people delegated this power to those who best represented them.','other',2271,2283.9),(2379,74,3198,3199,'Statutory interpretations are no different. They meaningfully change laws and reflect value judgments, and when such value judgments are made by the government by proxy of its agents, judges have no rights to intervene by the same exact reasons that they have no rights to intervene in an unambiguous statute as the court itself conceived.','other',2283.9,2298.9),(2380,74,3200,3200,'For these reasons, here are a couple of others.','other',2298.9,2301.3),(2381,76,3274,3274,'Can I be heard? Yes.','other',2910.8,2914),(2382,76,3275,3276,'What\'s my ideas? I\'m just trying to point out my ideas.','other',2914.6,2917.9),(2383,76,3277,3279,'Three major things I\'ll say to you. First, on the principle of this debate. Secondly, on the quality of the interpretations, lastly, on corporate culture.','other',2917.9,2953.9),(2384,76,3280,3280,'Firstly, let\'s talk about the principle of this debate.','other',2953.9,2956.7),(2385,76,3281,3283,'Our claim is simple. The lacuna, i.e. the vagueness that is currently made when an interpretation is processed, is not a bug, it is a feature, and we must protect this on our side of the house.','other',2956.7,2967.6),(2386,76,3284,3285,'Let\'s take a look at the feeble response we\'ve had on their side. The only one-liner that we\'ve heard from Donald Trump was to say, well, the purpose of the judiciary is to check the government out.','other',2967.6,2976.9),(2387,76,3286,3288,'Note, opposition\'s response is only valid in instances where governments act lawfully, unlawfully. But if you take a look at our model and the reasonable interpretation of this debate, when governments and agencies just go out of its mandate and just literally interpret it unlawfully, I think this is a claim even under our side of the house where it can\'t intervene. This is a framing that we gave them in reverse, and they even framed it for themselves.','other',2976.9,3003),(2388,76,3289,3290,'What this means is, I think this balance of separation of power wasn\'t our principle. In fact, our principle was about how we should ensure separation of power in our side of the house, which is a reason why they simply cannot win on this one-liner response.','other',3003,3018.2),(2389,76,3291,3291,'And they even concede that there\'s a sufficient number of cases where judges make their own decisions on their side of the house, because it was in these instances where we say that this is principally illegitimate.','other',3018.2,3028.9),(2390,76,3292,3292,'Why is it that the principle matters most in this debate?','other',3028.9,3032),(2391,76,3293,3294,'On intuition, these courts have no mapping. It doesn\'t really matter if people practically yield better outcomes.','other',3032,3038.1),(2392,76,3295,3297,'And the reason for that is simple. It is unjustified for courts to override democracy. If it were different, we would let courts legislate directly.','other',3038.1,3046.9),(2393,76,3298,3298,'Given that prohibition cannot stand by this, we think that principally, this is most important on intuition.','other',3046.9,3052.8),(2394,76,3299,3299,'But on weighing mechanisms, two things.','other',3052.8,3054.9),(2395,76,3300,3302,'One, uncertainty, realistically, sometimes courts have better interpretation. Sometimes agencies win. But the principle always holds.','other',3054.9,3063.5),(2396,76,3303,3305,'Secondly, in terms of subjectivity, because disputes that reach the courts are debatable and are too silent, this means on practical outcomes, there\'s no clear right or wrong. Hence, it is pointless to point out that this outcome is good or bad. In fact, we just fulfill the principle which is more important and is great.','other',3063.5,3080.9),(2397,76,3306,3306,'Now then, let\'s tackle the second clash between practical outcomes and the quality of interpretation.','other',3080.9,3085.8),(2398,76,3307,3307,'On this theme, I want to tackle two things.','other',3085.8,3088.3),(2399,76,3308,3308,'First thing I want to talk about is incentive of those two actors.','other',3088.3,3091.8),(2400,76,3309,3312,'The first thing I want to point out is that all of our second speaker work is just unrespondent to. We told you numerous reasons why agencies are still held accountable for the administrative law. So they\'re criminally liable when it goes against them. And the fact that they\'re just held accountable by voters.','other',3091.8,3106.9),(2401,76,3313,3315,'And they say, oh, but the voters just don\'t care about it and so forth, it is not visible. Here I want to bring back the impact he brought to you from our very first speaker that you ignored. That in this one single case of Brennan Plaza, literally 1,000 people were killed as a result of this one single interpretation.','other',3106.9,3123.8),(2402,76,3316,3316,'And if you look at their budget, and how judges have a holistic understanding all of which is contingent on cases where this has a large impact in the public, which is the reason why in important cases where there\'s an important delta on the two sides of the debate, I think it is fair to say that the public has an incentive to hold you accountable.','other',3123.8,3143.3),(2403,76,3317,3317,'The second thing they say is that all governments will abuse power.','other',3143.3,3146.4),(2404,76,3318,3319,'First, look at our model. When government agencies act improperly or raise, chances are that they violate one of the many legal restrictions they face in terms of the constitutional law, in which case I think the courts on both sides of the aisle can hold you accountable.','other',3146.4,3161.8),(2405,76,3320,3321,'Secondly and more importantly, I would note that this is not your benefit to be back to begin with, right? Because tax agencies are not needed and supervised by the elected government, and if the government agenda is to maximize the tax collection and just redistribute it into welfare, for example, that is a reason why I think government is not abusing, it is catering to the public.','other',3161.8,3180.1),(2406,76,3322,3323,'Third of all, I think checks and balances against corruption are built into a system because the majority of the legislators and other experts within the agency will need to collaborate to enable an agency to prevent the process of abuse to begin with. Opposition parties are not going to be benefiting from the abuse they say exists, which is the reason why corruption is non-minuscule in this debate.','other',3180.1,3199),(2407,76,3324,3324,'Finally, they say that agencies will uniquely have the incentive to improve on our side.','other',3199,3203.4),(2408,76,3325,3326,'One, I want to clap from the beginning. This is not really important because our incentive analysis is how we still don\'t have negative analysis and negative impact and negative interest is not static, so therefore even if there\'s a marginal increase in the palatability of their incentive, it doesn\'t really matter.','other',3203.4,3218.2),(2409,76,3327,3328,'Secondly, there\'s symmetric incentive to improve on both sides of the house. The fact that you want to cater to voters, the fact that there\'s internal competition to just climb up the hierarchy within the agency is the reason why we likely interpret an improvement on our side of the house as well.','other',3218.2,3233),(2410,76,3329,3329,'Therefore, I think this just deals with our claim in how there\'s permissible or harmful interpretation that exists in our world in the form of an incentive to improve on both sides of the house.','other',3233,3242.7),(2411,76,3330,3330,'Second theme within this theme, unequal capacity.','other',3242.7,3245.3),(2412,76,3331,3331,'I want to talk about two themes under this.','other',3245.3,3247.3),(2413,76,3332,3332,'Firstly, I want to talk about why expertise is important because proposition says that you don\'t need expertise in the beginning.','other',3247.3,3253.3),(2414,76,3333,3335,'Why do you need expertise? Because when discussing what the exact minimum wage is, the problem is it\'s not about the vague logic, right? It\'s about picking up metrics.','other',3253.3,3262.2),(2415,76,3336,3338,'When you talk about the sufficient safety measures, it\'s not about some random logic. It\'s about the expertise that exists within it, which is a reason why I think it is from the lens of the judges who don\'t know anything about this field. When you just, you know, even if you consult with multiple different people, if they give you multiple different advices, you just don\'t know what to do.','other',3262.2,3281.6),(2416,76,3339,3339,'Now then, let\'s take a look at their positive material.','other',3281.6,3284.3),(2417,76,3340,3340,'The first thing they say is that judges have a better understanding of the law. Three things.','other',3284.3,3288.5),(2418,76,3341,3342,'One, understanding that the law is just indirectly relevant in this context of the in-way. What does that law have to teach you about what the source means in this specific context?','other',3288.5,3295.7),(2419,76,3343,3343,'Two, government agencies employ legal advisors as well, which means that I think the law expertise is inexclusive.','other',3295.7,3302.2),(2420,76,3344,3344,'Three, again, from our side of the house, agencies are limited with boundaries of reasonable interpretation, bringing out the worst forms of harm.','other',3302.2,3309.7),(2421,76,3345,3345,'Finally, they say that the judges have a more holistic understanding.','other',3309.7,3312.3),(2422,76,3346,3348,'One, flip this argument. Judges are biased towards the case that they\'re looking at. Agencies are interpreting the statute with a specific case in mind.','other',3312.3,3320.4),(2423,76,3349,3351,'Two, the holistic understanding is something that compromises the specific contents of the law at hand. For example, judges broadly use the concept of precedent for their related field. I don\'t think this is a good thing.','other',3320.4,3332.2),(2424,76,3352,3354,'I think you should apply the expertise that\'s uniquely available from our side of the house. The implication of this being then is that when they\'re involved, you don\'t have the proficient capacity thereby. It just takes down the premise of their framing and how you are able to defer in instances if agencies are persuasive.','other',3332.2,3348.3),(2425,76,3355,3355,'Before I move on to corporate capture, I\'ll give you a hint.','other',3348.3,3351.2),(2426,76,3356,3357,'Let\'s talk about your example of the factory building on itself. If a bunch of architects and engineers come up to a judge and say, look, the factory\'s going to collapse on itself, why would judges allow them to do this by their interpretation?','poi',3351.2,3360.3),(2427,76,3358,3359,'So the first thing I want to point out is that your argument in how, oh, judges can still be able to just act better, is contingent on the fact that the harm\'s already been materialized in the real world. But in fact, in most cases, you still need to preemptively make the interpretation when a lot of the things are broad and vague.','other',3360.3,3377.3),(2428,76,3360,3360,'Now, let\'s talk about corporate captures quickly.','other',3377.3,3379.2),(2429,76,3361,3361,'I want to note that in court, that that majority of our things are misconstituted because the two responses they give you about incentive from the judges and disclosure mechanism largely doesn\'t really deal with the nuanced material we provided you from first speaker.','other',3379.2,3392.8),(2430,76,3362,3365,'But it wasn\'t about like things like you needing to sway one single judge. It was about judge shopping. It was about jurisdiction shopping. It was about corporations taking control of the system where you are likely to just pick and choose specific judges that literally align with the predisposed biases that you have.','other',3392.8,3409.5),(2431,76,3366,3367,'But even if you don\'t take that panel, I want to do a reign here. Let\'s take a look at the scale of the corporate capture we proved in our first speaker.','other',3409.5,3416.2),(2432,76,3368,3370,'Courts only adjudicate an issue when someone brings a case. These cases disproportionately come from larger corporations that exist in their world. This is because to sue an agency, you need to know the process in the first place and have the ability and the capacity and things like money to begin with.','other',3416.2,3432.6),(2433,76,3371,3371,'All of which is the reason why, in their world, I think the corporations and the large companies have to just initiate the case to begin with, in which case, they either win or lose on both sides of the house.','other',3432.6,3443.5),(2434,76,3372,3372,'I think they do lose.','other',3443.5,3444.6),(2435,76,3373,3373,'Thank you for that.','other',3444.6,3450.8),(2436,77,3378,3378,'I\'m trying to suppress the opposition that has been the majority.','other',3466.9,3487),(2437,77,3379,3380,'I feel it is a great opposition. We have three paths to victory in this debate.','other',3487,3522.4),(2438,77,3381,3381,'The mandate of agencies to interpret the law, the unique capacity to design better regulation, and the prevention of corporate capture.','other',3522.4,3528.4),(2439,77,3382,3383,'If you remember one thing from my speech, remember this. Enough of each of these is enough for winning this debate.','other',3528.4,3534.9),(2440,77,3384,3384,'Trump must defeat all three. Here\'s why.','other',3534.9,3537.6),(2441,77,3385,3386,'If courts have no mandate, obviously it doesn\'t matter if practically they would yield better outcomes. It\'s still unjustified for courts to override democracy.','other',3537.6,3543.8),(2442,77,3387,3389,'Had it been different, we wouldn\'t have settled for interventive power. We would have let courts obviously legislate directly. Since Trump doesn\'t, they obviously can\'t stand behind this.','other',3543.8,3552.2),(2443,77,3390,3390,'However, obviously the approach doesn\'t apply to us.','other',3552.2,3554.8),(2444,77,3391,3391,'If Trump proves a mandate, it only proves a justification for judges to interpret differently, not an obligation to do so.','other',3554.8,3560.6),(2445,77,3392,3392,'Lastly, even if both Trump, both proves a mandate and better interpretation, if we prove they use uncorporated capture, they should still lose.','other',3560.6,3569.4),(2446,77,3393,3393,'This is because if they lose this much, they effectively forfeit control of interpretation from courts to corporations, who would shake the precedence in their favor.','other',3569.4,3576.1),(2447,77,3394,3394,'Even if courts have the mandate and the better interpretation on their side, corrupt private actors surely don\'t, and on a comparative, even an imperfect government agency should still win.','other',3576.1,3584.2),(2448,77,3395,3395,'Again, the reverse doesn\'t apply to us.','other',3584.2,3586.3),(2449,77,3396,3397,'Even if Trump were able to prove regulatory capture is worse than hijacking of courts, this is necessary, but not a sufficient condition for them to win. That is because regulatory capture still exists on their side as well.','other',3586.3,3597.2),(2450,77,3398,3398,'This motion doesn\'t eliminate them, but only adds more ways for corporations to get their way even when lobbying fails.','other',3597.2,3602.4),(2451,77,3399,3399,'Hence, winning each of these clashes alone should suffice for us to win the debate.','other',3602.4,3606.1),(2452,77,3400,3400,'Thankfully, to make it clear, we won all three.','other',3606.1,3608.2),(2453,77,3401,3401,'First clash, better outcomes.','other',3608.2,3610),(2454,77,3402,3402,'The very first thing is on the navigation of technical fields.','other',3610,3615.2),(2455,77,3403,3403,'I responded to all of the three mechanisms they gave you first.','other',3615.2,3618.1),(2456,77,3404,3404,'So the intuition pump is this.','other',3618.1,3619.4),(2457,77,3405,3406,'Even if I were to be an economics expert, it is impossible for me to think lay ministers to explain economics to a judge who can reasonably understand and make well-reasoned decisions to it. The second thing under this clash is on time.','other',3619.4,3631.8),(2458,77,3407,3407,'There was just no response from their bench against this mechanism.','other',3631.8,3634.3),(2459,77,3408,3408,'Judges only get a few days in total to evaluate cases, and they have to handle many cases at once.','other',3634.3,3639.3),(2460,77,3409,3410,'Crucially, this means even if they have the best incentives, they just don\'t have the time to make the best decisions and to hear out everything. Thirdly, on a holistic understanding, because we flipped their line of mechanism, judges can only see the current case, but the agency can see the entire statute and all of the implications of that.','other',3639.3,3654.6),(2461,77,3411,3411,'There was no response even though this was raised.','other',3654.6,3656.2),(2462,77,3412,3412,'And second, the last glitch POI safe attempt from their speaker is this.','other',3656.2,3661.3),(2463,77,3413,3413,'If experts are correct, we would obviously do what they want.','other',3661.3,3664.1),(2464,77,3414,3415,'The problem is, one, I would start by pointing out that if Trump wants judges to defer to agency\'s expertise, yeah, that\'s defending our side. Second, note how lazy this response is, and it doesn\'t respond to a lot of our analysis.','other',3664.1,3674.9),(2465,77,3416,3416,'For example, if judges don\'t understand these facts, they can\'t be persuaded, right?','other',3674.9,3678.5),(2466,77,3417,3418,'If judges don\'t have the time to evaluate the analysis, they won\'t be persuaded by it. If judges only see one case and not the big picture, they obviously can\'t be persuaded by evidence that failed to manifest in this one specific statute either.','other',3678.7,3688.7),(2467,77,3419,3419,'Second glitch, on abuse then.','other',3689,3691.3),(2468,77,3420,3420,'First, on government abuse.','other',3691.3,3692.7),(2469,77,3421,3421,'The problem is we gave a lot of incentive and capacity analysis from second speaker.','other',3692.7,3697.3),(2470,77,3422,3423,'On the level of incentive, we gave you four mechanisms as to why agencies have the best incentives on our online favorability list. And on the level of capacity, there was a bunch of mechanisms from my deputy and from BIP about how we can out-regulate governments in this situation.','other',3697.3,3709.1),(2471,77,3424,3424,'The second thing on corporate abuse then.','other',3709.1,3711.9),(2472,77,3425,3425,'The problem analysis is revolving board.','other',3711.9,3713.8),(2473,77,3426,3426,'Obviously, we gave you all of the analysis as to why this is oftentimes not possible due to, for example, freeze periods.','other',3713.8,3718.8),(2474,77,3427,3427,'But also, just their analysis here is just bad.','other',3718.8,3720.8),(2475,77,3428,3429,'Their mechanism for lack of accountability here is, oh, you control the Senate, House, and Presidency, which means you can\'t hold them accountable. But obviously, agencies like the FBI that work to hold politicians accountable have nothing to do with the Senate, House, or Parliament.','other',3720.8,3733.9),(2476,77,3430,3430,'And then their comparative analysis is they have tenure and also judges face punishments.','other',3733.9,3738.1),(2477,77,3431,3432,'But judges face punishments, but so do agencies in our world. I don\'t really understand the comparative analysis.','other',3738.1,3744),(2478,77,3433,3433,'Our competing claim under this class was manipulation by big actors.','other',3744,3747.5),(2479,77,3434,3435,'Crucially, regulation is insufficient in responding to this because no regulation can sufficiently take away the rights to access legal procedure, nor the rights to, for example, withdraw from lawsuit. So nothing can stop corporations from withdrawing lawsuits with unfavorable judges, nor from assuming, again, if they have better legal standing, allowing them to keep going until they get the best judge.','other',3747.5,3766.8),(2480,77,3436,3437,'All of our mechanisms for corporate manipulation are not able to be regulated. They\'re inherent traits of the justice system that uniquely harm their rights.','other',3766.8,3775),(2481,77,3438,3438,'For these reasons, opposed. Thank you.','other',3775,3778.4),(2482,77,3439,3439,'Thank you for that speech.','other',3779.7,3780.1),(2483,77,3440,3440,'And I have a question, proposition, which I\'d like you to do in the case of Rob.','other',3780.1,3783.6),(2484,71,2862,2862,'this body of legal rights.','other',14.8,16.2),(2485,71,2863,2863,'Yes, you go ahead.','other',17.2,18.3),(2486,71,2864,2865,'The system is the last line of defense to protect the most vulnerable. And this can only happen if you give judges the ability to do their jobs without being significantly constrained by administrative agencies.','other',18.6,40),(2487,71,2866,2866,'There are two parts of setup there.','other',40.4,42),(2488,71,2867,2868,'The first is in terms of who these administrative agencies are. There are bureaucratic bodies such as the Environmental Protection Agency which are in charge of implementing laws by the legislature.','other',42.3,53.4),(2489,71,2869,2872,'But secondly, let\'s talk about where strategic interpretation takes place. The first is where statutory language is extremely vague because of the use of terminologies such as reasonable or appropriate. And for example, the vast majority of environmental regulations and statutes prohibit actions that lead to substantial impact. But it is extremely unclear as to what substantial impact amounts to in the first place.','other',53.8,78.8),(2490,71,2873,2873,'The second instance then is when technical terminology is significantly unclear.','other',78.8,83.4),(2491,71,2874,2875,'The opposition depends on where web codes are bound by the interpretation of these agencies. Our goal is one where codes independently interpret the statute to determine the most reasonable meaning.','other',83.7,94.4),(2492,71,2876,2878,'We can also apply the intensity for statute by consult agencies and administrations on our side of the house to get technical advice. And judges can also bring in expert testimonies and independent technical advices in instances where they are extremely unsure how to do it. In essence, judges can use administrative agencies and technical advices as tools in a toolbox to make the best possible interpretations possible on our side of the house.','other',94.7,119.3),(2493,71,2879,2879,'There are two constructive arguments coming out of our opposition.','other',119.6,122.5),(2494,71,2880,2881,'The first is in terms of why judges make better interpretations. But secondly, in terms of why administrative interpretations will lead to harmful outcomes on their comparative.','other',122.9,131.7),(2495,71,2882,2882,'The first argument then, in terms of why judges make better interpretations.','other',131.9,136.1),(2496,71,2883,2883,'This argument has three parts.','other',136.1,138.1),(2497,71,2884,2884,'The first is in terms of why judges are able to navigate technical interpretations.','other',138.4,143.6),(2498,71,2885,2885,'The mechanism for this is twofold.','other',144,145.6),(2499,71,2886,2889,'The first is that codes are specialized into many fields, such as labor law and environmental law and the people who work in these codes go on, people who work in these codes as lawyers go on to becoming judges, which means judges have years and years of experience within these specialized fields while also being able to perform fast court cases and their proceedings. The second mechanism of that is that codes can bring in expert testimonies that act as an independent translation tool to make sense of the science behind things like drug recommendation for instance. Because, first of all, it is quite intuitive that it doesn\'t take a scientist to understand that getting from a clinical trial that proves a certain drug is harmful, a certain drug is addictive, is harmful and should not be put out into the market. Judges can make these valid judgments on their own, on our side of the house.','other',145.9,191.3),(2500,71,2890,2892,'The second part of this argument that is in terms of expanding what even if judges don\'t have the same level of technical expertise as technocrats, while they are still able to navigate certain interpretation processes and understand the overall implications of their interpretations. The mechanism for this is quite simple because the index of different interpretations are simple to understand because you can see them manifest in the real world from the ground up. This can be easily illustrated by the case of Massachusetts versus GBA in 2007 where there was a question of whether CO2 should be listed under the harmful chemical under the CAA Act.','other',191.3,230.7),(2501,71,2893,2893,'In this case, the GBA rejected the interpretation that CO2 fell under the Act because they were lobbied by all companies such as Shell and Chevron.','other',230.8,239.9),(2502,71,2894,2896,'In the court proceedings, which included extreme levels of peer-reviewed studies, proved that the success of regulating CO2 and its importance. In the end, the court ruled that the EPA was able to interpret a CO2 big fall under this Act. This proves to you why agencies are extremely self-interested and by God, they make good decisions.','other',240.2,261.1),(2503,71,2897,2898,'Now, opposition might think that this proves that reasonability is enough to prevent harmful interpretations from taking place, but they cannot claim this benefit because this case was taken to the Supreme Court and this can only happen because deference is not relevant to the Supreme Court. Now, moving on to the third part of this argument, which is that the judiciary\'s job is to find the best possible meaning of certain statutes and laws, but agencies constrain them from doing this and restrict them to what is permissible under the statute.','other',261.4,293.4),(2504,71,2899,2901,'Before I move into that third mechanism, I will take that point. So how would a judge who doesn\'t really have any degrees in a specific field, are any who are able to just evaluate conflicting research like epidemiology, for example? I think our bar is significant on the extent to which we just now, a couple of minutes ago, which is that even on our worst possible case, where you cannot understand significant unscientific research, you understand the overall implications of those in the real world, we don\'t need to prove to you what you want to say, and we still have a monarchy and stuff like that.','other',293.6,324.9),(2505,71,2902,2902,'The third part of this argument is the argument in terms of why deference prevents the best possible interpretation from taking place.','other',324.9,331),(2506,71,2903,2904,'Opposition\'s world is one where the most permissible interpretation takes place, but panel, permissibility doesn\'t equate to harm reduction on that comparative. Like, remember the example of the EPA, where the permissible interpretation was to like equal amount of harmful pollutants, but to exclude CO2.','other',331.5,349),(2507,71,2905,2906,'See, this is harmful because CO2 will continue to be unregulated, harming millions of people on their side of the house, and pushing climate change to a point of no return on their side of the house. In that, on our side of the house, judges will not differ on our comparative, and they will understand that CO2 is bad and should be regulated, that is why we make the best possible interpretations on our comparative.','other',349.3,371.4),(2508,71,2907,2907,'The second argument then is in terms of why differing to administrative agencies will always lead to bad outcomes on their comparative.','other',371.8,379.6),(2509,71,2908,2908,'Let\'s first talk about instances where judges will disagree with agencies.','other',379.6,383.6),(2510,71,2909,2910,'The most likely manifestation on opposition is where permissible interpretations take place, but I\'ve already significantly established to you that the point is that even if an interpretation is permissible, it is still significantly harmful. For example, in the UK, the government can seize land for private companies under the guise that it is for public use.','poi',384,404.5),(2511,71,2911,2912,'The New London Development Corporation will find public use to mean economic development and they transfer that land to Pfizer. This hits millions of working class families and hundreds of thousands of them on their comparative.','other',404.5,418.5),(2512,71,2913,2913,'In our world, judges simply will not be satisfied that this is permissible and will push for the best possible interpretation, which is likely to create far more people on our comparative.','other',418.8,428.8),(2513,71,2914,2915,'The first part of this argument then is in terms of why agencies have incentives to give in to corporations. Three mechanisms. The first is that different presidents appoint their own heads of agencies based on people who follow their agenda, which this is exactly why the EPA cherishes the interpretation of what the law means with every change in administration.','other',428.8,449.3),(2514,71,2916,2918,'But secondly, Democrats heed to the death and call of politicians because of the revolving door theory, because they want to keep the private sector in that loop, also gain jobs in the corporate sector and push for jobs like consultancies with higher levels of pay on their comparative, so that direct incentives do not pass by the nation and not hit their public markets. But thirdly, let\'s talk about that, but the second part of this argument then is in terms of why does a lack of accountability prevent this. So even if opposition says there is separation of power act, we want other to find out that this is not a good enough accountability mechanism on their comparative in the context of party politics, where one party controls both the presidency and the legislature.','other',449.7,487.7),(2515,71,2919,2919,'For example, in like the US right now, the Republican party controls all three parties in the US, which means you have no accountability mechanisms on your comparative.','other',488,496.8),(2516,71,2920,2920,'The third part of this argument then is in terms of why deference prevents judges from making fun of deference prevents judges from doing their jobs, which is judging.','other',497.1,507.4),(2517,71,2921,2921,'And that is an absolutely violent form of better on our side of the house.','other',507.7,511.4),(2518,71,2922,2923,'The mechanism for this is simple, which is like judges have to use their lifetime to know, which means that direct incentives are not given to the benefit of the executive and to keep their jobs. But secondly, there are significant amount of penalties for giving in to bribes and things like that, and that we\'re both accountable to a significant extent for all the reasons we urge to side inside opposition.','other',511.4,530.3),(2519,71,2924,2927,'Thank you to Elfie, and can I please request first opposition speaker to open the case for opposition, Jair Bhim. Can I be heard at this point? In courtrooms across the world, scientific evidence is being ignored by judges who lack the capacity to understand it, and corporate money rewrites the roles that present us. We end this auction of justice by letting those who know the science, not those who know the judge, interpret our laws.','other',531.3,636.3),(2520,71,2928,2928,'We\'re very proud of those.','other',636.6,637.5),(2521,71,2929,2929,'I want to talk about two arguments in my speech.','other',637.8,639.7),(2522,71,2930,2932,'Firstly, on why agencies set better standards. Secondly, on corporate capture. But first, I want to make a few responses and talk about the setup.','other',639.9,646.2),(2523,71,2933,2933,'First, I want to talk about the second argument on how agencies are bad incentives.','other',646.2,650.5),(2524,71,2934,2938,'I\'m going to clash directly with their first argument on why judges have better incentives in my argument, so I\'ll talk about the second argument here. They say that they have bad incentives, they can do things like neglecting heads of agencies. But I have a few responses against this. Firstly, I would say that agencies are held to the standards of administrative law. This means that they are required to file RIAs, which exist in many developed countries in the world, which must demonstrate things like empirical data to factor decisions.','other',650.6,675.5),(2525,71,2939,2939,'You have to consult with impact stakeholders, you have to consider alternatives and justify rejecting them.','other',675.5,680.9),(2526,71,2940,2940,'These are the kind of accountability measures that exist for many agencies in the status quo.','other',681,684.6),(2527,71,2941,2942,'I really don\'t understand how their side will suddenly leave lobbyists with bad incentives by corporations. No, thank you.','other',684.8,693),(2528,71,2943,2943,'I\'m going to weigh this later in my speech as well.','other',693.7,695.7),(2529,71,2944,2944,'Let\'s move on to my setup.','other',696.1,697.1),(2530,73,3024,3024,'Legal interpretation has been the province and duty of the judicial branch for at least two centuries.','other',1239,1245.7),(2531,73,3025,3025,'We are very proud to propose in this motion because we think judges are fundamentally better actors.','other',1245.7,1250.9),(2532,73,3026,3026,'The first thing I\'m going to do is talk about the mitigation that they want to allow in terms of agencies.','other',1250.9,1255.4),(2533,73,3027,3027,'Here\'s why we believe the agencies are fundamentally bad actors and why their responses won\'t work.','other',1255.4,1262.2),(2534,73,3028,3029,'Firstly, in terms of their response in terms of RIA, the problem is that these standards are very hard to apply because of their own mechanism, because of why it\'s hard to take the agencies to court. But secondly, in empirically developed countries, it is just more likely that these agencies are able to get away with these sorts of things like we see in the United States, for example, where RFK Jr.','other',1262.2,1280.4),(2535,73,3029,3031,'But secondly, in empirically developed countries, it is just more likely that these agencies are able to get away with these sorts of things like we see in the United States, for example, where RFK Jr. , the head of the CDC, is claiming actual medical misinformation in terms of, for example, autism is caused by things like vaccines and whatnot. And the reason why agencies are able to get away with these sorts of things is because they operate in a top-down hierarchy where they hire and fire people based on the PR of their subscription and subsidy agencies, and they\'re able to gain support from the federal government.','other',1271,1300),(2536,73,3031,3033,'And the reason why agencies are able to get away with these sorts of things is because they operate in a top-down hierarchy where they hire and fire people based on the PR of their subscription and subsidy agencies, and they\'re able to gain support from the federal government. So even if a lawsuit is brought up against them, they\'re more likely to be able to win against them in the first place. And usually because the state prosecution is equated with the federal government as a whole, the incentive to actually hold these agencies accountable falls because that isn\'t in their incentive to do so in the first place.','other',1289.4,1313.6),(2537,73,3033,3034,'And usually because the state prosecution is equated with the federal government as a whole, the incentive to actually hold these agencies accountable falls because that isn\'t in their incentive to do so in the first place. But secondly, I also want to talk about the ability for you to apply this motion in developing countries, because this is especially true when all analysis becomes amplified in developing countries.','other',1304.6,1323.5),(2538,73,3035,3035,'This is because things like nepotism and corruption are more prevalent in these sorts of countries, while because of the separation of powers courts tend to be more independent, but I\'ll get into that later.','other',1323.5,1331.1),(2539,73,3036,3036,'I\'m going to talk about why politicization is extremely harmful, because primarily you assert arbitrary interpretations, which just harm people in general.','other',1331.1,1338.9),(2540,73,3037,3038,'But secondly, corporate capture by doing things like for example, lobbying these sorts of agencies is inherently already legal. Meanwhile, the revolving door incentive as well as the ability for these agencies to install people within these agencies probably means you\'re going to adhere to these agencies in the first place.','other',1338.9,1354.7),(2541,73,3039,3039,'And especially because developing countries tend to be more dependent on things like FBI.','other',1354.7,1358),(2542,73,3040,3040,'Any sort of counter mechanism in terms of backlash also doesn\'t happen simply because there are low visibility issues.','other',1358,1363.6),(2543,73,3041,3041,'The implication then is that even if these agencies have the expertise like I want to talk about, I fear to see why this actually is an incentive to utilize it objectively and effectively and not in sort of biased and politicized manner.','other',1363.6,1375.4),(2544,73,3042,3042,'Here\'s where we flag our compadre by flagging our positive construction here, in terms of why we\'re able to reform these agencies on our side.','other',1375.4,1382.6),(2545,73,3043,3044,'This is the case because on our compadre we are able to provide an active incentive for these agencies to reform. This is because on our compadre, we push the burnout proof into these agencies, where they themselves have to present a compelling and reasonable case for these courts to actually be willing to buy this interpretation.','other',1382.6,1397.5),(2546,73,3045,3046,'This is important because legal principles like the best possible reading. All this means now is that agencies have become more technocratic on our compadre because they are able to adhere to these standards.','other',1397.5,1406.8),(2547,73,3047,3047,'On opposition, they become more complacent, lazy and politicized like we see in status quo.','other',1406.8,1412),(2548,73,3048,3049,'And we see this empirically when the Australian High Court ruled out Chevron Deference very recently and we\'ve seen that Australian agencies tend to be more technocratic and stable. But importantly, I think inconsistency in terms of agendas and administrations, you have environmental agencies or other agencies constantly changing their interpretations of the law.','other',1412,1431.4),(2549,73,3050,3050,'Here is the impact of this and here\'s why it\'s so harmful.','other',1431.4,1433.8),(2550,73,3051,3051,'This is because in ambiguity, it enables different sorts of interpretations all the time, meaning like very important implications in terms of legal implications are brought down onto vulnerable people.','other',1433.8,1444.2),(2551,73,3052,3054,'For example, when in terms of ambiguous laws, in terms of immigration law, these people are actually legal people because they do not know all their rights and they\'re constantly being relocated or deported without knowing what due process means in the first place because that very due process is ambiguous. They do not know their rights and how to defend themselves. Being that is extremely harmful on their compadre.','other',1444.2,1461.9),(2552,73,3055,3055,'And now we\'re getting to the question in terms of judges.','other',1461.9,1464.5),(2553,73,3056,3056,'But before I do that, are there any POIs? Yeah.','other',1464.5,1466.3),(2554,73,3057,3057,'At least bad decisions by government agents can be corrected because voters can hold them accountable.','other',1466.8,1471.8),(2555,73,3058,3058,'A new government can step in and amend the law.','other',1471.8,1474.4),(2556,73,3059,3060,'The voters are powerless to amend the bad precedent in court. Okay, here\'s why I\'m talking about reversibility because that is a very convenient segue.','poi',1474.4,1480.7),(2557,73,3061,3061,'The problem is think about how high burden your metrics of reversibility are.','other',1480.7,1484.6),(2558,73,3062,3064,'You either have to A, go to the Supreme Court, which requires a lot of time, capital and political capital because you need a class action case. Or secondly, you have to vote in a whole new government. Think about the collective action that requires, the buy-in that requires.','other',1484.6,1497.3),(2559,73,3065,3067,'The problem is administrative agencies are just low visibility. Nobody votes in the government because of who\'s heading the Environmental Protection Service or the CDC, for example. People vote for the president because those are more high-level issues anyway.','other',1497.3,1509.4),(2560,73,3068,3068,'But also for stuff like populism, the incentive for you to care about technocrats when it comes to democratic processes is inherently low.','other',1509.4,1515.6),(2561,73,3069,3069,'But compare this to our comparative when it comes to reversibility.','other',1515.6,1518.4),(2562,73,3070,3071,'That is appeals. Appeals on our comparative become far easier.','other',1518.4,1521.9),(2563,73,3072,3073,'This is the case because now when you launch an appeal against a court that makes bad interpretation, you are only fighting against a singular court compared to the interpretation of an entire executive and administrative agency. This is very important because firstly, you will have less of a deterrent in terms of actually taking these things to court.','other',1521.9,1537.2),(2564,73,3074,3075,'But importantly, you do not have the negative feedback loop that proposition officership experiences. Because think about it, you have a bad interpretation that then has to be taken to a higher court.','other',1537.2,1546.3),(2565,73,3076,3077,'This higher court ends up deferring to that same exact agency because of the fact that that is past precedent and because that\'s how things are traditionally done and because of all the perceived analysis that they themselves present. It means it is very hard for you to actually go against these agencies because the precedence of their comparative is that these agencies are the ones who ought to be interpreting this.','other',1546.3,1563.7),(2566,73,3078,3078,'Now I\'m going to get into my clash in terms of judges.','other',1563.7,1566.2),(2567,73,3079,3079,'Primarily, the judges just aren\'t corrupt and they want you to believe.','other',1566.2,1570),(2568,73,3080,3080,'Corporate capture and the things that they want to talk about on that matter aren\'t realistic.','other',1570,1574),(2569,73,3081,3082,'They use rhetoric about whether you know the judge or not. But that is just blatant bribery and conflict of interest and courts work very hard to actually minimize the extent to which this is happening.','other',1574,1582.6),(2570,73,3082,3085,'But that is just blatant bribery and conflict of interest and courts work very hard to actually minimize the extent to which this is happening. And think about intuitive. Judges are going to have a lot to gain if they were to report things like attempted corruption by corporations because of their reputation and their pathways for success and all that. This just means that judges are more likely to report these things because it\'s good for their own safety as well and corporations are just going to get screwed over in the process.','other',1576.4,1597.7),(2571,73,3086,3086,'So corporations don\'t want to implicate themselves in these sorts of proceedings in any way.','other',1597.7,1602.3),(2572,73,3087,3087,'But there are a bunch of external mechanisms as to why you\'re able to hold judges accountable.','other',1602.3,1605.3),(2573,73,3088,3089,'Primarily because of judge rotations in terms of specializing in terms of these courts because often times the judges are randomized and aren\'t able to do a very last second. But also when you\'re fighting against agencies, you\'re able to for example, cases can\'t be dropped by the person who\'s been accused or the plaintiff.','other',1605.3,1621.1),(2574,73,3089,3091,'But also when you\'re fighting against agencies, you\'re able to for example, cases can\'t be dropped by the person who\'s been accused or the plaintiff. And if there is an actual extent of harm, the plaintiff is able to exceed things like a pro bono case and all of these things. So you\'re able to actually go through with the court even if the corporation doesn\'t like where this judge is coming from.','other',1614,1631.3),(2575,73,3092,3092,'But also you have judicial counsels, watchdogs, the separation of powers and social scrutiny, financial disclosures and fixed tenures which reduce the extent for these judges to actually take things like bribes in the first place.','other',1631.3,1642.6),(2576,73,3092,3092,'But also you have judicial counsels, watchdogs, the separation of powers and social scrutiny, financial disclosures and fixed tenures which reduce the extent for these judges to actually take things like bribes in the first place.','other',1631.3,1642.6),(2577,73,3093,3093,'But furthermore, we are now going to prove why judges have the inherent capacity to deal with these sorts of cases in the first place.','other',1642.6,1649.2),(2578,73,3094,3094,'Firstly, I want to argue that a lot of these instances of ambiguity is in the vast majority of technical specifics.','other',1649.2,1653.3),(2579,73,3095,3096,'In gun law stuff, common use doesn\'t require extensive knowledge about guns but rather how these things work. We think that they have, for example, lawyers who are competent and able to express their argumentation with implication of this analysis and we think that is effective.','other',1653.3,1665.3),(2580,73,3097,3097,'Second, you\'re able to gain things like expertise to more, you\'re able to simplify concepts.','other',1665.3,1669),(2581,73,3098,3099,'In debate, we are talking about these very technical legal concepts but you guys, an average intellectual board are able to understand these things. So we think it is possible for you to actually do these things.','other',1669,1678.8),(2582,73,3100,3102,'And judges are just inherently smart people. They\'re given the responsibility of whether corporations have to pay millions of pints or send someone to death by death penalty. Because that\'s a very high standard and tied to people who are broad and turned into judges.','other',1678.8,1689.7),(2583,73,3103,3104,'We think that these people especially have a greater capacity. And importantly, even if you don\'t understand these concepts, the board is able to understand the implication.','other',1689.7,1696.8),(2584,73,3105,3106,'If you\'re describing one definition over another, it just means that you\'re able to understand what sort of impact that has. And because judges care about broader metrics and not just, for example, the legal feasibility of an interpretation, we think judges are automatically more holistic on our side of the house.','other',1696.8,1711.5),(2585,73,3107,3107,'We think that because of these sorts of things, you\'re able to deal with these sorts of things.','other',1711.5,1716),(2586,73,3108,3108,'We think that for all these reasons, you should try and decide propositions.','other',1716,1718.6),(2587,75,3202,3202,'Here it goes.','other',2315,2315.7),(2588,75,3203,3206,'At the top of my speech, I want to point out that that third argument is absolutely ludicrous. The point of having a separate judiciary is to check the legislature. If the legislature passes legislation that is harmful towards individuals, if they are susceptible to corporate capture, the separation of powers exists so that the judiciary can check the legislature. Then conceding to the dissolution of the separation of powers is just harmful.','other',2315.7,2371.1),(2589,75,3206,3208,'Then conceding to the dissolution of the separation of powers is just harmful. Like, I will quote in the corporate capture cast when I am impacting the court, why not having a separate body to check the legislature and possible harmful interpretations of the legis