n numerous instances where, for example, you have had city development corporations that use vague interpretations of what public use is to perniciously use eminent domain clauses to give land to private corporations like Pfizer. Even though these standards exist, the fact that the heads of administrative agencies are pointed by the executive, that means that the administrative police that exist at the heads of these agencies are backed by the federal government, which means that even in the instance where you are able to take these people to court, there isn\'t much you can do about it. The next thing that I want to do is I want to respond to the thorough mechanisms that comes out in their first speech and is extended from their second as to how you are able to lobby courts on other parity.','other',2466.1,2514.2),(2596,75,3219,3219,'The first broad response I want to make here is that their mechanisms are incredibly resource heavy.','other',2514.2,2519.3),(2597,75,3220,3221,'Profiling judges having to go through years of constantly looking for their exhaustive mechanism are degraded. Years of filing and dropping cases, this is incredibly resource heavy.','other',2519.3,2532.2),(2598,75,3222,3224,'On the comparative, the revolving door on our comparative, or rather on their comparative, means that lobbying administrative agencies is just a lot easier. These governments are openly lobbying, the heads of the executive will appoint individuals that they want in the administrative agencies and therefore the statutory interpretation that they miss will now favour the lobbies that you take in lobbying and over the lobbying governments. So it is a lot easier for you to lobby administrative agencies than it is for you to lobby courts.','other',2532.2,2560.4),(2599,75,3225,3225,'But secondly, they have little to no accountability on their comparative.','other',2560.4,2564.9),(2600,75,3226,3226,'They won\'t use public backlash as a mechanism of accountability.','other',2564.9,2568.2),(2601,75,3227,3227,'We already pointed out in previous years as to why public backlash is just not likely to manifest.','other',2568.2,2573.3),(2602,75,3228,3229,'The first thing I want to also point out, in addition to what we told you in the previous speech, is that a lot of the times, instances of harmful statutory interpretations don\'t have sufficient visibility for you to be able to actually call people out on their comparative. For example, you may have a handful of homeowners that end up being displaced, maybe a part of the immigrants that end up being deported due to harmful interpretations, but that doesn\'t give sufficient visibility for you to get the level of public backlash that they require.','other',2573.3,2599.9),(2603,75,3230,3232,'But secondly, people are aware of the incredibly hyper-specific terms and jargon under which these forms of negative or harmful statutory interpretations take place. And people don\'t know what the maximum zones are, what specific standards and ambiguities actually look like. So you are not able to make positive interpretations on that comparative.','other',2599.9,2621.8),(2604,75,3233,3235,'The next thing that I want to also point out is that when federal governments are called out, they often defer accountability to the agency. So they call out the agency and they replace the head of the agency, but it is still another government body that ends up being the head of that agency. So it isn\'t true that on their comparative, you are directly able to get benefits, or rather directly able to work as an accountant through public backlash.','other',2621.8,2644.9),(2605,75,3236,3237,'The next thing that I want to also point out, or rather respond to, or rather bring up, is as to why our mechanisms of accountability are more likely to work. One, there is a massive level of scrutiny that judges are placed under on some comparative.','other',2644.9,2661.9),(2606,75,3238,3239,'You have to digitally disclose your financial assets when you take up these cases. Appeals courts exist.','other',2661.9,2667),(2607,75,3239,3239,'Appeals courts exist.','other',2665.7,2667),(2608,75,3240,3243,'It is very difficult, and I would also point out that judges are unlikely to want to accept these forms of lobbying. Because they have a lot to lose. They have life criteria. They have no reason to actively engage in taking up these forms of lobbying to begin with.','other',2667,2682.8),(2609,75,3244,3248,'I want to point out, and this weighing is important, even if it is true that there is a reasonable lack of technical expertise on our comparative, we have methods of mitigating against that on our comparative, because of having expert testimonials on our side, because of the fact that we are able to have technocratic advisors, because judges are able to do things like view clinical trial testing and understand data, and understand the impacts of certain interpretations, even if they don\'t understand the related processes of them. What this all means is that on our comparative, judges are better on our comparative. They have the capacity to mitigate against expert expertise. But on their comparative, they have no ability to mitigate against corporate action, because their mechanisms of accountability just don\'t work.','other',2682.8,2730.8),(2610,75,3249,3249,'Before I move on to the second question, I\'ll make that point.','other',2730.8,2733),(2611,75,3250,3250,'At all, don\'t let propositions of American-centered bias fool you.','other',2733,2736.7),(2612,75,3251,3251,'Most countries have reasonable electoral systems that prevent this from happening.','other',2736.7,2740.3),(2613,75,3252,3252,'For example, most countries require government agents to undergo a freeze period for a few years after finishing their term in their respective agency, where they aren\'t allowed to work for corporations related to their administrative role.','other',2740.3,2751.4),(2614,75,3253,3255,'How will corruption st