INSERT INTO `rounds` VALUES (1,'WSDC 2025 R1 - Japan vs Tanzania','2026-02-14 21:55:25',1,'external_video',NULL,'wsdc','THBT Panama should replace the US dollar with its own independent currency.','Currency','iSS4982_Rb8','public','{\"task\": \"transcribe\", \"text\": \"My name is Mau, I\'ll be your chair for this round. I will say the pronouns, and then you\'ll have to introduce yourselves. And then we\'ll start with the speakers. Hello everyone, my name is Peter. Fantastic. So, in preposition, we have in advance, you can hand and tell me your names, your seating order, your pronouns, and also, anyone else, okay, with the recording, the room, and all that. Hi, my name is Ruka, I\'ll be the first speaker for pronouns. Hi, I\'m Matsuki Moki, I\'ll be speaking third, speaker for preposition, and I am the second speaker. Dave? Hi, Seiji speaking second, he has... Right, but who did you not reply to? I\'ll be replying, so Seiji second, and then you\'ll ask. Hi again, Seiji speaking second. Hi, my name is Nora, I\'ll be the one for the last, thank you for that. Alright, thank you for that. Now, the seating order for the preposition, we have, Seiji? My name is Elin, pronouncer, he, him, and I\'ll be second speaker. I\'m saying he, him, on first. Mohammed speaking third, he, him. Alright, and who\'s doing the reply? Me, first speaker. And you\'re okay with the screaming? Yeah. Great. Alright, so I think we\'re all... Paper ballots? Yeah, yeah, we\'re doing that, thank you. So, I think that\'s the basics, you know, like, eight minutes and 15 seconds, and four minutes and 15 seconds to reply. I encourage you to ask and give POIs in your speeches, and of course, enjoy this round. Is there any debate about the iron, and how that can be equally similar to the clock? I don\'t know if you\'re going to super-iron it, or? Okay, I can do it on second. That would be awesome, thank you so much. So, without further ado, we can ask the first speaker of the proposition to explain away, right here. Two pieces on framing. First, what does a status quo look like? Currently, the USD is used as the main medium of transaction with the Valvo uptake to the USD, and there is no central bank that can control the monetary policy. Second, what\'s our policy? First, we will create an independent central bank. Our model is similar to the Chilean model, which has largely been successful. We will search for central bank leadership, both locally and internationally, requiring previous central banking experience. A two-thirds majority of parliament will be required to appoint or remove members. All of this will ensure that the bank is politically independent. Lastly, we will establish internal audit units. They will review decisions, as well as publish monthly units and quarterly reports to annual IMF reviews. This will create transparency, accountability, and credibility. Second, we will keep the Valvo as the official currency and phase out the exit of the dollar over five years. Initially, we will keep it fluctuating only up to 2% of the dollar. Each quarter, we widen this fluctuation band by 1%, giving markets time to adjust. After year five, the value of the Valvo will fluctuate greatly. Third, we phase out the legal tender of the dollar in those five years. Initially, it will continue alongside the Valvo, while we require all government transactions to be made in the Valvo, giving people time to get used to the new currency and learn to trust it. After year five, the dollar will lose its legal tender, but dollar accounts will remain legal for international trade. During those five years, the government will build up its foreign reserves to equal 100% of money in circulation. This allows Panama to sell US dollars and buy Valvo as to stabilize the currency, effectively increasing demand. Moving on to our first argument about economic growth led by increased international revenue. First, on why the USD rate is so high. The dollar is used by many as the world\'s reserve currency. Countries around the world hold literally trillions in US dollar reserves, increasing demand at its value. Second, why a farmstead flow of foreign money into Panama. A strong USD makes Panamanian exports more expensive, hurting its competitiveness against countries with lower currencies, such as Mexico or Vietnam. It also discourages foreign investment and tourism as businesses face higher costs. Third, on why foreign influx of money is crucial for growth. Exports make up half of Panama\'s overall economy. Tourism and services account for 80% of its GDP, increasing those who dramatically increase their income. Crucially, Panama is an ideal place for investors. Its canal access reduces transportation costs drastically, it has a high rate of unemployment population that makes cheap labor potential, and when the US tries to divert its imports from China, it is looking for new locations for manufacturing. The only reason Panama\'s economy has been stagnant so far is the overpriced currency, making them lose to competitors. The implication is that our policy doesn\'t only help self-existing exports, but attract MNCs to create new ones, kicking forward the manufacturing industry that is only 5% of the economy right now. Finally, on the impacts. Firstly, growth translates into increased revenue from tax collection. This allows the public sector to grow and provide people with better welfare, education, and police. It allows the government to pay its crippling debt. It creates a fiscal solution for economic crisis. Secondly, it diversifies the economy. This diversification is crucial. It makes Panama more self-sufficient and less reliant on imports in the long run, thus making it less vulnerable to tariffs and economic pressure from the US. It prevents economic shocks that impact one industry to drag down the entire economy. It creates more diverse job options, with manufacturing jobs being more accessible to the poor and underprivileged. It creates a workers\' cycle. Revenue from manufacturing exports can be reinvested into machinery and R&D that improves manufacturing productivity and creates even more growth. You can\'t improve the productivity of services with technology, so on top there\'s a ceiling to growth. Moving on then to our second argument about crisis. First, we think that the control of monetary policy is crucial for response to economic crisis. During COVID, Panama\'s income debt increased from 40% of GDP to 63%, and its economy shrank 18%. This is nearly double of what other countries with monetary policy experience, all because Panama only had fiscal tools. So why is that bad? Firstly, legislative processes decide fiscal policies too slowly. A bill needs to be written, opened for public comment, debated in parliament, amended and voted on. This process takes months. This means at best, the crisis drags longer. At worst, by the time you pass a bill, it is too late to solve it. On our side, the central bank can respond promptly with monetary stimulus by lowering interest rates. Secondly, fiscal solutions achieve link debt. When your only way to create stimulus is to increase spending in a time when there is less productivity and revenue, your only option is to borrow money. With monetary independence, you can create stimulus without a net crisis. Via interest rate reforms, money creation or quantitative easing. Thirdly, fiscal solutions are too politicized. Because fiscal solutions need to be passed in parliament, political incentives bias them. This looks like adoption of irresponsible and populistic solutions. This means no solutions to inflationary crisis as fiscally-poor in the market can only happen through cutting government spending, which is extremely unpopular. On our side, the politically independent central bank can design a responsible solution that will reduce the public pressure on government. Second, on why the Fed won\'t save Taiwan. Firstly, the Fed will only resolve an economic crisis that makes the US diverse. An economic crisis in Taiwan doesn\'t always mean an economic crisis in the US. Since the US has a much more diverse and wealthy economy, they are less likely to be hit with a concerning crisis. Secondly, even when a crisis does hit both countries, the Fed will intervene in ways that only benefit the US, not Taiwan. Firstly, because American interests favor prioritizing price stability at the expense of growth and unemployment. The American economy is high-tech and refinery-based. It buys raw materials and then refines them into high-quality products. Which means that when prices increase, it doesn\'t only increase American cost of living, but also cost of production, resulting in business collapse and job loss. In contrast, more developing economies are not as vulnerable to price increases. They are much more vulnerable to stagnation, as it makes them struggle to keep their underdeveloped industries afloat. This is why in the current economic crisis, the Fed has been very reluctant to reduce interest rates. While in countries like India, the central bank has already lowered interest rates several times. Panama\'s economy is much more similar to India\'s than the US, yet its response to crisis is tied to the Fed. Secondly, the Fed also tailors its policy to correspond with Congress fiscal policies, not with Panama\'s. When Congress issues large stimulus packages, like during COVID, the Fed is less likely to cut interest rates because recession risks decrease, and excess stimulus could cause inflation. But the Fed\'s US fiscal stimulus packages don\'t go to Panama\'s Indians, so the recession crisis persists. Finally, on how the harms of crisis can get even worse as a result. When crisis strikes, businesses close, people lose jobs, etc. Delayed aid cannot undo permanent damage. You cannot reconstruct a business that shut down in a stimulus package that arrived too late. You cannot reform a poor person who couldn\'t pay their mortgage with an aid package that arrived too late. Crisis is straggling longer. People who lost jobs in initial shocks spend less and therefore money circulation lowers further. Investors pull out when they see no response to the crisis and expect it to last. Even after the crisis, you suffer long-term consequences. Then, you have to pay for years and years with interest. The heightened risk of crisis deters investment and increases interest rate on loans. Even default is not an impossible outcome. It literally happens to everyone who already needs to process a debt crisis resulting from dollarization. Today, we reclaim what 120 years of dollar dependence stole, the economic prosperity of Panama. Thank you. The US is the main medium of oil. Main medium, I agree. They talk about that they have British Central Bank members, two-thirds of the parliament need to approve an internal audit. They claim that this would prevent any discrepancies in the parliament, which I think is fairly untrue because of the fact that corruption is so top-down. Corruption is so top-down. For example, in 2015, their president was actually jailed for embezzlement. Because of the fact that corruption is so top-down, even if you have two-thirds of the parliament, you will be unable to have the level of independence that the US Fed has, which will be explained in the monetary policy. Moving on to what else they say. In their model, they talk about they\'re going to peg to the US dollar over time. First of all, this is directly against the model. You can\'t peg to another currency because it\'s, by definition, a pegged independent. You can do whatever model you want, you cannot peg it because peg is what\'s happening right now. You need to make it an independent currency. Peg, no. Get rid of that. On to our points of framing. We have three points of framing. Firstly, is that the US dollar is a global reserve currency. I think we\'re mostly in agreement on this. I have three main reasons. We have global dependence. Everyone depends on the dollar. We have two real world trends. The dollar is really strong and dominates other currencies. And three, the USD is behind every other currency in the world, whether you like it or not. And four, it\'s historically present and tolerant. The dollar has gone from ups and downs to always coming back. So I think we can agree that the USD is the backbone of global finance. I don\'t think there\'s much to speak here. Second point of framing is what Panama looks like in the status quo. Here, I think we have a lot of disagreements. So Panama looks like four things in the status quo. One is Panama is a high income country, one of two high income countries in the Latin American region. The other one is Mexico, which is also dollarized, significantly up from its neighbors. Two is it is service based, which focuses on offshore banking, tourism, and the Panama Canal. Three is Panama has a special relationship with the US because 40% of the trade passes through the canal. And four is a small country, both geographically and populated. This characterization actually rebuts their argument on diversity and diversifying the economy and experts. And I\'ll show you why. So what do they tell you on their side? They tell you that why strong currency harms Panama. Because Panamanian experts become more expensive and Panama wants to manufacture less. Let me tell you why this doesn\'t work. So first of all, three responses. One, Panama\'s economy is service based, reliant on tourism and the Panama Canal. Devaluing the currency really hurts their main interest. Second is, it\'s a very small economy, both population and geographic wise. Which means the raw material access they have is really small. Even with a weaker currency. Panama would not be able to be a manufacturing based economy. They created it as if Panama was going to grow into this manufacturing powerhouse like China, which is simply untrue. There is no way they\'ll transition to a manufacturing economy while maintaining its high income status. And obviously that\'s a bad thing. And then three, even if our goal as Panama was to have a better manufacturing sector, which it\'s not. Even if it was. We need FDIs in order to diversify those sectors. Which I will show you in my first substantive as to why we use FDIs and investor companies. So that removes the diversity. Moving on. And the third point of hearing is a burden that I would like to push on the side of opposition. Because we know what to do. When you introduce a new currency, you need to show that the public will trust that new currency. Because if you introduce a new currency and it\'s not used by the citizens, that is pointless. You have to prove that the public will trust their new currency instead of just hoarding the US dollar and ignoring the competition. Our opposition has the fiat to introduce a new currency. They do not have the fiat to say everyone is going to be forced to use it. Moving on to argumentation then. So if your characterization is that Panama has a small economy, that only breathes into our analysis of how the US won\'t care about the Panama economy, thereby increasing the necessity of an independent monetary policy. I\'ll address that when I talk about Iranian security. So starting my first substantive. On better economic stability. The thesis of this argument is that Panama\'s use of the US dollar is superior for economic growth and stability. Panama\'s unique success in the region can be attributed directly to the US dollar. Why is the US dollar better for Panama? Three reasons. First is ultra-low inflation and built-in stability. Panama\'s inflation rate is just 0.7%, among the lowest in Latin America. The US dollar is a globally stable currency not subject to rapid devaluation. Crucially, Panama cannot simply print more money, which opposes automatic fiscal discipline. This matters because inflation is a direct harm to the working class, eroding wages, savings, and purchasing power. Stable prices are what protects real income. Do as I do. investment confidence in economic growth. The dollar is entrusted global currencies. That trust extends to PAN, which has attracted SDI with nearly 80% of its GDP. Investors know that the dollar is a safe bet, giving them larger incentive to invest as they don\'t face risks from currency fluctuations, inflation, and economic risks. So they say that FDIs are less likely, and they\'re arguing too, because of high prices. I think it\'s a lot more likely that capital flight occurs when I see a transition, because it\'s a very volatile moment when they\'re transitioning, and when they transition, I see it\'s a new currency, not trusted by anyone. I, as an investor, will now want to pull out, instead of an economy which uses the US dollar, which is globally trusted, which I think they pretty much would agree with. And three, easier and cheaper trade. How about imports? Most of this, because I told you, it\'s not a manufacturing company. From food to electronics, because it uses the US dollar, it can pay for imports directly. A local currency would require, a local currency would require, first, exchange local money with the US. If demand for a new currency is low, the exchange rate would be unfavorable, making imports more expensive. These extra costs are passed on to consumers as higher prices. Using the US dollar removes this inefficiency, keeping prices stable and essential goods affordable, which is very good for the working class of the US. Point. Why a new currency would be dangerous. Three reasons. One is investors like expensive debt. A national currency with no tracker could only inspire global trust. Investors would pull out or demand massive risk premiums. This will be important when I talk about emergency relief. Two is inflation risk reform of the swan commerce. Countries like Venezuela and Argentina fail to maintain and manage their own currencies, resulting in hyperinflation and eventual dollarization. Panama wants to monitor infrastructure to avoid a similar outcome. A new currency, even if they try and maintain stability, will never match the dollar\'s stability. Three is cost of trade and inflation spike. Panama was still in the US dollar\'s string of four. Without sufficient reserves, it would resort to printing money, causing devaluation. This pushes import costs higher, which drives inflation, weakens the currency even more, and this is a continuous spiral. And once that spiral begins, it\'s nearly impossible to stop. In fact, these are pretty intuitive. With a new currency, we get hyperinflation and lose the stability that we have in the status quo. We lose investor confidence and thus FDIs as a bonus, and we spoil the working class by decreasing their purchasing power with a new currency. Secondly, on the idea of crisis relief. Let me just, there are two types of crisis relief. There are one type which affects both Panama and the US, in which case we face the same problem. They claim that, no, it\'s different, but really, because we have the same currency and a very sling to economy, because 40% of the trade goes to Panama now. We have the same response. Either we need a stronger currency, in which case we raise our lower interest rates, or we need a weaker currency, in which case we raise our lower interest rates, depending on what we need. So that\'s out of the way. Secondly, the thing about it is that the price is unique to Panama. So there are a couple of reasons why we need short-term relief over them. When we need to have more investment coming, so we get cheap loans. In their case, we don\'t get cheap loans. We are more likely to get them. And two, we can\'t panic-fringe money, which leads to hyperinflation. In their case, we can just panic-fringe money, which they\'re so keen on, which completely screwed over Zimbabwe\'s economy. And you can see that. In our case, we prevent an economic crisis, and we have done so much better, directly in the product of the loans. I will turn now to the question of the US. This is a question of the US, because it is a proposition that we will literally end the period. It is shockable by its use of the US dollar to prevent it from diversifying, enriching a small elite while ensuring that the country remains one of the most unequal. Its link to the US dollar means that it lacks monetary independence to protect citizens from recurring crises, and leaves it at risk of a crippling financial crisis. Two simple questions. First, I\'m gonna deal with growth, and second, on stability, and possibly on extending third-party later in my speech. Firstly, on growth. The main mechanism I would like to, the main line of mechanism we get from their judge is about increased income costs. We would argue two things in response. One, we\'re going to argue that we would prioritize exports over imports, and second, I would argue that the increase in import costs will be marginal at best. Firstly, why wouldn\'t we argue the prioritization of exports is important? A few mechanisms. One, the increase in revenue is higher than the increase in cost because of the way manufacturing works. You buy cheaper, raw material, refine them, and then sell expensive, refined products. The final product to export is just much more expensive than the materials they import for, so the currency rate markup, which is the same for both, pays a more negative cost. Second, exports increase income, and imports increase the cost of living. Those cancel out. What doesn\'t cancel out is growth, which is the only lever that can make wages out in prices by increasing productivity in the economy. This is only enabled through exports. Third, on job creation, which is enabled only through exports, which is logically higher to the affordability of any imported goods. If goods are cheap but people are unemployed, they still can\'t afford them. Fourth, because expensive imports are outwards to short-term development. Because when market growth and diversification manifest, we will have more local production self-sufficiency. Import reliance will then solve itself over time, stagnation in knowledge. Fifth, Panama is surrounded by a lot of cheap exporters, as these make the majority of Latin American countries. It\'s not short for cheap import options. It is much more threatened by export competition. Furthermore, I would argue that there is a bunch of mitigatory mechanisms against expensive imports. First and most importantly, the vast majority of Panama\'s imports are just not necessities. The country is largely self-reliant on food, producing enough heat, fruits, and other stuff. Basic goods are only around 1.8% of all of Panama\'s imports. Import prices then don\'t impact cost of living as much as they impact the cost of luxuries for the well-fed elites. Second, many of Panama\'s products, many products that Panama imports, could be produced domestically. Guatemala, for instance, has seen its electronic manufacturing sector growing dramatically. The core difference between Panama and Guatemala is not population size or skill, but that the overvalued dollars means that it is far too cheap to import these goods, and there is no incentive to draw up these sectors and do it internally, nor for foreign-income investors to use Panama as a base of goods to export from. Third, companies use a different pricing system, accounting for different purchasing power in different countries. This means that prices of imports in the U.S. can become unaffordable with the federal law. By laws of supply and demand, companies will adjust prices down accordingly. This is why, for example, a Big Mac costs less in India than it does in the U.S. This largely deals with the third mechanism in the first argument about import-import costs. Crucially, I would also argue their two responses against our first argument in proving growth rates are sufficient. Their first response argues that due to the service-based