canals and shipping main. This keeps transport costs low. There\'s a shoot-and-shoot advantage. The US is trying to cut or reduce Chinese manufacturing, and Panama is among the best-placed countries to benefit. It is not far from the US as Vietnam. It has untapped labor markets, et cetera. But all of this, and the weaker currency, means exporting is easier as well. All of this means it\'s really possible for diversification to occur. All of this creates the first clash on growth. Second clash, on price and system. Their first argument argues, and there are two mechanisms behind this, argues low inflation and stability occurs as a result of the USD, and also investor confidence occurs. In response to this, I\'m going to prove largely a few things. Firstly, I\'m just going to prove that the central bank will be done well. Crucially, this will then mitigate against the vast majority of their analysis that argues, for example, elements like corruption or mismanagement of finances. Firstly, a lot of our model already mitigates the risk of corruptibility, as it prevents the government from doing things like impacting more broad board members without a broad consensus, et cetera. Second, other institutions can hold the central bank accountable. For example, the IMF will hold the central bank accountable, or also the strong financial sector in account, including corporations like, by banks like JPMorgan, also have incentive to pressure the government to avoid meddling in the central bank. Thirdly, corrupt governments are stupid, and no meddling with the voters\' bank, and no meddling with the central bank risks rapid inflation and demotion for their credit score, which voters will punish them for. Crucially, Hamill, these voters know of these crises, look, they know Argentina, for example, and therefore have high incentive to hold the government accountable in any case. Jack, go ahead. What incentive does JPMorgan Private Equity Firm have with Panama Central Bank? They literally operate in the country. If the currency in that country is screwed, then obviously they\'re also screwed. The second thing I would like to do in response to the first argument, though, is also just prove the stability of the currency. Hamill, there are four reasons as to why the currency will be stable. One, for the vast majority of their firms to materialize, they don\'t just need to prove devaluation, which we concede and welcome, but uncontrollable, rapid devaluation. This is unlikely for all of the obvious reasons, like central bank intervention happening. Secondly, we obviously preempted a lot of this from the brink. For example, our foreign reserves enabled fast response, and despite what they\'re claiming, it\'s within our fiat to argue that we will slowly reduce the peg. Thirdly, Panama has a good track record of financial responsibility. Like after COVID, it maintained fairly low wealth, fairly low debt, established a sovereign wealth, and et cetera. And fourthly, in the immediate term, like 6% of the global trade goes to the Panama Canal. It\'s a lot of transactions in the above wall, contributing both its stability and investor promise. Furthermore, Panama\'s importance also means that international actors have incentives to help ensure a smooth transition and avoid monetary crises, which means, for example, institutions like the IMF or nations have also incentives to help out here. All of this in response to their lack of orientation on investor confidence and stability. Finally, I also just want to quickly deal with their absurd assertion that the US, that economic crises can be solved in their world. Clearly, even if the US and Panama are both impacted by a crisis, we prove from our second argument that the method by which they would like to deal with the crisis is different, which is why their second response is going to work better. Oh, I\'m gonna do it, you can\'t be quicker. Starting in three, two, one. Panama. If you\'ve been in Panama for a