ng part in everyday central bank decisions, which is just unlikely and doesn\'t happen with most countries. And they also talk, it\'s also hypocritical considering they want a fast system, yet they want parliament to sit down every time the bank, the central bank has to make a decision regarding monetary policy and fiscal policy. It\'s not possible to have a fast system, yet always have to convince two-thirds of parliament to agree upon a monetary or fiscal decision. They also talk about this idea of a one to 2% float, which is essentially a very fake way to say they\'re going to peg to the US dollar, which again doesn\'t fulfill the requirements of the motion. The motion asks them to have independent currency, even if they would, to remove this peg after five years, we think would fall apart because again, Panama doesn\'t have the infrastructure to support it. And Panama as of today is facing very unfortunate circumstances of a largely corrupt government. Moving on to prop one, they talk about stronger USD earning exports. The thing is, Panama isn\'t export-based. We\'ve told you that, they\'ve told you that. Panama is mostly service-based and mostly transport-based. They have the Panama Canal. And that\'s the largest single individual contributor to their economy. So their idea to pivot to an export-based economy, I\'ll get to on later, but as of now, it\'s not true that Panama\'s export-based. Also, secondly, they talk about investors in the Panama Canal, and they want to use the Panama Canal as leverage, as they say, because it\'s a very strategic asset. We actually agree with that. We think that earning the Panama Canal is a very strategic asset, and that we will continue to be a strategic asset, which is why we don\'t think we should risk that strategic asset by switching to a flimsy currency and trying to make it more profitable. risk our strategic assets in the Panama Canal. We don\'t think that\'s a good move, because people actually, the US, 40% of US trade actually passes through the Panama Canal. And we don\'t think that there\'s going to be very positive US backlash once we switch currencies. We\'ve seen in the past, we\'ve seen in the past few weeks with the US invading Iran for small incentives. We think that switching currencies is a direct indicator to the US, say, hey, come take the canal that you\'ve literally built for us, because now we have incentive to use that as leverage against you. So we don\'t think the US is going to respond to that very positively. They also say that they\'re going to create more jobs because of a manufacturing and export-based economy. This, again, is an inherent contradiction, because they want to reduce imports, and they say that they want less imports. And they also agree to the point my first speaker made, when he said that Panama currently doesn\'t have the natural resources to support an export-based economy, so they will actually have to import the raw materials, which would, again, increase the import-based economy. So that\'s an inherent contradiction on their side, and should be taken into consideration with this motion. Firstly, next, they also talk about how control and monitoring policy is important for crisis management. We tell you this is a blatant lie, because for 100 years, Panama has been able to manage crisis with its fiscal policy. In the 2008 global recession, Panama did significantly better than any other Latin American country. In fact, the next year, it came in with a 7% GDP growth, something no other Latin American country had seen, and we attribute that to the US dollar. We don\'t think that that would have been possible with this new influence of currencies that they instead proposed. Moving on, they also talk about fiscal solutions accumulating debt. This, again, is not true of Yankee, and this fiscal policy is just a lever for the government to control spending and taxes. So the government\'s been doing this for a long time, for 100 years, since 1904