cy would be actively dangerous. Two points on this first. Exclusive harms. Corruption in monetary policy is hidden and devastating. So unlike fiscal policy, actually, where you can track budgets, investigate spending, and hold politicians accountable, that\'s not possible with monetary policy. A government can\'t track which politicians are putting pressure on which banker to actually print more money and cause inflation to fill their own pockets. This is possible with fiscal policy. Panama\'s been doing it for 100 years. Every other example of a Latin American country that\'s trying to implement this has failed. We\'ve given you examples like El Salvador. We\'ve told you about Argentina. We told you about Mexico. All these countries that are actually trying to impose things like this have failed because they don\'t have the infrastructure to support it. Moving on, we also talk about no infrastructure or expertise. Panama has never had to manage a full monetary system. It lacks a credible independent central bank. It lacks deep reserves to stabilize its currency. And it also has global trust in its economy of print. Building all this from scratch would take decades if it ever succeeds at all. Yet, why is this about monetary policy and non-fiscal policy? Some might say that Panama handles fiscal policy like taxes and spending and it hasn\'t collapsed, but that\'s exactly the point. Fiscal policy is more accountable, as I mentioned before. Moving on to the impact, Dean, what\'s at stake? Panama has remained stable while its neighbors like Nicaragua and Venezuela suffered economic collapse. That\'s not an accident. It\'s because Panama took one of the most dangerous levers of power, monetary control, out of local hands and gave it to a system that works creating national currency that reverses that. So, moving on to substantive three, which is stability and adaptation meets resilience in crisis. Now, we\'ve heard a lot about stability and resilience in crisis from side