old these governments accountable with the foreign side of the house. But even if you don\'t take that, and even if you buy into the thought that Panama is corrupt, that isn\'t becoming the reason why central bank will be corrupt. Because this assumes the most basic version of corruption and does not realize country can be corrupt in some ways, but not in others. The US is in fact corrupt, if you think about it, in the level of insider trade within the Congress. But that does not follow that Fed is corrupt, which is the reason why our current policy specifically ensures the incorruptibility of central bank, which is the reason why you just cannot throw away a lot of reasons as to why corruption is taking this debate already. The final thing I wanna do to deal with it under this implementation claim is your idea that you have to get two-thirds parliament approval every time. No, that wasn\'t our policy. Our policy was to only appoint and remove members and get consensus within it. But literally, it\'s not about every single policy that\'s just part of the debate. First things first, that\'s charitable economic growth. I wanna note strategically, this was a very charitable argument to begin with. Because we say devaluation in a currency is a consensus in a debate. It is their reason as to why we have more imports that are expensive. It is our reason as to why we have more cheaper exports. And here\'s where Seiji\'s analysis would be particularly important. Because what we told you from Seiji\'s speech was that in instances where Panama is currently relying on the service sector, it is critical for Panama to diversify its economy, it is critical for Panama to increase its revenue through the exports. And here is how the lack of responses to that and the immediate extreme, like the direct weighing mechanism as to why you want to bifurcate imports over the other. The final thing I wanna point out is that they just mischaracterize what the status quo of Panama looks like. Because what we have pointed out is that even though Panama Canal is giving you sufficient amount of revenue in status quo, you cannot rely on them in the future. Which is the reason why you must diversify economy. Which is the reason why you must access cheaper exports in order to just grow its jobs, in order to grow the manufacturing sector that is currently unreliable. Then they say diversification is impossible. Here is the process. One, on an employment level, you do not need natural resources or a giant population to create the manufacturing sector. Look at Taiwan, look at Singapore, both have few resources and a small population to get a strong manufacturing sector. Secondly, Panama is strategically located. If you set up a company in Panama, you are both the channels in the shipping lane. That keeps transport costs low and has a huge advantage in the use of our set of channels. Third of all, the US has spiked the cut where we use Chinese manufacturing in supply chains. Panama is also the best place for countries to benefit, which is the reason why it is likely to have the capacity to diversify our set in-house. Before I move on to experience the problems of the individual land. Which of these people do I prefer, unfair or comparative, in a political economy? Okay, are we giving you magnificent reasons? And furthermore, I don\'t think you can say, okay, who\'s out of the league? Now let\'s just experience the people that you think they think of their world. The first thing they may disavow is torture capital flow out of Panama. I think the first thing I want to point out is that all of our reasons and stability allow you to be anarchist. But secondly, the incentives we make make it to maintain your operations in Panama still exist with its panels and developed financial sector. It\'s the reason why it\'s all through the US capital is like spiked, vertical, but made. Even if it\'s true, only torture farm do you have comfortable in this debate, which is the reason why we are more than happy to just prioritize longer farm longer benefit. Last, last step in terms of stability. The first thing I want to explain is to explain why response to crisis is more important than the number of crisis that exist and they say is more powerful. Four main mechanisms. Firstly, some economic crisis are inevitable even in the strongest parties. This means a hard to inevitable response that is certain to manifest in some instances. In contrast, currency inflated out of control is speculative. It is a force of nature that has to happen in our sector and house. And our expectation being observed that their thing was about inflation happening, but they automatically assume that there\'s going to be a hyperinflation of our world, but all of their arguments only stand in terms of the most extreme spectrum, meaning that their argument is speculative. Secondly, our analysis of the response to crisis is logically prior to the markets of currency volatility. If we are able to properly and effectively respond in time, even inflation, we can just nip it in the bud. Thirdly, volatility risks are imperative. Opposition needs to recognize the risk that they allow on their world. The risk of over-reliance on the service industry, the risk of the Federal Reserve\'s policy causing a crisis in Panama, the risk of defaulting on debt, and the risk of fiscal policy fueled inflation. If anything, we think the risk is higher in their world as it is asymmetric on both sides of the house. It\'s our ability to respond to the crisis far better and far sooner in the house, which is the reason why the monetary policy, which we can flexibly use with Panama\'s situation, was critical to win a civil league class as well. We win with the metric as well. We hear a lot about diversity, but we don\'t hear what it actually looks like. Tourism, 8%, Panama Canal, 6%, banking, 12%, and exports, 11%. The rest is miscellaneous. What I\'m trying to say is, they say diversity has a pretty big push, but we don\'t actually hear why it\'s not already diverse enough. It\'s not like Saudi where it\'s only oil. We have other things. It\'s just mainly service-based. We\'re playing to our strengths, never being eligible. Two key things here. Firstly, on the framework, and second, on two key clashes. Firstly, on economic growth, and second, on stability and economic crisis. So, let\'s start with their framework. Why is their model so extremely problematic? Five reasons. One, they tell you about a fluctuating bank. Does anybody in this room know how problematic a fluctuating bank is? You know how much capital flight is there when you have a fluctuating bank. It\'s like saying, this economy is not working, so we\'re going to do this over five years, and then we\'ll get to a stage where we\'re good. In those five years, every investor is running because they know the current system is not working. Second, we tell you very clearly... that Panama lacks the expertise to be able to do this. They have no track record compared to the US, which is the best monetary policy maker in the world. And three, we tell you corruption is top down. We tell you self-interest is their main priority, and they bring up checks and balances, but we tell you these external checks and balances are not good enough because it\'s top down. The way they counter this is, four, they tell you corporations and all these people, you know what, the problem is that these corporations also prioritize self-interest. And they tell you, hey, even though they\'re prioritizing self-interest, they care about the economy because that also benefits their self-interest. Let me tell you something. The thing is, an economy could be falling. Everybody could be falling. But some people can benefit from it. We\'ve seen it everywhere. And these corporates and these corrupt people, top down, will benefit. Whether or not the poor people suffer or not, they don\'t care about this. What they care about is their self-interest. Are you gonna bring up corporates and JP Morgan and show why they care about the average person? When we very clearly tell you they can benefit even though the economy\'s failing because when they bring money, they can use it to their advantage. They can be very strategic in how they invest. They can use currency rates and be able to use that to solve the penny in currency and benefit from it. This is not a good check and balance to have. Moving on to the key questions, but before that, I\'ll take a point. How will a JP Morgan operating through a paramount economy be able to benefit from hyperinflation, which causes a crisis that means that your life saving doesn\'t mean anything at all? I very clearly told you that what they can do is they can take advantage of the interest rates. Knowing that the economy\'s going to collapse, they can very clearly sell all of their penny in currency and use that to their advantage because they\'re saying they will have trust. It means the penny in currency will be at a certain point and they can start selling all of it and benefit from that. I think that\'s pretty intuitive. Two questions then. First, you have economic growth. Let me flesh that out. What do we hear from them? They tell you that economic growth is better on their side because high rates of BSE hurts the sectors. A few responses to that. One, we tell you about the nature of Panama and its economy. We tell you it\'s service based and we see no issue with it. We tell you that it\'s diverse enough and we also tell you that we\'re playing to our strengths and we see nothing wrong with it. But two, we tell you that devaluing actually hurts these main sectors. So what they\'re trying to say is, hey, this is what we\'re nationally very good at, but let\'s hurt that and let\'s improve these weaker things that never have the potential to grow. Four, we tell you that this is not good enough in the first place. But three, we tell you even if you assume that the manufacturing can grow, the investor confidence we have is significantly more important than anything they can break off, which they lack with a new currency. But also four, we tell you the U.S.A. gives you such good leverage. When you have that high rate, when you deal with other countries, it actually helps you because you\'re the one growing. They present this problem of the U.S.A. growing as problematic to Panama. But guess who has the currency that is growing? Panama, because that\'s the currency we deal in. So all of our reserves are in the U.S.A. and you trade with other countries where our rate is higher. It means we\'re benefiting day by day. Take it from a Tanzanian, my currency from the day I was born was a thousand shillings to one dollar. Now it\'s 2,800. You could have a dollar and you could have doubled in Tanzania. Panama is better to take and that is clearly very true. But two, we tell you about the idea of exports and imports. Again, I repeat to you, we would rather play to our strengths and tell you that our strengths are diverse enough in nature. We tell you we have tourism, we tell you we have panhandling, we tell you we have banking. Just because we\'re service concentrated doesn\'t mean that we\'re not diverse. But two, we tell you that it\'s very unlikely for Panama to have a strong manufacturing base. This is for three reasons. One, it\'s a low GDP. Yes, I\'ve heard of a POI. It\'s an unfair comparison to the United States of America which has such a strong GDP. We tell you it\'s not an example game, but this is the structural reason we\'re giving you. Their GDP is not strong enough because of the population size. But two, we tell you we don\'t even have the natural resources. You literally lack the ability. In terms of growth, number three, we tell you that there\'s no wage stagnation. They tell you, hey, with the U.S. aid, it\'s only the job benefiting. We tell you that the workers also get paid in U.S. aid, meaning that there\'s no wage stagnation. What they can buy and what they get is pretty symmetric. What do we tell you then on economic growth? We face no reputation to this. We tell you that the U.S. aid actually helps them in terms of investor confidence, but it also gives them a high range of leverage and it allows easier and cheaper trade. To this, we receive absolutely nothing. We also bring up that a new currency is particularly bad for three reasons. We tell you that it doesn\'t have stability because of how small it is. Check South American currencies for reference. Two, we tell you that there\'s no public trust or investor trust. And three, we tell you we have costlier trades with the higher exchange rates. On win then, even if you\'re diversifying money hurting the economy, who\'s winning? But two, we tell you it\'s totally fine to not be diversifying. Even take extreme examples of Saudi who are so reliant on oil. They\'re doing totally fine. And if we have an economic life plan, we\'re totally fine with that and we don\'t see the problem with it. In the worst case. Second flashpoint, which is a bit smaller, but on monetary policy. Their theme is on crisis and they tell you U.S. and Panama are different interests. Four key responses. One, we tell you the monetary policy set by U.S. is going to affect both U.S. and Panama symmetrically. It means that they\'re not acting out for the best interest of Panama, but in acting in their best interest, it\'s helping Panama. But two, if crisis comes in two ways. One, it\'s a global crisis and it affects both of them. Then their reaction, which is lowering money, is the same. It\'s not like fiscal policy where you have to be significantly flexible. If you\'re in a crisis, you can just lower interest rates and it\'s going to work symmetrically. But three, that all tells you that you don\'t have to care about Panama for us to be doing well. But four, we tell you even when it\'s different, fiscal policy is better. Lastly then, on fiscal policy and why 