her or, you can choose. And we gave you two structural reasons as to why monetary policy, giving it to the enemies, is significantly worse than having them have fiscal policy, which have not really been done. We talk about monetary policy is so much harder to see and be accountable for. Because of the fact that you can audit a budget and you can\'t audit an interest rate. Because you can track the budget and see, oh, this money did not go where it\'s supposed to. I can clearly see, oh, there\'s corruption here. How am I able to see, oh, this interest rate isn\'t what it\'s supposed to be? Oh, this value isn\'t what it\'s supposed to be? It\'s so much difficult, which is why it incentivizes corruption, because it\'s so easy to hack. And secondly, even if that\'s not true, scale of impact is significantly worse if you mess up monetary policy, which I think they can see too. If you mess up monetary policy, it\'s a big deal. If you mess up fiscal policy, okay, $1,000 can\'t do it. You mess up monetary policy, you\'ve messed up the economy for like, structurally, for the next four years. Next 100 years, actually. The second thing on growth and stability. Okay, we told you two things in our first argument on growth and stability. We talked about the low inflation rate, which I think they agreed with, 0.7, which is ridiculously low in all of Latin America. And secondly, we talked about the issue of investor confidence in FDA. We very clearly told you how we have capital flight in our world, and I think they can see it. In the short term, capital flight will occur. And the problem is, you need that capital to diversify the economy as they really want. And that\'s something I\'ve not really dealt with. So in order to diversify, I don\'t know where that their money is coming from. It\'s just gonna, maybe they\'ll print it. And if they do print it, it\'s gonna lead to higher inflation again. So double bind here. Let me just, and again, let me just deal with this expert-based thing. First of all, we\'re not expert-based. We don\'t need to be expert-based. They said we don\'t need raw materials to be expert-based. We talked, we gave you the natural resources thing. If we truly don\'t need raw materials, then we\'re gonna import all those things. Then again, our import costs will be really high because we don\'t have the U.S. to trade it. So that\'s again, a contradiction on their side. So we don\'t want devaluation because of strong currency definitely. I don\'t know what we don\'t have growth. So even if I take them at their best, where I say, okay, we have big stagnation, the growth doesn\'t happen. Because of the fact that inflation is so low, at worst, because inflation and wage stagnation are equal, purchasing power is important for income class cases. And that is our worst case scenario. On their case, hyperinflation is a big risk on their side, which is definitely better than the class crisis. Now we\'re gonna go to a major thing that we talked about, which is crisis. So first of all, we talked about the crisis that affects wealth. I think this is pretty clear, because you just raise or lower interest rates. And because we have the same currency and our economies are there, it\'s pretty clear. The more important thing is that you need to plan. So three things here. First of all, we need investment confidence to get cheap loans. Without investment confidence, and I\'ve already shown that you can lose investment confidence, we get expensive loans and we get debt tax. Either we don\'t get the loans because they refuse to give it to us, or we get really expensive loans and suffer debt crisis. Like they claim we have 100,000 and they actually have 12,000. Second thing is you can prevent people from living with hyperinflation. Like Zimbabwe did. When they face a crisis, they panic rents, and they face hyperinflation and just mess up the economy completely. And then three, fiscal policy is significantly faster. It\'s pretty clear in this t