INSERT INTO `rounds` VALUES (2,'WSDC 2025 R2 - Sri Lanka vs Japan','2026-02-14 21:57:53',1,'external_video',NULL,'wsdc','THBT courts should not defer to administrative agencies in statutory interpretation.','Criminal Justice','6o5gDd9T3CY','public','{\"task\": \"transcribe\", \"text\": \"this body of legal rights. Yes, you go ahead. The system is the last line of defense to protect the most vulnerable. And this can only happen if you give judges the ability to do their jobs without being significantly constrained by administrative agencies. There are two parts of setup there. The first is in terms of who these administrative agencies are. There are bureaucratic bodies such as the Environmental Protection Agency which are in charge of implementing laws by the legislature. But secondly, let\'s talk about where strategic interpretation takes place. The first is where statutory language is extremely vague because of the use of terminologies such as reasonable or appropriate. And for example, the vast majority of environmental regulations and statutes prohibit actions that lead to substantial impact. But it is extremely unclear as to what substantial impact amounts to in the first place. The second instance then is when technical terminology is significantly unclear. The opposition depends on where web codes are bound by the interpretation of these agencies. Our goal is one where codes independently interpret the statute to determine the most reasonable meaning. We can also apply the intensity for statute by consult agencies and administrations on our side of the house to get technical advice. And judges can also bring in expert testimonies and independent technical advices in instances where they are extremely unsure how to do it. In essence, judges can use administrative agencies and technical advices as tools in a toolbox to make the best possible interpretations possible on our side of the house. There are two constructive arguments coming out of our opposition. The first is in terms of why judges make better interpretations. But secondly, in terms of why administrative interpretations will lead to harmful outcomes on their comparative. The first argument then, in terms of why judges make better interpretations. This argument has three parts. The first is in terms of why judges are able to navigate technical interpretations. The mechanism for this is twofold. The first is that codes are specialized into many fields, such as labor law and environmental law and the people who work in these codes go on, people who work in these codes as lawyers go on to becoming judges, which means judges have years and years of experience within these specialized fields while also being able to perform fast court cases and their proceedings. The second mechanism of that is that codes can bring in expert testimonies that act as an independent translation tool to make sense of the science behind things like drug recommendation for instance. Because, first of all, it is quite intuitive that it doesn\'t take a scientist to understand that getting from a clinical trial that proves a certain drug is harmful, a certain drug is addictive, is harmful and should not be put out into the market. Judges can make these valid judgments on their own, on our side of the house. The second part of this argument that is in terms of expanding what even if judges don\'t have the same level of technical expertise as technocrats, while they are still able to navigate certain interpretation processes and understand the overall implications of their interpretations. The mechanism for this is quite simple because the index of different interpretations are simple to understand because you can see them manifest in the real world from the ground up. This can be easily illustrated by the case of Massachusetts versus GBA in 2007 where there was a question of whether CO2 should be listed under the harmful chemical under the CAA Act. In this case, the GBA rejected the interpretation that CO2 fell under the Act because they were lobbied by all companies such as Shell and Chevron. In the court proceedings, which included extreme levels of peer-reviewed studies, proved that the success of regulating CO2 and its importance. In the end, the court ruled that the EPA was able to interpret a CO2 big fall under this Act. This proves to you why agencies are extremely self-interested and by God, they make good decisions. Now, opposition might think that this proves that reasonability is enough to prevent harmful interpretations from taking place, but they cannot claim this benefit because this case was taken to the Supreme Court and this can only happen because deference is not relevant to the Supreme Court. Now, moving on to the third part of this argument, which is that the judiciary\'s job is to find the best possible meaning of certain statutes and laws, but agencies constrain them from doing this and restrict them to what is permissible under the statute. Before I move into that third mechanism, I will take that point. So how would a judge who doesn\'t really have any degrees in a specific field, are any who are able to just evaluate conflicting research like epidemiology, for example? I think our bar is significant on the extent to which we just now, a couple of minutes ago, which is that even on our worst possible case, where you cannot understand significant unscientific research, you understand the overall implications of those in the real world, we don\'t need to prove to you what you want to say, and we still have a monarchy and stuff like that. The third part of this argument is the argument in terms of why deference prevents the best possible interpretation from taking place. Opposition\'s world is one where the most permissible interpretation takes place, but panel, permissibility doesn\'t equate to harm reduction on that comparative. Like, remember the example of the EPA, where the permissible interpretation was to like equal amount of harmful pollutants, but to exclude CO2. See, this is harmful because CO2 will continue to be unregulated, harming millions of people on their side of the house, and pushing climate change to a point of no return on their side of the house. In that, on our side of the house, judges will not differ on our comparative, and they will understand that CO2 is bad and should be regulated, that is why we make the best possible interpretations on our comparative. The second argument then is in terms of why differing to administrative agencies will always lead to bad outcomes on their comparative. Let\'s first talk about instances where judges will disagree with agencies. The most likely manifestation on opposition is where permissible interpretations take place, but I\'ve already significantly established to you that the point is that even if an interpretation is permissible, it is still significantly harmful. For example, in the UK, the government can seize land for private companies under the guise that it is for public use. The New London Development Corporation will find public use to mean economic development and they transfer that land to Pfizer. This hits millions of working class families and hundreds of thousands of them on their comparative. In our world, judges simply will not be satisfied that this is permissible and will push for the best possible interpretation, which is likely to create far more people on our comparative. The first part of this argument then is in terms of why agencies have incentives to give in to corporations. Three mechanisms. The first is that different presidents appoint their own heads of agencies based on people who follow their agenda, which this is exactly why the EPA cherishes the interpretation of what the law means with every change in administration. But secondly, Democrats heed to the death and call of politicians because of the revolving door theory, because they want to keep the private sector in that loop, also gain jobs in the corporate sector and push for jobs like consultancies with higher levels of pay on their comparative, so that direct incentives do not pass by the nation and not hit their public markets. But thirdly, let\'s talk about that, but the second part of this argument then is in terms of why does a lack of accountability prevent this. So even if opposition says there is separation of power act, we want other to find out that this is not a good enough accountability mechanism on their comparative in the context of party politics, where one party controls both the presidency and the legislature. For example, in like the US right now, the Republican party controls all three parties in the US, which means you have no accountability mechanisms on your comparative. The third part of this argument then is in terms of why deference prevents judges from making fun of deference prevents judges from doing their jobs, which is judging. And that is an absolutely violent form of better on our side of the house. The mechanism for this is simple, which is like judges have to use their lifetime to know, which means that direct incentives are not given to the benefit of the executive and to keep their jobs. But secondly, there are significant amount of penalties for giving in to bribes and things like that, and that we\'re both accountable to a significant extent for all the reasons we urge to side inside opposition. Thank you to Elfie, and can I please request first opposition speaker to open the case for opposition, Jair Bhim. Can I be heard at this point? In courtrooms across the world, scientific evidence is being ignored by judges who lack the capacity to understand it, and corporate money rewrites the roles that present us. We end this auction of justice by letting those who know the science, not those who know the judge, interpret our laws. We\'re very proud of those. I want to talk about two arguments in my speech. Firstly, on why agencies set better standards. Secondly, on corporate capture. But first, I want to make a few responses and talk about the setup. First, I want to talk about the second argument on how agencies are bad incentives. I\'m going to clash directly with their first argument on why judges have better incentives in my argument, so I\'ll talk about the second argument here. They say that they have bad incentives, they can do things like neglecting heads of agencies. But I have a few responses against this. Firstly, I would say that agencies are held to the standards of administrative law. This means that they are required to file RIAs, which exist in many developed countries in the world, which must demonstrate things like empirical data to factor decisions. You have to consult with impact stakeholders, you have to consider alternatives and justify rejecting them. These are the kind of accountability measures that exist for many agencies in the status quo. I really don\'t understand how their side will suddenly leave lobbyists with bad incentives by corporations. No, thank you. I\'m going to weigh this later in my speech as well. Let\'s move on to my setup. The model we defend, much like the precedent set in Chevron, is as follows. Firstly, we would only defer to interpretations in agencies\' formal public documents, ensuring transparency. Secondly, we would only defer to agencies when statutes are ambiguous. Thirdly, the interpretation must be a reasonable reading of the law. This means agencies are still limited in their powers, what the legislators intended. Fourthly, just like unambiguous statutes are up for judicial review, so is the interpretation of an ambiguous law. Meaning, if an interpretation makes a law violating the constitution, human rights, international law, or administrative law, it can be struck down by the Supreme Court. First argument then, on better or worse. There are two layers to this argument. Firstly, on the quality of decision, and secondly, on preventing unpredictability. First layer then, on quality of decisions. Why do agencies have the unique capacity in terms of the expertise and tools required to shape interpretations? I have a few mechanisms here. Firstly, on specialized knowledge. Judges might get expert opinions, but they lack the specialized knowledge to understand what experts say and weigh into the decisions. Unlike propositions, no expert can explain complex economics or epidemiology to a person without even a first degree in econ or medicine. On the flip, agencies regularly employ the best experts in their field for decision-making decisions. The Federal Reserve has the world\'s best economists, CDC has the world\'s leading medical scientists. Secondly, on dedicated time. This is where we think there\'s a huge difference. In the overworked and understaffed judiciary, time crushes nuance when judges handle like different cases daily, then revisit each case months later after forgetting crucial details. This is what happens in courts. But in contrast, government agencies are solely responsible for implementing laws in their field, allowing them to dedicate thousands of hours by a large team to work towards the best policies using data analysis and talking with stakeholders looking at comparable different policies in different countries. What are the impacts of this? Panel, having the best data-driven statutory interpretation is nothing short of a matter of life and death. In the lead-up to Raman Khandel\'s collapse in Bangladesh, factory inspectors identified cracks in urban evacuation. But when factory owners challenged what constituted immediate danger in the court, judges who never studied structural engineering couldn\'t evaluate the technical evidence. 1,134 died the next day. Technical experts saw death. Legal experts saw ambiguity. When the Philippines tried regulating prescription drugs, courts had to interpret what constituted essential medicines. Judges ruled that branded drugs couldn\'t be substituted with generics for safety reasons, but health experts knew the generics were identical. So medicine prices stayed 300% higher than neighboring Thailand, pricing out millions of Filipinos. Judges couldn\'t evaluate the pharmaceutical evidence showing bioinfluence. When human lives are on the line, we\'re proud to stand on the side of expertise over ignorance. I want to talk about the second layer in my argument, on preventing uncertainty. The mechanism for this is on preemptive versus reactive setting of standards. Judges only interpret laws after disputes have occurred, which leaves stakeholders uncertain about their rights and duties. What\'s even worse, under a COPS rule, even companies that fully follow agency rule can still be sued if one viable. Agency sets standards in advance on the other hand. This gives market actors certainty about the regulatory framework they have to work with. The impact of this layer is twofold. Firstly, principally, this is wrong because relevant parties are punished for breaking rules they couldn\'t foresee. But secondly, on a practical level, the uncertainty around the rules makes laws harder to enforce. This not only raises litigation costs in general, but becomes an additional barrier for small businesses that cannot afford these costs, creating a disproportionate chilling effect on their operations. Before I move on to my second argument, I\'ll take the floor. Trump\'s DOJ and DOD uses vague interpretations of how they legislate to unjustly deport people. Courts cannot rule against this because they have to refer to agencies. What have your administrative law standards done to stop this? Yeah, I think I gave you a bunch of mechanisms on why there are accountable measures against agencies exist in the status quo, on how they have to release probable measures, and they have to talk to different stakeholders. I\'m really not sure why that is only possible under a set of COPS. But if you want to engage in this, I\'m going to talk about corporate capture and things like lobbying exists more on your side of the house in my second argument. Big corporations regularly abuse legal procedures and set precedents that benefit them at the expense of ordinary people. This looks like courts allowing pollution under vaguely defined safe levels and let industries associations be EPA, harming the health of local communities. This looks like courts letting insurance deny life-saving treatment by narrowly confining medical necessity and reclining the state of California. These bad rulings then become precedent for all future cases. Why is it easier for corporations to influence courts than agencies? Four mechanisms here. Firstly, the process of persuasion. In courts, one judge is lawyer, often in many cases in private chambers and decides alone, whereas in agencies, multiple experts must agree, transparent records, oversight, and public pressure exists. Secondly, on exhausting the opposition. Like corporations use the economic asymmetry between them and the plaintiffs to file endless appeals and challenges, knowing that the weaker party has less capacity to weather through the trials and will eventually drop out. their cases. In contrast, you can\'t just exhaust a politician or an agent, you must actually successfully persuade them. Thirdly, on judge shopping. Corporations often exploit the system through judge shopping to get favorable legal rulings. They file lawsuits and drop them when getting an unfavorable judge, only to file them again as many times as needed until being assigned a favorable one. In contrast, when lobbying politicians or bureaucrats, you can\'t just choose which people you need to convince. You must persuade whoever is in power and persuade a large group of people to get your way. Fourth, on forum shopping. In many regulatory fields that involve transnational litigation like IP, privacy, and tech, MNCs even get to choose the jurisdiction that they file a suit in, allowing them to be judged under the most favoritism regimes. In contrast, with lobbying an agency, they must convince the agency their own jurisdiction, which significantly limits their ability to evade liability. Panel, even if you don\'t buy this claim, assume both courts and government agencies engaged in lobbying, we\'d argue that lobbying courts is comparatively more harmful than lobbying agencies. This is because even the lobbied politicians and government agencies are directly subject to public support in the election. This is the most proximate and existential consideration for politicians and the agencies controlled by them. If the public doesn\'t support them and don\'t vote them into office, lobbying just doesn\'t matter. This isn\'t true for courts that are perceived and preferred to be independent, they\'re not democratically elected, and their terms are much more long-term, so we don\'t want them to be swayed by the whims of politics. This illusion of impartiality uniquely allows room for particularly pernicious forms of corporate actor that disproportionately favor large and powerful actors, even at the expense of the interests of people on the ground. Justice must not be an option for very powerful police. That depends on what the field is. Legal interpretation has been the province and duty of the judicial branch for at least two centuries. We are very proud to propose in this motion because we think judges are fundamentally better actors. The first thing I\'m going to do is talk about the mitigation that they want to allow in terms of agencies. Here\'s why we believe the agencies are fundamentally bad actors and why their responses won\'t work. Firstly, in terms of their response in terms of RIA, the problem is that these standards are very hard to apply because of their own mechanism, because of why it\'s hard to take the agencies to court. But secondly, in empirically developed countries, it is just more likely that these agencies are able to get away with these sorts of things like we see in the Unite