INSERT INTO `rounds` VALUES (3,'WSDC 2025 R3 - Japan vs Croatia','2026-02-14 21:58:16',1,'external_video',NULL,'wsdc','This House Opposes the prominence of celebrity conflict* in pop culture *examples include beef on X (formerly Twitter), rap battles, roasts, etc.','Media, Morality','ImAb5w6Xo2k','public','{\"task\": \"transcribe\", \"text\": \"I\'m Kentaro, Ishida. Nice to meet you guys. Good evening. Hi. I\'m Seiji, speaking second, Hihem. I\'m Natooki, speaking third, Hide. Perfect. And your position? I\'m Mila, of Shikhar, speaking first. Thank you. I\'m Lana, Shikhar, speaking second, and I\'m Lai. Thank you. My name\'s Kihei, speaking third. Thank you. Perfect. Three bits of housekeeping for myself. I\'ll be timing the round of speeches and indicating time markers via a clap at the first, seventh, twice at eight, and at the conclusion of 8.15, I\'ll clap twice again and indicate that\'s when you should end your speeches. But I highly recommend voting to time themselves, as my focus will be on the round. Second thing, we are quite blessed, I guess, to have a lot of observers in the room. Please do maintain the quorum and try not to communicate with the relevant observers. Third, there is a recording feature going on here. Can we have a positive confirmation and consent from both teams? Are they happy for this? Yeah. Perfect. At any point in which you would want this to be changed, please indicate this at the start of your speech. And one final point, I encourage both teams to take an offer PY as part of WSEC. If there are any auditory accessibility concerns that you want us to reflect as part of disturbing your speech, please do make that clear, otherwise you\'ll follow normal WSEC procedure. Does anyone have any queries before we start? On that basis, I\'d like to invite the first speaker to proposition to begin the debate. Yeah, yeah. Am I audible at this point? Yes. Thank you. When in a rap battle, people often say, your mama this, your mama that. People laugh, people around you also make fun of you. In the pop world, this is a joke. This is entertainment. But for many, like me, it wasn\'t a joke. It was a destruction of my dignity. We\'re proud to stand on proposition. I\'ll talk about three things in my speech. Firstly, on unjustful say. Secondly, on ingraining bad ideas. Before that, I want to talk about framing. Firstly, on frame, what does the prominence of this look like? We think this is largely the massive popularity of increase, often the intentional way to gain attention for many artists. Secondly, what does the counterfactual look like? We think it can be resolved by two things. Firstly, on private conflict. But secondly, we think reasonable discussions in public will happen. But note here that things like violence and physical harm is unlikely on their side of the house. This is for two reasons. Firstly, on regulation and laws, we think people are likely to follow these things. But secondly, on private protection, like things like bodyguards also exist for many artists. We think things like fighting each other in dark places is unlikely to happen. Third, an important thing on small artists. We think this context is only about big actors. This is because small artists instigating conflict against bigger actors is never going to gain attention in the first place. We think this debate is about big actors. With that being said, I want to move on to my first argument on why this leads to unjustful hate as a result of associations. Three layers to this argument. First layer, on why does this create hate? There are two reasons why this is true. A, direct harm. People like family members are harmed. Kendrick Lamar literally made a 10 minute song talking about the mother, father, and son of Drake and literally making fun of those people. Also, people like fans are often harmed in these conflicts directly. Often, artists call out the other fans as being uncool or lame to gain attention and to gain leverage over other fans. B, even without things like association, we think that simply being involved with that person or associated with that person actively creates harm. Like fans, you listening to Drake or you having Drake in your profile bio often means that people make fun of you for listening to Drake. But also, family and friends are also harmed. You being part of their life, like you being the mother of Drake, often creates harm. But also, people living in local communities are also made fun of because artists often mention these places in their songs to make fun of them. Which means that fans who are also involved in that area are often harmed. Second layer on why this is uniquely bad. Two things. Firstly, on scale and intensity is far greater in our context. Because artists actively allow this and instigate it to happen. This doesn\'t normally happen in things like rivalry between artists because artists don\'t mention this in the first place. Artists saying it intentionally and pushing for it is a unique tipping point. It creates a unique validation for fans to act upon this and to create active harm. Crucially, artists cannot control how their fans act. So at the point in which when this happens, this creates immense harm. Secondly, on sense of direct attack when both sides criticize, fans feel that they need to defend themselves. If not, when they don\'t retaliate against other sides, it creates a perception that their side is weak and losing. The impact of this is threefold. Firstly, on mental harm. This leads to things like bullying in places like schools or even in universities. If you get called losers, if you are fans of Drake, this has happened many times after the Drake and Kendrick conflict. Also, we think things like social media hate and death threats often happens to fans as well who are following Drake or even to Drake himself as well. These things are anonymous and cannot be regulated. Also, things like, an example of this, Sabrina Carpenter fans being set death threats by Olivia Rodrigo fans for supporting Homewrecker. Second, on physical harm. Things like doxxing have happened in the past. Eminem\'s daughter being stalked by fans who hate Eminem has happened. But note that all of these are extreme because people idolize their celebrities. Third, impact on principle. Consumers who did nothing wrong and just simply listened to their music are harmed as well. This is a principle harm because they never opted into this or created this harm for themselves. They never agreed upon this. Before I go on to my second argument on ingraining bad ideals in daily life, I\'ll take that deal on. If your friend supports a problematic person like Jay-Z who was a domestic abuser, isn\'t it good that you know that your friend did this? You can call them out and actually talk about it and say that this behavior doesn\'t happen again. Like I talked about, many of the promises are fake. This culture often creates things as lying and just simply making fun of the opposition for the culture, for the fame, for the clout. This is something that cannot happen on your side of the house. This is not an active accountability measure that exists on your side of the house. With that being said, I want to talk about ingraining bad ideals in daily life. A characterization of this is that things like rap battles on YouTube, Kendrick Lamar beef, making fun of other artists are something that are popularized, largely consumed, and perceived as cruel. With that being said, first layer on why this is likely to happen. Three reasons under this. Firstly, on increasing instigation of conflict. So you feel that you get attention when you do these things in daily life. For students, if you do a rap battle in a school cafeteria, there are many people watching you. If you make a good line or something, you get clout. There\'s a huge incentive for you to do this and start a conflict in the first place. But secondly, the way conflict happens is much worse on their side of the house. Because the nature of rap battles or things like that is a very problematic way. You\'re allowed to do things like lie or sacrifice accuracy for the fact that you made fun of other people. Or you\'re allowed to make fun of family members or things like that. Something that doesn\'t happen in normal conflicts. But it simply just lowers the burden to make fun of other people. Things like bullying, you have a rational idea that these things are bad. But the fact that this is created as entertainment and something that can be done, and is something that is done by popular artists, creates a reason for young people and children to often do this as well. C. The way we resolve conflict is also worse on their side of the house. Because you\'re unlikely to actually resolve the conflict once it happens. Artists who do the same thing, who create these conflicts, never resolve these conflicts. And the reason for this is that if they back out, they\'re perceived as weak and they have lost the conflict. And people on the ground who consume these types of media are likely to perceive this as well and are likely to follow this narrative and never resolve the conflict themselves. Why is pop culture unique in creating conflict? I know opposition is going to come up here and tell us that teachers are going to tell them to follow morals or not do these things in the first place. The reason why this is not true is for a few reasons. Firstly, you see pop culture to be cooler and modern than teachers and adults. The people who are telling you to not bully or things like that are people who are much older, who are less cool than people like Hendrik Delmar that you\'re listening to on social media and on Instagram. Secondly, it\'s harder to realize that it\'s bad in the first place. Things like bullying, like literally hitting someone or kicking someone, you know these things are bad. But the fact is that these things are created as pure entertainment or as a joke. This makes it easier for people to actually do this in the first place. C, it\'s more accessible. Most people watch these content on social media. Also, most people are not malevolent individuals. So they\'re not gang affiliated and don\'t know like punching other people or stuff like that. But normal people are allowed to do these things because they consume things like normal music. What is the impact of this argument? Mental harm is you face immense mental harm when you\'re made fun of. But crucially, it\'s really hard to opt out on their side of the house or like regulate this on their own. This is true for two reasons. Firstly, it\'s perceived as cool and as a joke. As I told you, if you try opting out of this scenario, you\'re probably going to be perceived as someone that is uncool or like someone that is boring who is unwilling to participate in rap battles. This creates an incentive for you to stay in the conflict. Secondly, it\'s popular, right? Artists constantly create new views and new content to allow this to happen. Which means that insofar as you want to be trendy and be trend, like stay on the trend in school, you\'re unlikely to leave this and opt out yourself. For all those reasons, we\'re very proud of this Danhok proposition. I\'d like to thank the first speaker of the proposition for his refined speech in the length of 8 minutes and 12 seconds. Now I\'d like to invite the first proposition speaker to begin the opposition for the time being. Thank you. Can you stop for a second? We\'re in the water. Can you get the other section? Thanks so much. Doesn\'t he do it too? No, I don\'t. Can we get Kantaro\'s back and you\'re done with it? Sure. Thanks so much. Thanks. Alright, can everyone hear me? Okay, then I\'ll start in a few seconds. Drake was called out for being a pedophile and Kendrick for being abusive to his wife. I think the benefit of them being called out is that it changes the culture and the narrative and them actually getting in trouble and losing their support is how it raises any of the points of proposition here. Firstly, I\'d like to frame this debate in a simple way and that is to tell you that celebrity conflicts have a few intricate aspects. Firstly, it\'s the legitimate ones and secondly, does the PR beef, the legitimate ones, seem like artists who generally dislike each other because of a few reasons. A, because they\'re simply in the same social circle, they have interactions with each other and they simply don\'t like each other\'s characters. B, they\'re in the same field and they\'re competing for the same audience. For example, they\'re both competing for the album of the year, which means there\'s already inherent tension between them. C, they\'re simply people who dislike each other, even if they don\'t have a valid reason, just from the first impression. But D, they\'re very likely part of different dynasties and labels. For example, East and West Coast of rappers in the USA have a historical beef between them and then people inherit this beef from their mentors and their idols. But the second type of beef is the PR beef, which is just engineered beef from PR teams to promote albums, products and collabs, which happens but isn\'t as likely because it\'s very easy to spot and it\'s quite boring because it\'s not as engaging for fans. Because you can only engage in soft topics that they both are disagreeing on, which is like someone\'s male, someone\'s female, they\'re doing similar things. Now I want to move on to four main points of rebuttal. Firstly, on to their point about how this curves with the family of the person. Firstly, I\'d like to note here that the family is likely to take a stance. They\'re probably likely to say, no, we don\'t support our son who raped this person. And if that is true, no fans will ever actually attack this person because they dissociate themselves. Secondly, it means they don\'t do this. For example, if they stay quiet or they keep supporting their son, their family member, we think it\'s quite okay for them to also receive some sort of canceling or hate from the fans because they are in fact enabling this behavior. But also, thirdly, I want to say here is that artists aren\'t even likely to attack other people from the family because this is engaging. Because if somebody only attacks someone\'s daughter, they seem winded. Why? Because why shouldn\'t you attack the artists themselves? Are they good enough? Are they such good artists you cannot point any dirt at them? You have to actually talk about your daughter and you seem weak and this doesn\'t make you a winner of this conflict. But also lastly, if it\'s something so horrible about their family, your PR team would never allow you to do this because they do assert some sort of accountability. Second thing I want to note is why these things are fake, why these things that are called out are legitimate. Because if you talk about some things without evidence, you seem weak. Fans know what stops a wording, fans stop supporting you, you lose these fans and you cannot actually win. But if it\'s something that\'s completely made up, you seem weak because you cannot actually find a reason why you are better than the other artists. Nobody wants to do this. Thirdly, under the mental harm of people and like bullying on social media when people talk about it, I just want to say that people on social media who are a type of people who go on Instagram comments and approve hate comments about each other are just a type of people who would do this on any other video. There wasn\'t like this rap or they would still like receive hate and have hate comments. This doesn\'t like change their side at all. But also they can always like get out of control by saying they stop supporting the artist. And making your point about doxxing is like simply hilarious. I have no idea how a random anonymous person from Canada who I\'m fighting with, I can suddenly know all of your information as a regular user and doxx them. And now fourthly, under last part, which is how this actually still lowers to like conflicts in like schools. For example, there have been cafeteria battles, like rap battles. I tell you that this is not, like this is, like, my diss is not a bad thing. It\'s just this is extremely easy to spot. Because if there is a rap battle inside the cafeteria, every single teacher and staff member can actually see this. These people can be detained, they can be expelled. Or for example, it happens on online media. There are videos of that. When people have rap battles, they\'re filmed, they will be like, it\'s very social media. It\'s very easy to spot and blame and like these people to get your repercussions. On the other hand, if this bullying will take place, for example, someone whispering like swear words to another person, it\'s very hard to prove and this person isn\'t very likely to be detained in school, which means we actually have like less bullying in school on our side. I don\'t think it\'s the only way. Yes or no, do you agree that these celebrities have a cult-like following? A cult-like following? I think some celebrities do have a cult-like following. And I think it\'s unnecessary for every single fan to like disown their artists because there will be like a brand canceling from other people who aren\'t inherently fans. But I think the large amount of fans also are likely to call someone out because sure, they like Drake, but like firstly, they are like a woman who actually doesn\'t like support someone being a pedophile and similar things. Now I\'d like to move on to our argument here, which is on accountability. Okay, so firstly, I\'d like to talk about two things, capacity and intent. Let\'s talk about capacity. We tell you that these people who are artists don\'t really know a lot of things that they could like, see here, they could dig out on the other person. Why? Because they like move around same social circles. They hang out and date celebrities. For example, look at Kim K, Ariana Grande, Timothée Chalamet. They all have like a hangout with similar people. Why? Because these people can relate to them best. And if celebrities are normal people, they\'re scared that these normal people are only out for money and fame and similar things. And the implication of this is that they all the time hear things and they know secrets. This means they first hear something about a guy, then they say their friend knows something about a celebrity that they dislike, or a lot of people simply have same managers, so through them they can hear the dirt about these people. But B, even if they don\'t know the secrets at the moment, they have the capacity to find out. Because they\'re very rich, they have PR teams, they can just send someone to observe the other person and dig something up. Now, why does incentive exclusively change on our side? Because in proposition, there isn\'t an incentive to simply call out this person once, because they\'re afraid they will be ostracized from their society. Because if you look down upon from the other celebrities, the other celebrities will think they\'re really risky, they don\'t want to talk to you, because every single celebrity has some of their secrets, and they\'re afraid that if they talk to you, all of their secrets will be out in the open, which means that people will ostracize you, and this isn\'t enough to persuade you to reveal this information. Now, how does this incentive exclusively change our opposition? Because these beats start to... and they only scale up. These are conflicts who start because of this, like the intentions for framing, but then what happens, they start small. Like for example, oh, I\'m just a better rapper than you, for example, Kendrick, who are just arguing who will be the next Michael Jackson. Then it starts like, oh, you smell your mama\'s ointment, but it escalates to people saying, oh, your girlfriend is not attractive, and