plained why it can\'t come from government. So, the only way to get this information, to get accountability is to give other artists incentives to call each other out. We provide the most obvious incentive and that\'s profit, right? Like, insofar as you call out someone by making a song that\'s going to be insanely popular, you just get yourself money. Notice why is this profit incentive necessary? Why they can\'t just post on Twitter, oh Drake is a pedophile? Because they just won\'t care otherwise. Like, they necessarily aren\'t that close friends. They likely just don\'t care about the fact that he talked to minors and so on. But B, you always face a risk of harms happening to you, right? Because someone can always retaliate. If Kajit just posted, oh Drake is a pedophile, there is a huge risk of Drake just retaliating and then they both go into a lose-lose scenario. Yes, this risk exists in the state of scope. But A, they can justify it because yes, you risk losing some fans and someone retaliating, but you also get a lot of money in return. That\'s a profitable incentive for you, incentive that doesn\'t exist on their side of the house. But B, at the point, this only happens at the point when you are deep in the beef, right? Like, that\'s good to this point. And now you don\'t want to back out on Arsenal House because you just risk losing the beef and seeming weak. That\'s why even if it\'s risky, you still do it on Arsenal House, but on their side of the house, you don\'t do it. Notice I just proved to you why we have a unique way to get accountability on Arsenal House. They cannot just cope this by saying, oh, we get government\'s help. I\'ll take that to your right. The incentive is symmetric, not just because there\'s competitive incentive, but also not reporting crimes oftentimes just implicates you in the crime as well. No, it doesn\'t implicate. It\'s very hard to prove that Kajit knew about these things. But also, there\'s no profit incentive on their side of the house. You just go into a lose-lose scenario with both of you. You have no positive impact on the one, on the artist reporting the crimes. Then they tell you, oh, but this call-out culture just doesn\'t work because these people have a cult-like foaming. A, I want you to notice this is largely untrue, right? Because no one is a die-hard Drake fan. You\'re just a die-hard rap fan, which means you\'ll be listening to both Kajit and Drake. But B, this is largely unresponsive to our materials because our accountability isn\'t hinged on these people losing their listeners, but rather it\'s being dropped by their record labels. And notice this still happens. So why does the record label drop you? A, they want to sign other artists that don\'t want to cooperate with the one that\'s being implicated in these sort of harms. So the record label must choose. They like to choose the majority. But B, you have the fans from other artists that don\'t have cult-like followings toward the original ones also backlash against the record label, don\'t want to cooperate with them, don\'t want to purchase their products, their music and so on. If they don\'t drop the artist, this is why it happens. We see this in status quo where Adidas literally dropped Kanye West even though he still has a ton of one month listeners. Then they tell you D or C, okay, but sometimes you lie. Why you don\'t lie? Because you want to show evidence. Insofar as the other fans have cult-like following towards the artist and you want to convince them that he\'s doing this sort of harm and a bad thing, you show active evidence for it because otherwise you won\'t be able to convince anyone. This way false accusations are largely unpopular. Lastly they tell you oh, but we can\'t punish them as people. A, we can\'t because I just proved to you that white courts don\'t work. This means that the only other possibility is to punish them as people. But B, we have a right to information. Here they tell you oh, but no, these people have a right to privacy. No, insofar as you don\'t give someone any information, their life is significantly harmed. You owe them to give that information. For example, if I have an STI, I owe it to someone to tell them I have an STI before having sex with them because this will largely impact their lives. I want a way of why this is the most important point insofar as we prove it. A, on justice. This is the only way to give these people justice. This is like a core principle in society. Insofar as these people did these bad things, they did these crimes, we ought to punish them at any expense possible. I want to weight off especially this with collateral damage. I don\'t care if fans suffer or families suffer in the process. I care more about justice. Why? Because we do this in the status quo every single time. Insofar as a man supports his family working two jobs, but he rapes someone, I will still put him in jail because he did a bad thing. Even though his family will suffer, they won\'t be able to pay rent that month, they won\'t be able to feed themselves. I don\'t care how rapists should be put in jail and should be punished. But B, on practicality. We get the biggest practical harm avoided in this debate. Artists just become much more careful on the outside of the house. They just avoid doing these things. This means just less people get sexually harassed and so on. Second clash then on collateral damage. Opposition has two points here. A, on families. This rarely happens. Why? Because proposition frames this debate about big actors. Notice they have PR teams and managers. These teams won\'t allow the artist to attack the families or just call out someone for being gay because this is seen as a weak, as a low blow. Especially because the other artists will call you out. Like why are you attacking my family instead of me? Are you weak? You have nothing to prove against me. This way you attack the person directly. But B, this can also backfire, right? Because your fans won\'t support you insofar as just attack innocent families. We saw this happen every time with Eminem\'s daughter. Whenever someone attacked her, they lost massive popularity. But also there\'s little impact here on the country. So why? Why? Because they are detached from fans. These are rich people who go to private schools. They live in gated neighborhoods. Nothing can really happen to them. But also they get harassed very often online insofar as they are just connected to these artists. If they give a bad song, I don\'t think there\'s much delta here. So for all these reasons, please... I would like to invite the opposition to go as a final speech, a length of 8 minutes and 18 seconds. Now to conclude the opposition case, I would like to invite the opposition vice speaker to deliver a speech no longer than a minute. Stay here. I think that Japan cannot win this debate by trying to outweigh some more bullying in schools, by thousands of lives of people who have been constantly under the oppression of domestic abusers, of people who are afraid to come out because they were sexually assaulted. I cannot see how this can be a winning case for the side of opposition, so I just want to do a little weighing and say why it would clearly win this debate. First, let\'s talk about accountability of artists. One, why is private not enough? We told you that Japan never proved that victims would have an incentive to come out and engage in a lawsuit. We told you they would be afraid someone would dig up the dirt on them if they engage. They\'re afraid they will lose because they\'re going up against a large artist and they will lose money and time and face a lengthy lawsuit. We all debated a law case one day ago. We\'re aware how these cases can be dragged out. People don\'t want to engage with them. We told you that only if people were going to come out, if they have a monetary incentive, and they will only have this monetary incentive if they can