y, letting them progress their career as they want to. So I just want to outweigh, say why does this outweigh any of the collateral damage they\'re trying to push onto us? Because first, they\'re trying to push onto us families who say that they\'re rich, they have PR teams that actually shield them from these kinds of stuff and they\'re already a family member of a celebrity, this is not going to be so much greater impact on them. Secondly, on fans, we told you that if you engage with these kinds of content, you\'re likely to engage with other kinds of content which can actually bring you backlash not so impactful and we want to flip this, if you\'re not accountable, we\'re okay that you actually get some backlash because we don\'t want you to become like those who you support. And thirdly, on artists, we said that even if some artists are wrongfully accused because they\'re rich, because they have good PR teams, they\'re not going to be the ones who suffer a lot because of this. So I just want to weigh why, I want to say that this is like a trade-off we\'re willing to make. So why do we weigh? Because of the intensity of our impact, because if we prove to you that we have the accountability on these people, we say that artists are going to be deterred from doing stuff like this, they cannot just hide behind their money and just continue being domestic abusers, continue being pedophiles and having so much victims under them just because they\'re rich, we deter these people because they are afraid they\'re going to get called out and B, we have less other people who are going to be encouraged by these kinds of behavior because they see their favorite artists going away and not getting punished because of all of these things, which means that they are less likely to actually continue with this violence. Because these are victims of domestic abuse, they\'re not like kids being bullied in schools, please oppose. I\'d like to invite the opposition to the floor. I\'d like to invite the opposition to the floor. First on the clash of accountability, from Prague we argued that we actually do call-outs better for two reasons. The first reason was that we force individuals to choose better, more accountable and effective call-out mechanisms. The point is that public accountability is oftentimes useless. Drake is still rich, still employed at his label and still popular despite his actions and despite the public accountability that came. We would argue that the types of accountability methods that we incentivize are just better. But also secondly, on zero-sum attention, because actual call-out is actually empowered in our world rather than crowded out by random hate and media frenzy, we would argue that we do call-outs better. Their mechanism for doing call-outs better is largely on the level of incentive, i.e. that there\'s higher incentive to call-out in their world due to the profit incentive. From POI, we pointed out that the incentive is largely symmetric. One, oftentimes there\'s still just competitive incentive to out-compete other people in the same market that you are in, but also secondly, that not pointing out or not reporting oftentimes implicates you. Maybe this is not criminally, although oftentimes it is if you look at, for example, these parties, but also just on the level of public court, we would argue that it still implicates you in a lot of ways. We would argue then that the incentive to call-out is symmetric. On the actual whether we can do call-outs symmetrically in our world level, we would argue that this washes out at worst for three reasons. One, we told you that legal avenues exist, and though there\'s a mirage of responses that come from their world about how there\'s a lot of non-immoral, non-illegal immoral things, the actual worst-case harms are always going to still be illegal. We are still going to be able to persecute, and even if victims don\'t come out, we can still anonymously report things, etc. We would also pose that a second and third alternative would still work. Our second alternative about how we can still call-out on social media without having to instigate conflict and create a rap battle about it, and this would still create accountability. And we told you that we could still go to the label, and this still creates the same fear of this information going public without the information actually having to go public, and they will still then, according to their mechanism, repair. Second question, on innocent individuals. The first thing is innocent celebrities, and we would argue that innocent celebrities getting called out is a fairly large problem, and their level of response on the incentive is to argue that you don\'t want the other side to attack you, and the other cult-like members to attack you for the lack of evidence. The problem is not only do a lot of these celebrities never have to and never do publish their evidence, but also not be mechanized as why you are more likely to care about your own cult-like following than you would the other side. On the level of capacity, they would argue that PR teams don\'t allow this, but not only is their profit incentive to allow this, but you can create plausible deniability in your gray areas by doing things like what Kendrick does by saying he\'s probably a minor and not actually a minor. This impact is important. Even if these people are rich, and even if these family members are used to it, we would argue that rich people can still face harm. Suicide rate among celebrities is higher than the average job, and you\'re not really used to it. You\'re a family member. Families rarely get attacked. You don\'t know Taylor Swift\'s mom, but if Taylor Swift was in a rap battle, the mom would get attacked, and fans don\'t have any of these mechanisms anyway. So fans, then, are they even innocent? Because they would argue that there\'s no moral distinction between a murderer and the family of a murderer. The problem is, there is obviously not only a moral distinction between active harm and not, but also sometimes you just don\'t know about this until it goes public, and you\'re still able to get attacked for it. You can\'t punish people for not being constantly online. The harm is fairly real as well, and they would like to say doxxing is not possible from a distance. That\'s literally just what doxxing is, but just on a principle level as well, we mechanize why this is principally wrong. Third and final thing in the speech, though. For the average person, we would argue that the types of conflict that they promote are harmful, not just because you instigate more conflict, but the way by which that conflict occurs, or the fact that you will never end these conflicts. We posed three reasons why this was unique to no response from their side. We told you about the coolness of this. We told you how hard it is to realize it\'s bad when it\'s glorified and made as entertainment rather than portrayed as harmful and delight. We talked about the accessibility by which you get this type of information. We would argue this is a massive mental harm, especially because it\'s very hard to opt out of these types of conflicts, because when other people instigate it, it seems like you\'re weak for opting out. We would argue this is a real harm that Mitsuki weighed from his whip as well. For these reasons, we propose. I\'d like to thank the proposition by our speaker, who has defined peace and length of four minutes and 40 seconds, and all those who played around. Just two points. If you can leave first and then obviously make friends for a start, but also be conscious that there are other debates going on. If you\'re going to talk, just be aware of where the roots are. Don\'t go too far away. Thank you.\", \"words\": [{\"end\": 0.4, \"word\": \" I\'m\", \"start\": 0.0}, {\"end\": 0.96, \"word\": \" Kentaro,\", \"start\": 0.4}, {\"end\": 1.56, \"word\": \" Ishida.\", \"start\": 1.1}, {\"end