INSERT INTO `rounds` VALUES (4,'WSDC 2025 R4 - Japan vs Switzerland','2026-02-14 21:58:39',1,'external_video',NULL,'wsdc','THS European countries gradually withdrawing from NATO and establishing an independent military presence (NATO).','Military','Zp2-Uo_FRbg','public','{\"task\": \"transcribe\", \"text\": \"NATO is an organisation that was established to defend against Russia, but it is precisely because of this that Russia is so threatened in the status quo and is necessitated to act out in aggressive ways. We aim to restore true peace on cyber-opposition today, so proud to propose. I have two arguments to make from cyber-opposition today. Firstly, on why we get less Russian aggression, and secondly, on why US reliance is bad. But before that, I have three pieces on framing. Firstly, what does gradually withdrawing from NATO look like? We would obviously do this in a fairly smart manner. Firstly, we think that we\'d set up necessary alliances before leaving. But secondly, we think that the primary funded nations of NATO will likely be the last to leave NATO so that it remains functional in some sort of manner before eventually closing. A second piece of framing. We think that we still have the EU mutual defence treaty under our side of the house. So how is this different from NATO, which opposition side we use to defend? We think that it is different in mainly three ways. Firstly, we think the structural purpose is basically different. We think that NATO is basically a military agreement, and on the other hand, we think that the EU is a purely economic agreement, which means that there is less active military and it is only relevant for self-defence mechanisms. But secondly, we think that the degree of interaction or the degree of military intervention from NATO and EU is basically different. We think that NATO has active military intervention, whereas EU is mainly incredibly passive. But thirdly, we think that the frequency of military operation is also different. We think that for NATO, there is a daily operation and daily training that happens on the ground, whereas for EU, there is basically none unless conflict is actively instigated. But third piece of framing then. We think that these countries that fall out of NATO will still be able to defend themselves in the long term. And this is the implication of all of this framing. A, because of the EU mutual defence treaty, but B, because of the individual agreements that are formed between European countries. We think that countries like Germany still have a huge incentive to defend their borders against any aggression from Russia, which is why they likely have tried to set up buffer zones in different countries and therefore militarise other countries and stuff like that. But lastly, we think that they also have the capacity to defend against other countries right now. We think that in the status quo, the reason why European nations are not spending enough money on the military is because there is a collective action problem. Because they think that the US, through NATO, will defend them. Because they know that the nature of NATO is very interventionist. We think that these funds that are not being used on their military in the status quo will be unlocked once they actually go outside of NATO and join the EU, in which they can actually now spend their money on their military. I\'ll then go into my first argument on why we get less Russian aggression. The thesis of this argument is to state that NATO is incredibly, incredibly threatening, which is why in the status quo continues, we think that Russia will be even more and more aggressive. We think that this is basically three reasons as to why NATO instigates so much aggression in Russia. A. Because of the offensive nature of NATO. Obviously, as I told you in training, they do things like active military training. It says that it\'s in the defence pact, but it really isn\'t when you see all of its PUS operations in Africa, all of its PUS operations in Yugoslavia. So why does this instigate a Russian conflict then? We think that Russia will believe that they will be attacked anyway because NATO is such an active military threat, which basically means that if they\'re going to attack them anyway, might as well attack them first so that you can have an upper hand in the conflict and actually be the one negotiating where the conflict takes place and stuff like that. In the comparative, we think that the EU is a purely economic alliance. So even if countries do go into the EU defence pact, we think that Russia, firstly, will think that there\'s no reason to attack them to begin with because they won\'t be attacked unless you attack them first. But secondly, the fact that these countries made an active transition and made an active decision to actually transition to a less aggressive treaty means that Russia will view these countries much more favourably and then instigate much less conflict. But B, we think that another reason why NATO is so threatening is because of ideological tensions that it has with Russia. NATO was literally instigated and established to be anti-Russia. We think that Putin has demonised NATO to a large extent over the course of history. And from the eyes of Putin, NATO is imperialistic, it is expansionist, it is against everything that Russia stands for. We think that it is because of these reasons that if NATO does attack or if NATO continues to exist, Russia is necessitated to be more violent against them. Because if it backs down against NATO, there will be basically an ideological loss and stuff like that. They need to maintain their legitimacy, which is why they will be more aggressive against NATO. In the comparative, we think that the EU is much more of an economic alliance as we told you. It is never anti-Russia. We think that Russians have never demonised the EU before, historically speaking, which is why they are much less likely to be aggressive and stuff like that. We think the impact of this one is simple. We think that Russia will be much more willing to negotiate with the EU when the EU does something a little bit aggressive because they do not think it is an ideological loss to negotiate with them. We think that the third mechanism under this is that there is a perception of power imbalance. And that is to say, we think that NATO is expanding literally by the minute, with Ukraine wanting to join NATO and Georgia trying to join NATO. So if Russia were to attack NATO, it is only now in which they would be able to gain a strategic advantage over NATO, because this is when NATO is the weakest. In the comparative, we think that the EU, the last country that joined the EU was literally in 2004, which was Croatia. And we do not think that the EU will expand a lot in the future, which is why Russia will not be as panicked as to aggressively attack the EU as it will be to Russia. All of these prove that Russia will be much more aggressive towards NATO and why we were able to defend that on our side of the house. Our second argument is on why we think that the US reliance on these countries\' debt to NATO is very, very bad. So firstly, we think that the US is very, very likely to leave NATO or defund NATO in the very near future. And this is because there is a growing anti-NATO narrative within the United States, because it literally says the United States no geopolitical funds and funds so much into NATO but is literally getting nothing back in return. And that is the reason why they are likely to leave. We think that in the comparative, the US won\'t immediately leave on our side of the house when countries start leaving NATO. And this is because the US doesn\'t have to spend as much resources on NATO anymore when more countries are leaving, right? So the implication of this is that in the process of countries gradually leaving NATO, we don\'t think that the US will get up and leave and will be able to maintain some scope of geopolitical stability in the short term. Why is the US ultimately leaving in the long term very, very bad? We think that this is bad because countries will be able to defend themselves immediately. And it is impossible for these countries to be able to develop that with individual militaries in time for two reasons. A, because it is unpredictable when the US is likely to leave. We think that the policies of Trump are very, very volatile. We think that the really tight electoral nature of the US means that you never know when which political parties are going to be voted in, which is why it is impossible to preemptively develop your military in time. But B, it also just takes a lot of time to develop your own military. All of this to prove that when countries remain in NATO, they face really short term risks that occur of the US leaving. For these reasons, sir, I\'m proud to stand on this side of the opposition today. Thank you. I\'m going to hand over to our speaker for the speech and I will open the debate for the opposition. Hello, friends. Today I\'m going to be talking to a couple of people from Brussels, from their first speaker, and then going to our main point. First, I just wanted to say to our first speaker, when they talked about, when they gave us this narrative that NATO, that European countries should leave NATO because this just leads to Russia and Russian aggression, and that NATO, that they speak, that Russia is demonizing NATO and are just scared of NATO and that\'s why they\'re attacking it. But, panel, this is inherently wrong. We\'re telling you Russia didn\'t attack Ukraine because they were scared of NATO. Russia didn\'t attack Ukraine because they were scared of NATO. They just attacked Ukraine because they wanted a land in Ukraine. They didn\'t care, they weren\'t scared, they wanted a land. Because, in the end, Russia was so afraid of NATO and if they just attacked because they\'re scared, then they would just not attack the first place because of the fact that they were there. If they attacked Ukraine, for example, or if they attacked a NATO country, they know that NATO is going to respond. So, if they were so scared of NATO, they wouldn\'t attack them in the first place. They\'re always holding on to the fact that Russia, all they care about is getting more land. Putin attacked Ukraine because he wanted more land, not because he was scared of NATO. Secondly, they also tell us that the US won\'t be NATO until the very end. At the beginning, the US would probably still stay NATO. But this is also just inherently wrong. So, don\'t be told what the US actually wants from NATO. They don\'t care about the money. The US has so much money, they don\'t want a lot from NATO. What they care about is getting intelligence from NATO. What they care about is getting access to bases. And these are the sort of things that are going to go away at the very beginning when NATO splits up. Their money might still be there until the end, but that\'s not what the US cares about. So, the US is actually set up to leave quite early. And they\'ll stay there because what they actually care about will be gone from quite early. Now, going to some pieces of data from our side in order to make this matter. So, firstly, this looks like a complete removal of all European countries from NATO. This looks like there\'s stopping of sharing of intelligence between NATO countries. There\'s stopping of base sharing. All the countries, all the troops from different countries in different NATO countries will be pulled back to their original countries. This is going to be extremely expensive because logistically you\'re going to have to move away a lot of different people from different bases. All these people are going to have to get new jobs, they\'re going to have to train themselves in a new way because they\'re being moved. Logistically, also the process of separating all the systems, deciding who gets what when you\'re splitting up. It\'s going to take a lot of effort and money. Now, going to my first slide, I\'m going to talk about how this is actually better protection for all NATO countries. Both from the outside and also inside and minimizing conflicts inside NATO. Now, I\'m just talking about how this actually protects NATO countries from non-NATO countries. Firstly, when we look at NATO\'s collective forces, which are a much stronger force than each individual country, they present a much bigger force and it basically scares away the enemy and stops the attack. A lot of people do not want to attack NATO. The first thing to do is to know that if they attack NATO countries, all the other NATO countries are there. They have so many resources, they have so much intelligence that they will probably not stand a huge chance because all the other countries are forced to actually intervene and help the country or defend the country that has been attacked. The second thing you\'ll see is that if a country gets attacked, there\'s just a lot more intelligence. All the NATO countries, through their intelligence, they most probably can predict the attack much easier. They have so many more resources figuring out how the attacks are going to be. The element of surprise is just way less and they\'ll be able to deal with these attacks much easier. Because of all the intelligence, Trump will be able to pool all the different resources that NATO has. So yes, they\'ll be able to pool all these resources. Now why is it impacted? We see that the fact that if we catch 12 people from attacking both, if NATO catches 12 countries from attacking NATO countries in a second, if we catch 12 countries from attacking other countries, this of course stops wars from happening in the first place because if people won\'t even attack other countries, which of course is a big lie by the way. They could have attacked Ukraine decades ago. The reason why it happened now is because Ukraine tried to rejoin NATO. NATO is expanding and that scares Russia. Obviously it hurts Russia. But in the end, Putin still only attacked Ukraine because he was flat right. We see that Putin slowly attacked Ukraine. Putin slowly attacked Ukraine because he was flat right. He didn\'t attack them in the sense that he said, I just want to, because he\'s so scared of NATO that he has to attack them. No he didn\'t because he actually was flat right. And to a certain extent, he is scared. When Ukraine is part of NATO, he\'s so scared. And there\'s a reason why Putin actually attacked Ukraine and other European countries that are inside NATO. Because they\'re already there. At that point, Putin can tell you that he\'s actually scared to a certain extent because he\'s actually stopped him, as I said before, he\'s stopped him from attacking those countries because he knows all the NATO countries will have to come to their defense. So that actually makes no more point about the fact that Ukraine was part of NATO and they were already, but he wouldn\'t have attacked. But he wanted to land. That\'s why he attacked. He only was able to do that because they were part of NATO, which will defeat all other European countries if they split NATO up. Now, only kind of saying this after a conversation with this kind of situation within European countries. Firstly, when we see that most European countries are in NATO, we see that most of them therefore feel protected for each other. They know that their allies work with all of them and they feel protected with each other. They have quite low incentive to attack each other because they also depend on resources that they get from each other. They have more intelligence that they get from other countries when they depend on the resources. So there\'s no sense in attacking other countries. You\'ll see that any conflicts that arise within European countries can be much easier to govern because they\'re all part of NATO. There\'s so many more diplomatic processes that are in NATO. Peo