irly simple, why is the U.S. still in NATO, and why were they, why did they originally join NATO? Well the original reason was fairly obvious, it was expansion of soft power in Europe, well our second argument for you, that the incentive for the U.S. to remain in NATO It was decreasing day by day. The more and more, for example, the US creates treaties with the countries in Europe to get intel, which is their mechanism for US staying in NATO, and the more and more, US becoming less reliant on Europe for their defense, we would argue that the US is likely to lead. And in such a context that we live in, the deterrence factor that opposition would argue, not only need to resist, and our second argument directly has their line of analysis in the first argument, but furthermore, that there\'s an instigating harm in the short term of extreme weakness in European nations at the point in which the US leaves before the countries are able to expand their militaries. Crucially, our second argument was then fairly simple. Rather than allowing for Trump to dictate when NATO falls, we should be the one to dictate it, because we can then structure our militaries in a better way. But before I get to that, I wanna first deal with the best case case-care to wonder prediction from their report. This is a situation in which the US is still in NATO, and the US still funds NATO to some extent, which side then still better protects countries. Our claim from first was this, that the NATO and its structure and its family, in every way of all, it directly aggresses Russia. Crucially, even if they\'re able to prove mechanisms like deterrence and intelligence, as long as we\'re able to synthesize these to some extent, the active aggression we prove in this argument is the tipping point for action in the period. Your response here is simple. They argue that Russia doesn\'t attack because of fear, it is simply about land and expansion of their horizons. The problem is, as my POI pointed out, then this would have happened decades ago, and would have happened to a more significant extent. The reason why Ukraine was attacked is because they attempted to join NATO. The reason why these countries are under attack right now, and the reason why countries around, for example, Ukraine, and most U.S.S. nations have to be scared is because they\'re leaning towards the West. Crucially, obviously, Russia isn\'t showing on land. The land in Ukraine is not that unique to Russia, considering how massive geographically Russia is. Obviously, it\'s not about the land. But secondly, even if this response were to stand, it doesn\'t engage to our analysis, because even if this were true, and that Ukraine was attacked because of land, our mechanisms prove that this type of attack is still likely, regardless of what uniquely happened in Ukraine. Go ahead. Yes? You said U.S.S. nations are not as scared as they\'re leaning towards the West. So are you saying that we\'re bottom of the world, but Ukraine is not a leader team to have connection with the West? No, no, no, no. They can be left leaning, but the way they should, they can be Western. The way they should be Western is not to aggress Russia by attempting to join NATO. We could argue that NATO as a function is wrong. If you want it to be Western, you can do it, but the way you ought to do it is don\'t aggress Russia by doing it through NATO. Let\'s respond to their analysis. Their analysis proves two things. First, on the turrets, and second, on intelligence sharing, both of which argue that they protect countries better. Crucially, at the top, I would note that our line of analysis actually supersedes the vast majority of their analysis, because all of their analysis are only about current NATO nations. Our analysis also subsequently proves possible NATO nations are likely to get attacked to preemptively prevent these countries from being able to join NATO as happened in Ukraine. So even if they\'re able to be both deterrents and intelligence, non-NATO nations, who are prospective NATO nations, still face harm. Let\'s actually engage them. Against deterrents, two main levels of response. One, our argument that deterrence is, to some extent, at the very least symmetric in both worlds, because deterrence is not necessarily about, because deterrence need not be extreme advantage against Russia, it just needs to be sufficient, and we would argue that EU mutual defense treaty and defense treaties that countries have with one another is to some extent symmetric. Crucially, as long as we prove symmetric, we can take this deterrence class, because then, it all uniquely proves a tipping point for deterrence no longer being a factor because of things like aggression. In response to intelligence, then, the argument largely argues about how you can preemptively nullify attacks. Obviously, this can symmetrically be done in the EU, where countries can still share information with each other, and we would argue that other countries are still likely, for example, to create treaties around this, because they have, obviously, the incentive to nullify attacks. Furthermore, I would secondly argue that you oftentimes just don\'t know what these attacks are. Nobody predicted that Ukraine would attack. If they were to claim that intelligence knew of it, they\'re wrong. Third, yeah, sure, actually go ahead. No, it\'s not exactly true, but it was because Ukraine\'s lawful and legal, therefore, they were able to create this attack, and therefore, whistleblowers were able to do it. Yeah, but no other country knew about it, either. Like, no European nation and no current NATO nation knew about it, either. Okay, this is assuming that the best case for the US remains. Our second argument proved that the US is likely to leave, and their response actually doesn\'t deal with this. Crucially, this argument is subsequent to their analysis, not just because it\'s the more realistic situation, but also on the scale of harm. Final thing in my speech, then, third argument. We would argue that, at worst, this war thing is just, in general, the unlikely impact on this debate. We don\'t think this is a very likely thing, on that, either meant. We would argue that the NATO as a structure that forces nations to spend vast amounts of their GDP on the military itself is a negative harm, and an important one. Third argument, on forced GDP spending being bad. Firstly, what is the problem right now? The problem right now is that NATO, in its constitution, fundamentally demands all nations to spend what was originally a few percent, which is now 5% due to Trump terrorists, of their GDP on the military. Crucially, the reason why this is really bad is because it takes a lot of money away from other things that are more important to these countries. The important analysis here is this. A lot of countries don\'t need 5% GDP spending for their military to function in the way of deterrence and action. Japan doesn\'t spend 5%, and neither do a majority of these European nations who currently are in NATO. For example, if you look at, for example, Italy, or Greece, or Portugal, or Spain, who are just so geographically distant from Russia that invasions against them are highly unlikely, 5% GDP spending, which is a massive portion of their income, billions of dollars, is not important. The impact is fairly simple. These tax dollars can be used not just to reduce tax rates to allow for more economic circulation to occur, but also, for example, reinvestment into more important things for these countries, like education in Italy, or welfare, or research and manufacturing sector in Portugal, to allow for economic development to happen and for the quality of life in these countries to improve. This argument alone shouldn\'t win us this debate, because this is the most certain impact on this debate. 5% is being spent, and 5% will be spent, and this alone is a harm year on year. Even if you would argue that war is a speculative harm in this debate, this is the certain harm in this debate, and one that we should care about. Crucially, then, while all of this analysis proves not only do we win on the likelihood of war occurring, but also we would argue that we have largely synthesized all of our mechanisms, and we prove a certain harm in this debate. Final thing in my speech, then. At worst, even if we don\'t prove a certain harm, we would argue that, at worst, we would argue that all of the talking about deterrence and intelligence are just as symmetric as if we already proved that. For these reasons, propose. We may reject this as a question that\'s neither more than a speech, and that would appear to be a very good proposition to be put at the center of the debate. Firstly, I would broadly respond to their argument on Russian incentives and why they truly have to win. I would make it crystal clear whether their argumentation is deeply flawed. Firstly, the other response on why Ukraine was not attacked decades ago, and it was attacked in the second half of the century. Many decades ago, the USSR was still established. If you look at the number of countries, if you look at, because the USSR, let\'s say, ended in the, close to the late 20th century, there have not been many years where Russia has time to independently build a infrastructure, have capabilities to start a full assault. But then even if we say that, okay, they could have done it before, the critical argumentation is that, just because people were doing the right thing for the first 20 years does not mean that suddenly we can expect them to be, we cannot expect complete moral and parliamentary improvement later on. But we see that how it would be due to the annexation of Crimea and due to the fact that, due to the fact that of the limited, let\'s say, American or NATO response to the Russian aggression, we see why Ukraine were completely justified in seeking a patchwork to NATO because they recognized the defense