INSERT INTO `rounds` VALUES (8,'Panama WUDC 2025 Round 5','2026-02-14 22:01:04',1,'external_video',NULL,'british_parliamentary','This House Believes that the student organisers of the July Revolution should not transition into formal political roles (e.g., run for elected office, form their own political party)','Social movement','tCrVGvuPocs','public','{\"task\": \"transcribe\", \"text\": \"A bit of characterization then. These are liberal university students as organizers who oppose the Hasina\'s authoritarian rule. Specifically, they were opposed to the spoiled system that gave jobs and social benefits to the descendants of people involved in the 1971 liberation war. They took the cause of the street personally, the national military cracked down, and therefore the government has been overthrown ever since. First argument then, democratic consolidation. The goal of this debate is on OG, we want a stable transition to democracy. Context, Bangladesh is at an extremely enormously pivotal and fragile moment of transition. This is a post-revolutionary state. The military still has huge amounts of influence and large fractions of the military still have ties to the fallen Hasina government. On top of this, Bangladesh is also a relatively impoverished country with high levels of ethnic and religious divisions and tensions. Therefore, there are a lot of instability that\'s currently growing within the region. What then happens when student organizers directly run for political roles? We say this is quite bad for a couple reasons. One, it actively reduces buy-in to the prospect of a future democratic government, especially the perception of it, which often impacts how it plays out in the long run. Why? Because often you still have religious conservatives and rural populations in Bangladesh. And on top of that, people unsupported through loyalty to the old regime who are deeply skeptical of these people running for office. Why? The specific concern with this crowd of student organizers running is that it alienates a substantial amount of people who fear that these student organizers are not striving for a truly democratic government, but instead, just authoritarianism run by the opposition party of they themselves instead. What is the impact of this mechanism? This often means that there is likely going to be less buy-in for institutions, i.e. for example, people don\'t pay as much taxes. And that\'s a big problem for Bangladesh right now, by the way. Or second, it is easy for radical opposition parties to characterize student-led parties or governments as radical and violent. If you view these parties as radical and violent, you are more likely to vote for a radical, ultra-nationalist or Islamist party and you often do things like exacerbate ethno-nationalism and politics and you actively bring more instability. Second, this is also bad because it reduces electoral competition. How does this actually happen? Because many people involved in these protests are almost assuredly going to vote for these student leaders when they run. Why is this the case? Because often they have already built cultural personality when organizing these revolutions. They often have a good amount of charisma, which is what allowed them to organically rise to the top of these revolutions in the first place. And there is a track record of their efforts and their success. And, most importantly, these leaders are also likely to run in the same party or same government to actively capitalize on the July Revolution\'s support, which is unprecedentedly large compared to any efforts they have made in the past. Why is this terrible, then? Because this actively increases the fear of further democratic backsliding. If you are already a person on the fence and skeptical of the revolution, when there is last-line support for these figures that cannot really be challenged in any meaningful way because the Aon government and their credibility is down the drain, you fear they become a missusina even if they never end up like that. This is a comparative argument. Why? Because if you counterfactualize, there are still going to be parties that run to appeal to these students of protesters. But there are going to likely be more competing parties and political figures because they cannot all unite under the silver bullet that we took down the Aon government. They all would have to do so much more to differentiate themselves and to make sure they are passing or they are proposing new policies. But also, second, these leaders face less political accountability on their side. The students who led the protests are, while very affable people, no, not actually infallible. Power tends to corrupt even when there are good intentions in some cases. When you give large amounts of power to a small group, they often have selfish incentives to engage in things like ethnic favoritism because you need to do that. You need some level of majority support to make sure that you can stay in government for a bit to pass the policies and ideals you want to pass because you generally believe ideologically these are good policies and you want to help the Bangladeshi people. You also sometimes might engage in corruption because you believe if there is no way out to get funds, besides actively pander to the military in some cases, even if you think that these are all done for good causes, this often leads to democratic backsliding even when intentions are not harmful. Often I say that no, no, but these leaders are good. We agree, they are probably good, but when they get into power, they oftentimes change. And we argue that change is likely, especially in these circumstances of large amounts of instability. But third, what this means is that leaders cannot be held accountable to the ballot box. And this is no longer that different from the old regime. This is terrible. The counterfactual is we likely think there is a multiplicity of parties and leaders are likely to contest in these elections. Student leaders likely support a variety of parties. Political competition is good. Why? Because you often do panders to more groups, more minorities, and so it\'s harder for you to alienate them. You often prevent backsliding and they hold each other accountable, which is crucial in a moment of democratic transition. Second argument, before that closing. Yeah, so if the anti-discrimination student movement doesn\'t climb, what other force has the institutional capacity to form a party? Or is it just going to be lots of small, independent groups of two or three, all split up, fighting against each other? Well, I mean, I think this is a question to be answered in a round. I\'m not exactly sure right now. Brian will do it. Second argument, this reduces immediate political instability. It is not even clear that we will get elections or when we will get elections. So therefore, we need stability to make sure the transitional government works in the interim because these are lives of people at stake. There\'s families to feed and there\'s people we need in the house. Why does this actively benefit the transitional government then? Because here\'s the comparative. It is far more likely they get a military coup on opposition. The way you should weigh this argument is that even if this happens, like 30% more likely, that is a huge risk we would not wish to take because often that leads to blood on the streets. Often that means that people\'s livelihoods are destroyed and you lose faith in democracy, which ends up being irreversible on their side. The military currently is very powerful in Bangladesh for inclusive reasons. They\'ve always been given huge levels of control of the Bangladeshi economy since the Cold War. They\'re politically influential with the connections to political parties. Problem, the military backs the interim government, but they have qualms with the leaders of protest movements. Why? They do not want the leaders of protest movements involved because the leaders specifically are people and icons and figureheads of the anti-military movement then when they were fighting in the revolution. When student organizers begin running for office, you view democracy as a threat to Germany\'s inequal grasp of power. This is a perception issue on their side. The counterfactual by no means is perfect on our side, but student protesters are more able to portray democracy as good for the military. We can better ally with the military rather than go directly against it. And even if it\'s not a coup on our side, they still undermine elections. There\'s more violence against protesters and it\'s all on that far worse. Very proud to be a post. We now welcome the leader of opposition. Please begin the opposition campaign. Explaining two simple things in opening opposition. First, why leadership is better. Secondly, why the world in which these student leaders engage outside the democratic system is far worse. I\'m going to prove how you get instability that OJ thinks is so important as well as better policies. So firstly, why don\'t we get better leadership within the system? So we agree with the premise of opening government that these student leaders are likely to win because they\'re all uniquely inspiring. Many people supported the movement in the first place in order to overcome the dictatorship, indicating a large effect of support. The current leader of the provincial government, Mohamed Younis, is likely to back these individuals as well. They\'re just generally relatable to youth and uniquely inspiring because they represented the movement that gave them democracy, that represented the fall of the dictatorship that oppressed so many individuals. Why is this so good? I have a couple of reasons why people make better leaders that are uniquely able to represent the youth and make their voices important in the political sphere in the first place. And the premise here directly challenges what we get in an opening government that says there will be incentives to represent the youth on either side of the house. If we prove that youth otherwise would be politically disengaged, this means there\'s less political incentives when we cater to them because you don\'t see them as viable voters to the upper house in the first place. Why is this the case? Firstly, youth deeply distrust the establishment not only due to decades of oppression under a dictatorship or a pseudo-dictatorship and a legacy of corruption associated with other parties like the BNP as well, but also that youth is generally associated with the state itself as a source of oppression, but also that structurally youth often have incentives not to engage in rational ignorance where you believe that your vote doesn\'t matter and you have less incentives therefore to be politically engaged. It\'s not worth it to put in the efforts to vote and to try and learn about political parties if you don\'t think your vote is going to matter in the first place. And even when you do consume media, it often feeds you negative narratives about, for example, why democracy is a sham, why horrible things in your country are happening. It\'s not worth voting because media often sensationalizes the worst things that happen because it\'s the most eye-catching. Which means youth that often disproportionately, who appeal to these things, often have no incentives to want to engage politically otherwise. So it\'s uniquely only the leaders that are presented in transition to democracy that you are going to fight to be passed or that make you engage in the political system and have some degree of trust to want to vote in the first place. On their side, they have no doubt or challenge. The second reason why youth are going to be disproportionately not politically represented is that they are economically weak as well. For A, this is just likely because youth are quite young, they have less time to accumulate wealth in the long term which gives you political and economic leverage. This time itself hurts. But secondly, Bangladesh is currently going through an economic transition where they are going to be relatively poor, to a more middle and upper income country which has resulted in large amounts of unemployment in the short term. Because as the country becomes richer, as wages rise, it means that as a country gets to transition, many people are left behind and left unemployed. This disproportionately harms the youth because their lack of experience and time means they are not likely to be, for example, high skilled and middle manager type people and more likely to be workers which has left many of them economically in the dust. This means that they have less, for example, lobbying power but also less economic power to be engaged in the system, to be able to take time off to vote as well and to do things like attend political rallies. Especially in the context of a post-revolution society where a lot of their will to engage and their will to protest has been exhausted as well. This means that in the absence of these individuals, they get no representation for the youth. Furthermore, I want to explain why youth leaders themselves are likely to be incredibly good. This is because there is likely to be a selection effect where the youth leaders are likely to be people that have strong liberal democratic values to the extent that they were willing to fight and potentially be incarcerated or die for these values in an oppressive regime in the first place which suggests a deep ideological attachment to things like democracy and the liberalization of society. What is the impact of all of this? I think it\'s first and foremost good that we have good democratic liberal values against corruption and pro-things like meritocracy. But also on a political policy level, it\'s good that we have demands to cater to people that have long term interests, i.e. the youth that are going to live for much longer than people who are not the youth. This is important because this demands for example a transition away from textiles and other forms of low skilled industry which is bad in the long term even if it is desirable for politicians in the short term. Why are these things bad in the long term? Things like for example textiles or other forms of manufacturing often are unsustainable as Bangladesh becomes more economically wealthy. As incomes rise, exports become less competitive because you have higher costs of inputs. This is bad because it\'s hard to outcompete other industries. But also things like automation and the rise of automation would otherwise force these countries like Bangladesh to pretty much really de-industrialize as well. Why is it the case that alternatives are bad and are going to care for these short term interests? First, let\'s begin by characterizing who these alternatives are. Firstly, it\'s likely to be for example the BNP, an old establishment party which is likely to have the resources and the legitimacy to succeed by being an opposition party to the Obama league. And this is a problem because they have a long history of corruption and institutionalized corruption which is likely to continue because as a leader of a party that is engaged in corruption and tax economics, they adapt to have large amounts of wealth as a source. But also B, are likely to be politically short termists as well. They\'re likely to have connections with, because as an old establishment party, you have large connections with industry, for example manufacturing or textiles, which is often the tipping point for you catering to their interests. Not only because they have personal connections, but because they\'re self-competing on alternative incentives. So for example, many other workers in society are somewhat short termists, going to keep their job in the short term because it\'s bad for the country in the long term. So these two aspects of the Bangladesh are often the tipping points for alternative parties like the BNP to overvalue the short term and prioritize things like textiles as opposed to adapting and investing in better long term economic plans. Secondly, another alternative is likely to be Islamist parties as well because Bangladesh has a large Islamist population, or Muslim population, which is likely to be incredibly oppressive towards Hindu minorities. This is likely to be the case because Islamist parties have used this as a primary means of differentiation as a political party against other parties, which is likely to lead to a more extremist type of rhetoric and policy stance. Secondly, I want to explain why it is worse for these leaders to engage outside the political system before that political movement. UNIS has been a powerful democratizing and anti-corruption force, even just in August, and over time has seen it with the cause of momentum, which can be channeled to and strengthen existing political institutions, which young people now, and not in the past, have desired. Right, but the problem is it\'s associated with these student leaders alongside UNIS, who are seen as a front for pro-democracy movements when they don\'t run. They\'re also likely to do things like be critical of the otherwise democratic system, where the upper parties and the counter-factionals are going to exploit now. In the short run, we think in a world where these people do not run, it leads to more instability, where, for example, these student leaders are more likely to do things like stop protests, because they believe these other, alternative parties are bad and anti-democratic, which they\'re also likely to be, which we proved in our previous point. So they do things like protest on the streets, even if it\'s not all of you, it\'s likely to be the leaders and the close followers who now are not choosing to run but still have the original passion and desire to be engaged. This is a problem because these types of protests, firstly, lead to things like democratic backsliding and the stress of the government that OG thinks is so harmful, but also creates instability itself, which leads to things like investor flights. Finally, I think the overwhelming dominance of the student party that would run on our side is good in terms of avoiding gridlock that occurs more when you have other plurality of parties on their steeping-hoes that are likely to have an overwhelming majority, and gridlock itself is often inherently bad when you have one, for example, like parties that have an incentive to sabotage the main party in a coalition because they want them to succeed less, they can win in the next electoral cycle, which is really harmful because you don\'t pass basic infrastructure bills that the big banks of the nation need in a closed public society. Thank you very much. Thank you very much. Thank you very much. and the protest movements who need to be represented by their lovely figure, Mugabe. We all know how that story ends, hence not well. The thesis of opening up is straightforward. Power corrupts. The student protesters did a good thing by toppling Hasina\'s regime. The best thing they can now do is to stay on the outside, to criticize, yes, to protest, as opposed to become corrupted by the political establishment. Three points in this speech. First, on youth voting. Second, the counterfactual. Finally, democratic consolidation. First, on youth establishment. This is opening opposition\'s main point. I have four lines of response. Response number one, the premise of opening opposition\'s claim is that the youth are otherwise unlikely to engage in politics. That\'s unlikely because student organizers don\'t disappear on our side of the house. They still exist, they still have charisma, they still have cults of personality, and have strong incentives to encourage youth voters to turn out and vote. And the reason why this is probably a message that will be received well by many individuals is for all the reasons that opening opposition themselves name. For example, the fact that these leaders have unique power over student protesters. The fact that these people have charisma. The fact that these individuals are seen as having accomplished a revolutionary movement. Which means even if there is a marginal reduction in the youth vote, it\'s not 60%. Rather, you\'re probably getting a high level of youth turnout on either side of the house mitigating opening opposition\'s claim. Response number two, political parties, most obviously the BNP, have strong structural incentives on our side of the house to appeal to our youth voters. And this is for three reasons. Reason number one, the August protest movement energized youth votes in a way that was not true historically. So opening opposition is right. Historically, young voters in Bangladesh did not participate in democratic politics. But the fact that they\'ve now been energized and mobilized by the most powerful moment in post-independence Bangladeshi history means that youth voters probably feel empowered to participate in democratic politics, even if their leaders do not run. Reason number two, political parties have strong incentives to appeal to our youth voters to deter any potential violence. Hint, what did student protesters do this August when political parties did not represent them? They turned to the streets. They rioted. Foreign investment plummeted, which means even if the BNP is corrupt and selfish, its corrupt and selfish interests align with appealing towards youth voters to ensure there is not a second July revolution. Reason number three, on our side of the house, we have durable fiat to assume these leaders do not suddenly form a political party. But if you\'re the BNP, in the next 5, 10, 15 years, you\'re always concerned that if you do not appeal sufficiently well to youth voters, these guys might come out of retirement and actually run against you, which means the theoretical potential that these organizers could potentially run in elections is a powerful preemptive deterrent against neglecting or ignoring the youth vote. Response number three to opening opposition, we\'ll flip their claim. Youth distrust will be worse on their side of the house. This is a argument that applies to the longer term prospect, not the immediate term. Both teams agree in the short term that people who participate in the protests vote overwhelmingly for these figures. But what happens in the long term? Student protesters are likely to feel enormously disillusioned with Bangladeshi politics. Why is that? Because when these people become political operators, when they run parties, when they\'re in government, they have to make decisions in trade-offs that protesters feel violated the principles they pushed for. These people have to accept campaign contributions from corporations. They have to engage in political horse trading to overcome the gridlock opening opposition describes. Here\'s the problem. When the person that you saw leading the revolution is now engaging in the very same kind of political shenanigans that you opposed and you fought against, that is when you are much more likely on their side of the house to feel personally attacked. Now, to be clear, on both sides of the house, some political party has to engage in horse trading, campaign contributions, et cetera. It\'s better when that\'s a mainstream political party like the BNP, as opposed to the figureheads of the movement violating their trust, which flips their claim on engagement long term and also increases the probability of protests on the streets. This comprehensively flips opening opposition\'s claim and mitigates it to the point they cannot win on this argument. Point number two, what is the counterfactual? Opening opposition says that the likely counterfactual is the Bangladesh Nationalist Party. We agree. There\'s three reasons. First, this party has existing infrastructure to defend the main opposition movement. Since its rise to power in 2009, they\'ve been the main secondary party in Bangladesh since the 80s. Reason number two, the AL is overwhelmingly unlikely to win votes in the upcoming election. Why is that? They\'ve been enormously discredited. Their leader literally fled to, I think, India. The point is that the AL is at an all-time low, which means the BNP has enormous capacity to win the election because they have existing infrastructure. Reason number three, just empirically, polling shows that the BNP is probably going to be the largest party, whatever the next election happens to be. Now, opening opposition\'s claim here is twofold. First, they say that radical Islamist parties gain power. Second, they say the BNP is bad. Let\'s work through both. Their first claim on radical Islamist parties doesn\'t work for three reasons. Reason number one, there\'s a huge problem with this argument, which is they\'re right. Islamist parties can get some power. But the groups that are voting for Islamist parties would never be the student protesters on our side of the house. The reason why is that the vast majority of people involved in the student protest movement were liberal, democratic, or university students. They are overwhelmingly secular or not highly religious, which means if the organizers of the protests don\'t run for office, then the people who would have voted for them are not suddenly turning to radical Islamist parties because they do not believe that is a legitimate conception of democracy. Therefore, the likely alternative is they move towards secular parties, like, for example, the BNP. Response number two, it\'s unlikely that Islamist parties gain substantial power because there\'s a game theoretic disincentive to vote for these parties. The reason why is that you know that the percentage of votes that goes to any individual Islamist party is low, especially because there are multiple of them and they split the vote. So you as a voter have a game theoretic incentive to vote for a secular party that\'s likely to get a greater share of votes because that\'s more likely to affect the outcome of an election. See ya. Why are you drawing a distinction between, why are you saying that the BNP is the secular party? They\'re clearly the non-secular party. No, no, no. So just to be clear, the claim I\'m making here is that the radical, fundamentalist, Islamist parties are unlikely to gain power. Our claim is the BNP is preferable. This deals nicely with opening opposition. Second claim, they say the BNP is short-termist and corrupt. A lot of problems here. Response number one, the BNP can be held accountable on our side of the house with the specific advocacy criticism and protesting of powerful organized movements. The BNP has incentives to listen to these people, even if they\'re not involved in politics, to ensure there is not another wave of revolution. Secondarily, the BNP understands that it has to earn the support of these voters because these voters, for all the reasons opening opposition identifies, are distrustful of mainstream parties, and legitimately so. So the BNP, unlike a student organizer, cannot take these votes for granted, which means the BNP has strong incentives to rebrand itself, to internally purge corrupt members of its party. But thirdly, and this is critical, we are fine with gridlock. Here\'s why. Because the biggest problem facing Bangladesh is not that you don\'t have policy. The biggest problem facing Bangladesh is that an authoritarian movement in power does not have checks on its capacity to legislate and turns Bangladesh into a secondary authoritarian movement. Gridlock is good because it moderates policy. Even if you\'re on Islamist parties, they are counterbalanced by secular parties. All of which means that it is good to have gridlock on their side of the house to increase the probability of backsliding and military violence repercussed. Thank you. All right. Are you guys good? Yep. Good. I thank you very much for this speech. And I now welcome the Deputy Leader of the Opposition, Hellodon Khopar. Yeah. Thank you. First, I want to claim out the BN claim on the likelihood of a coup. It\'s extremely unlikely you\'d get a military coup. One, the military lacks funding. FDI is declining. The military cares about funding because democratic institutions actually favor the military. When parties compete against each other on the potential of a democracy, they are also likely to compete for the military\'s favor, which is why they are likely to give the military funding. However, this depends on having access to foreign investment. Second, the youth movement is a substantial unifying force. And the military doesn\'t want to go against the entire unifying force of student leaders and the young youth movement. That\'s why the military chief, Wako Uzuman, gave concessions to the transitional government under U.S. This is why the youth movement are specifically less likely to be anti-military after this point, meaning the military struggles to challenge the youth movement. In contrast, the military probably has substantial capacity to challenge the BNP that doesn\'t have the same degree of legitimacy that the youth movement has. As a consequence, a military coup is, if anything, more likely on their side. Third, there\'s likely to be Western leverage. Bangladesh is in the middle of a $4.7 billion IMF playoff. And the biggest market for its goods is the EU, which is looking for a trade deal. Because in 2029, the EU\'s favorable trade conditions are set to expire, meaning Bangladesh can\'t export to them. However, the West will not use its leverage against a coup if the BNP is in power. This is because Kamala Zia\'s administration was extremely unpopular among the West, precisely for engaging in the same kinds of democratic backsliding as Sheikh Zainab. So a military coup might actually be favorable for China or the West in the presence of the BNP, but not in the presence of a transitional government. So, if anything, a military coup is more likely on their side. No, for this flip to work, we don\'t have to prove that a military coup is more likely than not. The risk of a military coup is a massive detrimental impact to Bangladesh, because investors are terrified of military governments, and they pull out capital, meaning Bangladesh struggles to transition its economy in expected value terms that is larger than any of the other impacts of the opening government. Let\'s talk about the plausible alternatives in this debate, beginning with Kamala Zia\'s BNP. The first point I want to make is the BNP will not face political competition in the short run. So I think Zain agrees the BNP will indeed be in power, but critically, it will not be held accountable. It will not face political competition, and there\'s also a structural risk of youth leaders re-entering politics, because of how the BNP will establish itself as having an overwhelming advantage in politics. Reason one, they have a leg up in terms of funding. They have name recognition and existing stockpiles of funding, plus large amounts of corruption from being in opposition for a while. This funding is critical to finance electoral campaigns. Here\'s the thing. Youth leaders have a unique moment of legitimacy right now after the July revolution, but people\'s memories of revolution are short-lived. The reason why is Bangladesh is facing an economic transition. It is at the risk of a foreign exchange crisis, which is why it is experiencing a bailout from the IMF. Youth unemployment is in record highs. What does this mean? It means when the BNP has substantial amounts of funding, youth leaders, if they re-enter politics in a future moment, when people experience economic crisis and don\'t have needed memories of the July revolution, so they don\'t just look at a youth leader and automatically think they\'re good, funding is extremely important in elections. Funding is also important because voting is hard in this economic situation. Liz\'s argument was not just young people aren\'t motivated to vote. It\'s that they don\'t have the capacity to vote. Because if youth unemployment is high, you don\'t have the time to take time off work in order to stand in line to vote. This is ours and ours, and the BNP probably doesn\'t want to encourage young voters, precisely because, as Ryan\'s argument concedes, it is scared of them. So the BNP is lucky to make voting specifically hard, the same tactics that the Awami League engaged in. But the youth aren\'t terrified to engage in a second protest. The protests already led to massive humanitarian losses. It was literally gunning down protesters. Even if it led to success at the end, the youth aren\'t tired of continuing to engage in future protests. So there isn\'t a substantial risk of a protest. What this means is, young people will struggle to turn up to vote, and the BNP\'s political power is likely to be entrenched, because when it is in power, it will continue gaining access to funding by being in power and by being the incumbent party, meaning future political competition is unlikely to threaten it. No, thank you. Third, the BNP\'s existing voter block is much less fractured. Historically, people who are worried about Indian infrastructure in Bangladesh\'s politics are people with more of an Islamist name have sided with the BNP, and their voter block is unlikely to fracture in the future, meaning other political parties will simply be absorbed by the power of the BNP. As a consequence, the BNP is unlikely to face large political competition, and in their best case, the coup does not happen. Why does this matter? Because the BNP is, first, because at least all the PM\'s arguments on political competition are wrong. Political competition is symmetric. But second, then you listen to the BNP\'s arguments on young people and why the BNP will cater to them. First, the BNP will not cater to them. What are their arguments here? One, they say youth voters are energized for the protests. This is an assertion. I\'ve demonstrated the substantial exhaustion among youth voters after the protests, combined with complacency about Bangladeshi politics because the specific actions of the Awami League, for example, reservations for military members from the 1971 revolution are gone. There is a combination of complacency and exhaustion, which means youth voters aren\'t energized and aren\'t as likely to turn up. Their second mechanism is security organizers have credibility, and they have incentives to tell them to turn up and vote. Here\'s the problem. Youth leaders are likely to back the BNP. Why is this likely to be true in the absence of them coming into positions of power? Because the BNP has a unique narrative. Kavita Zia was arrested by Sheikh Hasina, which means they\'re able to use the rhetoric of saying, we are the BNP, the party that was persecuted by the Awami League. This power gives a substantial amount of influence among young voters. So the initial involvement and endorsement of security organizers is for the BNP, plus the BNP has power, so it has the ability to do things like pay the pop, especially in this environment that\'s often what they want. They are likely to continue spreading their values or encouraging young people to turn up, plus unexplained why young people don\'t have the capacity to, even if they want to turn up, which is why youth turn up in Bangladesh continues to be low. Third, they see there\'s a risk of coming out of retirement. And it\'s clear why in the long run, which is the potential time period that they will come out of retirement, one, they have damaged their credibility because they have made a promise to exit politics, and two, they will not be seen in the same way because memories will be short-lived. All of this means there will not be a meaningful challenge to the power of the BNP. The BNP will crush political competition. Here\'s the thing. Youth leaders will be better than the BNP. Their argument is youth leaders will be totally insulated from political competition so why would they give a fuck? Here\'s why. Because youth leaders aren\'t a monolithic group. The fact that even some youth leaders are potentially ideological faces substantial amounts of pressure, no thank you, to ensure that basically youth leaders push for democratization and similar norms. Closing? You haven\'t? Yeah. Your own argument on why these leaders are non-monolithic is the precise reason why there\'s likely to remain political competition on our side given that different factions of the organization usually come back to their parties, including the Islamist parties described in the Halal. No, but I\'ve just given you a bunch of reasons why the BNP will beat all these potential parties. In a first-class proposal system, strategic voting means always back a single party. This and the BNP will act as a selling point. Thus, they will use Brexit to get them in even if they have differing views. However, if they are in politics, even if they are a temporary monolith, if one of them starts engaging in democratic backsliding, other youth leaders can turn against them and form parties of their own which can drive political competition which can help foundation. Here\'s the argument that they totally missed from Liz\'s speech. The BNP has strong economic connections with existing textile industries. Textile industries have backed them for a substantial amount of time, so they depend on them for their resources. They owe them favors. They have family members appointed to top positions in a bunch of textile industries that didn\'t want the Iwami League to win. Why does this matter? Because Bangladesh needs to stop industrial policy favoring textile industries. As wages rise in Bangladesh, it can no longer compete with countries like Ethiopia on textiles. Plus, Bangladesh is in the midst of an emerging market crisis and needs to transition up the value chain in order to grow, and this growth is necessary to do things that create youth unemployment. Even if the BNP cares about the youth, it cares about the funding it gets from corporate backers which it can use to win way more votes than just the votes of the youth. This means the BNP will be structurally politically corrupt when we take stock on opening opposition. And we now welcome the member of government to begin her remarks. Thank you. We provide a variety of reasons to believe that even if students are uncorruptible, immoral, politically accountable, they will be ineffectual and they will cause the greatest degree of harm in this day. I\'m gonna start by positioning this extension against the government. Open government, I think, assumes that students are very likely to win when they explain that young people will be almost automatically behind them. But then they also explain that there are a series of perception issues that might block young people out of power, which I think kind of mitigates the harms that that can trigger. I think then, subsequently, it provides a series of good potential risks that could occur where students are, for instance, corrupt or overly radical, but I think this potentially requires a relatively uncharitable view of the incentives of these students, who I think, to their credit, are pretty pro-democracy, are pretty enlivened, and subject to kind of counter-billing incentives that both are queen\'s names. So, we will make a more modest, certain, and internally consistent claim. Regardless of the virtues of these students, if they pursue their demands, it will damage the legacy of the revolution and its touched political program, which is bad for our national law. I want to explain is that the perception of these students is cynical. And open composition points to generic reasons why people are suspicious of politicians, which I think are true, but I think there are some more specific reasons why people are likely to be particularly skeptical of students. Crucially, I think youth and radicalism is something that is viewed more fondly without politics than within, which is just to say that people understand things like youth and radicalism as virtues in many instances, but they also understand, for instance, that they were far more soridid in their youth, that their views change over time, that young people are still developing and do not always have broad ideological views, which I think is, in general, pretty bad in terms of young people getting elected, in terms of them attacking older people\'s votes. But I would additionally note that it poses a barrier to collaboration with older people in politics. Like, I think it is quite likely that even probably pretty good politicians who are older, who are more experienced, are pretty skeptical of these young people, making it far more difficult for them to achieve their aims. The second thing I would note is that the specific demands, not all of them, but some of them, made by these young people are both unachievable and undesirable. And I think this is probably a better explanation of the problems of these young people than open government, who I think assumes that they\'ll do things that are particularly corrupt and particularly immoral. But we\'re just going to explain that if they pursue the political program outlined in their 9-point plan, that is bad. What is their political program? A couple of things. Firstly, they want to fire a tract of police, commissioners and superintendents who are involved in student casualties. Secondly, they want to fire a tract of vice chancellors of universities all across Bangladesh. And thirdly, they want to remove security personnel at large from university campuses in Bangladesh. The first thing I\'m going to do here is explain why those political positions are likely inflexible, and then I will explain why they are bad. The importance of explaining why they\'re inflexible is it shows why you would sort of, no thank you, will not be strategic and adopt particular positions in order to avoid this backlash that I\'ll explain later. Why are they inflexible? Well firstly, I would note that these things are highly public, highly published, they\'re in the 9-point plans, which I think makes them relatively difficult to walk back, because they are a stated ideological commitment of these young people. But secondly, I think that they likely believe them, both ideologically, but also incredibly emotionally, because fundamentally these sorts of demands represent revenge on the kinds of people whose students viewed as being particularly punitive or particularly immoral. That, I think, additionally means that this is likely an expectation of their peers in the movement, making it socially very difficult to avoid. Additionally though, I would note that the most radical young people are likely to be at the forefront of politics. Those are the people with potentially the most charisma, those are the people with the strongest belief that pushes them into political roles relative to more moderate young people. Finally, I would note that they probably have a pretty rational desire to disempower political enemies like vice chancellors, like police, but I\'m going to explain now why that is probably a very bad thing. So, young people enter politics with a list of demands about disempowering people like vice chancellors, like police. This, I think, is highly unachievable. I think that this is a set of people who are obviously incredibly empowered in society, they have access to incredibly important institutions like universities, like police, and they are important, like obviously it is important to have police in Bangladesh, and that means I think that they are particularly powerful enemies. And this provides to you, I think, a far more specific reason why I think that who\'s all more moderately institutional resistance is inevitable, because the set of people specifically who these young people have upset are the ones who their political programs irritate, who are the ones who their political programs continue to provoke. But I would also note that these are numerous enemies, like we\'re talking not just about powerful people, but about like hundreds, probably thousands of police. These are people who are going to be motivated against these students. It also alienates you from potential political allies, because it\'s pretty unlikely that, for instance, the BNP is particularly keen on like mass firing security from university campuses. I think a lot of adults are probably pretty like, they think security is a good thing to have at university in some level. I\'ll take you in a second. All of that, I think, means that young people are likely to suffer in terms of getting traction, in achieving their political goals. They\'re likely to be ineffective, and that, I think, is something which is far more likely to lead to problems with youth engagement described by both opening teams. I\'ll take that opinion now. If the impact is a coup, then there\'s still a coup. But if they attempt to pursue an anti-dressalist policy, if the impact is a coup, and just they lose political support, they lose the next election, they will never find a new policy. I already explained why they\'re likely to be euphristic, why they\'re likely to be strident and inflexible. But I also just explained why there are a series of less-high things about a coup that are still bad. Things like institutions refusing to cooperate with you, things like potential political allies refusing alliances with you. The next thing I want to explain is that formal politics uniquely leads to division and confiding, which is bad. And I don\'t know, I think this is actually kind of conceded by everything Oxford does, who says, well, it\'s fine if they become a bit corrupt because they can just condemn their peers. Formal politics is more divisive than civil society, firstly, because you are required to take far more specific political positions, which opens up far more ground to disagree with each other, to have petty differences. Secondly, politics is competitive and sorry-some. You\'re likely running against each other in things like preselections, and it\'s the kind of personalities that are trying to gain notoriety, trying to gain media coverage. Thirdly, I regret that these are likely people with reputations as firebrands. They want to prove it by holding those around them accountable, by being particularly patriotic in discussions. Why is this division so bad? It\'s the quickest route to absolutely destroy the credibility of this student movement. You\'re required to, one, incredibly public spat to make people distrust these leaders, to make them, you know, to be more sceptical of them. Secondly, it splits the movement all the way down because the peers, the people who follow these leaders, are likely to want to pick sides. And thirdly, it obviously just has the opportunity cost to becoming a far more unifying civil society leader. I\'m going to weigh civil society here because I think opening government is a bit vague about what civil society figures can actually achieve. Why is civil society important? Firstly, it\'s far more accessible. Voting opposition tells you it\'s incredibly hard to vote in elections, there are an enormous number of barriers. But things like social media, things like accessing media, are incredibly inclusive and allow people to participate in politics far more easily. It\'s important to be there. But secondly, civil society is more resilient. It is harder to cut down on civil society figures because they\'re perceived as less than equal because it\'s opposed by international actors. Which means it\'s important to seize your notoriety to be in these civil society figures. A place where it\'s important to have powerful individuals, weakening politics is probably already covered by individuals being motivated to vote, caring about politics in a historically unique way. I\'m going to just read the speech so I can get my bag. Or if anything, I can come back in time to read it. What if I get my bag? Never mind. We thank the speaker very much for their speech and we now welcome the member of opposition. Firstly on the future political system of Bangladesh. Secondly on stability and why the BNP would be bad for it as well as incredibly bad governments. Let\'s start with the future political system of Bangladesh. Because we think the biggest of the fault in this debate isn\'t about who wins government the next time. Because what\'s happening at the moment is we have an interim government and before we go to another general election, they\'re going to have an election before that to form the special assembly to rewrite the Bangladeshi constitution and reform the government institutions of that country. And the most important thing in this debate is who has power in that to form the long term constitution of Bangladesh, what the formation of government is going to look like. And that very intuitively outweighs the rest of the debate. So they\'re talking about all of the government structures that inform every single election into the future. What are the problems with Bangladesh\'s political system at the moment? First of all, the massive rigging of elections. What was supposed to happen is that the government prior to an election would give up power to a caretaker government such that they weren\'t in power during that election so that they couldn\'t rig it. The Awami League refused to do that and that\'s why the BNP boycotted almost every election in the last decade. So, secondly, there\'s an enormous amount of corruption in Bangladesh\'s government. That\'s to say there\'s an enormous amount of embezzlement, an enormous amount of people who are appointed bureaucrats who continue to preserve their own image. There\'s also an enormous amount of judicial problems. Judicial actors, members of the judiciary essentially act in line with political parties. All of them were supposed to mass resign in protest because they were trying to bring back Shere Khasana after she had fled to India. There\'s an enormous amount of problems that need to be solved in this constitutional assembly. And currently, the interim government has established a large number of commissions under Mohammed Hassan and also with the involvement of the student leaders to reform these policies. Why is it dead? Yeah, Yunus has a notable primacy. Do you think that the international actors are likely to run and rotate within or are you averse to this trend? Hassan is in general running the next election. We believe he\'s turning a caretaker, which is what he did in the last caretaker election about 20 years ago. He\'s very explicitly a caretaker. He\'s not here to be a long-term political actor in Bangladesh. So, why is it that it is necessary to have these people involved in the constitutional assembly? First of all, the existing forces in politics benefited from each of these systems. All of their allies, they were put in place by the existing political system. So they\'re incredibly unlikely to want to change it. But secondly, without a force, without a specifically large institutional force against this, it\'s likely that any smaller forces that OG talks about, like individuals or small groups of two or three running, would be able to get this change through. They\'re likely to be bought off or horse-traded by the larger parties. They\'re less likely to have an agreement on what this reform should look like. So even if they all agree there should be reform, if they don\'t agree on it, they\'re on net and are going to get outvoted. And also, they weren\'t involved in the creation of it. The student leaders got Mohammed Hassan in. They served in roles with those commissions developing it. So, they are the ones who are involved in the development and can get it passed through. Why is it that they can only get this done if they are a party? First of all, being a part of a party is necessary for branding and selling your candidate. And secondly, elections are big business. You need operatives, you need money, you need ads, you need institutional capacity. You can\'t do this with two or three individuals. The next thing I want to prove is that other institutional power in Bangladesh is very, very rare. Because they can always go to other institutions where you might support. But there\'s good structural reasons to believe there aren\'t a lot of other large institutions in Bangladesh that can form themselves into political parties. First of all, it\'s an authoritarian country. Secondly, the Oman, which meant that they were going to shut down any alternative institutional power base. Secondly, the Omani League tried to position itself as a big tent party that could include anyone in. So, they had an incentive to try and bring in any other institutions and align themselves with the Omani League. And thirdly, Bangladesh has been in chaos for many years. The initial protest against the quota system started in 2018. And in that context of chaos, it\'s very difficult to form institutional power. So, that means that there aren\'t a lot of other large institutions that could step into this gap. The best comparison then is the BNP or other smaller parties. Open government always says, well, students are seen as bad, they\'re seen as irrational, they\'re seen as too young. But the problem with this is that this party isn\'t just going to run students. They\'re going to bring other people in. For instance, they brought in Hassan in the short run. Also, they brought in a large number of university professors who actually designed the original system of reform. They worked with their own professors. And military leaders, as Open Opposition points out, military leaders who told the military to put down their arms, not to oppose this. And those people have almost nothing in common, except for the fact that they were brought together by an anti-discrimination student movement. That is why they\'re likely to be taken seriously. And they\'re also robots closing government, because, like, well, they\'re incredibly terrible. But they brought in a large amount of support. The key thing is that they are important for bringing those people together. But I also want to note here, not only is it important that they\'re part of the Constitutional Assembly to solve all those problems, but it\'s also important that they are the first government afterwards. Because as I allude to, the entire Supreme Court of Bangladesh was forced to resign because they were seen as trying to bring back Sheikh Hasani. That means that they\'re going to get to appoint the next set of justices who will make the first interpretations of the new Constitution that will serve as the baseline precedent against which that Constitution is interpreted. And that\'s incredibly important. They also set the norms of how it\'s interpreted. And if another party wins, they have incentive to show that the new rules that they don\'t like are very bad and very difficult in order to manufacture consent to get them taken away. It\'s very important that they are not only involved in the right to do it, but that they are the first party to interpret it and to appoint the justices who do so. What are the benefits of this? It means that you get a government that functions, which is important for your economics. It also means you get rid of the quota system, which is not only un-democratic, but also incredibly bad for the functioning of government, because you appoint a large number of freedom fighters to the system, not because they\'re good. And it makes, and crucially, it makes the Bangladesh Public Service not politically neutral, because the freedom fighters that are appointed have political allegiances. And it means you don\'t have a neutral public service. That pretty much weighs pretty intuitively over the rest of the debate. Second extension. Why is it that you get more stability? Opening government says you get the military coup. Opening office pretty astutely points out that the military can explicitly allow a moment to come through. You\'re very unlikely to get the military coup. Why is it that the BNP would be incredibly bad at this ability? First of all, their leader is not somebody who can legitimately run, he\'s been charged with anti-corruption, and different courts have split over his actual legitimacy as a candidate. So, that means that they\'re incredibly likely to be protested. Secondly, there\'s very likely to be hindered protests, where this aggressively pro-Muslim party wins, who have done terrible things to women and minorities in the past. And thirdly, your relationship with India is likely to be substantially worse, and they\'re likely to foment unrest on the ground. We already saw Indian forces in the country protecting Sheikh Hasana on her side during the last revolution, and they have an intention to foment chaos in the country in order to re-establish their influence. And I know these aren\'t the only reasons why you don\'t get stability. There are also reasons why the BNP is bad. Like, they are anti-Hindu, we should care about Hindu. They are anti-India, we should care about your relationship with India, your trade relationship, your security relationship. They have done corruption, which is not only that you can\'t legitimately run, but also, you know, corruption is bad. Additionally, I think it\'s important that these student leaders are there to be able to tamp down protests. Open Opposition says, well, student leaders might do protest, but I think that\'s the lower impact argument, because their protests are naturally pretty good and pretty reasonable. What\'s more important is the bad, more aggressive sets of protests that might come from other actors, which we think they\'re uniquely good at tamping down and stopping the Indian violence. First of all, people are going to feel lied to if these people don\'t follow through on their promises. Secondly, they are the powerful figures who can come out persuasively and urge people to tamp down. But thirdly, the protests are more willing to negotiate with them when they\'re in government, because they have a history of being receptive to protest, rather than turning to violence because they feel like the government will never listen, because protests will never work. That was good. I thank the speaker very much for this speech. Now I\'d like to give the government whip to follow up government venture. First, I\'ll explain why closing the government wins the debate, and secondly, why the Opposition bench loses. Firstly, why did we win the debate? I think we made kind of two contributions. The first is just explaining in substantial detail why these student leaders would be both bad at politics, but also, perhaps more importantly, good at civil society, and impacting why that is a society and politics changing force. And the second, I think, is just outlining that most of these specific demands of the anti-discrimination student movement, in their highly publicised 9.0 plan, published after closing the scene up, are bad. And of course, as this team might respond, some of those demands are like, for instance, asking for compensation for families who have protested to a hill. That\'s fine. But those kind of demands are not sensationalist, nor are they radical. They\'re extremely unlikely to be the focus of campaigns, of opposition, of media, of the things that they are held to by political opponents, and that are indeed the spotlight for the media at the moment. Many of those demands are incredibly bad. Things like firing huge tracks of police who are related to the university. or whose jurisdiction is the universities where protest was suppressed, even if those police were not directly involved, like they didn\'t kill a person, they just have jurisdiction over those universities, no security at the universities, firing fuse attacks at VCs, and national boundary alarming the uni-quake. And I think those policies are bad in a few respects. Firstly, I don\'t suppose many of them on face value on their grounds alone. For instance, I don\'t think you should ban student movements of most political parties until they pass a minimum and extremely high threshold. I think that is probably something we should be conservative about, not restricting free speech. I think police who are often underpaid, who are not personally responsible for violence against students, who happen to be part of a systemically corrupt institution, but don\'t have much choice in that, should not be fired from their jobs. I think those are immoral demands and we should not stand for them. Secondly, those demands are literally impossible to fulfil. As Ellie points out to No Response, without powerful enemies in the form of the whole police force, this is incredibly scary. This doesn\'t work, which at very worst for us, which is an enormous opportunity cost of resources, a failure to get your policies passed. But also I think it\'s incredibly embarrassing and delegitimising for these student protests and for all the civil society organisations and movements that we\'ve heard that they\'re attached to, when they can do good work out of politics. Maybe, for instance, you want to go into politics later, you can\'t because you have an initial track record of doing terrible things and failing to pass through a policy. The response we get to this from opening opposition in a POI is, firstly, well if there\'s a risk of coup, then you moderate. I think Ellie pre-empts this in quite a lot of detail. We explain people aren\'t rational, that it\'s very unlikely that there\'s a risk of a coup on their side, and you\'re not thinking about the future to that extent. But it\'s incredibly embarrassing and maybe, for instance, brings backlash or political violence in the short term to backtrack on your established demands. And importantly, even if some people are rational in this way, the most prominent leaders of that movement will not be. The second plan they made is, well if you don\'t moderate, you\'re going to lose the election. But firstly, I don\'t know if that harms the movement of anti-authoritarian student protesters. Regardless, even if it\'s true that you lose the election by having these demands, it still hurts that movement. It hurts, for instance, initial movements by students, any student in Bangladesh against corruption, against authoritarianism in the future. They are targeted that brush. It\'s really idiotic for opposition parties to media outlets to pick up on. But secondly, maybe at their very best, this plan hurts OG, because we explain our why involved in civil society. Successfully put politics later, for instance. And you could run more successfully put politics later, if you\'re not targeted this brush. If, for instance, you have demands that are bad, that can\'t operate in civil society, probably still do with. And in politics, in representing this constituency in parliament, you cause huge amounts of chaos. You shift the Overton window. You mean that people discredit other, more sensible left-wing policies, like restrictions on police powers. You mean that there\'s gridlock in parliament. All of that is terrible. Why does this then get us above opening government? I take a fairly generic criticism of young left revolutionaries on the basis of their radicalism, but also the tempting vices of politics like corruption. But the problem with this is, firstly, claims in terms of entitlement to corruption, while true, I think seem relatively symmetric, given the features that this team themselves point out in terms of the political system in Bangladesh at large. But secondly, radicalism, of course, and our opening text, hand you back, but can also be put, right, at deeply radical movements, like, for instance, anti-caste movements or feminism, are both good ideologically, but are deeply attractive to people, even though they ask for very large changes to the status quo. We explain that the changes we\'re asking for are bad, which means they\'re both unpopular, but also undesirable. But finally, we impact the alternative. Civil society, not just politics, which makes society and makes government better. We win. Let\'s now talk about opening opposition. Opening opposition, I think, is dangerously pessimistic in their vision of the status quo. They say that the NDP will have no political competition without students to push back. The reason for this is because it\'s prohibitively hard for the youth to vote, the BNP receives money from lobbyists, they\'ll always get young people on side, students don\'t win. This is dangerous because it\'s a self-defeating argument for opening opposition. If the BNP is so powerful, then students collecting and testing elections will fail regardless. And their one attempt to bridge this gap is to say, well, people care a lot about youth and revolution now, we have to take that opportunity. That isn\'t sufficient for a few reasons. Firstly, it could lose that momentum, right? Like, if you present yourself as political critics, part of civil society, it is likely that anti-authoritarian momentum lost. But secondly, if opening opposition is right, there is an attitude of our type in supporting this specific political party, especially because their power is in opposing the AL, not in creating and enjoicing their own political platform, which I know requires many different skills and virtues. Finally, they say the BNP has a textile contract, which is bad. One, this is self-correcting. Yes, lobbying is powerful, but votes matter for the individual people whose wages are going down. There are other competing businesses that don\'t like cronyistic contracts. Contracts expire. Or secondly, if the textile industry is so cemented, and that means that other parties who are established also need money, also take that money. Finally, let\'s provide closing opposition. Firstly, they say the young people in politics will help the future political system in terms of the changes to the constitution. One, it\'s unlikely that those young people are elected or listened to if they are elected, for the reasons of youth. They don\'t have the agile and institutional capacity that this team describes as necessary. That is overpowering. Secondly, Yunus has decreased corruption massively, and it\'s likely that his oversight as the caretaker government will make sure that the special assembly is good, that it\'s not done corruptly, and so on. But finally, they say that students have united lots of different parts of society. That\'s true with regards to taking down the AL. It\'s not true with regards to rewriting the constitution, where there are hugely different interests, if you are the military, as opposed to if you are students. They say next that the BNP will fail. Firstly, nice of your opening, because they are guaranteed success no matter what. But secondly, the barriers they put up are transitory. They say the leader is bad and you get a new one. Again, Yunus has massively decreased corruption in Bangladesh. They say your relationship with India is bad. I\'ll flip that. It\'s way worse, because India hated the student protesters, set on with police to target them and bring them down. This ruins your relationship with India, or rather hurts it, because obviously India doesn\'t like the student protesters. It doesn\'t like it when the special assembly supports them and has them in their midst. We didn\'t speak very much for their speech, and we now welcome the opposition to close off the debate. Firstly, closing government, opening government, the way in which closing government and opening opposition will be interpreted in their news. Firstly, closing government, we now ask two questions. Firstly, will the youth leaders win elections? And secondly, what policies are they going to implement when they leave the government? Firstly, will the youth leaders win elections? The first reason why I think this is pretty obvious, is that they have already proven themselves to be highly effective political organisers. This seems dismissive about the amount of effort they\'ve applied, because the protests have been placed for a long period of time, and involved more than just organising people to vote on the streets, but organising highly complex networks of people to operate around an infrastructure system that cracked down on those students, and then after that, being able to negotiate the process of establishing a relatively democratic transitional government in order to manage the process of moving towards democracy, and establishing a series of missions in order to deal with and analyse the problems that existed in the previous system, which could suggest to me that these protests are efficient in organising, are likely to be good in winning elections. Secondly, we think that the BNP themselves have lost a lot of institutional knowledge about winning elections, and think that while they are probably likely to be effective, they are unlikely to be unbeatable. That is, firstly, because they\'ve been disempowered by the army, they\'ve been in long-term opposition for decades, which suggests they haven\'t had that much infrastructure around winning elections. But secondly and additionally, they\'ve boycotted elections for a long period of time, which means that infrastructure around elections is likely to be lost. And I\'d like to sort of deny this opening opposition, we agree with opening opposition that in the counterfactual, without students, it\'s likely that this BNP is going to win the election without student opposition. What Matt says is not that that is never going to happen, but that if it did happen, it would be bad because the BNP is a mortal party. Thirdly, their unpopular policies are outweighed by a series of very popular figures that are firstly coming from outside the establishment, because although it\'s true that people have some skepticism of students and whether or not they\'ll be anti-democratic, they know for certain that the BNP has been corrupt. It\'s led by a person who is currently being charged with money laundering. It has previously, or has been previously, fought out for corruption, which guarantees them some amount of leverage over them. Secondly, the people in Bangladesh want democracy. Living in a dictatorship sucks. Having at least one party that is working hard towards democracy is a meaningful draw for people, even if they have a series of unpopular policies. Thirdly, the students aren\'t a super racialised religious party, which is a characteristic of a BNP that has alienated a lot of people in Bangladesh who see that racialisation is something that causes division, escalates tensions in the region of India, and is detrimental towards their likelihood of getting trade and economic development in the future. Fourthly and finally, they\'re not children. These are university students as well, pretty old university students, who have been politically organising for a long time, who are experienced in some level of politics at the youth level, where these people are likely to be participating. But Susan Wang, so he says it won\'t work. This is obviously going to be a hard thing to achieve. But now is the best time to achieve for any kind in the future. The Awami League is split. The Awami League is on the run, and their leader has left India. They lack a firm opponent towards their leader, someone who can organise them. The students have a lot of momentum at the moment, and the BNP is at a critical point, but it is also unlikely to succeed. For the reasons that I previously explained, it\'s previously explained about lacking the infrastructure around elections for men. So now is the time when it could work, and that\'s when it\'s important. So when you\'re around the likelihood then, first you\'re going to need to prove high likelihood of success. In fact, this is conceded by a Prime Minister, who claims that. They\'ll be so popular and unbeatable, they\'ll have unchecked power, the result of them becoming evil. Which therefore makes the quasi-government a nice, hopeful government as well. But secondly, even if they don\'t, it\'s still good for them to have some level of power. Firstly, because it means better accountability on the BNP and the Awami League, if they are the ones who benefit from power. But secondly, it\'s important for building momentum towards future political successes, as you build institutional knowledge around elections, into and towards the future. Netcessent talked about whether or not their policies are bad. They outlined that some of these policies might have negative consequences. Firstly, many of the people who they want to remove from power are extremely unpopular. For example, police commissioners in Bangladesh are notorious for being permissive of things like police officers engaging in violence, engaging in rape. They are people who others also want to see removed. Secondly, it\'s not necessary to remove these figures in order to remove corruption. A lot of these people are aligned with corrupt political figures from the Awami League, which makes removing them necessary in order to improve the infrastructure that allows corruption to continue. Because even if you change who\'s in power, the BNP comes in power, if you permit the lower levels of corruption to continue to permeate the system, the BNP then becomes the corrupt political party that\'s in power. Thirdly, it\'s necessary to get trust in government, to resolve the aforementioned problems, the fact that they are corrupt, and the fact that they are associated with previous members of the Awami League, and the fact that they\'re unpopular. If you want trust in any new government, it needs to remove the infrastructure of the old government, which is unpopular and evil. Fourthly, and most importantly, it\'s important for justice. These people are evil people who deserve punishment. The Student League, Sophie wants to say, is a fucking terrorist organisation. The Awami League\'s youth group should be disseminated and destroyed. They are a group of people who deserve justice to come to them. Could these youth leaders finally then be flexible, even in the instances where these policies are bad? Firstly, they have explicitly, I think in a moment, set up a series of commissions to redesign their policy platform with experts involved. That legitimises changes in refinement of their current policy, not as factors, but as academic refinement on the policies they have established, which makes that change legitimate. But secondly, they\'re likely to believe in that possibility and not be so everyone can use, because they\'re working with a new set of people. They\'re working with people like Hassan, the interim leader of the government, like the figures and academics they have put into these commissions in order to redefine their policy. They have a revealed preference towards redefining and establishing a new political platform that is likely to have positive outcomes, even in the instances where their policies are bad. Fifteen seconds. OTR gave five reasons the DNP is accountable. Students are a newly-entered graduate, and one thing. Why? The DNP raised it before. The DNP must satisfy professors\' demands for colleges and schools of mentoring. They can still operate outside politics, and the DNP needs to prove it has changed from the past. This beats your extension directly. It doesn\'t. Both allowing the DNP to come into power at a moment of unique political vacuum and crisis and high instability, with a system of corruption already in place, means that none of those systems of democratic ability exist. They might have potential pressure from young people or from other political parties, but if they rig the next election and after this next election, then they win, they don\'t get any of those better outcomes. But if you believe that\'s true, and the DNP should have accountability, they get better accountability when there is more youth representation in parliament. Next to respond to opening government, first they say this is going to empower parties that weaponise scepticism that people have of youth leaders. Firstly, directly contradicted by their later claim, which is that student leaders will be so popular they will end up having unchecked power. Secondly, these people are likely to be sceptical of anyone protecting the branding of being the consequence of a revolutionary movement. Next they say that student leaders will have too much unchecked power and will become corrupt in the end. Firstly, if there is any meaningful degree of scepticism, that explains where the democratic ability on student leaders comes from. Like if you believe that the DNP is likely to be successful or at least push back, like they say, usually that is the pressure that forces them to continue to be democratic because there is someone pointing them out and forcing them to be better. How are they likely to go about gaining their legitimacy? Firstly, doing the things they set out to do, abolishing the quota system, which only Matt explains real harm of. Secondly, gaining the backing and approval of various groups who supported them in the crisis of the revolution, like university academics and the commissions they have set up. Thirdly, maintaining their commitment to democracy into the long term. So if you believe that the DNP is a meaningful opposition, that explains why youth leaders will continue to be given long term gifts everywhere. Finally, to prove the DNP wins, that\'s good. The DNP is horrible. Firstly, they are horrible to the communities of minorities. Secondly, they are being led by a corrupt person. Thirdly, they bring anti-unionism, which is likely to escalate political tensions. But also, better competition makes the DNP better. So believe that they are going to bring power to the movement. That\'s a great part of the process. Thank you, Speaker, very much for the speech. I thank everyone for the excellent debate. Please cross the floor, shake hands and make your way outside. We will call you back in with the closing.\", \"words\": [{\"end\": 6.3, \"word\": \" A\", \"start\": 5.82}, {\"end\": 6.48, \"word\": \" bit\", \"start\": 6.3}, {\"end\": 6.6, \"word\": \" of\", \"start\": 6.48}, {\"end\": 7.18, \"word\": \" characterization\", \"start\": 6.6}, {\"end\": 7.48, \"word\": \" then.\", \"start\": 7.18}, {\"end\": 7.94, \"word\": \" These\", \"start\": 7.76}, {\"end\": 8.14, \"word\": \" are\", \"start\": 7.94}, {\"end\": 8.54, \"word\": \" liberal\", \"start\": 8.14}, {\"end\": 9.02, \"word\": \" university\", \"start\": 8.54}, {\"end\": 9.4, \"word\": \" students\", \"start\": 9.02}, {\"end\": 9.56, \"word\": \" as\", \"start\": 9.4}, {\"end\": 10.08, \"word\": \" organizers\", \"start\": 9.56}, {\"end\": 10.46, \"word\": \" who\", \"start\": 10.08}, {\"end\": 10.8, \"word\": \" oppose\", \"start\": 10.46}, {\"end\": 10.92, \"word\": \" the\", \"start\": 10.8}, {\"end\": 11.34, \"word\": \" Hasina\'s\", \"start\": 10.92}, {\"end\": 11.78, \"word\": \" authoritarian\", \"start\": 11.34}, {\"end\": 12.14, \"word\": \" rule.\", \"start\": 11.78}, {\"end\": 12.76, \"word\": \" Specifically,\", \"start\": 12.36}, {\"end\": 13.0, \"word\": \" they\", \"start\": 12.92}, {\"end\": 13.1, \"word\": \" were\", \"start\": 13.0}, {\"end\": 13.38, \"word\": \" opposed\", \"start\": 13.1}, {\"end\": 13.5, \"word\": \" to\", \"start\": 13.38}, {\"end\": 13.6, \"word\": \" the\", \"start\": 13.5}, {\"end\": 13.96, \"word\": \" spoiled\", \"start\": 13.6}, {\"end\": 14.38, \"word\": \" system\", \"start\": 13.96}, {\"end\": 14.7, \"word\": \" that\", \"star