INSERT INTO `rounds` VALUES (9,'Panama WUDC 2025 Round 7','2026-02-14 22:01:40',1,'external_video',NULL,'british_parliamentary','This House Prefers the citizen welfare standard to the consumer welfare standard','Politics','SFWz9wKwvuM','public','{\"task\": \"transcribe\", \"text\": \"This is a debate about courts and justices and judges. So judges will interpret this as a sit and preside over certain appeals over mergers and acquisitions after regulators have regulated and companies sue if they think that this is unfair. Secondly, I think this framing is important because regulators will know that this is a legal doctrine that judges and courts will adopt, so therefore the way we approach regulated mergers and acquisitions will keep a broader scope in mind. For instance, they know they can get away with being able to complain that this merger acquisition has failed to conduct some environmental information surveys on how this merger acquisition would deplete energy resources or for instance, pollute in the local environment. This is important because our first argument is that the consumer welfare declines on the consumer welfare doctrines in the SAS rule are too narrow. The first thing I want to talk about here is that we\'re going to be able to essentially create companies to self-regulate before they go into the merger and acquisition process. So here\'s the through line, right? They will perceive that regulators will regulate against them if they do not give out information on things like the way they influence them like that. So therefore, they will preemptively have this information on hand in order to prevent things like like getting sued and stuff like that. The reason why this is important and the reason why they\'ll do this is threefold. Firstly, investors are wary, so they\'re going to be scared if this is too risky because of the media attention that will happen if they get sued for instance, other considerations. Secondly, legal fees are incredibly expensive to have lawyers and things like that. Thirdly, if you fail the first process in the district courts, you have to appeal again and appeal again and appeal again. These appeal phrases take a long time for you as well. So as a company, you want to make sure that you cover all your cases. Fourthly, you are acquiring another firm. So there\'s likely that that other firm might get cold feet if they feel as though you have not prepared enough for this process as well. The importance for this is because we reduce information asymmetry. So here\'s how I want to contextualize this. If a company knows that they will have the merchant acquisition locked because they have not provided information on pollution, what they will do is they\'ll go and hire consultants to make sure they conduct stuff like an environmental impact statement that reveals the total GHG emissions that will result from this merchant acquisition. The reason why we think this is fine is because if you have a locked capital, this means you have to have a locked capital in order to acquire another firm. So this is going to be calculated in your decision-calculating process. The reason why this information is good, firstly, I think it\'s good for the public to be aware of this information. That\'s to say, if you live in these communities that are going to be near these factories, near these data centers, your energy prices might increase if they come in. It\'s good for you to do things like have a long campaign or local campaigning for your local politicians to create ordinances against these type of companies coming in. But also secondly, I think it\'s really good for investors too. You might want to invest in this company after the merchant acquisition to know the type of things that they\'re getting into. Revealing all this information to the public is good for the company and for the market as a whole. The second argument I want to talk about is that we have more political will on our side of the house. I want to note that regulators do want to regulate. Because on their side of the house, they might think that regulators are going to do this in a bad way. But firstly, the reason why they do so in a bad way in the status quo is because they have too narrow a stick to adjudicate mergers and acquisitions. If it is the case that you can only block an M&A if they reduce the competition and prices, it is a lot easier for a company to budge the numbers, to hire people to say actually no, the competitor prices are going to decrease. But because we\'ve seen a proliferation of M&As in the hundreds, like 450 in the past year, the Department of Justice and the FTC currently do not have enough resources to investigate all of this. That\'s to say it is easy for M&As to go unnoticed. Secondly, they also have a repeal preference for doing this because for instance, they want to get promoted, they want to get more funding from Congress, they want to get more media attention on their agency so that they\'re able to do this. And thirdly, because there\'s bipartisan support. That\'s why for instance, a lot of people like Lisa Tan, because Lisa Tan is really harsh against tech, that\'s why we have things like conservatives, right? And for instance, because of this bipartisan support, we\'ve seen a lot of interest in this as a whole. The reason why this independently is not enough for them to block more M&As is because using this as a legal document is too low. The second argument I want to, the third argument I want to posit this week is that on the comparative, it\'s far worse. On the comparative, it is far worse to get environmental accountability and privacy accountability on their side. The first thing behind this is because things like privacy laws and environmental laws lag behind industry. So for instance, laws regarding the protection of consumer, like a welfare on data and AI, oftentimes have to catch up to the technology that\'s emerging in the South. Things like the environment, like EPA and stuff like that, constantly get repealed in the South. Like for instance, the Halliburton loophole, which allows the crafting companies to not have to dispose of wastewater properly is something that was repealed by corporate interest. I\'m going to prove to you why these type of regulators are not susceptible to these flaws, but before I continue, I\'ll take a few questions. Just still kind of explain why you think you have to solve any of these problems. Like why does it help with the environment? Why does it help with privacy? Why does it help with any of these things? So the thing that I mentioned is that it\'s a preemptive thing, right? So this is proactive. So before the M&A already occurs, they have to prove that, they have to use data studies and stuff like that to prove that there\'s going to be less environmental harm and stuff like that. So this happens before the M&A happens. And then I\'m going to prove to you why you now give courts more ability to have legal prosecution on R7,000. All right, so what happens on R7,000? What we think happens is when regulators, I don\'t know, when regulators, like in the deal chain and stuff like that, like regulating M&As, they\'re not susceptible to acts and stuff like that. They get to create their own doctrine when they\'re trying to do this. So when these regulators get sued, if other companies think that this is weak, what happens is this goes through the court process and this creates cases for future court decisions to be based on them. So for instance, if a utility company gets sued, then the facts of that case gets to be applied to future courts and things like that. The reason why this is important is because these doctrines actually give future courts legal basis to determine this information. The third argument I want to posit, sorry, the impact of this is that, firstly, what happens is we get less M&As on R7,000 because the barriers to that is much, much higher. So therefore, you have to ensure that the information you put out there is strong enough. Third argument, I\'ll close. Thank you very much for that speech. Now I\'d like to welcome the leader of the opposition, here. This is a follow-up, the lesson is well-understanded, and we can deal with the sets of problems that they talk about with other forms of direct regulation. If they want to say, just stay this week, it\'s not enforcing that direct regulation, those are the same weak states who will rightfully, incredibly badly, not to do the sets of things that they want to do, and instead subvert democracy to make things worse. Two arguments promote the opposition. First of all, this massively frees down investment and mergers and acquisitions, both of which I would. Secondly, I\'d like to see a worse, less easy, and ultimately more harmful stance to aim for. Let\'s start with why this frees down investment. The first thing to say is that it massively broadens the scope of what can be used. So the first premise here is why does this make mergers way? Firstly, this massively broadens the scope of what can be used to reject the merger. Secondly, that scope becomes nearly boundless, but it\'s non-economic harms, the nearly boundless categorization. And it\'s incredibly easy, even if there are codifications, to argue for further expansion of it, because you can say, well, the combination of this right and this right implies this further right, this implied right, natural laws that we ought to care about. So it\'s incredibly easy to go further. Thirdly, these are much more interpretable, because they\'re a lot more about value trade-offs, which also means that you raise the uncertainty, because judge-specific risk is more specific, it is higher, because particular judges are going to interpret this in many more different ways, whereas judges at the moment, when it\'s a less subjective stand, are likely to have more correlated views of how it works. And it also shows the willingness of courts and of legislators to continue to expand antitrust legislation into the future, which means that you\'re incredibly likely to have people think that mergers are less likely. Why is this a problem? First, because you get less mergers, and secondly, because you get less initial investment. Let\'s start with mergers. Obviously, when it\'s harder to get a merger approved, you\'re less likely to attempt that merger, because a high level of investment that has to go into setting up a merger and finding a merger partner, you have to do things like due diligence, set it up, that is an incredibly high cost, it won\'t do, or you might want to put it on, like, clearly you want to do it sometime, but on the margin you\'re less likely to do it, at the point where it\'s less likely to get through. And we think that mergers are on net good, because they lead to larger scale. It means you can operate more efficiently, because you get economies of scale. It makes firms more durable to idiosyncratic market shocks, so they\'re less likely to go down, leaving consumers without goods and workers without jobs. It means you can reach more consumers more easily, so you have more scale. It means you can invest more on net, you can do more innovation. It means that profitable parts of the business can cross-subsidise non-profitable parts of those businesses, though that means that these mergers are on net good. And I\'ll note that those mergers are likely to be pro-competitive, for the reason that, first of all, that under the consumer well-being standard, they wouldn\'t be let through, if they were not pro-competitive. This team says we just might not notice the fact that a merger had happened. In almost every jurisdiction around the world, and in a few months in Australia as well, there\'s mandatory notification of the regulator when you reach a certain threshold. They\'re not going to ignore that from happening. But secondly, I would argue that mergers are on net pro-competitive. And the reason for that is that without mergers, you often get one or two large firms who rise to the top, and then no one else is ever able to compete with them, or grow to compete with them. Because if it\'s theoretical competition, but I\'m 80% of the market and you\'re 2% of the market, you\'re never going to have the kind of economies of scale to be a reasonable competitor. But when all the smaller firms can amalgamate, they can then become reasonable competition. So actually, mergers are on net pro-competitive. I\'ll also note that there\'s now an incentive for larger firms not to provide business-to-business goods that allow a fixing of those externalities that would allow smaller firms to merge. So to take an example of this, Google letting you use their login on their website is really important for you being able to ameliorate privacy concerns. But if Google knew that it would allow the small players in the market to amalgamate, because there might be a concern otherwise that they would have too much data and there would be privacy concerns around it, they\'re unlikely to let those small players use that Google login. So they don\'t want to provide goods that allow the amelioration of those externalities, because they know that the amelioration of those externalities makes it easier for there to be competition with them. So now we get more competition. I\'ll also note that this now means that competition law becomes less popular on their side. First of all, which is now making decisions about things that aren\'t universally agreed on. Secondly, it\'s associated with the left, because under the status quo, the right can be pro-market, but can say, oh, this is necessary for the market to function efficiently. Whereas now, you\'re saying we should trade off market efficiency for other outcomes, which makes it far less popular. It can also be framed as a form of corruption, because you\'re using it as a political weapon. And you\'re now likely to have lots of people who lose their jobs and can make the argument that my job is to trade off for fucking squirrels. That makes competition law less popular, which means you\'re less likely to be forced in the first instance, if they\'re worried about competition. We now get less investment into really important things. And if you don\'t do the initial investment, if you\'re worried you might not be able to merge or acquiesce, first, you often do the initial investment because you want to get bought out in the end. And you\'re more likely to be able to make your business bigger if along the way you can purchase out other firms. So the lack of ability to emerge, it means that my initial investment is worth less, which means I\'m less likely to do that initial investment, yes. The issue is when these firms become too big to fill, you\'re unable to have these environmental and privacy regulations in the future, because a fine is a drop in the bucket for these large companies. You need to have this broader regulation. Then have larger fines. Like there are incredibly large fines that have been put against huge companies in the order of billions of dollars, where you just ban and you send people to jail. Like the regulation doesn\'t always work. On-net regulation is followed. Come on, most debater I\'ve seen in the world. Second argument, why is this a worse standard to aim for? Because when you\'re optimizing for their standard and more things, you can less optimize for our standard of economic things. I\'m here to explain why the economics is more important. First of all, it\'s easier to be able to optimize for. This is many more steps removed from the company. So you do the chain of causation is less certain. That makes all of the harms less speculative. It\'s also more dependent on exogenous outcomes that they can\'t control. So it\'s much harder to optimize for. And it\'s a value distinction, right? We don\'t want, it\'s trivially good that cheaper prices and better quality is better for everyone. But this is about a series of value trade-offs which makes it incredibly difficult to optimize for. But even if we could optimize for both equally, we would say this is a more important standard because economic conditions are the most important thing to most people around the world living close to the property line. Because this matters to everyone, not just people who particularly care about pricing. It matters to consumers, it matters to firms and their growth and their workers when they can get cheaper input goods. And we can probably predict it much better on our side when we have things like market modeling. We have lots of super analogous examples. We also get far better, I\'ll also note, we can on our side regulate out the set of things that they want to talk about. Because we can set a baseline. We can say you can\'t do these bad things for instance dumping, but you can\'t set a baseline for competition law because you\'re worried about, for instance, breaking out supply. You can\'t set a baseline for how much innovation you have to do. And the next thing to say is that consumer law actually feeds into other things. The consumer standard feeds into other things because when goods are cheaper, consumers can more optimize for themselves what kind of privacy they want because they can discriminate between goods far easier when the cost of doing so is less. Because economic development allows you to develop things that end up in the future contributing to those externalities. We also get less corruption on our side because when they talk about democracy, I don\'t think it\'s a good idea that the government is able to make decisions about who can and cannot speak. I think that the government shouldn\'t be making that decision. I think that in the worst case when democracy is actually under threat, that is incredibly likely to get weaponized in order to throw down your political enemies. That is a huge harm. For a speech now I\'d like to welcome the Deputy Prime Minister to the air. Okay, can everyone hear me if I speak like this? Two questions in this speech. First, on which side is regulation more likely to be successful? And second, the side that provides the most impactful regulation. On success, OO makes a number of claims here. The first thing they say is that this makes mergers and acquisitions more difficult because it relies on a more nebulous legal standard and more nebulous people using force of law. First, I want to point out that this doesn\'t interact with the material you get from Victor\'s hypothetical claims, why M&As are likely to be harmful in the first place, and in that list, which side is it? in the symmetric sizes of this content. I will explain why they\'re particularly harmful, why these harms are incredibly pernicious to individuals on the ground. But secondly, I just don\'t think this is true, right? Like I think many a time, the legal system is able to establish legal standards for things like harm to the environment, harm to privacy, for example, are very easy to prove because we have a broad sense of what kind of data we don\'t want to be in the public domain, or the kinds of data that we don\'t want to be in the hands of corporates. Crucially, I would flip this, because the test for increased prices, for example, is vastly more speculative. This is the aim of margins. Often it\'s obvious where there\'s a violation of privacy, and with the average of increased scrutiny on privacy, due to things, for example, like artificial intelligence, like abortion legislation, and so on, this is likely going to improve over time. With prices, what\'s different is, it is difficult for you to prove that the margin with which you reduce or increase prices is a margin that is likely to be consequential. Is a $1 increase enough? Is a $2 increase enough? That\'s what they are. They then say that a reduction in M&As is harmful in two ways. First, because on their side, in the absence of M&As, companies oper