INSERT INTO `sentences` VALUES (1,6,'Ladies and gentlemen, welcome to the grand final of the 29th World School Debating Championship in Bali, Indonesia.',1,18),(2,6,'My name is Nadia and I will be the chairperson of this debate, while Divta will be the timekeeper.',19,37),(3,6,'In this debate, each speaker will have 8 minutes to make their speech.',38,50),(4,6,'After 1 minute, the timekeeper will signal once like this.',51,60),(5,6,'After this, the debaters on the other team may offer point of information to the speaker.',61,76),(6,6,'The debaters must stand up when they offer point of information.',77,87),(7,6,'The speaker who has the floor may choose a step or decline a point when it\'s offered.',88,104),(8,6,'At 7 minutes, the timekeeper will again signal once like this.',105,115),(9,6,'After this, no more point of information may be offered for the rest of the speech.',116,131),(10,6,'At 8 minutes, the timekeeper will make a double signal like this.',132,143),(11,6,'After this, the speaker must conclude within a few seconds.',144,153),(12,6,'There will be a short break at the end of the speech for the judges to complete their notes.',154,172),(13,6,'After 6 main speeches, each team will have the opportunity to make a 4-minute reply speech.',173,189),(14,6,'This can be made by either the first or second speaker on each team.',190,203),(15,6,'No point of information are allowed during the reply speeches.',204,213),(16,6,'The opposition will make their reply speech first.',214,221),(17,6,'After 3 minutes, the timekeeper will signal once to let the speaker know they have 1 minute left.',222,239),(18,6,'After 4 minutes, the timekeeper will signal twice.',240,247),(19,6,'Before the debate begins, I would like to remind you to switch off all mobile phones and anything else which might disturb the debaters.',248,271),(20,6,'Should you need to leave or re-enter the room for any reason during the debate, please wait until the end of the speech to do so.',272,298),(21,6,'Audience members should be aware that they are not participating in the main debate.',299,312),(22,6,'I will now allow to introduce the judges for this debate.',313,323),(23,6,'They are Kip Obanda from the Philippines who is the chair judge for today\'s debate.',324,338),(24,6,'Aisha Ahmad from Pakistan.',339,342),(25,6,'Lungisit Lamini from Swaziland.',343,346),(26,6,'Yana Gilkel from Germany.',347,350),(27,6,'Kevin Moore from New Zealand.',351,355),(28,6,'Julio Meyer from Mexico.',356,359),(29,6,'Karin Markens from the Netherlands.',360,364),(30,6,'Yoni Cohen from Israel.',365,368),(31,6,'And Tamara Fernando from Sri Lanka.',369,374),(32,6,'In proposition for this debate, we have the team from England.',375,385),(33,6,'Their first speaker is Arthur James.',386,391),(34,6,'The second speaker is Edward Pressey.',392,397),(35,6,'The third speaker is Ken Zewields.',398,403),(36,6,'And the reply speaker is Edward Pressey.',404,410),(37,6,'There will be two other members of the team who will not be speaking this afternoon.',411,426),(38,6,'But we would like to acknowledge their role on the team.',427,437),(39,6,'They are Hannah Taylor and Tara Salis.',438,444),(40,6,'And in opposition, we have the team from Singapore.',445,453),(41,6,'Their first speaker is Wei Shu Yang.',454,460),(42,6,'The second speaker is Ryan Wee.',461,466),(43,6,'The third speaker is Aowei Ho.',467,472),(44,6,'And the reply speaker is Ryan Wee.',473,479),(45,6,'There will be two other members of the team who will not be speaking this afternoon.',480,495),(46,6,'But we would like to acknowledge their role on the team.',496,506),(47,6,'They are Xiao Van Tan and Jonathan Yap.',507,514),(48,6,'The motion for this debate is, this house supports restrictions on free speech to combat the rise of right-wing populism.',515,535),(49,6,'It gives me a great pleasure to invite the first speaker for the proposition team to open the debate.',536,554),(50,6,'Please welcome Arthur James.',555,558),(51,6,'Ladies, gentlemen, fellow wizards, muggles.',559,563),(52,6,'Right-wing populist parties, individuals and activists worldwide are a threat to minorities and to democracies.',564,579),(53,6,'Side government solves this problem.',580,584),(54,6,'What are our definitions in this debate?',585,591),(55,6,'We think courts should decide if these are right-wing populists using five criteria.',592,605),(56,6,'The first is nativism, i.e.',606,611),(57,6,'the sake-goating of immigrants and ethnic minorities.',612,619),(58,6,'The second is hostility towards elites, particularly institutions like media and the courts.',620,632),(59,6,'The third is excessive authoritarian approach to law and order.',633,642),(60,6,'The fourth is a disregard for political correctness and truth.',643,652),(61,6,'And the fifth, and crucially, is that because of a desire to appeal to a broad coalition of disaffected individuals, spanning the socio-political divide and often with different political desires, they often have a shallow policy agenda designed to appeal to as many people as possible.',653,699),(62,6,'What does it look like?',700,704),(63,6,'It looks like Trump in the US, Duterte in the Philippines, the AFD in Germany.',705,719),(64,6,'What\'s our model for dealing with these individuals and groups?',720,729),(65,6,'We would institute a range of restrictions on free speech of political parties and their activists.',730,745),(66,6,'That goes from shutting down websites and individuals which advocate on their behalf, like Breitbart or Milo Yiannopoulos, to not providing them with a platform, not reporting on them in the media or allowing them to speak in town hall events, to banning them from social media or, in some instances, being happy to ban them from contesting elections entirely.',746,805),(67,6,'We think that\'s an extension of the precedent set in the banning of the Neo-Nazi party in Germany and of states around the world in banning hate speech.',806,834),(68,6,'We would note that while the unique phenomenon of right-wing populism is a problem today, where a similar problem to arise with left-wing populism, we would support this policy there too.',835,867),(69,6,'We have two burdens in this debate.',868,874),(70,6,'The first is to show you that this policy is justified, and the second is to show you that this is an effective way of dealing with the danger that right-wing populists present.',875,908),(71,6,'With that in mind, we\'re bringing you three arguments.',909,917),(72,6,'The first is that we combat the threat to minorities.',918,927),(73,6,'The second is that this harms the development of solutions to the problems which lead to right-wing populists coming about at all.',928,950),(74,6,'And the third, in Ed\'s speech, is how this is a fundamental affront to democracy.',951,965),(75,6,'Before I move on to my first argument about how we actually combat the threat to minorities, what are the principal parameters within which this debate takes place? Two things.',966,995),(76,6,'The first is that democracy necessitates a balance of rights because the rights of individuals sometimes contradict each other.',996,1014),(77,6,'The fact that I have my wand in the air contradicts Kensa\'s right not to get poked in the eye.',1015,1034),(78,6,'We believe that protecting the most vulnerable from physical harm weighs up more than the ability of certain individuals to have freedom of speech.',1035,1058),(79,6,'I\'m going to be dealing with that principally in my speech.',1059,1069),(80,6,'The second principal parameter which this debate takes place within is that democracy can only happen if it\'s truth-led.',1070,1089),(81,6,'When you are anti-truth, you forfeit your right to the protections of free speech.',1090,1104),(82,6,'Ed\'s going to be talking to you about that principally in his speech.',1105,1117),(83,6,'Before I move on to my first argument about how we combat the threat to minorities, what exactly is this one characteristic that is so exclusive to right-wing populism?',1118,1147),(84,6,'Wouldn\'t you end up banning a lot of other ideologies that have similar characteristics?',1148,1161),(85,6,'Okay, we told you that courts are good at deciding whether things and individuals fall into certain categories.',1162,1179),(86,6,'We gave you literally five characteristics that define right-wing populist movements.',1180,1191),(87,6,'If courts decide that an individual or a group is part of that movement, then they\'ll ban those movements, then they\'ll institute these kinds of free speech protections.',1192,1219),(88,6,'Not sure what they\'re getting at there.',1220,1226),(89,6,'On to my first argument which is about how we combat the threat to minority groups.',1227,1242),(90,6,'Because right-wing populism thrives on riling people up and channeling their anger.',1243,1255),(91,6,'That\'s because right-wing populists are overwhelmingly problem and not solution-oriented. Why?',1256,1268),(92,6,'Because the reason that they\'re so popular is that they\'re able to appeal to diverse coalitions of individuals.',1269,1286),(93,6,'Often with very little in common.',1287,1292),(94,6,'Someone living in a trailer park in northern Florida has basically nothing in common with a soccer mom living in the Midwest.',1293,1314),(95,6,'But they\'re united by being broadly disaffected with the status quo.',1315,1325),(96,6,'The policy demands of these people, economically and socially, are very different.',1326,1337),(97,6,'So, in order to maintain this coalition, right-wing populists do two things.',1338,1350),(98,6,'Firstly, they promise very shallow things.',1351,1356),(99,6,'They have very unspecific policies or unsubstantiated, catch-all, magic wand policies.',1357,1368),(100,6,'Secondly, they focus on the problems that their followers face as opposed to the solutions which divide them.',1369,1386),(101,6,'In an attempt to flatten the causes of these problems into something which can nominally easily be solved, they often blame minority groups for all of these issues.',1387,1414),(102,6,'That means drug dealers in the Philippines, Mexicans in the US, Moroccans in the Netherlands, are blamed for all of the problems which the followers of populists face.',1415,1442),(103,6,'The reason that blaming minorities leads to increased violence is threefold.',1443,1453),(104,6,'The first is that they take the anger that individuals have and they direct it at a minority for a political advantage.',1454,1475),(105,6,'This anger goes beyond normal anger because the issues that these individuals are facing every single day suddenly becomes tied to minorities.',1476,1497),(106,6,'Secondly, they change permission mechanisms because they make these views mainstream.',1498,1508),(107,6,'That means people think it\'s okay to be or act racistly because views have been legitimized by others, whether in rallies or in votes.',1509,1532),(108,6,'Thirdly, when these populist parties get in power, because they can\'t implement legitimate policies without alienating a segment of their voter base, instead they\'re forced to use discriminatory policies to distract from their failure to solve problems.',1533,1569),(109,6,'So Trump is forced to try and implement a Muslim ban to distract from the fact that he can\'t actually solve the problems that his voters are facing without alienating other parts of his voter base.',1570,1605),(110,6,'What are the impacts of this?',1606,1611),(111,6,'You create a society where minorities live in fear.',1612,1620),(112,6,'Fear of being attacked, fear of being spat on on their way to school, fear of not being able to get a job because they\'re discriminated against in interviews.',1621,1649),(113,6,'In extreme cases, their freedom of movement is restricted when they\'re too afraid to leave their homes.',1650,1666),(114,6,'Look, we don\'t think it\'s surprising that in the aftermath of the Brexit vote, hate crimes went up 40%.',1667,1685),(115,6,'That during the US presidential election, hate crimes against religious and ethnic minorities went up 41%.',1686,1701),(116,6,'That\'s not surprising, and our policy solves this by reducing the ability of right-wing populists to spread their hatred.',1702,1721),(117,6,'My second argument is about how this harms the development of solutions which affect the disaffected.',1722,1737),(118,6,'Look, we think there\'s a legitimate debate to be had over some of these issues, right?',1738,1753),(119,6,'We think that disaffected people\'s concerns unquestionably should be listened to, and there\'s a conversation that we should have about, for example, how undocumented workers are able to undercut wages.',1754,1783),(120,6,'Why does the free speech of right-wing populists actually undermine that debate? Two reasons.',1784,1798),(121,6,'First, because it drags politics to the right-wing populist gutter.',1799,1809),(122,6,'Because right-wing populists try to take chunks out of the voters of centrist parties by doing things like scapegoating minorities.',1810,1830),(123,6,'Those centrist parties are forced to move to the right in order to try and keep hold of those voters.',1831,1850),(124,6,'The DPP in Denmark was able to pull the Danish Liberal Party right to the point when a party that previously was pro-immigration and asylum seekers ran on a platform of explicitly limiting the number of asylum seekers able to come into that country.',1851,1895),(125,6,'The second way that they distort the politics and they harm the development of solutions is because of a lack of nuance in the language that these politicians use.',1896,1924),(126,6,'Populist candidates use charisma and shocking language to get free air time and to show themselves to be saying what the people are thinking.',1925,1948),(127,6,'Other parties, in order to compete, are forced to also use shock tactics.',1949,1961),(128,6,'So One Nation in Australia was able to change the debate on immigration to the point when the yellow peril was a phrase that was actually commonly used.',1962,1989),(129,6,'We think you can\'t have a proper political debate when the language that you\'re using is un-nuanced, when you\'re conflating asylum seekers and refugees and immigrants in a way that only populist parties force politics to do.',1990,2027),(130,6,'On our side of the house, we\'re able to escape the noise of populism.',2028,2041),(131,6,'We\'re able to rise above the din and we\'re able to have a conversation between centrist politicians which leads to actual nuanced legitimate solutions to these problems.',2042,2068),(132,6,'In Canada, they were able to have a nuanced conversation about immigration free from the noise of populists which means they were able to impose an immigration policy that is actually fair and that actually helps the individuals on the ground.',2069,2109),(133,6,'Because Team England is the side which protects minorities and actually helps solve the issues which lead populist parties to come about in the first place, I beg you to propose.',2110,2140),(134,6,'The first proposition speaker spent 8 minutes 1 second.',2141,2149),(135,6,'I thank the first proposition speaker for their speech.',2150,2158),(136,6,'And now to open the case for the opposition, please welcome Wei Shu Yang.',2159,2172),(137,6,'Proposition\'s argument is that right-wing populism is an ideology that can be distilled into five key characteristics.',2173,2190),(138,6,'That\'s actually scientific dissection.',2191,2194),(139,6,'Honestly, I think it\'s very demeaning for their side to say that a quarter of all French people and millions of people all around the world are fundamentally nativistic.',2195,2223),(140,6,'They\'re not politically correct.',2224,2227),(141,6,'They just want to lash out at their establishment.',2228,2236),(142,6,'I think that what they\'re doing is to reduce the legitimate viewpoints of all of these people and I think it\'s evidence of their liberal arrogance to say that these views should be dismissed from society.',2237,2272),(143,6,'We stand against that.',2273,2276),(144,6,'What\'s our stance?',2277,2279),(145,6,'One, we will not impose any restrictions on right-wing populist speech.',2280,2291),(146,6,'Two, obviously we don\'t have to stand for threats to an individual\'s safety like death threats and we will prosecute for acts of violence accordingly.',2292,2316),(147,6,'Three, we broadly believe in the value of free speech, even those that are racially tinged.',2317,2332),(148,6,'There may come a point where the intent is so malicious and the expressive function of such speech is marginal.',2333,2352),(149,6,'In those cases, we will judiciously prosecute for hate speech.',2353,2362),(150,6,'Four, proposition\'s burden is not just to defend current laws restricting free speech, but they need to defend restricting the entirety of this political ideology.',2363,2387),(151,6,'Not just the white noise, but also the very legitimate viewpoints that are communicated.',2388,2401),(152,6,'We\'re going to forward three arguments.',2402,2407),(153,6,'One, that right-wing populist speech is legitimate and should not be restricted.',2408,2420),(154,6,'This deals with the fundamental premise in their case that right-wing populism is something we don\'t want in society.',2421,2440),(155,6,'Two, on the risks of government abuse.',2441,2447),(156,6,'And three, my second speaker will explain how they harm political discourse.',2448,2459),(157,6,'But before that, some rebuttals.',2460,2464),(158,6,'They first told us that a ban was justified because a democracy is about a balance of rights.',2465,2482),(159,6,'But this assumes that their policy is the only way to protect minorities.',2483,2495),(160,6,'This is not true because ethnic and racial minorities can always vote for the Democratic Party.',2496,2511),(161,6,'With democratic chants and melances, they are in place.',2512,2520),(162,6,'But you know who doesn\'t have an alternative?',2521,2528),(163,6,'It\'s the millions of disenfranchised workers within the Rust Belt who cannot get another way to express their political views on their political spectrum.',2529,2552),(164,6,'We allow for representation.',2553,2556),(165,6,'We allow legitimate viewpoints to be expressed.',2557,2563),(166,6,'They then say, but these are just distractions.',2564,2571),(167,6,'It\'s just white noise.',2572,2575),(168,6,'Right-wing populism isn\'t solution-oriented.',2576,2581),(169,6,'I think this is honestly very demeaning for, to see that half the people in America don\'t want to vote for actual policies.',2582,2604),(170,6,'There are very legitimate policies that can be carried out.',2605,2614),(171,6,'For instance, immigration quotas, trade protectionism, etc.',2615,2621),(172,6,'It\'s not just all white noise.',2622,2627),(173,6,'They then talk to us about how we may instill fear in minorities.',2628,2640),(174,6,'Now a person\'s response to such speech could be fear, but it could also be one of moral indignation.',2641,2659),(175,6,'And there are many reasons to suggest why moral indignation is the more likely response, rather than feeling threatened.',2660,2678),(176,6,'The reason for this is because the cost of expressing free speech in a status quo is very low.',2679,2697),(177,6,'A lot of this speech happens within social media.',2698,2706),(178,6,'So these are people who agree with you, etc.',2707,2715),(179,6,'There\'s a reason why when many African Americans listen to Trump\'s tweet, the way they respond is not a fear or threat.',2716,2737),(180,6,'But they respond by retweeting anti-Trump messages and having them shared and affirmed by people who share their beliefs.',2738,2757),(181,6,'Before I move on, yes.',2758,2762),(182,6,'Do you support restrictions on hate speech that, when spread by.',2763,2773),(183,6,'.. I just told you my policy as to why, at a certain point, where if it\'s so egregious and the intent is so malicious, and there\'s like very marginal expressive function, we think we will censor those.',2774,2810),(184,6,'But we think for the vast majority of right-wing populist speech, it is not like that.',2811,2827),(185,6,'That\'s a majority burden we have to prove.',2828,2835),(186,6,'Their second argument to us was about discourse.',2836,2843),(187,6,'The fundamental premise here is that right-wing populism is harmful.',2844,2854),(188,6,'It never provided us an alternative to the problems we see today.',2855,2866),(189,6,'My first substantive will now show you why right-wing populist speech is legitimate and should not be restricted.',2867,2885),(190,6,'The premise of my first argument, why is free speech valuable?',2886,2896),(191,6,'Our identities don\'t exist in a vacuum.',2897,2903),(192,6,'They only gain meaning through expression and interaction with other beliefs.',2904,2914),(193,6,'Hence, free speech is a fundamental right that we should not discard for mere convenience.',2915,2929),(194,6,'Why is right-wing populist speech particularly vital?',2930,2937),(195,6,'Since the 1990s, there has been a radical convergence towards neoliberal centrism in politics.',2938,2951),(196,6,'Until recently, most major parties were pro-free trade and globalization.',2952,2962),(197,6,'Democrats under Clinton converged with Republicans on free trade, while Labour in the UK co-opted many aspects of Thatcherism under Blair.',2963,2984),(198,6,'Why is this consensus harmful?',2985,2989),(199,6,'One, because a democracy without choice is not a democracy.',2990,2999),(200,6,'When there\'s no party that comes close to representing your interests, then democracy has failed you.',3000,3015),(201,6,'What right-wing populism does is to embrace and endorse viewpoints which are perfectly defensible, like the right-wing populist speech.',3016,3036),(202,6,'government having a foremost duty to its citizens.',3037,3044),(203,6,'So when they tell us it\'s white noise, we think it\'s a legitimate standpoint.',3045,3058),(204,6,'Two, because it disenfranchises the millions of workers in the Rust Belt whose livelihoods have been imperiled by free trade and immigration.',3059,3080),(205,6,'They are neglected by a Democratic Party preoccupied with identity politics and a Republican Party that stands for an unbridled free market.',3081,3102),(206,6,'These are problems that exist now, they have no solution.',3103,3112),(207,6,'Those left behind by free trade are left voiceless.',3113,3121),(208,6,'What right-wing popular speech does is to signal that one, these communities have been disenfranchised and two, that they are electorally determinate.',3122,3144),(209,6,'Its upsurgence demands the attention of the political establishment.',3145,3153),(210,6,'It has catalyzed the rise in class-based left-leaning politics as Sanders rises in the Democratic Party and Corbyn brings Labour back to its socialist roots.',3154,3180),(211,6,'So even if we can agree that right-wing populism itself is a bad idea, it does result in a recalibration of the political spectrum that we think is good for representation.',3181,3212),(212,6,'Now we acknowledge that right-wing popular speech may have unsavoury elements and this deals with their premise that this unsavoury speech is valuable.',3213,3236),(213,6,'But before I move on, yes.',3237,3242),(214,6,'Right-wing populists like Trump are known for fake news and lies.',3243,3254),(215,6,'Why does that provide a valuable choice for voters if their votes are based on misinformation?',3255,3270),(216,6,'One, I think the fact that they believe in this fake news or even know it\'s fake news but still vote for it is still a legitimate form of political expression that they may not care this much about these facts.',3271,3311),(217,6,'My second argument will show you how you worsen everything you are talking about.',3312,3325),(218,6,'We think that even unsavoury elements of speech has immense expressive value for two reasons.',3326,3340),(219,6,'One, because it is unreasonable and elitist to demand that a disenfranchised speak only in the language and vernacular which the elites find palatable, what they call in their policy, political correctness.',3341,3372),(220,6,'Anti-immigration sentiment expressed without extensive qualifications and hedging that we are familiar with in our political discourse does often sound quite racist, but not everyone has access to the sanitised language of Team England, particularly for the worst of.',3373,3412),(221,6,'Two, because problematic language has a unique signalling effect.',3413,3421),(222,6,'Saying we need to overhaul immigration quotas doesn\'t convey the same intensity as saying immigrants should get out of our nation.',3422,3442),(223,6,'The latter represents a fundamental disillusionment towards globalisation and the belief that integration is hopeless.',3443,3457),(224,6,'Hence, right-wing populism is an indispensable part of speech and democracy that should be preserved.',3458,3473),(225,6,'My second argument is on the risks of government abuse.',3474,3483),(226,6,'My thesis is that propositions policy is a vast expansion of the state\'s regulatory power over free speech.',3484,3501),(227,6,'Given all the ambiguities involved, this is a huge target for abuse.',3502,3513),(228,6,'Just listen to the five criteria they presented.',3514,3521),(229,6,'What is nativism?',3522,3524),(230,6,'What is an attack on the establishment?',3525,3531),(231,6,'If I critique the New York Times, is that considered under their policy?',3532,3544),(232,6,'This means that all their anti-truths and hate speech are incredibly fake.',3545,3557),(233,6,'And this is particularly true because fake news, as they want to talk about in the POI, heavily involves judgements of character, like claims that Bill Clinton is a womaniser, or causal judgements that the police shot a black man because of racism.',3558,3600),(234,6,'The truth of these claims cannot be objectively proven.',3601,3609),(235,6,'Furthermore, whether something passes the threshold of hate speech is highly dependent on context and intent.',3610,3625),(236,6,'They can\'t just throw this away with a mechanism in their policy.',3626,3637),(237,6,'For instance, Jeremy Corbyn has said that immigrants undermine labour standards, but presumably he wouldn\'t be prosecuted under proposition.',3638,3656),(238,6,'Therefore, the content of such speech is subjective.',3657,3664),(239,6,'But beyond that, the prosecution is subjective as well.',3665,3673),(240,6,'In most of criminal law, governments don\'t prosecute every case because there are too many of them.',3674,3690),(241,6,'This means they pick and choose.',3691,3696),(242,6,'This is itself political because the legal process only begins once a case is brought to court.',3697,3713),(243,6,'However, prosecutors have very strong personal incentives to act in the interests of their partisan masters because they want to gain a promotion and curry favour.',3714,3739),(244,6,'This means that often, the kinds of speech that are curtailed are those that hurt the government\'s partisan interests.',3740,3758),(245,6,'This means that they allow governments to artificially entrench power because the prosecution will only choose to attack those news and those forms of hate speech that disfavours them.',3759,3787),(246,6,'It also means that on their side, journalists are either forced to self-censor because they don\'t want to be dragged to court or they\'re forced to review confidential speech and confidential sources when they have to defend themselves in court.',3788,3828),(247,6,'Therefore, we believe that silence is oppression. We oppose.',3829,3837),(248,6,'The first opposition speaker spent 8 minutes 14 seconds.',3838,3846),(249,6,'I thank the first opposition speaker for their speech.',3847,3855),(250,6,'And now, to continue the case for the proposition, please welcome Edward Bracey.',3856,3868),(251,6,'It is problematic for side opposition to stand up and simply deny the racist vitriol that is not only endemic when we look at actual examples of right-wing populists on the ground, the Front National in France, Trump in the United States, Duterte in the Philippines, Gert Wilders in the Netherlands, the list goes on.',3869,3924),(252,6,'But second of all, when we give you three mechanisms in our first speech as to why this kind of racist vitriol is not only endemic and the result of the fact that you have a very wide but very shallow coalition, but second of all, is almost inevitably transferred to the minorities about whom it is made.',3925,3982),(253,6,'That\'s not good enough from side opposition in this debate.',3983,3992),(254,6,'I\'m going to bring you an argument as to how right-wing populism constitutes a fundamental affront to democracy and the rights of everyone else in a democracy who does not vote for these people.',3993,4027),(255,6,'But before that, five pieces of rebuttal.',4028,4034),(256,6,'The first thing they say is that minorities don\'t actually feel afraid.',4035,4046),(257,6,'And the only evidence they give for this in their first speech is, oh, you have some African-American women retweeting anti-Trump tweets. Two responses.',4047,4072),(258,6,'First, they do not engage with the three lines we bring you in our first speech as to how it is actually horrific to have someone who is in their political incentives to stand up and say that you are a less valuable human being than someone else.',4073,4120),(259,6,'But I challenge side opposition to tell the women who are raped by Duterte\'s army because he legitimizes that, or, for example, the women who face race-based abuse in the United States as a direct response of Trump\'s riots, the Mexicans who are beaten up after Trump rallies, that they shouldn\'t be afraid in any meaningful sense of the word.',4121,4181),(260,6,'Second thing they bring us is that, ah, these are voicing concerns that would otherwise be avoided.',4182,4198),(261,6,'Four responses here.',4199,4201),(262,6,'First, they concede in their very generic characterization of freedom of speech that, and when they stand up and say we support things like hate speech, that there are circumstances under which we are happy to tell people that they cannot vote for a particular individual.',4202,4247),(263,6,'That is, if we can prove to you that the harms to the wider society, i.e.',4248,4264),(264,6,', the democratic norms of that society and specific minority groups are undermined.',4265,4276),(265,6,'That is why they themselves do not believe that people should be able to vote for the neo-Nazi party.',4277,4296),(266,6,'And in doing so, they concede that democracy has to operate within a range.',4297,4310),(267,6,'Second of all, they give us no analysis as to why specifically addressing the concerns of these people is unique to right-wing populists and rather just right-wing movements.',4311,4340),(268,6,'We are pointing to, for example, the Republican Party in the U.S.',4341,4353),(269,6,', the conservatives in the United Kingdom, groups that attack the very kinds of policies that they stand for on their side of the House, things like having tighter immigration controls, but do so without saying that Mexicans are rapists.',4354,4392),(270,6,'Third, this falls down because they fundamentally have to prove to you that the benefits accrued to these people are actually achieved.',4393,4414),(271,6,'We gave you structural analysis in our first speech that has not been responded to as to how the shallowness of your coalition necessarily means you do not help the people in the Rust Belt, you do not help the people who fundamentally give you their democratic mandate.',4415,4462),(272,6,'Third point of rebuttal, they say these are legitimate policies.',4463,4472),(273,6,'Again, two responses.',4473,4475),(274,6,'First, we would challenge the factual legitimacy of lots of the policies that individuals like Duterte or Trump have passed, because the vast majority of them are not restricting trade or placing limits on the power of big businesses or giving more welfare to the working man.',4476,4522),(275,6,'They are instead avoided because they recognize there is inherent conflict between supporting Obamacare for those that want it and also not supporting Obamacare for those in your mandate who do not want it.',4523,4556),(276,6,'The kinds of policies you actually get passed are the ban on trans individuals in the army, the ban on Muslims from particular countries, because those are the ones it is easiest to unite your base behind.',4557,4593),(277,6,'Second of all, again, this is predicated on these policies actually being fulfilled and people actually wanting them.',4594,4611),(278,6,'We point to the example of the fact that only 28% of United States citizens now support Donald Trump as evidence of the fact that no matter what he says about these policies, he is probably not implementing them.',4612,4650),(279,6,'Fourth and finally of all, they throw out this really generic argument about how this policy could possibly be abused because of the vague criteria we implement.',4651,4677),(280,6,'We tell you that the courts make exactly this kind of calculation the whole time.',4678,4692),(281,6,'And you know how we know that is effective?',4693,4701),(282,6,'Because they stand by exactly the same mechanism when they talk about things like slander, when they talk about things like hate speech, when they talk about things like incitement to violence.',4702,4733),(283,6,'If you can disseminate specific criteria and attach them to those instances of racial abuse, presumably we can also attach them to the five criteria we brought you in our first speech.',4734,4765),(284,6,'Before I move on to my argument about how right-wing populism is fundamentally a front to everyone\'s rights, yes, come on.',4766,4787),(285,6,'This obviously operates on a scale, right?',4788,4794),(286,6,'So on the smaller end of things, if an individual person is racist and inciting other people to go and physically harm someone else, then possibly, if that person does so with 100,000 Twitter followers and the direct response of that policy is that hate crime increases 41% in the week after they are elected, yes.',4795,4851),(287,6,'Okay, my argument, right-wing populism and the affront to democracy.',4852,4862),(288,6,'We think right-wing populism undermines the integrity of democracy and is therefore a threat to the democratic rights of all. Two strands.',4863,4885),(289,6,'First, it poisons real democratic debate.',4886,4891),(290,6,'There are a couple of pieces of context we need to understand for this argument to make sense.',4892,4909),(291,6,'First, right-wing populists abuse the truth and shift their stances.',4910,4920),(292,6,'That is because they have broad but shallow coalitions brought together by conflicting vague promises, i.e.',4921,4937),(293,6,'they promise simultaneity to slash Obamacare but also to provide welfare to those in need.',4938,4952),(294,6,'We put you to the example of Duterte as someone who literally has a team of 20 people hired to act as internet trolls and spread misinformation on the internet.',4953,4982),(295,6,'Second of all, they dress up their opponents as conspiratorial elites and fundamentally use that as a tool to not engage with them.',4983,5005),(296,6,'They demonise their portrayal of their opponents and as a consequence, when you try and challenge someone like Duterte or Trump or Gert Wilders on their prejudices, the response you get is, see, see, this is exactly what the liberal media is doing time and time again.',5006,5052),(297,6,'Third thing on this, they rely on propagating fear and prejudice.',5053,5063),(298,6,'Again, because they cannot unite their coalition through legitimate policy, because the people they draw together are drawn under vague and conflicting terms, they are reliant on common fears and prejudices.',5064,5094),(299,6,'The takeaway from this argument is that the key tenant, the key appeal of lots of these individuals, is inherently irrational.',5095,5115),(300,6,'That is to say they rely on the propagation of a fear that probably should not exist.',5116,5132),(301,6,'All of these fundamentally undermine the debate necessary to productive, competitive electoral politics. Why?',5133,5146),(302,6,'First, because when you twist and muddy the truth of a debate, you challenge the reality in response to which that debate existed in the first place.',5147,5173),(303,6,'When fake news and alternative facts are placed on the same pedestal as actual factual realities, you cannot have a functional discussion about certain issues.',5174,5198),(304,6,'You cannot have a functional discussion about undocumented workers in the United States when it is a fact that Mexicans are rapists in the same way that it is a fact that Nieto is the president of Mexico.',5199,5236),(305,6,'Second of all, likewise, you also cannot have a debate between two reasonable sides that engage with each other and provide for the voter fundamentally a response, that is, which of these two options is probably better for your interests, when one of those sides systematically fails to engage with the other, both of the consequence of the fact that they use literally who those other people are as a way of attacking them rather than attacking their policies,',5237,5315),(306,6,'when second of all, they have terrorized the people that support them and struck fear into their hearts to the extent that they will not hear logical reason.',5316,5343),(307,6,'Second strand of this argument is that these individuals attack the very framework of democracy.',5344,5358),(308,6,'Part of their anti-establishment narrative on which they rely is to attack institutions like courts, like electoral processes.',5359,5377),(309,6,'That is why we see individuals like Gert Wilders in the Netherlands and Donald Trump not only threatening to punish the courts if they get into power but refusing to accept election results.',5378,5410),(310,6,'Insofar as democracy relies on the balance of power that is supported both by the fact that you give a massive mandate to the individual that you give your vote to but also by the fact that there are some checks and balances by an independent body to stop that person abusing that power, democracy is fundamentally perverted when justices and the courts are fundamentally too afraid to do their job.',5411,5481),(311,6,'The takeaway of this whole argument is twofold.',5482,5489),(312,6,'First, if you weigh this debate based on democratic integrity and the rights of individuals, the very presence of right-wing populists makes it harder for people on the ground to come to terms with who they are voting for, why they are supporting that person and ultimately what they should draw out of that.',5490,5544),(313,6,'We would draw the analogy to perjury and so far as we\'re happy to limit free speech in the short term if it ensures the integrity of a process that is vital to our society in the long run.',5545,5583),(314,6,'So proud to propose.',5584,5587),(315,6,'The second proposition speaker spent 8 minutes 8 seconds.',5588,5596),(316,6,'I thank the second proposition speaker for their speech.',5597,5605),(317,6,'And now to continue the case for the opposition, please welcome Ryan Wee.',5606,5618),(318,6,'Let me be clear.',5619,5622),(319,6,'This is a debate about the individuals whom politics has forgotten.',5623,5633),(320,6,'The individuals alienated when factories closed down.',5634,5640),(321,6,'Individuals who may not be experts in governance but are experts in their own lives.',5641,5655),(322,6,'Team England wants to talk about fear?',5656,5662),(323,6,'I tell you, what you fear is when you lose hope because mainstream political parties no longer represent you.',5663,5681),(324,6,'And honestly, it is very demeaning for Team England to come up here and tell us that the millions of individuals have been duped, that they have been deceived and that they do not know what is right for them.',5682,5721),(325,6,'We reject that on Team Opposition. Two questions.',5722,5729),(326,6,'First, why is right-wing populism a legitimate viewpoint?',5730,5738),(327,6,'Second, how do we improve politics?',5739,5744),(328,6,'First, why is it a legitimate viewpoint?',5745,5751),(329,6,'And I would like to point out that the characterisation actually shifted from first to second.',5752,5767),(330,6,'And first it was about these nice five scientific characteristics about what right-wing populism is.',5768,5783),(331,6,'But second speaker is incitement to violence and hate speech.',5784,5793),(332,6,'To be clear, we were very clear on our stance.',5794,5803),(333,6,'All their problems about violence, we can prosecute the act of violence.',5804,5815),(334,6,'They need to tell us why prosecuting the speech is the most important thing in this case.',5816,5832),(335,6,'Furthermore, we talked about how we are willing to err on the side of caution when it comes to hate speech.',5833,5853),(336,6,'But we think it is generally clear when conditions are egregious enough.',5854,5865),(337,6,'I want to be clear, they did not actually touch our analysis about the general centrist consensus.',5866,5882),(338,6,'All they said is that, but the Republican Party can represent you.',5883,5894),(339,6,'Do you know why the Republican Party represents them now?',5895,5904),(340,6,'Because Trump was elected.',5905,5908),(341,6,'Because the Republican elites realised that for far too long they have been leaving these individuals behind and not caring about their interests.',5909,5931),(342,6,'To be clear, the Republicans did not represent these people before the right-wing came to power.',5932,5948),(343,6,'That is why when they say this drags politics into the gutter, I would say it is not a gutter.',5949,5968),(344,6,'I would say it is views that people want, views that they actually stand for, policies they actually care about.',5969,5988),(345,6,'And I think that these individuals do know what is good for them.',5989,6001),(346,6,'So we can agree with our first speaker\'s principle that democracy is about a balance of rights.',6002,6018),(347,6,'Yes, we think they are taking away the fundamental right for these individuals to have a party stand for them.',6019,6038),(348,6,'And we think that is a right we should protect.',6039,6048),(349,6,'They said, ah, but that\'s fake news and it\'s misinformation and people aren\'t debating on truth and facts.',6049,6066),(350,6,'I think generally people are becoming more aware of partisan biases.',6067,6077),(351,6,'I think democracy actually solves for fake news because fake news justice credits its source.',6078,6092),(352,6,'Think about it.',6093,6095),(353,6,'Alex Jones basically spread the lie of how Sandy Hook was a conspiracy created by the government.',6096,6112),(354,6,'After that, he pretty much lost all the credibility he had.',6113,6123),(355,6,'I think people can discern and people who are voting for Trump, maybe they understand there is fake news.',6124,6142),(356,6,'But if they decide that the policies that he is standing for are more important than his fake news, then I think that is a choice they should be able to make.',6143,6174),(357,6,'That even if they don\'t believe everything he says, that the fundamental things he stands for are important.',6175,6192),(358,6,'No thank you.',6193,6195),(359,6,'Furthermore, we think that on our side, we pointed out very clearly how sometimes what they call unpleasant are things that we need to respect.',6196,6220),(360,6,'They completely dropped my first speaker\'s two tiers of how sometimes unpalatable language is essential to expressing thoughts.',6221,6238),(361,6,'How in team affirmative, they police thoughts in a way that discriminates against the not well-off.',6239,6255),(362,6,'Being born into an elite aristocracy is not a privilege we all share.',6256,6268),(363,6,'For some people, it is understandable that it is important for them to be able to express their thoughts in this language.',6269,6290),(364,6,'We think that is an important thing we should respect.',6291,6300),(365,6,'Furthermore, we talked about the colour and intensity of thought, how sometimes things they call unpalatable are ways and the only way for these individuals to express their true political choice.',6301,6331),(366,6,'The solutions that these politicians stand for may not be perfect, but we think that the point is that these solutions are far closer to what people actually want.',6332,6360),(367,6,'Even if they\'re not perfect, you\'d rather have an imperfect solution that at least is congruent with what people want rather than a perfect solution that does not be in line with what they want at all.',6361,6397),(368,6,'To be clear, Trump has done things.',6398,6404),(369,6,'He has withdrawn from the TPP.',6405,6410),(370,6,'He has gotten many automobile factories back, creating jobs in America.',6411,6421),(371,6,'We think these are important things we should have.',6422,6430),(372,6,'If trans-popularity is forced, that\'s precisely the point.',6431,6439),(373,6,'If a politician doesn\'t deliver, he\'ll become less popular, so democracy corrects for this.',6440,6453),(374,6,'Before I move on, yes?',6454,6458),(375,6,'The act of violence is the status quo.',6459,6466),(376,6,'What did that do for the hundreds of Muslim women in America who were attacked after Trump was elected?',6467,6485),(377,6,'Sir, we think that they just failed to deal, failed to actually show us the link between how right-wing populism is this exclusive thing that results in all of this violence.',6486,6517),(378,6,'We think that all the mudslinging has been happening for ages and the point is that they have failed to show us what exclusively this is the way they can have.',6518,6548),(379,6,'Furthermore, we think they were very clear that the idea of violence is something we can prosecute and it is decreasing when we prosecute this violence.',6549,6574),(380,6,'Moving back, we think that the point is that we told you how they undermine democracy and this deals directly with their second speaker substantive.',6575,6599),(381,6,'We think it is not irrational when these individuals engage in this politics.',6600,6612),(382,6,'There was never a golden age of politics when we were all rational and debated on all purely facts.',6613,6631),(383,6,'Politics has always been about character, always been about who people are and we think that if people on the ground vote us, decide that that is what is most important for them, that is something that we should respect.',6632,6671),(384,6,'Furthermore, they said, but you attack institutions.',6672,6678),(385,6,'We think it is generally a good idea if you keep a check on courts.',6679,6693),(386,6,'When courts have been filled with elites who do not care about people on the ground.',6694,6709),(387,6,'When the entire judicial system is stacked against you because it is filled with elites who do not understand what you are.',6710,6731),(388,6,'I think that is fundamental violence upon your dignity.',6732,6740),(389,6,'So if they want to talk about violence, I would say the lack of representation is violence to these individuals.',6741,6760),(390,6,'Furthermore, they just dropped my first speaker\'s analysis about how it is incredibly insulting for them to tell us that minorities always cower in fear.',6761,6785),(391,6,'We think that\'s not true.',6786,6790),(392,6,'We think many minorities are indignant, particularly in social media.',6791,6800),(393,6,'Like they said, when there is actually a community of people around you who will back you up, who will tell you why this is important to you.',6801,6828),(394,6,'We think on this ground alone, minorities are protected on our side.',6829,6840),(395,6,'But the point is, they did not drop our second substantive on abuse, on how these very five scientific criteria are incredibly subjective.',6841,6863),(396,6,'What is political correctness?',6864,6867),(397,6,'What is nativism?',6868,6870),(398,6,'And in the case, we told you how they exercise a chilling effect on discourse.',6871,6885),(399,6,'They literally give governments the tool to crack down on individuals.',6886,6896),(400,6,'We think that\'s wrong.',6897,6900),(401,6,'My constructive analysis for today on how Team England harms political discourse.',6901,6912),(402,6,'So this argument takes them on the absolute highest.',6913,6921),(403,6,'Even if this debate is to be judged on the metric of preventing misinformation, even if it is to be judged on a metric of making speech more palatable, why is it that on our side, we are able to better these metrics?',6922,6964),(404,6,'Note that Team England\'s policy is necessarily known to the public.',6965,6975),(405,6,'For example, if Breitbart is punished, it will have to retract its stinks and provide a public apology to people on the ground.',6976,6998),(406,6,'This leads to a few harms.',6999,7004),(407,6,'Firstly, Team England pushes people towards less credible news sources.',7005,7014),(408,6,'This policy literally reinforces the one strongest narrative that drives distrust of traditional media sources like CNN, that the liberal establishment is out to control what you read.',7015,7042),(409,6,'Now that everything that is published in the traditional media has the imprimatur of government approval, it is seen by right-wing populists as an extension of government control.',7043,7071),(410,6,'This is terrible.',7072,7074),(411,6,'There will always be alternative news sources that provide a less credible form of news.',7075,7089),(412,6,'And obviously, we cannot completely censor WhatsApp chains, forums, face-to-face conversations that occur in daily life.',7090,7107),(413,6,'Therefore, on their side, they ensure that people turn towards these less credible news sources.',7108,7122),(414,6,'And to be clear, this is not about the most extreme individuals.',7123,7134),(415,6,'It is about the vast majority of individuals on the fence who subscribe to both Breitbart and the New York Times on Twitter, but whom Team England pushes straight towards less credible news sources.',7135,7168),(416,6,'Secondly, Team England only ends up amplifying the unsavory aspects of right-wing populism.',7169,7182),(417,6,'In the status quo, many right-wing populist leaders are exercising a significant moderating effect because they want to become more politically viable.',7183,7205),(418,6,'In France, Marine Le Pen kicked her father out of the National Front because he was very strongly in favor of Holocaust denial.',7206,7228),(419,6,'We want this effect to continue.',7229,7234),(420,6,'However, the main reason why these leaders can engage in moderation is because they are currently trusted by their supporters as legitimate, ideological leaders.',7235,7258),(421,6,'When these leaders say that something is wrong, their supporters trust it.',7259,7270),(422,6,'But if the state itself starts to crack down on these forms of unsavory speech, these leaders lose their moderating ability because it now looks like the state has forced them to make concessions.',7271,7304),(423,6,'Any attempt to moderate now looks like it has been engineered by the liberal elite.',7305,7319),(424,6,'So, if they want to talk about making political speech better, I think it is harmed on their side.',7320,7338),(425,6,'If they want to talk about decreasing misinformation, I think it is harmed on their side.',7339,7354),(426,6,'From first week, we were very clear.',7355,7361),(427,6,'We stand by the baseline that silence is oppression. We oppose.',7362,7372),(428,6,'The second opposition speaker spent eight minutes, seven seconds.',7373,7381),(429,6,'I thank the second opposition speaker for their speech.',7382,7390),(430,6,'And now, to continue the case for the proposition, please welcome Kenza Wilkes.',7391,7403),(431,6,'You know, the second speaker is right when he says that this is about people that don\'t have representation.',7404,7422),(432,6,'But what side opposition has thus far failed to prove in this debate is why, once these populists move out of the fore and stop poisoning the well of discussion that happens in democracies across the world, while right-wing politicians cannot step in in their stead to support these people in a way that does not contradict all of the five reasons that we told you these populists behave that undermines democracy.',7423,7495),(433,6,'Their last speaker wanted to stand up and say, well, you haven\'t shown your links to violence.',7496,7512),(434,6,'Ladies and gentlemen, that is fake news as we see it.',7513,7523),(435,6,'Because all they wanted to put forward is moral shame.',7524,7533),(436,6,'What are your categories?',7534,7537),(437,6,'How do we know if they\'re a populist?',7538,7545),(438,6,'We give you five distinct reasons as to why we think these individuals are likely to fall into identifiable categories of being populist.',7546,7568),(439,6,'But ultimately, they wanted only to engage with the extreme end of our scale, i.e.',7569,7584),(440,6,'the banning of these individuals from running on the ballot box.',7585,7595),(441,6,'Remember, all of the other policies that we support, like no platforming them at universities, like stopping their social media pages from operating, these are all ways in which we can try and decrease the influence these populists are able to have.',7596,7637),(442,6,'But finally, when their last speaker wants to raise a point about the chilling effect, ultimately, that is for worse when debate cannot happen in nuanced and moderate ways like we have today, because instead people are standing up and shouting that Mexicans are rapists.',7638,7682),(443,6,'Three areas of clash in this debate.',7683,7689),(444,6,'Firstly, is this legitimate?',7690,7693),(445,6,'Secondly, the provision of solutions and the effects on minorities.',7694,7703),(446,6,'And thirdly, the stability of democracy.',7704,7709),(447,6,'So on the first of which, is this legitimate?',7710,7718),(448,6,'They stand up and give a principle that says, free speech is valuable and these individuals need to be able to engage.',7719,7740),(449,6,'No, this does not directly engage with the principle that we give you about the balance of rights for individuals to be safe from fear.',7741,7765),(450,6,'If you create an atmosphere of fear and an ignorance of the truth, you\'re ultimately far more likely to persecute vulnerable minorities.',7766,7787),(451,6,'The response that they give as well, lack of representation is violence.',7788,7799),(452,6,'Ladies and gentlemen, no, violence is violence.',7800,7806),(453,6,'And given that they\'ve done nothing to contend the fact that we\'ve seen increased rates of attacks on vulnerable minorities in the aftermath of the US election or the aftermath of Poland, the Law and Justice Party being put in power, or the example that we know all too well, that of Brexit and individuals that no longer feel safe in their homes, on their streets or communities because of rises',7807,7877),(454,6,'and attacks and persecutions on you just because you\'re wearing a hijab.',7878,7889),(455,6,'But secondly, they say, well, these people need to have a choice.',7890,7901),(456,6,'We question how meaningful that choice actually is if this politics is skewed away from key issues.',7902,7918),(457,6,'Because what we told you is that populist parties overwhelmingly cannot have specific policies because they have to please a broad coalition of voters.',7919,7942),(458,6,'It\'s very hard for you to have policies that both say we all provide welfare for the people at the bottom, i.e.',7943,7965),(459,6,'Obamacare, but we also want to repeal those very acts that protect people.',7966,7978),(460,6,'That means you don\'t get tangible policies to protect people, but only assertions.',7979,7991),(461,6,'But finally here, they say, well, we got to give the people what they want.',7992,8006),(462,6,'Ultimately, that is not the obligation of a democracy.',8007,8015),(463,6,'We think that we should have parameters within the discussion occurs, that we do not allow antisemitism just because it\'s what some people want.',8016,8039),(464,6,'That is called a tyranny of the majority that overwhelmingly harms the most vulnerable members of our society.',8040,8057),(465,6,'But finally, they say, well, these politicians will likely moderate themselves once they\'re put into power.',8058,8073),(466,6,'See the example of Le Pen.',8074,8079),(467,6,'No, crucially, this is reliant on giving them a platform that allows them to get votes in the first place when the very worst circumstances make it into power.',8080,8108),(468,6,'Given the author gives you analysis as to why, once they are in power, they\'re likely to put forward policies that harm those very minorities.',8109,8133),(469,6,'We\'re not willing to allow them to moderate.',8134,8141),(470,6,'We would rather nip them in the bud before they are allowed to wind up populations into voting them into power.',8142,8162),(471,6,'Let\'s look secondly, at the provisions of solutions and the effects on minorities.',8163,8175),(472,6,'We give you concrete reasons as to why these populist politicians are reliant on harms to minorities in order to get into power.',8176,8198),(473,6,'Firstly, we tell you that they overwhelmingly scapegoat these people to hold a coalition because they look to simple answers for complex questions.',8199,8221),(474,6,'The only response that side opposition give is, well, some African Americans are not upset.',8222,8236),(475,6,'They just retweet their opposition.',8237,8241),(476,6,'No, crucially, that is an assertion that the vast majority of African Americans behave in that way.',8242,8258),(477,6,'But secondly, we think there are many vulnerable individuals that would actually be quite insulted by your characterization of these people willingly just brushing aside these accusations of their lack of humanity.',8259,8290),(478,6,'But secondly, more crucially, a permission mechanism is created and they do nothing to engage with this analysis because if the orange man at the front of the room can stand up and say, well, it\'s okay for me to grab whatever by whatever, or it\'s okay for me to decide that Mexicans are all rapists, then people who are not predisposed to violence under the status quo are far more likely to engage in those very acts of violence.',8291,8370),(479,6,'That\'s what we see.',8371,8374),(480,6,'What were ordinary Americans now deciding to punch Black Lives Matters protesters in the face?',8375,8389),(481,6,'We think that is a fundamental harm that arises from populism itself.',8390,8401),(482,6,'But thirdly and finally, they get into power and they have the ability to implement policies.',8402,8417),(483,6,'And this is a direct clash with the material about, well, we need to give these people some kind of choice and some kind of representation.',8418,8443),(484,6,'If emboldening these individuals by giving them a populist platform from which to spew their views, you actually harm the debate and harm the rights of these individuals, it cannot be legitimate.',8444,8475),(485,6,'But next here, they say, well, this is the, and I quote, elitist vernacular.',8476,8489),(486,6,'And the logic here is that less educated people are unable to have debates about immigration unless someone stands up at the front of the room and shouts, get the immigrants out.',8490,8521),(487,6,'Fundamentally, we think that is a patronizing characterization of how these individuals are able to engage in debates.',8522,8539),(488,6,'But secondly, we think it\'s what is far more likely is that you shut out nuanced discussions.',8540,8556),(489,6,'Because if you think about things like the US primaries, individuals like Cruz or Pence wanted to have discussions about the policies and the ailments that globalization has caused for these people.',8557,8588),(490,6,'Instead, we end up having conversations about the length of Donald Trump\'s fingers.',8589,8601),(491,6,'Ultimately, we think that is far worse for the people that you want to protect under your side of the house.',8602,8622),(492,6,'Because remember, we can provide real alternatives.',8623,8629),(493,6,'So when they say, and I quote, not giving these individuals representation is an act of violence, if other politicians, as they likely will, step into the fray and represent these people, instead, when populists cannot fill that vacuum and pollute the discussion, we are far more likely to protect these individuals.',8630,8681),(494,6,'Let\'s look thirdly and finally at the stability of democracy.',8682,8691),(495,6,'Their major claim here is to say, well, these courts act in irresponsible ways because, and I quote, prosecution is subjective.',8692,8712),(496,6,'Now, they obviously ran into a problem with this when they also supported defamation suits and those very same courts making subjective decisions.',8713,8735),(497,6,'Notably, they drop it in their most recent speech.',8736,8744),(498,6,'But secondly, on your side of the house, what Gert Wilders can do is intentionally try and get defamation suits brought against him and use the fact that those suits have been brought to him in order to gain more popular support.',8745,8786),(499,6,'If we shut down his Twitter page, he is unable to get those channels of discourse that ultimately mean he is more likely to get votes and more likely to be put into power.',8787,8820),(500,6,'But finally here, they say politics is about character and it\'s okay for people to make decisions on this basis.',8821,8840),(501,6,'Instead, you create a chilling effect on discourse, which harms those individuals.',8841,8852),(502,6,'Ultimately, we think it is okay with limiting the amount of discourse that individuals like Breitbart or organizations that actively try to incite violence or limit the rights of minority groups through populist measures, if that means that we are far more able to secure the rights of vulnerable individuals.',8853,8902),(503,6,'At the end of this debate, you have to remember we had two criteria.',8903,8916),(504,6,'The first of which this was justified for the protection of minority groups.',8917,8929),(505,6,'And secondly, that it was effective in challenging the disproportionate control that populists are able to wield over the most vulnerable.',8930,8950),(506,6,'For all of these reasons, so proud to propose.',8951,8959),(507,6,'The third proposition speaker spent eight minutes to second.',8960,8968),(508,6,'I thank the third proposition speaker for their speech.',8969,8977),(509,6,'And now to continue the case for the opposition, please welcome Ah Wei Ho.',8978,8991),(510,6,'The rise of right-wing populism is not a communicative issue.',8992,9002),(511,6,'It is a socioeconomic political issue.',9003,9008),(512,6,'That\'s why when their policy was to remove symptoms like fake news and to sanitize racist language, it honestly misses the point because we will never realize why these individuals have these opinions and these beliefs to begin with.',9009,9047),(513,6,'That\'s how we actually nip the problem at its source.',9048,9057),(514,6,'That\'s why we think on our side of the house, it\'s incredibly important we hear why they believe in these things.',9058,9078),(515,6,'Even if the way they say it is imperfect, it\'s important that we know what these beliefs are.',9079,9096),(516,6,'That\'s the first strategic mistake coming from their side.',9097,9105),(517,6,'The second strategic mistake is their lack of response to all of analysis since first about how this policy allows for massive government abuse.',9106,9129),(518,6,'Mind you, this policy literally gives the ruling elite the ability to silence one third of all Americans and all opposition parties, which are quite powerful right now within the political scene.',9130,9161),(519,6,'This is the greatest expansion of government control of free speech we have ever seen in the last few decades.',9162,9181),(520,6,'I think they need to engage- No, thank you.',9182,9190),(521,6,'Three areas of clash.',9191,9194),(522,6,'First, who are the victims?',9195,9199),(523,6,'Second, what opinions are valid?',9200,9204),(524,6,'And third, why all opinions are attacked on their side of the house.',9205,9217),(525,6,'First, who are the victims?',9218,9222),(526,6,'We said from the beginning of this debate that minorities are victims, but so are those who have been left behind but in the dust of unfettered capitalism.',9223,9250),(527,6,'So are those who have been sidelined by the political elite that see them as irrational bigots that ought not to be listened to.',9251,9274),(528,6,'These are people that currently don\'t even have a political party to cater for their class discrimination.',9275,9291),(529,6,'These are people who don\'t have the courts to defend their supposed constitutional rights because they\'re seen as stubborn people that cannot engage in discourse, as they say on the other side of the house.',9292,9326),(530,6,'These are victims that live in fear because they\'re caricatured by the liberal elite as immoral, deplorable bigots.',9327,9344),(531,6,'They\'re labelled as someone who\'s stubborn, irrational, uneducated, by team proposition, by their own leaders within these countries.',9345,9362),(532,6,'That is the atmosphere that they currently live in.',9363,9371),(533,6,'And they are victims that we need to protect in this debate.',9372,9383),(534,6,'We thought they were important in this debate.',9384,9391),(535,6,'We had absolutely no response as to why they protect them as well.',9392,9404),(536,6,'The response I thought was that, but they can still run for office, but we just won\'t let them campaign or talk about what they want to do in office.',9405,9434),(537,6,'That\'s honestly nonsensical.',9435,9437),(538,6,'So how do their rights compare the rights of minorities?',9438,9447),(539,6,'Now we understand that minorities are important in this debate.',9448,9457),(540,6,'What we do think is that minorities have access to a lot more protection than this specific group of individuals who have been casted away by unfettered capitalism and globalisation.',9458,9487),(541,6,'Why is this the case?',9488,9492),(542,6,'One, because minorities can be empowered if they respond with indignation and not fear.',9493,9506),(543,6,'We talked about how the domino effect on social media means that you genuinely have the capacity to feel strong and safe within your communities.',9507,9531),(544,6,'And is this perfect?',9532,9535),(545,6,'No, but we think that on the comparative, it\'s a lot more likely that you have an anti-democratic party willing to defend you that you\'re much more better off on your side of the house.',9536,9571),(546,6,'But second of all, the herd is asymmetric, which is to say that many of these individuals have been equally as slandered and threatened on the streets by the liberal elite coming from your side of the house.',9572,9609),(547,6,'If this is a response out of fear, then I think that\'s something that\'s legitimate as well.',9610,9626),(548,6,'So the important point to take away from this is that current minorities who already have a democratic party to defend them have access to many other forms of protection.',9627,9656),(549,6,'Are they perfect? Absolutely not.',9657,9661),(550,6,'But for these individuals who currently have been sidelined by entire political elite, this is the only way they can have their voices heard.',9662,9685),(551,6,'That is the comparative we\'re willing to defend.',9686,9693),(552,6,'Eventually, they shrunk their case because their first speaker said the speech was really bad.',9694,9708),(553,6,'But they say, oh, no, no, no, it\'s only speech that results in hate crime.',9709,9723),(554,6,'Here\'s the problem.',9724,9726),(555,6,'We said from the beginning of this debate that we\'re happy to prosecute for the physical crime of hurt in itself.',9727,9747),(556,6,'The problem with their case is that they were never able to prove structural reasons why all forms of right-wing populist speech will always and necessarily result in hate crimes coming from their side of the house.',9748,9785),(557,6,'There might be a certain correlation, but no definite causation coming from their side of the house.',9786,9802),(558,6,'So yes, we agree, this debate is about a balance of rights.',9803,9814),(559,6,'There are boundaries to what\'s acceptable in democracy.',9815,9822),(560,6,'But this is the boundary we need to accept.',9823,9831),(561,6,'For too long, we have remained in our neoliberal consensus and we ought to expand the boundary of opinions that these people have access to.',9832,9856),(562,6,'Second point of contention.',9857,9860),(563,6,'Point of information.',9861,9863),(564,6,'Before that, yes.',9864,9866),(565,6,'Why, when we stop people from running on a populist platform, will they not choose to appeal to these forgotten individuals on a purely right-leaning platform?',9867,9893),(566,6,'No, because the only way you can truly and genuinely represent these people\'s beliefs is to use the same populist rhetorics that actually represent how disgusted they are with the current rate of globalisation and unfettered capitalism.',9894,9930),(567,6,'They say that the current system of right-wing populism has a disparate coalition.',9931,9944),(568,6,'But they say in the same speech that there\'s economic dislocation which binds them all together.',9945,9960),(569,6,'Here\'s the thing.',9961,9963),(570,6,'No one is saying we\'re only going to have a single policy.',9964,9975),(571,6,'Many of these politicians can practise being a right-wing populist politician and craft better policies in the long term.',9976,9995),(572,6,'But the important point here is that if so many people are affected by economic dislocation, regardless of whether you eventually get a policy, I think it\'s important that these voices are at least heard in Parliament through these politicians to begin with.',9996,10038),(573,6,'No, thank you.',10039,10041),(574,6,'Furthermore, we said that racist speech was still valuable in a democracy.',10042,10053),(575,6,'Firstly, because not everyone has access to the same education you had.',10054,10065),(576,6,'Most of us in this room, probably in a debate speech, once said something that was insensitive when we talked about women\'s rights or minorities.',10066,10090),(577,6,'We were fortunate enough that a debate coach told us that that was wrong and to never say that again.',10091,10110),(578,6,'Unfortunately for many people living in the Rust Belt, they did not have access to that kind of education.',10111,10129),(579,6,'I think it\'s wrong for you to punish people for their vocabulary and tone.',10130,10143),(580,6,'But second of all, it\'s an important signalling effect.',10144,10152),(581,6,'We think that saying that we should have a massive overhaul of the immigration system compared to all immigrants should get out now are two very different opinions because the letter shows how much disgust and how much intensity you believe in those opinions.',10153,10196),(582,6,'And that quality is important for us to present as well.',10197,10207),(583,6,'That\'s why when they wanted to have a substantive about the sanctity of institutions, I think it\'s good that we criticise institutions.',10208,10229),(584,6,'When these institutions are dominated by the elite, we ought to criticise them as well.',10230,10244),(585,6,'But here\'s the fundamental problem with that case.',10245,10252),(586,6,'They assume that these beliefs are superimposed by politicians, that otherwise these people would not have had these ideas.',10253,10271),(587,6,'This is incredibly false.',10272,10275),(588,6,'Those racist beliefs that they talked about are understandable aspects of human nature that are in response to economic hardship.',10276,10295),(589,6,'People see this all around them.',10296,10301),(590,6,'Let\'s not kid ourselves and pretend like the Philippines isn\'t dominated by a significant number of Chinese businessmen.',10302,10319),(591,6,'Let\'s not pretend that there\'s no genuine relationship between migrant inflows and wage decrease for these individuals.',10320,10336),(592,6,'These are things that politicians tell you.',10337,10343),(593,6,'These are things that people observe in their daily basis and make those connections for themselves.',10344,10359),(594,6,'So at the point at which their only policy is to remove politicians parroting these ideas, they don\'t actually solve the root cause of this problem.',10360,10385),(595,6,'In contrast, they force people into WhatsApp groups, into Facebook groups, into the same echo chambers that they think are so horrible, without the same defamation lawsuits that they think can be used to regulate these opinions.',10386,10422),(596,6,'That is why when they were never able to engage with the analysis of ordinary Americans, ordinary people on the ground, and what their policy would do to them, DELSA has lost this debate because they never presented us a meaningful solution and mechanism to the problems they\'ve identified.',10423,10471),(597,6,'But lastly, why all opinions are under threat.',10472,10479),(598,6,'The only response you get to this is, but you still have defamation lawsuits, so that\'s really bad.',10480,10497),(599,6,'Here\'s the thing, obviously defamation lawsuits are a lot more restricted within the status quo.',10498,10512),(600,6,'The kind of criteria are a lot more restricted compared to five words or five ideas you give us, or the generic idea of right-wing populism.',10513,10539),(601,6,'And furthermore, it\'s not even a criminal case which is a crime.',10540,10551),(602,6,'which is prosecuted, which your policy does.',10552,10558),(603,6,'But the important thing to take away from this is that necessarily all of this is subjective and context specific.',10559,10578),(604,6,'Jeremy Corbyn might very well say that the inflow of immigrants might reduce labor standards for every single person.',10579,10597),(605,6,'He in fact did say that, but is he a right-wing populist?',10598,10610),(606,6,'No, this reveals to you how many of the statements that are made are context specific.',10611,10626),(607,6,'It requires you to know what\'s going to happen.',10627,10635),(608,6,'There was no response, therefore, to all of analysis about prosecutorial discretion, how the Attorney General can make decisions of which political dissidents to take out.',10636,10661),(609,6,'This is a very dangerous power for us to give governments to begin with.',10662,10675),(610,6,'For all of these reasons, it\'s outside that understands that these people are victims that need to be protected.',10676,10694),(611,6,'Silence is oppression.',10695,10697),(612,6,'The third opposition speaker spent eight minutes, 16 seconds.',10698,10706),(613,6,'I thank the third opposition speaker for their speech.',10707,10715),(614,6,'We now move to the four minutes concluding reply speeches.',10716,10725),(615,6,'To deliver the opposition\'s reply speech, please welcome Brian Wee.',10726,10735),(616,6,'Members of the House, violence isn\'t just physical.',10736,10743),(617,6,'Violence comes from an empty stomach where you have no more money to buy food because immigrants have taken away your jobs.',10744,10765),(618,6,'Violence comes from anxiety over the future of your children because you do not know if you will be able to send them to college.',10766,10790),(619,6,'I think that for far too long, the political establishment has enacted acts of violence against one third of people in America and around the world.',10791,10816),(620,6,'We have taken away their jobs, their livelihoods, and their dignity.',10817,10827),(621,6,'And our team affirmative wants to take away their voices too.',10828,10838),(622,6,'We do not stand for that.',10839,10844),(623,6,'Let\'s look at the two words that emerged at the end of this debate and understand how exactly team affirmative\'s world was oppressive and chilling.',10845,10869),(624,6,'In the world of team affirmative, they do not understand what is happening in politics.',10870,10884),(625,6,'They do not understand of how the centrist consensus has been created ever since Blair and Clinton.',10885,10901),(626,6,'However, ever since that consensus was created, millions of people around the world have not had any politician to vote for or to support who represents their true views.',10902,10930),(627,6,'They say, but why can\'t the person just be right wing and not right wing populist?',10931,10946),(628,6,'Because the point is that we told you right from first, the structural incentives for people to remain in the center of how the structural incentives means that if you\'re slightly to the right of the center, you can capture all the voters on the right.',10947,10992),(629,6,'And therefore there is no incentive to move further to the right once this consensus has been created.',10993,11010),(630,6,'And that is why we don\'t actually know where they\'re drawing the line.',11011,11023),(631,6,'What exactly is the line between a right wing person who is not that racist and a right wing populist who is a bit more racist but represents different views?',11024,11053),(632,6,'Where exactly do they draw the line?',11054,11060),(633,6,'I would suggest that if their main harm is minorities, or does that they have to err on the side of caution.',11061,11082),(634,6,'They have to take away a huge swath of views that legitimately represent what these individuals stand for.',11083,11100),(635,6,'And to be clear, in team affirmative\'s world, it is not only about people running for office.',11101,11117),(636,6,'It is about your ability to express your deeply held beliefs, to express your discontent with a political elite that has neglected you, to show that you are disgusted that they have left you behind.',11118,11152),(637,6,'So it is not about just running for office.',11153,11161),(638,6,'Don\'t let the other speaker misrepresent our case.',11162,11169),(639,6,'In the world of team affirmative, they stand for expression, but not too much, just so you can\'t cause violence.',11170,11189),(640,6,'But then we told them, that\'s a pretty draconian world.',11190,11199),(641,6,'Many people call other things many other people\'s names.',11200,11208),(642,6,'There are many acts that cause violence in the world today.',11209,11219),(643,6,'On the other side, they have subjective criteria in the world of team affirmative that allow governments to abuse it.',11220,11239),(644,6,'They said, ah, but you have laws on your side too, so it\'s a bit different.',11240,11255),(645,6,'No, I would suggest the difference is that on our side, we are willing to err on the side of free speech, whereas on your side, you told us very clearly that in Team England\'s world, they are going to err on the side of caution because minorities are the priority in this debate.',11256,11309),(646,6,'We think that on their side, they exercise a chilling effect on discourse.',11310,11322),(647,6,'But you know what?',11323,11326),(648,6,'If their problem is about minorities, I think they are harmed in the world of Team England because in my substantive, I told you very clearly about the moderating effect.',11327,11356),(649,6,'Now, they just give a one-line assertion back in third speaker that, oh, it\'s when they come into power.',11357,11376),(650,6,'No, I told you about the structural incentives that these individuals have to moderate and how you disempower the people who take away the racist and antisemitic rhetoric that you don\'t like.',11377,11408),(651,6,'So in the world of Team Affirmative, that happens even more.',11409,11419),(652,6,'So let us escape the world of Team Affirmative and go to the world of Team Opposition.',11420,11436),(653,6,'The world of Team Opposition was very clear on our stance.',11437,11447),(654,6,'We believe that these individuals need a voice.',11448,11455),(655,6,'They need someone to stand up for them and they need someone to be there to represent their views.',11456,11474),(656,6,'We understood that sometimes, unpleasant language is the only way for these individuals to express their views because it expresses the color, the tone, the intensity and the beliefs they have.',11475,11505),(657,6,'Analysis we gave you from first, not responded to in all these speakers and it\'d be a bit too late if they try to respond in reply.',11506,11532),(658,6,'In the world of Opposition, we understood that we are prosecuting violent acts and therefore that is not part of the debate.',11533,11554),(659,6,'We understood that the true victims here are the individuals who have been left behind.',11555,11569),(660,6,'I want all of you to ask yourselves, why do you debate?',11570,11581),(661,6,'I think all of us debate because we believe in what we do.',11582,11594),(662,6,'We believe in the power of words.',11595,11601),(663,6,'We believe in the power of discourse.',11602,11608),(664,6,'We believe in the power of speech.',11609,11615),(665,6,'But this is a power that we should not just keep to ourselves.',11616,11628),(666,6,'This is a power that we should give to people who we do not agree with.',11629,11644),(667,6,'People who have been left behind and for whom speech is the one way they can reclaim their dignity.',11645,11663),(668,6,'We understood on Team Opposition that silence is oppression.',11664,11672),(669,6,'We are very proud to oppose.',11673,11678),(670,6,'The Opposition reply speakers spent four minutes, 11 seconds.',11679,11687),(671,6,'And now to conclude the case for the proposition and the debate as a whole, please welcome Edward Brissey.',11688,11706),(672,6,'The reason side opposition lost this debate is that I\'ve said some dodge stuff in my time.',11707,11723),(673,6,'But Lewis and Fish pointed out time and time again and I stopped saying that stuff.',11724,11739),(674,6,'But nobody tells Donald or Duterte that saying that Mexicans are rapists or that you can grab women by whatever is unacceptable.',11740,11761),(675,6,'And in fact, when he says it, when he gets cheers, when he takes votes from legitimate politicians, he not only poisons democracy, but he makes people feel afraid.',11762,11790),(676,6,'He makes people feel worthless and he violates their rights on a fundamental level.',11791,11804),(677,6,'Two questions when adjudicating this debate. First, legitimacy.',11805,11812),(678,6,'Second of all, outcomes for the public.',11813,11819),(679,6,'On legitimacy, the proposition thrust went like this.',11820,11827),(680,6,'We said that there existed a balance and if the existence of one group led to the fundamental abuse of another, that was unacceptable.',11828,11851),(681,6,'But they said, oh, but these people don\'t actually feel fear because they retweet things the whole time.',11852,11869),(682,6,'We said, is that a joke?',11870,11875),(683,6,'No, no, no, guys, you\'re right.',11876,11881),(684,6,'Women don\'t feel fear when they are terrified of being raped by an army that Duterte says is legitimate.',11882,11900),(685,6,'Muslim men and women in the United States don\'t feel fear when they see on their screens, night after night, people and families just like theirs being beaten in the streets in the aftermath of Trump\'s election.',11901,11937),(686,6,'But they came back and said, ah, we can prosecute for these crimes.',11938,11950),(687,6,'We pointed out not only that we already prosecute for these crimes and racial hatred still happens, but second of all, that is after the fact.',11951,11976),(688,6,'If a group leads to abuse, irrespective of whether you then prosecute that abuse, that group is illegitimate.',11977,11994),(689,6,'But they then said, ah, you constrict the fundamental rights of individuals to access democracy.',11995,12009),(690,6,'We have three responses here.',12010,12014),(691,6,'We said, first, that is factually inaccurate.',12015,12021),(692,6,'If you look at the early debates between Cruz and Rubio, they are talking about exactly the issues you discussed.',12022,12041),(693,6,'They\'re talking about tighter immigration controls.',12042,12047),(694,6,'They\'re talking about how we distribute welfare to the most vulnerable.',12048,12058),(695,6,'But second of all, we took them at their best.',12059,12068),(696,6,'We said, okay, why don\'t you have individuals stepping in to replace these people?',12069,12082),(697,6,'Because Macron\'s party is only one year old, and why would you not step into the void?',12083,12099),(698,6,'And they said, ah, crucially, the language that these people use is what means they are the only people that can do it.',12100,12122),(699,6,'We asked them in a POI, why do Mexicans have to be characterized as rapists for us to have a discussion about immigration?',12123,12145),(700,6,'Now, the fact that they could not reply to that, and they could not tell you fundamentally why that language of abuse and that language of intimidation is so essential is part and parcel of the reason they lost today\'s debate.',12146,12186),(701,6,'The third thing we put into this was we said, this is contingent insofar as there\'s no point having your representation is not functional if that representation does not bring anything for you.',12187,12219),(702,6,'And that\'s the point at which the fact that they did not engage with our first speaker\'s analysis about broad but shallow coalitions that operate under a veneer of legitimacy that trick people into thinking that they\'re being represented but are not is so problematic.',12220,12264),(703,6,'But we said, okay, you want to talk about democracy and rights?',12265,12276),(704,6,'Let\'s talk about the fundamental abuse of the rights of everyone else in a democracy to make fair and free democratic choices and how that is undermined.',12277,12303),(705,6,'And if you check your notes, I think you\'ll find they did not respond to four minutes of analysis from our second speaker.',12304,12326),(706,6,'Problematic, to say the least.',12327,12331),(707,6,'Second area of this debate, outcomes for the public.',12332,12340),(708,6,'A proposition line went as follows.',12341,12346),(709,6,'In our first speech, in our second argument, we told you that it is harder to address the issues that they claim to care about because you spread people so thin across this coalition.',12347,12380),(710,6,'And we told you that factually, this is not happening.',12381,12390),(711,6,'Policies are being passed like the Muslim ban, policies are being passed like banning trans people from the army.',12391,12409),(712,6,'And the fact that 28%, only 28% of the United States public continues to support Trump is indicative of the fact that he does not have the tools to empower people and give them what they want.',12410,12446),(713,6,'They did not respond to this argument at any stage, but they ran their own principle about representation.',12447,12464),(714,6,'We asked you a fundamental question.',12465,12470),(715,6,'Is representation meaningful if that person does not do what you want them to do?',12471,12485),(716,6,'And for all the arguments they threw out in their second speech and then dropped about shilling effects, about less creditable news sources.',12486,12508),(717,6,'By the way, what is a less creditable news source than Breitbart?',12509,12520),(718,6,'They could not engage with the core tenant of this debate.',12521,12531),(719,6,'And that was how do we provide for people issues that they actually need?',12532,12545),(720,6,'And the last thing they threw out right at the end in their third speech is, ah, these people will be encouraged to moderate.',12546,12569),(721,6,'Because yeah, you\'re right.',12570,12573),(722,6,'Trump has really moderated himself since he got into power.',12574,12583),(723,6,'He stopped saying all of these horrific things.',12584,12591),(724,6,'He stopped banning Muslims from countries.',12592,12597),(725,6,'He stopped cracking down on people.',12598,12603),(726,6,'He stopped cutting welfare to fundamentally the most vulnerable. You\'re right.',12604,12614),(727,6,'Duterte hasn\'t encouraged his army to go out and rape women.',12615,12625),(728,6,'Oh wait, that is the status quo.',12626,12632),(729,6,'That is the reality they had to defend this debate.',12633,12642),(730,6,'And that is why they lost.',12643,12648),(731,6,'The proposition reply speakers spent four minutes, ten seconds.',12649,12657),(732,6,'Thank you to all speakers for this debate.',12658,12665),(733,6,'The judges are now going to deliberate in a separate room and make their decisions.',12666,12680),(734,6,'They will then announce the result of this debate. Thank you.',12681,12691),(735,6,'Ladies and gentlemen, that was the Grand Final World School Debating Championship 2017.',12692,12704),(736,6,'For the next few moments, we are still waiting for the results of the evaluations from the adjudicator or the jury.',12705,12725),(737,6,'So don\'t go anywhere, we will be right back.',12726,12734),(738,6,'Ladies and gentlemen, please welcome one of the judging panel members to give feedback on the debate and announce the winning team.',12735,12756),(739,6,'First of all, as finals goes, this is one of the better finals we\'ve had in several years.',12757,12774),(740,6,'So congratulations to both teams.',12775,12779),(741,6,'Excellent across the board in style, content and strategy.',12780,12788),(742,6,'So I\'ll go through them one by one.',12789,12796),(743,6,'This was one debate where style.',12797,12802),(744,6,'.. ..had a slight stylistic advantage to another team.',12803,12810),(745,6,'And in fact, it actually swayed the debate for some adjudicators, particularly the use of emotive language to prove the point.',12811,12831),(746,6,'In terms of strategy, we did think that teams focused on the right things, allocated enough time to arguments.',12832,12850),(747,6,'It was a dynamic debate that consistently evolved and shifted grounds.',12851,12861),(748,6,'So that\'s not an issue, right?',12862,12867),(749,6,'So the issue for today\'s debate is really content.',12868,12876),(750,6,'And we looked at that in several issues.',12877,12884),(751,6,'First is the question of legitimacy.',12885,12890),(752,6,'Can you limit free speech?',12891,12895),(753,6,'The answer both sides is yes.',12896,12901),(754,6,'But the extent of that limitation became where the debate boiled down to.',12902,12914),(755,6,'Props says this speech is potentially harmful and they proved it with compelling examples which swayed some of the panellists around issues like Trump, political parties in Europe and my personal favourite, Duterte.',12915,12947),(756,6,'Opposition counters this by saying, sure, if the speech is harmful, then punish the action, not the speech.',12948,12965),(757,6,'And they said correlation is not causation.',12966,12972),(758,6,'We didn\'t feel this was significantly forwarded in the round.',12973,12982),(759,6,'We felt that there might be a slight advantage on the legitimacy question to the proposition, but there are other issues in the round.',12983,13006),(760,6,'So let\'s move on to the second issue.',13007,13014),(761,6,'On a question of minorities and marginalised groups.',13015,13022),(762,6,'As you can tell, I\'m not gonna announce the result at the start.',13023,13035),(763,6,'So on minorities and marginalised groups, we felt that proposition had very compelling arguments about what will happen to religious people, to women when the speech propagates the kind of culture of hatred I see in my president.',13036,13073),(764,6,'This we felt was a wise move and actually felt a lot of the panellists did feel that you actually won this part of the debate.',13074,13099),(765,6,'Some judges, however, saw it quite differently and looked at the metric that proposition used and said, if the metric is fear and violence, then marginalised sectors, people have been disenfranchised by globalisation and the capitalist world do experience the same degree of fear and violence.',13100,13145),(766,6,'And therefore, they should have a voice in this democratic institutions.',13146,13156),(767,6,'England says, well, it doesn\'t matter because if they represent you, the policies are new ones.',13157,13172),(768,6,'They pander to the least common denominator.',13173,13179),(769,6,'Essentially, there won\'t be any proper representation in the outcomes of the policy.',13180,13192),(770,6,'Singapore counters that by saying, well, maybe it\'s not coming from the politician.',13193,13205),(771,6,'Maybe it\'s coming from the lived reality of people.',13206,13214),(772,6,'That\'s why these things are so popular.',13215,13221),(773,6,'Things, for example, like jobs being gone or people in a little bit of an exaggerated fashion starving or getting hungry.',13222,13242),(774,6,'So, these are individuals that Singapore was fighting for.',13243,13251),(775,6,'Individuals who\'ve been left behind by centrist politics.',13252,13259),(776,6,'Singapore also says that if your standard is fear and violence, then the chilling effect that you will have on the opposition parties will be a massive problem.',13260,13287),(777,6,'What\'s going to happen, for example, to Corbyn?',13288,13295),(778,6,'And to be fair to proposition, they did say that courts that you trust should be able to adjudicate these particular matters.',13296,13317),(779,6,'But some judges didn\'t feel this was enough of a response because of the selective prosecution point that came in from the opposition bench.',13318,13341),(780,6,'England then came back and said that the discussion will be toxic and undemocratic.',13342,13355),(781,6,'Whereas this other side of the house said that, well, toxicity of the conversation depends on who\'s viewing the conversation.',13356,13375),(782,6,'For a lot of these individuals, that\'s the language that best represents their emotions and feelings.',13376,13391),(783,6,'So, it all boiled down to, and for most of the judges, on .',13392,13404),(784,6,'5 margins, which team reduces fear, trepidation, and violence on the part of individuals, particularly the minorities concerned?',13405,13422),(785,6,'Most of the judges were persuaded that the working class is equally as hurt as most minorities.',13423,13439),(786,6,'So, in an eight-one split, this debate goes to Singapore.',13440,13450),(787,6,'We invite all the members of the judging panel to come on stage to present the trophy to Team Singapore.',13451,13470),(788,6,'Ladies and gentlemen, your new world champions, Singapore. Yes, Mr.',13471,13480),(789,6,'Speaker, that was the live broadcast of the 2017 Grand Final World School Debating Championship between England and Singapore.',13481,13499),(790,6,'After each debater presented their opinion on the ban on free speech, the judges decided that Singapore would be the winner of the 2017 World School Debating Championship.',13500,13527),(791,6,'Congratulations to both teams for making it to the 2017 WSDC Grand Final.',13528,13540),(792,6,'And congratulations to Team Singapore for winning this tournament.',13541,13549),(793,6,'With the end of the 2017 WSDC Grand Final between England and Singapore, our program ends.',13550,13565),(794,6,'We remind you that later tonight at 7 PM Central Indonesian time, you can watch the closing ceremony of the 2017 World School Debating Championship.',13566,13590),(795,6,'Stay tuned to the educational television because we will continue to broadcast programs that are engaging and inspiring.',13591,13608),(796,6,'So, keep moving forward, Indonesian education, and keep moving forward, Indonesia.',13609,13619),(797,10,'of major democracies.',13624,13626),(798,10,'Thank you very much.',13627,13630),(799,10,'Good luck to all teams and the House recognizes the Honorable Prime Minister.',13631,13643),(800,10,'Am I horrible?',13644,13646),(801,10,'Before I start my speech, everything I\'ve achieved and everything I\'ve done in the debate is thanks to and for my parents.',13647,13668),(802,10,'They are the bravest people I know. Los amo.',13669,13677),(803,10,'This have been the hardest weeks of my life so I want to thank the friends who have been there for me.',13678,13699),(804,10,'You kept me going and I\'ll text you later.',13700,13708),(805,10,'Speaking Worlds has been in the back of my mind for years at Worlds Finals and I want to thank every single teammate, coach and student on Team Mexico for making me who I am today.',13709,13744),(806,10,'Special thanks to Mark Weber who started the dream for all of us, to Angelica Cardiel who introduced me to this activity and to Julio who stuck with me for the longest.',13745,13776),(807,10,'Thank you to the Churchill School and Debates for giving me the best opportunities and shout out to the BQDC.',13777,13796),(808,10,'To Ayuka who is an amazing friend and a brilliant mind.',13797,13807),(809,10,'She had not debated in two years at all, came back and finaled Oxford in Worlds.',13808,13823),(810,10,'You are brilliant and one of the best minds I\'ve ever met.',13824,13835),(811,10,'This speech, like every single one I\'ve given, goes to the Mexican and the Latin American circuit.',13836,13852),(812,10,'It was f- it was f-ing hard but we made it, we\'re here and we had representation in every single final.',13853,13874),(813,10,'Finally, there are grounds to believe a genocide is being committed in Palestine.',13875,13887),(814,10,'Over 9 million people have been displaced and tens of thousands have died at the hands of a regime that has no regard for human life and international law.',13888,13916),(815,10,'Standing with Palestinians is a moral obligation we all have.',13917,13926),(816,10,'You all study at some of the most privileged universities in the world and you can make a difference.',13927,13945),(817,10,'Please ask to divest, ask your fr- ask your friends to make this a more inclusive environment and remember, even if you stand by your principles, you can still final the World Championships.',13946,13978),(818,10,'For far too long, populations have been failed by politicians and political groups that through the veils of identity politics have told them that they are represented when in reality they are not.',13979,14011),(819,10,'When in the vast majority of instances, the policies contradict the very thing individuals believe their party represent and when in many instances it is about a dichotomy which lies to you, strips you of your taxes and instrumentalizes you to put politicians in office that do not represent you.',14012,14061),(820,10,'Or you can, I believe, that the shift that we\'ve had where individuals are number one, rejecting their affiliation in many instances to particular like- like sides of the political spectrum, i.e.',14062,14094),(821,10,'I am not necessarily a leftist or a right wing, but I am rather like an issue voter, for example, or I believe that climate change matters or I believe that abortion matters.',14095,14127),(822,10,'Still, you can- you can obviously have a history of voting for a particular party because you might prefer the particular politicians or the policies, but not to be affiliated fully or for this to decrease over time is what we support.',14128,14169),(823,10,'So what does this look like?',14170,14175),(824,10,'Yes, first of all, it looks like less identity politics.',14176,14185),(825,10,'It looks like having more things in the middle.',14186,14194),(826,10,'In fact, it\'s having more types of identification within political parties.',14195,14205),(827,10,'It\'s the fact that people within the left, for example, or within parties such as the Democrats in the United States say, I do not work in the same way that others do.',14206,14238),(828,10,'I am not a traditional leftist politician because while I do support, for example, abortion and the environment, I just believe that we should be fiscally a little bit more conservative and the same is true across the other side of the aisle.',14239,14281),(829,10,'So it is more specific representation.',14282,14287),(830,10,'It is also more parties because I think that when you have more like- of a discourse that says it is not just the left and the right.',14288,14315),(831,10,'People who have been historically abandoned by the political establishment, like female parties, like indigenous parties, are a lot more likely to arise.',14316,14338),(832,10,'I come from a country where the S.L.N.A.',14339,14349),(833,10,', an indigenous group from- like an indigenous community from Chiapas who had been taken out by the political establishment, who wasn\'t allowed to join the political forces, for far too long believed that they just couldn\'t be represented because it was something that only people who looked a certain way could participate in or only people who historically had a, for example, like a trajectory with the Catholic Church could participate in.',14350,14421),(834,10,'But saying it is no longer that allows for more individuals to enter in the first place and I think this is more democratic.',14422,14445),(835,10,'Most importantly, I think that this looks like past elections where individuals, for example, in Mexico or in the United States have rejected particular political parties and in interviews to politicians or in democratic debates where they get to ask politicians, they say, I do not have an affiliation to a party but I just want to know who the fuck is finally going to solve climate change, who is actually going to help me with abortion.',14446,14522),(836,10,'The first argument I\'m going to make is that this is a lot better and creates a lot more incentives to reach across the aisle because note, one of the main reasons why governments aren\'t ever forced- formed and why in many instances there are government lockdowns where you literally don\'t have a government for fucking days and the country has like inflation and the country has many issues that have to be resolved',14523,14596),(837,10,'or security issues can\'t be addressed or not necessarily just not forming government but just not compromising across the political spectrum is when you believe that within your party there is a historical thing that you have to do.',14597,14635),(838,10,'And I think this is a lot more likely to happen on opposition side.',14636,14649),(839,10,'When you believe that historically we never vote in favor of abortion because we are the historically right country such as PAN in Mexico or such as the Republicans in the United States.',14650,14682),(840,10,'But I do genuinely think that when you identify that this is possible, you are number one a lot more likely to reach across the aisle which is good for voters but it is also better for social discourse.',14683,14721),(841,10,'Because I think one of the biggest issues is that when you go to a- like a meal- have a meal with your uncle during Christmas, when you\'re actually building politics in your university, you reject individuals from the other side of the political spectrum because you believe that they represent a whole host of different things that they might not necessarily represent.',14722,14784),(842,10,'Individuals for example who were abandoned by Obama because they were not given the appropriate economic policies but who do support actually having climate change policies but they are consistently pushed out of universities because they are not woken up.',14785,14824),(843,10,'Or the opposite being true, right?',14825,14830),(844,10,'Where I just think some individuals- where I just think some individuals on the left are not allowed in some positions of power in corporations, in financing for example, because they seem to be representing different things.',14831,14867),(845,10,'I envision what will happen in five to ten years which is that the youth is no longer as affiliated to political parties.',14868,14890),(846,10,'It is about specific issues and I think identity politics falls.',14891,14901),(847,10,'This is broadly what this debate starts to do.',14902,14910),(848,10,'The third thing I want to note here is that you have a lot more challenges to the political establishment and this happens for a host of different reasons.',14911,14939),(849,10,'The first is clearly because you have more faces and parties that are likely to arise.',14940,14955),(850,10,'You gain political capital from distinguishing from the political establishment both in saying I am not X and Y but also in saying here are the new things that I can offer.',14956,14987),(851,10,'I think it is frankly ridiculous that the average American leftist in the United States believes that a Kamala Harris, a person who is literally funding bombs to children and the non-redistribution of resources and the non-significant liberalization of healthcare was the only option for them.',14988,15035),(852,10,'I genuinely think that when I sat in my dining hall at the most woke fucking liberal college in the United States, I would talk to people and they would be like how dare you even remotely support the Green Party.',15036,15076),(853,10,'You are just stealing votes from the actual party even though it was the only real option that was actually going to solve climate change to an extent or actually not support a genocide.',15077,15110),(854,10,'All I am trying to say here is individuals have a lot more incentives of pushing politicians to become a lot more issue like voting and whatnot.',15111,15137),(855,10,'Maybe opposition says like oh this party is just like divide the vote and that is why Trump won for example.',15138,15158),(856,10,'That is just empirically not true and I think everyone knows that.',15159,15170),(857,10,'Also I think most importantly I think what ends up happening is that on yours like in this type of instances the Democratic Party has to go more to the middle.',15171,15201),(858,10,'So the end goal of this is you also have less radicalization.',15202,15213),(859,10,'Instead of you tapping into the things that you as a political party or politician identify as the most representative of the political party you go towards the middle which is more representative of individuals, less radicalizing and I think it is better for democracy.',15214,15258),(860,10,'Yeah, why don\'t we see this collaboration you speak of?',15259,15268),(861,10,'Things seem pretty difficult to get done in Congress these days.',15269,15279),(862,10,'No I think this is literally true.',15280,15286),(863,10,'In the last Mexican elections basically the main political party once it won included many of the policies from the second party because they realized that if they didn\'t in the next elections since people are no longer just party based but they were policy based just supporting some of those policies was useful.',15287,15340),(864,10,'I do genuinely think that in the vast majority of the developing world it is happening like I\'m trying to use the example of the US because it\'s the most comparative one but obviously it\'s not necessarily like it\'s just not the most representative of this.',15341,15386),(865,10,'I think in the vast majority of the world you do have these types of policies where there\'s a lot more coalitions in Europe that talk about the environment.',15387,15415),(866,10,'It is a lot more unpopular to be radically anti-environment in Europe precisely because of this because they have realized that it is something that it is not just an issue of the right and the left.',15416,15453),(867,10,'I also believe that just in general the degree to which individuals have less information on their parties is affected by identity politics. I.e.',15454,15478),(868,10,'I think it is there are some individuals like I think the vast majority of individuals when they vote on religious lines for example when they vote on things like who their parents voted for or just believe that there is a party that is intrinsically the only leftist option that party has this incentive from actually representing them in policy because they can just count on those votes always being there.',15479,15550),(869,10,'And the issue is on our side of the house you have to be a lot more explicit about having debates that explain particular policies.',15551,15575),(870,10,'You have to question other politicians on their policies themselves and not just question on who they historically represent.',15576,15594),(871,10,'It is no longer a class war where you\'re like oh you represent the poor whereas I represent in the like sorry you represent the rich whereas I am a revolutionary but it is rather about more political specificity which I think is better for voters to be better informed.',15595,15644),(872,10,'At the end of this debate you just have to remember from opening government that this is not a full shift in the political environment.',15645,15669),(873,10,'All we require to win this debate or like like just it\'s not like everyone is doing this but there is a continuous diversification of ideas there is a lot more diversity for individuals in terms of options in terms of preferences and I think that is what matters the most.',15670,15720),(874,10,'We are incredibly proud to propose.',15721,15726),(875,10,'Thank you very much for that speech.',15727,15733),(876,10,'Now I\'m very happy to welcome the leader of the opposition.',15734,15744),(877,10,'The greatest battle that politics ever fought was against poverty.',15745,15754),(878,10,'It was against the crushing blow of hunger, the crushing blow of homelessness, the slow death of uneducation, of illness and of hopelessness.',15755,15777),(879,10,'That battle liberated millions of people across the developed world and the developing world from those conditions but it is a battle that is unfinished and because of this shift it will remain unfinished because this shift has meant a shift from primarily economic positioning around the political axis to primarily social ones.',15778,15830),(880,10,'Social ones that do not achieve liberation from poverty and social ones that do not even achieve their own ends.',15831,15850),(881,10,'I\'ll first explain set up then I\'ll explain why this led to the abandoning of the working class then I\'ll explain why this was very bad for social change finally I\'ll talk about stability.',15851,15884),(882,10,'First on set up the most important manifestation of this shift has been a shift from primarily economic based positioning that is where both parties define themselves around being left or right pro redistribution or against it towards positioning on social issues.',15885,15926),(883,10,'That is because of educational polarization because elites took control of both parties and oriented themselves around cultural issues.',15927,15945),(884,10,'That shift has involved the deprioritization of economics.',15946,15953),(885,10,'It\'s why Biden and Trump largely agree on tariff policy.',15954,15963),(886,10,'It has involved the prioritization of social issues.',15964,15971),(887,10,'That is the kinds of things they describe.',15972,15979),(888,10,'Things like the rise of women and indigenous parties in Mexico, the rise of immigration as a key issue in Germany and France, the rise of Hinduism as a key issue in Indian politics, the rise of indigenous rights as the polarizing issue in my country.',15980,16025),(889,10,'That is the primary shift of this debate.',16026,16033),(890,10,'Why has this shift led to the abandoning of the working class?',16034,16045),(891,10,'Firstly this is by far the most important part of this debate.',16046,16057),(892,10,'The working class should matter most for three reasons.',16058,16066),(893,10,'Firstly numerically they are the majority compared to every other minority that will be discussed in this debate.',16067,16084),(894,10,'The working class constitute the vast majority of most countries.',16085,16094),(895,10,'Secondly oppression on all axes manifests through class.',16095,16102),(896,10,'Oppression on race, oppression on sexuality, oppression on gender manifest themselves through access to class, access to money, access to wealth, access to education.',16103,16126),(897,10,'So redistributive policies help all of the groups that are described in this debate.',16127,16140),(898,10,'But thirdly because class is a fungible term.',16141,16148),(899,10,'Class is something that you can change over time and that means everyone believes it\'s your fault if you are poor.',16149,16169),(900,10,'Everyone believes it\'s very hard to unionize.',16170,16176),(901,10,'It\'s not like something you can identify in everyone else.',16177,16186),(902,10,'Something you can see in the color of another person\'s skin.',16187,16197),(903,10,'It is a fact that\'s about yourself.',16198,16204),(904,10,'You believe often because internalized classism is your fault.',16205,16213),(905,10,'So why did this norm lead to the abandonment of the working class?',16214,16226),(906,10,'For three reasons.',16227,16229),(907,10,'Firstly it took away an enormously important way for the working class to mobilize and vote for parties that supported them.',16230,16250),(908,10,'It led to the decline of parties like labor parties.',16251,16260),(909,10,'Parties that supported things like unionization, supported things like redistribution.',16261,16270),(910,10,'You can observe this empirically in the rates of unionization and the rates of inequality which have increased as this shift has taken place.',16271,16294),(911,10,'And you can observe it in the fact that these parties are ones that are easy to access if you are someone that is low information.',16295,16320),(912,10,'If you are someone that is tired after a long day at work.',16321,16333),(913,10,'These are the kinds of parties you can join.',16334,16342),(914,10,'And maybe they say these parties are imperfect but the parties that were replacing them are certainly worse.',16343,16360),(915,10,'The parties of the republicans, the parties of the BJP are far worse for redistribution.',16361,16375),(916,10,'Secondly the existence of a leftist party created an incentive for the other parties to position themselves around the middle class.',16376,16396),(917,10,'That is if you cannot win the poor\'s votes you must win the votes of other groups in society.',16397,16415),(918,10,'So rather than the perverse American style elites work with the poor you had a positioning around the middle class and a positioning around poverty.',16416,16440),(919,10,'Both sides were ultimately advocating for things like upwards mobility that cross-vested, that benefited one another.',16441,16457),(920,10,'Thirdly because economics shifted and was no longer at the forefront of politics.',16458,16470),(921,10,'People no longer thought about economics as the primary goal of politics.',16471,16482),(922,10,'Focus groups no longer conducted on the way to best analyze how voters responded to economics.',16483,16498),(923,10,'People no longer conducted studies on which economic policies would be the most successful because politicians knew the positioning around their rights, positioning around abortion, positioning around democracy was far more important, far more able to win elections, far more likely to matter in the minds of voters which meant they no longer thought about economics as a mechanism to change. Yes.',16499,16560),(924,10,'Despite this rhetoric the Tories just suffered their worst electoral defeat in a century because British voters were enormously dissatisfied with their mismanagement of the British economy.',16561,16587),(925,10,'Clearly working class voters still have strong incentives to punish incumbent parties that mismanage the economic situation.',16588,16604),(926,10,'Certainly they obviously have incentives to punish parties whose economic policies underperform but the problem is that is no longer at the forefront of the debate.',16605,16630),(927,10,'That is no longer the thing that\'s primarily discussed.',16631,16639),(928,10,'For the reasons that the opening government team set up and the closing cannot knife this is a debate about social positioning.',16640,16661),(929,10,'It is a debate about the role of women and religious minorities in parliament.',16662,16675),(930,10,'Their role as representatives of politics.',16676,16681),(931,10,'That is what this shift has encompassed.',16682,16688),(932,10,'Why is this shift enormously bad for social change?',16689,16697),(933,10,'Firstly because it\'s led to polarization on social issues.',16698,16706),(934,10,'Whereas in the past many political parties engaged in forms of consensus building.',16707,16719),(935,10,'That is for instance both Republicans and Democrats supported immigration as a social policy because it wasn\'t really debated.',16720,16738),(936,10,'It wasn\'t really the focus or the crux of their debate.',16739,16749),(937,10,'Now it has changed.',16750,16753),(938,10,'Now social issues are the focus of political debate.',16754,16762),(939,10,'That has led to three things.',16763,16768),(940,10,'Firstly forms of sporadic violence are far more common.',16769,16777),(941,10,'Things like violence around people that you perceive to be immigrants.',16778,16788),(942,10,'People that are discussed in media and extremist ways is more common.',16789,16800),(943,10,'Second people position themselves against it.',16801,16806),(944,10,'Right-wing parties or parties that call themselves right-wing are now far more against social issues.',16807,16823),(945,10,'Far more against immigration.',16824,16827),(946,10,'Far more against gay people than they were 10 or 20 years ago.',16828,16840),(947,10,'Thirdly it makes them seem like elite issues.',16841,16848),(948,10,'These issues are less compelling to other people because parties are identified with the elite are now the ones pushing social change.',16849,16870),(949,10,'They\'re now the ones advocating for social change.',16871,16878),(950,10,'Secondly this is bad for social change because this shift has encompassed a shift towards extremism.',16879,16894),(951,10,'There are three reasons that is the case.',16895,16902),(952,10,'Firstly there\'s a broader spectrum of politics.',16903,16909),(953,10,'That is there are many more ways you can position yourself on a social axis.',16910,16924),(954,10,'They don\'t line up neatly.',16925,16929),(955,10,'Where almost all economics lines up on the line of do you support redistribution or not.',16930,16945),(956,10,'Things don\'t line up neatly on an axis of do you support gay marriage or immigration.',16946,16961),(957,10,'Those things are causally unrelated.',16962,16966),(958,10,'So there are far more parties in politics.',16967,16974),(959,10,'Secondly it\'s far easier to about politics.',16975,16981),(960,10,'An enormous amount of what people believe about social issues comes from social media.',16982,16995),(961,10,'It comes from their feelings about society.',16996,17002),(962,10,'But policy is far more based on statistics.',17003,17010),(963,10,'Even if TikTok is bad there\'s still need to be statistics on those TikToks to convince people.',17011,17027),(964,10,'Thirdly in many cases this has led to a shift towards ethnic and religious messaging.',17028,17042),(965,10,'In India this shift away from left-right positioning did involve a greater prevalence of Hindu messaging in the base position of both the BJP and the INC.',17043,17070),(966,10,'Finally this shift has led to enormous disruption.',17071,17078),(967,10,'Necessarily as this team countenance this shift was a disruptive one and do not devalue the value of stability in these countries.',17079,17100),(968,10,'Firstly it provides investment stability.',17101,17105),(969,10,'When you know that countries are likely to be stable over time.',17106,17117),(970,10,'That the change in political power is likely to be stable over time.',17118,17130),(971,10,'That there will not be new parties popping up.',17131,17139),(972,10,'That is when you\'re certain your investments will stay.',17140,17148),(973,10,'You\'re certain you can negotiate with both parties which creates wealth.',17149,17159),(974,10,'It creates generation.',17160,17162),(975,10,'Secondly political accountability is far superior when parties change power often.',17163,17173),(976,10,'When parties have incentives to hold one another in check.',17174,17183),(977,10,'Then when new parties are arriving old parties are dying.',17184,17193),(978,10,'They\'re not being held to account.',17194,17199),(979,10,'Thirdly parties no longer get the time to implement policies under their system.',17200,17212),(980,10,'Many incumbent parties died in 2024.',17213,17218),(981,10,'Those parties did not have the time to implement their policies.',17219,17229),(982,10,'They did not have the time to pass things to see those changes manifest and that led to enormous numbers of people becoming far worse off.',17230,17255),(983,10,'Very proud to be part of this.',17256,17262),(984,10,'Thank you very much for that nice speech and to continue the government case a warm welcome to the Deputy Prime Minister.',17263,17284),(985,10,'Am I audible?',17285,17287),(986,10,'Before my speech I have a few quick words of gratitude.',17288,17298),(987,10,'First and foremost I\'d like to thank my high school debate coach Nimuach for jump-starting my debate career and being the first to believe in me.',17299,17325),(988,10,'To my friends Anuka, Achir and Mishka who are on Team Mongolia with me I love you and thank you for fostering my love for this activity.',17326,17352),(989,10,'They were all instrumental in Team Mongolia breaking in WUSDC for the first and only time in 2021 despite the lack of a circuit, development opportunities or institutional knowledge.',17353,17381),(990,10,'I am extremely proud to have represented Mongolia on the world school stage and now here at the finals of WUDC.',17382,17402),(991,10,'Most importantly I would like to thank Manuel for being an amazing friend and a lovely partner.',17403,17419),(992,10,'I\'m immensely proud of him for all that he has said and for staying true to himself and his morals.',17420,17439),(993,10,'And finally I would like to send my love and immense gratitude to my lovely friends and family for supporting me in every way and also a shout out to Bates and all the opportunities that it has offered me.',17440,17479),(994,10,'Opening opposition\'s case is dependent on the fact that there are certain people in our world who have economic interests that will not be voting on those economic interests.',17480,17508),(995,10,'We\'re gonna flip that on their side for three reasons.',17509,17518),(996,10,'First of all on our side if this is the most important issue for you in a democracy it\'s actually extremely unclear why.',17519,17541),(997,10,'This is not going to be the most proximate issue and therefore the most like top issue on your ballot.',17542,17561),(998,10,'And secondly now on our side you actually have a diversity of choices.',17562,17574),(999,10,'On their side what you force them to do is have the one major party or two major parties that tell you that your culture is directly tied to the party and therefore the economic policies of that party as well.',17575,17615),(1000,10,'On our side we maximize the choices you have because not not only do you have one Hindu nationalist party that claims to have these set of economic policies you also have another Hindu nationalist party another Hindu party that defends a certain other set of economic policies that may be more beneficial for you.',17616,17670),(1001,10,'And therefore since you fulfill the fact that you are able to meet your religious priorities and also your economic priorities we are far better for these people on our side.',17671,17701),(1002,10,'Therefore benefiting like therefore we flip all the benefits that OO wants to claim.',17702,17715),(1003,10,'Additionally if they want to say that the shift will be more of a shift towards nationalism I think it\'s actually extremely unlikely that the shift towards nationalism will be will be the case in the first place because you already see extremely nationalist rhetoric in places like India that they want to talk about.',17716,17770),(1004,10,'And secondly if you have more diversity in politics and diversity in discourse this is far more likely to be a more like productive conversations.',17771,17795),(1005,10,'A few reasons on why we take this debate.',17796,17804),(1006,10,'First of all on their side of the house they what they have is radicalization that gets more and more radical as time goes on because now it\'s an us versus them policy and a demonization like tactic throughout the entirety of your politics.',17805,17848),(1007,10,'When you have a Trump you have someone worse and like in the next election.',17849,17863),(1008,10,'Secondly when you we tell you that we replace the identity politics with more of an issue based politics no response from OO on this part.',17864,17889),(1009,10,'Thirdly we tell you that we actually improve the discourse that exists in these democracies because now in the status quo in on our side people are more willing to engage with you and start the conversation with you in the first place because in the status quo a Trump supporter is going to be automatically dismissing a leftist based on the fact that their identity is a leftist and vice versa right.',17890,17962),(1010,10,'Secondly when you engage in these conversations the discussions themselves are far more likely to be productive because it\'s not about the individual it\'s not about changing someone\'s mind it\'s more likely to be towards reaching a concession no thank you.',17963,18003),(1011,10,'Secondly on in terms of policy creation we are far more productive because currently when you have major parties that are diametrically opposed to each other they make make it so that when you have a member in Parliament and when you have a representative in the house in the house or Senate they necessarily have to be loyal to the party in order to be like in the next elections in order to be elected through the primaries in the future to protect their career',18004,18089),(1012,10,'and therefore you get things like voting on issues based on your loyalty to the party rather than the actual merit of the issue itself you\'re far more likely to feel free to vote to like to choose based on like the merit of the issue on our side of the house.',18090,18141),(1013,10,'Second of all you increase the likelihood that people reach across the aisle which OO does not respond to at all.',18142,18162),(1014,10,'In the status quo the biggest problem for policy creation is the fact that you have a lot of democratic deadlock where you can\'t actually reach a consensus or a decision or can\'t actually fulfill the policy and can\'t create the change we change that and we make sure that people are actually able to make actual real-life change in their lives.',18163,18225),(1015,10,'Thirdly we are far more representative this is perhaps the most important thing in this debate because we give more representation to a lot more people.',18226,18251),(1016,10,'This looks like people who in the US elections that just happened who wanted to but didn\'t vote for Jill Stein because of their disagreements with Harris on things like Palestine on the environment and they were forced to vote for Harris because the other votes seemed like a non vote.',18252,18302),(1017,10,'We give them more of an option on our side when it\'s not bipartisan like polarization and party politics and second of all you have more of a mixture of policies it\'s more likely in the US now that you see a woman running for elections who is fiscally conservative but pro-choice so as a Republican woman you don\'t always have to vote against your like bodily integrity and fourthly we tell you that there\'s going to be more challenges to the political institution that exists as it is.',18303,18392),(1018,10,'Why is this important?',18393,18396),(1019,10,'Because the current political institution in places like the US in like in the West in general they have historically like historically marginalized the most certain communities and these disenfranchised communities now have the opportunity to like have a party that is willing to capitalize on them and represent them in a previously like unseen way and therefore this is something you care a lot in this debate because these are probably the most vulnerable people.',18397,18472),(1020,10,'Before I move on I\'ll take a few from closing.',18473,18482),(1021,10,'What is the point of discussing social issues?',18483,18490),(1022,10,'The point of discussing social issues is to create change and on our side we are able to do that best.',18491,18511),(1023,10,'Why is that?',18512,18514),(1024,10,'We win on a few things.',18515,18520),(1025,10,'Firstly we win on the fact that we create more change and progress and we make that easier for five reasons.',18521,18541),(1026,10,'Firstly senators are not voting on party politics you\'re voting on the actual issues and therefore the Democratic deadlock is broken.',18542,18562),(1027,10,'This is perhaps the most important because even if opposition can talk about the fact that they may care about good policies if they don\'t prove that they actually get those policies passed they cannot win this debate.',18563,18600),(1028,10,'Secondly we tell you that you are more able to reach concession like that isn\'t dependent on like your party and thirdly you\'re more informed on a particular on your particular policies because you care about the issues rather than someone like Trump telling you that I represent you because I am part of the people.',18601,18656),(1029,10,'Fourthly we tell you that we have better discourse on our side which leads to people being more likely to be empathetic towards each other and discussing each other\'s like needs and we also have less polarization and which means that parties are able to cooperate with each other more.',18657,18706),(1030,10,'Lastly and most importantly we give you a more representative democracy in which people have more of an option of an option when you actually want to create change.',18707,18735),(1031,10,'Why does this matter?',18736,18739),(1032,10,'Because on our side what we prove to you is that we actually create change on the ground and when we create this change it reflects the needs of the people the most and it reflects historically disenfranchised people.',18740,18778),(1033,10,'Also what this creates is that you create less political violence.',18779,18789),(1034,10,'If people feel as though their voice is represented and if they don\'t have to resort to extra legal measures to make sure that your voice is represented you\'re far more likely to be trusting of the political establishment.',18790,18828),(1035,10,'We give a lot more people the agency and voice very very proud to stand in OG.',18829,18845),(1036,10,'Thank you very much for that nice speech and to conclude the top half of this debate a warm welcome to the deputy leader of the opposition.',18846,18872),(1037,10,'When the Ranjha Modi puts Hindu nationalism at the front of the BJP\'s policy platform instead of economic development that is bad.',18873,18894),(1038,10,'It is bad because Hindu nationalism is exclusionary it divides people on the basis of religion and it otherizes Muslims but it\'s also bad because it sucks air away from the need to pull 1 billion Indians out of poverty and that is our contention.',18895,18939),(1039,10,'The BJP doesn\'t need to focus on economics because it can use religion to get votes when otherwise it would need to deliver economic policy and it also changes the incentives of the opposition who now need to talk about Hindu nationalism, co-opt Hindu nationalism or reverse Hindu nationalism rather than focusing on economics.',18940,18994),(1040,10,'That is our case it is incredibly simple and it will win this debate.',18995,19008),(1041,10,'Let\'s talk about the theory of what this shift is and how it occurs.',19009,19022),(1042,10,'I think we have a very simple explanation.',19023,19030),(1043,10,'Throughout the vast majority of history of democracies the political spectrum has been one of economics between left-wing parties that cared about the poor and redistribution and right-wing parties that cared about keeping money in the hands of the richest they fought against each other.',19031,19077),(1044,10,'The change that we point to here is that things like education polarization have brought cultural issues to the forefront because those are the things that elites people are rich enough to not need to care about economic issues are deeply concerned with and they prioritize them at the expense of economic issues which is why if you look at political parties today the issues at the forefront that they talk about are primarily social issues.',19078,19153),(1045,10,'Republicans and Democrats differentiate themselves on the basis of how they feel about trans people about how they feel about immigration not about how they feel on economic policy notably Biden and Trump have basically the same economic policy they both support tariffs they both support bringing back manufacturing jobs and that is bad because it means that people have similar economic policies if you care about economics you can\'t vote for different policy',19154,19227),(1046,10,'but it also means people care less about economic policy because you\'re told the issues that matter are social issues instead of economic issues which makes it far harder to care to mobilize and to do things in that direction.',19228,19267),(1047,10,'Closing government POIs us and says well you know the Tories were voted out in England so clearly bad economic performance still matters and economics hasn\'t disappeared.',19268,19294),(1048,10,'The problem with that is firstly consider the counterfactual how bad did the Tories have to fuck the economy in order to get voted out they had to do Brexit and then Liz Truss had to come in and literally torpedo the guilts like obviously comparatively they\'d have been turfed out far sooner.',19295,19347),(1049,10,'Secondly though we would point out that the reason it was so hard to turf the Tories out is because everyone was so worried about immigration they didn\'t care about the economy being fucked but thirdly Labour\'s economic policy is not very different from the Tories economic policy Rachel Reeves basically abandons any form of real risk redistribution and only does minor things like maybe adding VAT to you know private school attendance which doesn\'t really do anything it\'s precisely our argument they\'re',19348,19430),(1050,10,'so busy positioning themselves over social issues they don\'t get to do the important stuff.',19431,19445),(1051,10,'Let\'s talk then about social issues because the opening government says social issues are really important so maybe if there\'s more discussion of it it\'s good.',19446,19471),(1052,10,'The problem is social issues and economics occur in very different ways.',19472,19483),(1053,10,'Economics is zero-sum which means it\'s a constant battle and it\'s one that must be waged at every time but social issues do not work in that way.',19484,19512),(1054,10,'Having a party advocating for social issues is not actually particularly useful.',19513,19524),(1055,10,'Think about the way we get changed like gay marriage it\'s by activists on the ground convincing people changing hearts and minds and the addition of a political party not only doesn\'t help is often counterproductive because that form of signaling is what locks people in it\'s what polarizes people it\'s what makes them think I am anti gay marriage I am Republican I will never vote in favor of it when on our side those activists are far more likely to be seen as nonpartisan to reach across the aisle to make that case to far more people',19525,19623),(1056,10,'and convince far more people to go on and the same is true for climate change the same is true for indigenous rights the same is true for every social issue you don\'t need politicians on one party on board you need to convince hearts and minds and once you do both parties will get on board because they have incentives to want people to like them and if one party adopts it',19624,19696),(1057,10,'and it\'s not political the other party will adopt it so they don\'t lose voters on the basis of that particular issue.',19697,19718),(1058,10,'Which is why on our side you see social issues converging towards a majority opinion and activists are far more effective at advancing social causes.',19719,19743),(1059,10,'The long arc of justice bends in the direction of opening opposition there.',19744,19756),(1060,10,'Finally we point out though that there\'s just a large amount of distraction on their side.',19757,19772),(1061,10,'Politicians can position themselves on social issues and never have to do anything.',19773,19785),(1062,10,'Saying you\'re pro gay marriage but you can never pass a gay marriage policy because you\'re only one party wastes a whole bunch of time doing nothing when you could have been advocating policies that would actually make people far richer.',19786,19826),(1063,10,'Secondly this team says compromise is important that obviously occurs anyway.',19827,19837),(1064,10,'Firstly moderation is very popular with voters because it allows you to appeal to a broader set of voters.',19838,19856),(1065,10,'Voters tend to be normally disputed you want to be towards the center for reasons suggested by median voter theory.',19857,19876),(1066,10,'Secondly empirical examples think about our Clinton and Blair in New Labour and you know third way moderated in order to win after the Democrats and you know the the Labour had been out of power for a large amount of time.',19877,19918),(1067,10,'Think about our Bush in 2000 wins because of compassionate conservatism.',19919,19929),(1068,10,'We\'re not going to build conservatism on the backs of the poor we\'re going to moderate on economic issues.',19930,19948),(1069,10,'So those kinds of things occur anyway.',19949,19955),(1070,10,'Voters love bipartisanship it occurs anyway.',19956,19961),(1071,10,'They say you can\'t reach across the aisle it\'s hard on their side.',19962,19974),(1072,10,'There\'s more aisles because there\'s more parties under their understanding.',19975,19984),(1073,10,'Those parties are smaller and more ideological so less willing to compromise because they define themselves around their position-taking so they don\'t change and engage in the way that this team needs them to.',19985,20019),(1074,10,'So they don\'t get compromised.',20020,20024),(1075,10,'Finally weigh this against stability which is a more important question and they lose on stability because they say they want to shake up the status quo.',20025,20051),(1076,10,'They say they want to challenge the institutions that creates instability.',20052,20062),(1077,10,'More parties creates instability because it\'s harder to form coalitions it\'s harder to get agreement particularly when you disagree not just on economics as you might on our side maybe there are five parties with different economic views but they\'re on a spectrum and you can ally with the ones closest to you very easily.',20063,20117),(1078,10,'Now you disagree not just on economics but on a myriad of additional issues not all of which align in any one way.',20118,20140),(1079,10,'So it\'s hard to get politics and that matters for the reasons we tell you.',20141,20155),(1080,10,'It means less business confidence.',20156,20160),(1081,10,'It means less capacity to implement policy.',20161,20167),(1082,10,'It means less accountability because low information voters don\'t know what\'s going on.',20168,20180),(1083,10,'They don\'t understand multiple parties.',20181,20185),(1084,10,'They don\'t know what\'s happening when parties change. Closing.',20186,20194),(1085,10,'What is the biggest threat facing major democracies today?',20195,20203),(1086,10,'We would contend that the biggest threat facing major democracies is a lack of capacity to help the poor because when the poor are not fed that is what creates huge amounts of instability.',20204,20237),(1087,10,'That is what allows revolutions to form and that is what allows autocracies to come to power.',20238,20254),(1088,10,'When you do not cater to the most basic needs in the debate that is when those harms occur and the weighing here is very clear.',20255,20280),(1089,10,'The poor are by far the largest group in the debate and even if you care about other oppressed groups the way that they are disadvantaged is primarily through poverty.',20281,20310),(1090,10,'It\'s through the tool of class.',20311,20316),(1091,10,'So we help them the most.',20317,20322),(1092,10,'If you believe for example that black people are disadvantaged often that makes them the poorest group and the group that is helped more most by redistribution.',20323,20349),(1093,10,'That is the most fungible form of oppression that we uniquely target.',20350,20361),(1094,10,'Finally on representation this team says more parties means more parties that you\'re happy with.',20362,20376),(1095,10,'You feel better when you vote.',20377,20382),(1096,10,'We don\'t care if you feel happy when you vote.',20383,20392),(1097,10,'We care about tangible outcomes at the end of the day and more parties does not mean better policy for minority groups because large parties are broad tents that have incentives to cater to them anyway.',20393,20428),(1098,10,'We explain why social change goes better on our side.',20429,20438),(1099,10,'Coalition agreements mean that those minor parties have to compromise anyway and often just lose out on being part of those coalitions and representation for one minority group is counted by a party that is opposed to that minority group.',20439,20478),(1100,10,'These parties are niche.',20479,20482),(1101,10,'They struggle to get anything done.',20483,20488),(1102,10,'They struggle to really represent people.',20489,20494),(1103,10,'Maybe you feel a bit happy about voting for them.',20495,20504),(1104,10,'What we care about is that food goes in people\'s mouths.',20505,20515),(1105,10,'What we care about is that the poor actually have something to help them.',20516,20529),(1106,10,'Thank you very much for that speech.',20530,20536),(1107,10,'Now we\'re moving back towards the bottom half of this debate and to introduce us to the bottom half a warm welcome to A couple of brief thank yous before beginning.',20537,20567),(1108,10,'I\'ll keep these brief.',20568,20571),(1109,10,'First, to Tejas, Liz, Matt, I\'m enormously grateful for everything you\'ve done for me.',20572,20585),(1110,10,'To the many people who\'ve supported me on my journey, Sophie, Beasley, Julia, Wajih, Manuel, Yuso, Team Mexico, my parents, Connor, Kate, the entire Dartmouth team, thank you all so much.',20586,20616),(1111,10,'The biggest threat facing major democracies is not poverty.',20617,20625),(1112,10,'It is that major democracies are less democratic than they ever have been.',20626,20638),(1113,10,'That most democracies are facing a major crisis of democratic institutions.',20639,20649),(1114,10,'That support for institutions is at an all-time low.',20650,20659),(1115,10,'That support for democratic backsliding is on the rise.',20660,20668),(1116,10,'That Trump, an explicit anti-democratic demagogue, was elected.',20669,20677),(1117,10,'That the BJP is gaining ground in India.',20678,20685),(1118,10,'That anti-institutionalist sentiment is on the rise.',20686,20693),(1119,10,'The thesis of closing government is straightforward.',20694,20700),(1120,10,'You need to upend the conventional left-right spectrum to maintain support for democratic institutions, to re-energize the working class in a way that empowers anti-complacent sentiment and hence reduces the overt power of technocratic institutions and anti-democratic demagogues.',20701,20742),(1121,10,'How do we reduce democratic backsliding through upending that spectrum?',20743,20752),(1122,10,'Three mechanisms from closing government, then weighing why this wins on opening opposition\'s metric on working class politics.',20753,20770),(1123,10,'First, the working class, contrary to opening opposition\'s claim, was historically marginalized by the left-right spectrum.',20771,20787),(1124,10,'Now, opening government, to their credit, does name drop the problem of educational polarization.',20788,20801),(1125,10,'We\'ll make the argument.',20802,20805),(1126,10,'Here\'s how it works.',20806,20809),(1127,10,'Historically, politics was dominated by center-left and center-right parties, as O.O. correctly notes.',20810,20825),(1128,10,'That\'s the SPD and CDU in Germany, PASOK and New Democracy in Greece, the Democrats and GOP in the United States.',20826,20846),(1129,10,'Importantly, historically, there was a strong correlation between your economic status and your views on social issues.',20847,20863),(1130,10,'That is, on average, people who were lower income tended to be relatively more supportive of social reform, and people who were relatively higher income tended to be relatively more supportive of traditionalist institutions.',20864,20897),(1131,10,'Why was that?',20898,20900),(1132,10,'Because historically, that correlation held, given that relatively impoverished groups stood to benefit from upending social structures that disadvantaged them, and relatively wealthy interests stood to benefit from a maintenance of the status quo.',20901,20934),(1133,10,'That correlation is no longer true.',20935,20940),(1134,10,'Empirically, the most progressive people today are also the highest income, and the most conservative people socially are those who are the lowest income.',20941,20964),(1135,10,'Why is that? It\'s simple.',20965,20969),(1136,10,'It\'s because the rate of social progress in the last 30 to 40 years has dramatically outpaced the rate of economic progress.',20970,20991),(1137,10,'Intuitively, the level at which human rights and socially leftist issues have been protected in the European Union post-2008 is literally multiple times greater than the average GDP per year growth in the EU.',20992,21026),(1138,10,'The reason why, of course, is because over time societies become increasingly more liberal as you become increasingly more exposed to progressive values, but the same does not apply to economics.',21027,21057),(1139,10,'There are diminishing marginal returns as countries further develop.',21058,21066),(1140,10,'They hit the middle income trap.',21067,21072),(1141,10,'The further returns to capital become diminishing.',21073,21079),(1142,10,'Here\'s why that\'s important.',21080,21083),(1143,10,'What this means today is that the relative balance has shifted.',21084,21094),(1144,10,'There is an anti-correlation between income and socially progressive values.',21095,21105),(1145,10,'The most conservative people on immigration and on culture are often working class, and the people who are the most progressive are those who are the wealthiest.',21106,21132),(1146,10,'Why is this framing essential?',21133,21137),(1147,10,'It demonstrates why there is a necessity to upend the traditional left-right spectrum.',21138,21151),(1148,10,'Historically, center-left parties and center-right parties were mutually exclusive on both economic and social issues.',21152,21168),(1149,10,'So the center-left could be pro-the welfare state and pro-LGBTQ rights.',21169,21182),(1150,10,'The center-right could be pro-neoliberalism and relatively opposed to gay marriage.',21183,21195),(1151,10,'And that worked because the relative center-left and center-right bases agreed broadly on those two factions.',21196,21213),(1152,10,'The problem is you need to revert that trend to represent the vast majority of contemporary voters who are empirically working in middle-class voters for the simple reason that the 1% are called the 1% because there\'s only 1% of them.',21214,21255),(1153,10,'The reason why this is important is twofold.',21256,21263),(1154,10,'Firstly, absent the rise of parties that, for example, like the National Rally in France, can join left-wing economic stances, for example being pro-the welfare state, with right-wing cultural stances like opposing further immigration, the likely consequence is huge levels of alienation, disillusionment, and active complacency amongst the vast majority of voters who simply do not believe that they can fit into a binarized political spectrum.',21264,21332),(1155,10,'Credit to opening government.',21333,21336),(1156,10,'They reasonably make the claim that many people feel disenfranchised by the left-right spectrum.',21337,21351),(1157,10,'Our extension analytically explains not why this is to some voters, but why this constitutes the vast majority of voters in the status quo.',21352,21375),(1158,10,'Why is this important?',21376,21379),(1159,10,'Setting aside the fact that this unlocks opening government\'s principle, it also has critical implications for democratic backsliding. Here\'s why.',21380,21399),(1160,10,'Because contrary to popular opinion, most democratic backsliding does not happen because people actively hate democracy.',21400,21415),(1161,10,'Democratic backsliding happens because the average voter simply does not believe that democratic institutions are worth protecting.',21416,21432),(1162,10,'You do not turn out to a protest if you don\'t think elections represent you.',21433,21447),(1163,10,'You don\'t care about assaults on the courts if you don\'t believe the judges appointed by parties to the courts are representative.',21448,21469),(1164,10,'What you need to prevent backsliding, what you need to energize rallies that call out the BJP\'s democratic backsliding, such that the BJP did suffer a major electoral loss relative to expectations in this year\'s election, is to energize people who otherwise do not feel represented.',21470,21515),(1165,10,'That is why closing government\'s stance is that the rise of right-wing populism is probably good.',21516,21532),(1166,10,'There are huge numbers of working-class people across Europe who feel disenfranchised by parties like the Labour Party, and like the SPD, and like PASOK, that refuse to recognize reality that working-class voters fucking hate immigration and they want change.',21533,21574),(1167,10,'They will never get it.',21575,21579),(1168,10,'The way that you get people to care again about democracy, given that democratic backsliding trends happen on both sides, is to unlock that side of the political spectrum.',21580,21608),(1169,10,'Before I move on to our second mechanism, O.O.',21609,21618),(1170,10,'This shift away from traditional left-right spectrum wouldn\'t, sorry, it doesn\'t matter if there\'s an anti-correlation between social views and economic views when you prioritize economic views and social views are not politicized because you can just have social change outside the political spectrum.',21619,21664),(1171,10,'No, because you obviously, the first, the last premise is intuitively untrue.',21665,21676),(1172,10,'You obviously need legal protections for a wide range of social issues, for example, protections for abortion rights and so forth.',21677,21697),(1173,10,'But more importantly, people do care very deeply about cultural issues because they\'re often tied to their identity.',21698,21715),(1174,10,'For example, a huge number of people are often pro-life in a way that is tethered to their religious conception of the good life, which is why you necessarily cannot compromise and why you need parties that are both left-wing on economic issues and right-wing on cultural issues.',21716,21766),(1175,10,'Secondly, on technocratic backsliding, a major crisis facing major democracies is that the vast majority of policy is no longer controlled by democratically elected politicians.',21767,21791),(1176,10,'It is controlled by judges, by bureaucrats, by technocrats, by international institutions like the European Union.',21792,21807),(1177,10,'Why has this happened?',21808,21811),(1178,10,'Because the convergence of center-left and center-right parties and the historical dominance of those parties gave rise to a neoliberal consensus.',21812,21834),(1179,10,'The fact is that Clinton and Reagan were in many ways identical in their views, for example, on financial regulation and the role of the state in the economy.',21835,21863),(1180,10,'The problem with dominance of center-left and center-right parties is that both parties stand to benefit from outsourcing ever more control to non-democratic institutions because they converge broadly on a range of economic and social issues.',21864,21902),(1181,10,'But this fundamentally subverts democracy by reducing the capacity for democratically elected officials to make critical decisions about whether women can access the right to abortion, about whether tax rates should be higher or lower, about the extent to which regulation occurs.',21903,21944),(1182,10,'We fix that under our side of the house by challenging that conception as O.O.',21945,21960),(1183,10,'concedes because we prevent the greatest threat to democracies, proud to win from closing government.',21961,21975),(1184,10,'Thank you very much for that very nice speech.',21976,21984),(1185,10,'And to introduce the final team in the round, I would like to welcome the member of opposition. All right.',21985,22004),(1186,10,'Can you hear me clearly?',22005,22009),(1187,10,'Okay, that\'s fine.',22010,22012),(1188,10,'I really hope you won\'t immediately stop listening, but Francis Fukuyama was right.',22013,22025),(1189,10,'We did manage to reach the end of history, but we managed to squander it.',22026,22040),(1190,10,'We live in a time where we waste our precious political capital discussing non-issues, being distracted in terms of faithfulness and in terms of targeting groups that often have the same interests as ours rather than genuinely advocating for our own interests.',22041,22083),(1191,10,'What I want to do in this extension is I essentially want to win the characterization clash.',22084,22100),(1192,10,'I want to explain to you why the sorts of positive sources of differentiation that government and the government bench discuss are actually being trumped by the negative sources that opening opposition wishes to bring in this debate.',22101,22138),(1193,10,'Before that though, I wish to bring three pieces of framing.',22139,22149),(1194,10,'The first one is that the left-right spectrum still regularly features multiple parties with differentiation between them.',22150,22167),(1195,10,'Crucially, it\'s possible to have different axes along which parties are left or right, be it the economy or be it on social issues.',22168,22191),(1196,10,'But also this means that you have a genuine degree of choice and accountability between those groups, especially because it\'s very clear where it is that they\'re able to win voters from another.',22192,22224),(1197,10,'That is, the center-right criticizes the right-wing precisely because those are the voters that you can flip over.',22225,22244),(1198,10,'On their side, it is far more difficult to exercise this type of accountability when it\'s not clear who your voter base is.',22245,22267),(1199,10,'And therefore this makes political conversations worse.',22268,22274),(1200,10,'The second piece of framing, however, is that the left-right balance can shift over history.',22275,22290),(1201,10,'A hundred and fifty years ago, I would have been considered a radical, if not insane, for wanting to give women the vote.',22291,22313),(1202,10,'And the thing is, this is not the same as crossing across the aisle.',22314,22327),(1203,10,'It still is the case that you have parties genuinely disagreeing, but it means that a lot of the things that were considered left are now considered commonplace even by parties that support the establishment.',22328,22362),(1204,10,'But the third and important piece of framing is that life for the longest time continued to get better.',22363,22381),(1205,10,'Yes, the financial crisis, yes this and that, but what I want to note is that since the rise of the alt-right, since these new politics that have been introduced, we have seen living conditions degrade for many individuals.',22382,22421),(1206,10,'We have seen rights get lost and we have seen rates of social inequality increase more and more as time goes on.',22422,22443),(1207,10,'And this is exactly where the extension comes in.',22444,22452),(1208,10,'I\'m going to prove to you why it is that basically this new politics that I want to introduce you is largely a facade.',22453,22476),(1209,10,'Why it is that the social issues we\'re solving aren\'t genuine and therefore proving how it is that you harm those groups.',22477,22498),(1210,10,'Why is this true?',22499,22502),(1211,10,'Two main reasons, one.',22503,22506),(1212,10,'The first one is that it\'s far easier to cause moral panic on social issues.',22507,22521),(1213,10,'This is precisely because most individuals have a far weaker understanding of the economy or of broad and complicated concepts, be it inflation or international trade.',22522,22547),(1214,10,'The easiest way to ensure you\'re able to agitate individuals is to convince them that there is someone out to get them.',22548,22569),(1215,10,'And the thing is, for most people, this doesn\'t even need to be true.',22570,22583),(1216,10,'That is, if you can convince middle class individuals that trans women entering women\'s bathrooms is a problem and that this is more important than the class disparity between the rich and the middle class or the working class, that is precisely when you\'re most able to persuade them.',22584,22632),(1217,10,'The second reason, however, is that the hateful content that people see is unavoidable.',22633,22646),(1218,10,'And this is very important because of the pipeline effect it has on individuals.',22647,22660),(1219,10,'And I want to be very clear what it looks like.',22661,22671),(1220,10,'Because yes, you may initially be willing to scrutinize the things you see.',22672,22684),(1221,10,'But the problem is that when you do go online and you constantly see this content, when it\'s around every corner precisely because it\'s what you\'re most likely to click on, exactly because it\'s something that appeals to your sense of morality, to your sense of safety, to your sense of well-being, what happens is you stop questioning.',22685,22743),(1222,10,'You eventually surrender yourself to what this content has to say to you.',22744,22756),(1223,10,'And the problem is that that is when people have stopped critically reflecting on what it is they\'re being exposed to you.',22757,22778),(1224,10,'What I want to basically now prove is why it is that this hateful content actually isn\'t productive.',22779,22796),(1225,10,'The first reason is that minorities are definitionally minorities.',22797,22805),(1226,10,'And therefore, it\'s the case that the majority of the content you\'ll see generated will be targeting those groups insofar as on either side of the house, they don\'t have enough of a voice to push back.',22806,22842),(1227,10,'That is, when you have the majority of society telling you that these people are harmful, that is what is going to dominate the online narratives.',22843,22868),(1228,10,'The second reason, however, is that I want you to question who it is that these cross-party populists are.',22869,22888),(1229,10,'People like Meloni, people like Bolsonaro in Brazil.',22889,22896),(1230,10,'The reason that these are going to be overwhelmingly politically negative is precisely because they\'re most able to control the funding that is in interest of to basically support those parties.',22897,22927),(1231,10,'That is, who is it who wants to distract you from the real issues?',22928,22941),(1232,10,'The people who have the most to gain.',22942,22949),(1233,10,'The people who have the money and who don\'t want you taxing it.',22950,22962),(1234,10,'And this is true for two reasons.',22963,22969),(1235,10,'The first one is that money controls politics.',22970,22977),(1236,10,'How many advertisements you can buy, how much lobbying you can do, basically controls what politicians say and how many people see that message.',22978,23001),(1237,10,'Therefore, the side that aligns with big business interests will be the one basically getting most of this money.',23002,23020),(1238,10,'But also, secondly, they just don\'t have an incentive to genuinely solve these issues for you.',23021,23036),(1239,10,'They want to continue keeping you angry because you don\'t vote for these parties because you genuinely believe in them.',23037,23056),(1240,10,'Rather, you vote for them exactly because they make you angry in this manner.',23057,23070),(1241,10,'I\'ll take a POI from opening, please.',23071,23077),(1242,10,'The lack of questioning occurs when you assume that certain economic positions are intrinsically tied to social ones.',23078,23095),(1243,10,'People in Catholic and Muslim countries have voted against their own economic interests because they are told that it is intrinsically anti-Catholic or anti-Muslim to redistribute.',23096,23123),(1244,10,'This is what we prove in opening government.',23124,23131),(1245,10,'They don\'t respond to.',23132,23135),(1246,10,'.. More of this in WIP, but I think my framing already defeats this, basically.',23136,23149),(1247,10,'Let me do some impacting.',23150,23154),(1248,10,'The first problem is a direct and accelerating degradation of political systems.',23155,23166),(1249,10,'And this is because a political system isn\'t just about a set of formal rules.',23167,23181),(1250,10,'It is about the interaction of parties within those systems.',23182,23191),(1251,10,'This looks like the cohesion when your party can caucus and have discussions on issues.',23192,23206),(1252,10,'This looks like essentially having the ability to govern in an effective manner insofar as you have long-standing mechanisms, insofar as the party has a reputation to maintain and there is consistency in what the voter base expects of your party.',23207,23248),(1253,10,'You have been in government before and you are someone who basically shows your ideas to the people who vote for you.',23249,23270),(1254,10,'Not to harm them on their side.',23271,23277),(1255,10,'On their side of the house, it is when you see these broad-tent coalitions that opening government treats with rose-tinted glasses, but lead to the repeated collapse of the French government basically, where you try to compromise on far too many ideas instead of being able to reach common ground.',23278,23329),(1256,10,'But more importantly, you divide people.',23330,23335),(1257,10,'And this is true for a very simple reason.',23336,23344),(1258,10,'You basically make people feel alienated.',23345,23350),(1259,10,'Because note what the prerequisite is to the genuine political conversation they want on government bench.',23351,23366),(1260,10,'You need to feel safe within your beliefs.',23367,23374),(1261,10,'You need to know that you exist within a community that supports you and that you are not the only one who bears those ideas.',23375,23399),(1262,10,'The problem is that when you feel like what you are expressing or voting on is on the fringe, the moment somebody else questions you, you feel on edge.',23400,23428),(1263,10,'You go on the defensive.',23429,23433),(1264,10,'And this is precisely when you lack this high degree of confidence within the political system or within your beliefs, that you are far less likely to engage in these conversations or hold yourself accountable to them.',23434,23470),(1265,10,'Not a comparative on our side.',23471,23476),(1266,10,'We don\'t motivate people by anger.',23477,23482),(1267,10,'We motivate them by hope.',23483,23487),(1268,10,'We tell them that the system works and has provided for you.',23488,23499),(1269,10,'Yes, things are imperfect, but things have, can, and will continue getting better insofar as you work with us.',23500,23518),(1270,10,'Parties are imperfect, but we think they are far more accountable than what you get on the alternative.',23519,23536),(1271,10,'Importantly, we think that parties are better when they are merely seen as occupants of government.',23537,23552),(1272,10,'We don\'t want these short-term single-issue politicians, and we think you uniquely get good political engagement on our side.',23553,23573),(1273,10,'And for all these reasons, I have never been prouder to oppose.',23574,23585),(1274,10,'Thank you very much for that speech.',23586,23592),(1275,10,'And to conclude the government side of this debate, please, a big round of applause for the government whip!',23593,23611),(1276,10,'Can I be heard?',23612,23615),(1277,10,'To be clear, the closing government extension is not only about democratic backsliding.',23616,23628),(1278,10,'It is fundamentally about characterizing why the traditional left-right spectrum no longer reflects the views of the median working and middle class voter.',23629,23652),(1279,10,'And this is necessary analysis for the round to happen because it shows, therefore, that the traditional understanding of a left-right spectrum is no longer democratically representative.',23653,23680),(1280,10,'I\'ll do a few things.',23681,23685),(1281,10,'Firstly, I\'ll just quickly take out CO and then I\'ll talk about OOA and weigh ourselves against them.',23686,23703),(1282,10,'Firstly, then, CO.',23704,23706),(1283,10,'I think the two main lines to come up with is, firstly, there is still going to be differentiation in the left-right spectrum.',23707,23730),(1284,10,'So you have center-left, center-right, far-left, far-right, which often mean there is still a level of multiplicity when it comes to policies and ideology.',23731,23758),(1285,10,'For one, like, obviously this is comparative.',23759,23765),(1286,10,'It is still likely highly rigid because often you have to work within party lines in many countries.',23766,23783),(1287,10,'But second, let\'s actually flip the argument.',23784,23790),(1288,10,'When parties can only compete within the left or right-wing spectrum, they are more likely to radicalize on their side of the house because they have to appeal further to the left and further to the right-wing basis. Why?',23791,23831),(1289,10,'Because often the far-left and far-right within these parties, those who hold the most extreme views, often are ones who have lots of power.',23832,23857),(1290,10,'They\'re often the ones who have the loudest say.',23858,23866),(1291,10,'They often are the ones who are more likely to veto and not be willing to horse trade if you do not pander to their views.',23867,23892),(1292,10,'All of this means it is actually far worse on their side because actually having a left-right spectrum gives rise to people on the very ends of it.',23893,23921),(1293,10,'It often disproportionately weighs their voices louder.',23922,23928),(1294,10,'That is far worse on their side of the house.',23929,23938),(1295,10,'The second thing that CEO says is, but the politicians that rise out of a break of the left-right spectrum will be politicians who are bad.',23939,23965),(1296,10,'Those who have lots of power, money, resources, they are politically negative, they actually don\'t really want to solve issues that people face on the ground.',23966,23991),(1297,10,'First, let\'s actually flip this argument.',23992,23997),(1298,10,'For one, the center left and right dominance traditionally has favored powerful institutions.',23998,24010),(1299,10,'Most center-right parties in Europe have ties to the church, IECF, the CDU in Germany.',24011,24026),(1300,10,'The rise of new parties is what actually creates opportunities for disruption.',24027,24038),(1301,10,'This is evident in the status quo.',24039,24045),(1302,10,'That\'s why often political campaigning by Trump, who actively shows that they\'re favoring the working class and breaking through traditional norms of what the left and right had traditionally stood for, are people who have become immensely, immensely politically appealing in recent years.',24046,24088),(1303,10,'Moving on then, I want to talk about OO.',24089,24097),(1304,10,'The first thing I want to do is I want to weigh against OO.',24098,24111),(1305,10,'I think our extension directly beats OO for a few reasons.',24112,24122),(1306,10,'We explain that the working class voters are more involved in politics on our side because unconventional parties can speak directly to their left-wing economic views and their right-wing cultural issues and their concerns.',24123,24158),(1307,10,'OO says that people care more about economics than culture, but recognize cultural issues are often not insular from economics.',24159,24178),(1308,10,'Often cultural issues can affect how you view something economically, i.e.',24179,24190),(1309,10,'often immigration is tied to what you think, for example, a certain economic policy should be.',24191,24206),(1310,10,'Do you think these people are someone who are threatening your jobs?',24207,24218),(1311,10,'Often these things are interlinked.',24219,24223),(1312,10,'You often connect the two issues.',24224,24229),(1313,10,'Therefore, these are not insular issues, and therefore it is necessary for us to consider this awakening.',24230,24246),(1314,10,'Secondly, moving on then, I want to provide an explanation on OO\'s case on the working class.',24247,24263),(1315,10,'I want to explain why economics will likely always matter in voting, regardless of whether it is the locus of mobilization among the working class, and then I\'ll compare it against the alternatives they give as to what our side has to stand for.',24264,24307),(1316,10,'First and foremost, it is likely that middle and working class people will always care about economics.',24308,24324),(1317,10,'This is the most fundamental part of their lives.',24325,24333),(1318,10,'This is the first condition they need to meet to access all their other rights.',24334,24348),(1319,10,'For most people who struggle from paycheck to paycheck to meet their day-to-day needs, economics is all you can consider.',24349,24370),(1320,10,'This is why voters on both sides of the House will likely care.',24371,24383),(1321,10,'But here\'s what\'s the important part.',24384,24389),(1322,10,'It is not to say that everyone who is disenfranchised has to act as a monolith when it comes to economic issues, that single mothers who want maternity leave might not meet the same set of economic policy as the middle class worker who now will fall into a new tax bracket and want something else.',24390,24445),(1323,10,'And it is precisely because the poor and the working class, who were previously viewed as monolithic voters, are now shocker, can vote differently, which means that parties across the aisle are now forced to the table to introspect and reconsider what policies they want to craft to better represent voters on our side of the House.',24446,24502),(1324,10,'But second, flip, the reason why we think violence is wrong is not only because of the pain it causes to you, but because it subjects your will at the disposal of someone else.',24503,24536),(1325,10,'When you are handstrapped and you cannot make a choice.',24537,24546),(1326,10,'Poverty is violence for many people who are strapped at the hands of the left-right spectrum because in the past, if you wanted economic policy, you also had to subscribe to racism.',24547,24579),(1327,10,'You had to deny women the right to abortion.',24580,24588),(1328,10,'Our side on the comparative energizes people as for Ryan\'s extension.',24589,24599),(1329,10,'It energized disparaged voters to come out to vote, to defend their rights, to go to rallies, to fight for economic liberty.',24600,24621),(1330,10,'This argument is logically prior to the argument of O.O.',24622,24632),(1331,10,'So if you believe Ryan\'s argument on our side of the House, you energize a new group of voters that were previously not unblocked on their side.',24633,24659),(1332,10,'We actually gain most of the benefits they want to push for. Opening.',24660,24672),(1333,10,'Yeah, you don\'t preserve democracy by upending it, and the harms you identify clearly stem from the shift away from traditional politics.',24673,24694),(1334,10,'Like Trump, who literally adopted democratic positions on protectionism and social services and ran on immigration.',24695,24710),(1335,10,'That is the problem.',24711,24714),(1336,10,'You defend it.',24715,24717),(1337,10,'This is true to some degree.',24718,24723),(1338,10,'Some politicians have done this, undoubtable.',24724,24729),(1339,10,'The concern is obviously there are checkbacks against this.',24730,24738),(1340,10,'There\'s obviously a rise of alternate discourse that likely happens, and we\'ve seen this in the current status quo as well.',24739,24759),(1341,10,'We don\'t have to defend isolated examples of it.',24760,24768),(1342,10,'We can prove that the overall trend is good. Second, O.O.',24769,24780),(1343,10,'says that the politicization of social issues is bad on our side and will happen, which will lead to radicalization.',24781,24800),(1344,10,'But the counterfactual is lots of these issues get sidelined.',24801,24810),(1345,10,'It is that disenfranchised groups are never ever likely to win on these issues. Why?',24811,24825),(1346,10,'Because historically, they have been disempowered economically, which often means it is harder for them to do things like pool resources, to mobilize and to create change.',24826,24852),(1347,10,'The DLO\'s proposal is social issues should be reached by activists, and they can change hearts and minds.',24853,24870),(1348,10,'But one, you need legislation and policy to protect rights, to not only make sure people can go out to the streets in the first place, but to make sure that more people are feeling safe enough they can do so.',24871,24911),(1349,10,'You need parties to protect rights like the right to protest and the right to free speech, to make sure advocates can speak and to encourage people to come out.',24912,24941),(1350,10,'But also, second, hearts and minds only get changed when you force these issues to the forefront.',24942,24958),(1351,10,'Most people only care a little bit about racial equality and the conditions migrants face, but cognitive dissidence means you forget about it.',24959,24981),(1352,10,'Therefore, often these issues being talked up front is what actually raises awareness and gives advocates the rhetoric and the language to use their advocacy outside of politics as well.',24982,25011),(1353,10,'The last thing I want to talk about is quickly on stability as per the O.O.',25012,25028),(1354,10,'case, that it\'s good for things like investment and accountability.',25029,25038),(1355,10,'Like, obviously the shift away from the left-right spectrum is likely a gradual one as we\'ve seen over the course of history.',25039,25061),(1356,10,'Issues of capital flight and investors leaving is probably a silly one that doesn\'t really apply to this debate much.',25062,25081),(1357,10,'And also, second, our extension flips the idea of accountability because in both sides of the house, political institutions are not always easy to access for anyone, but it is the people who can counter it that also matter.',25082,25120),(1358,10,'We explain to you why on our side of the house people are far 