't matter if the effective degree of representation goes down.',25384,25396),(1379,10,'So yes, there may be lots of far-right parties in parliament talking about things, but they can\'t put together an effective government because they are platforms based on hate which no other party will agree with.',25397,25433),(1380,10,'That is exactly what you see in countries like the Netherlands for instance, where you have a total collapse of the government as a result of the rise of the far-right, which means that no voters can be represented, left or right.',25434,25476),(1381,10,'This is why the end outcome of their extension, which is restoring faith in democracy, doesn\'t happen because when your government doesn\'t do anything, you have no faith in democracy.',25477,25506),(1382,10,'This part is additionally untrue because the way these parties situate themselves is as outsiders to the democratic system, precisely in the language of CG, upending the democratic process in order to put a breath of fresh air into it, which is precisely why they won\'t restore faith in the way that they suggest.',25507,25560),(1383,10,'The second big thing that CG says is that there is technocratic backsliding, no thank you, in the world of opposition.',25561,25581),(1384,10,'I would note firstly that this is just a subset of the OG material on challenging the establishment so it\'s largely derivative, but more importantly, the way you get to technocracy is precisely when politics becomes more unstable and no longer works.',25582,25623),(1385,10,'When the elected government cannot deliver anything because it\'s stuck in fractious coalitions based on identity politics in the world of government, that is when policy making is devolved to the courts or bureaucracy.',25624,25657),(1386,10,'No thank you.',25658,25660),(1387,10,'That is exactly what you see in the US, when parties are no longer able to come to the table and compromise as a result of the populist rhetoric of the government side, that is when the Supreme Court and other unelected government agencies make policy instead.',25661,25707),(1388,10,'No thank you.',25708,25710),(1389,10,'So that is a problem for their side.',25711,25718),(1390,10,'Weighing against OO then, OO says two big things.',25719,25727),(1391,10,'The first is that economic issues are extremely important.',25728,25736),(1392,10,'Question, why is it that the new parties on government bench will always and everywhere sideline economic issues?',25737,25754),(1393,10,'I note that many of opposition\'s examples fail.',25755,25762),(1394,10,'For example, the BJP did prioritise a significant degree of economic development in India.',25763,25776),(1395,10,'We give you a far, because as OG notes, surely voters do care about economics to a significant degree and therefore these new parties will be responsive to these issues.',25777,25806),(1396,10,'No thank you.',25807,25809),(1397,10,'The better response that comes to you in Chris\'s argument is that even if this is true, the solutions to economic problems that these parties give are not constructive.',25810,25838),(1398,10,'Because the way these far right parties tell you you need to save the economy is not by restructuring the welfare system along the technocratic lines as OO points out, but just kicking out all the immigrants because the immigrants are the ones draining the welfare money.',25839,25885),(1399,10,'So even if it\'s true as government suggests that these new parties also identify economic issues, those parties are uniquely unable to solve these economic issues precisely because they peddle on hate in the way that only Chris describes.',25886,25924),(1400,10,'No thank you.',25925,25927),(1401,10,'The second issue with OO is that they don\'t prove why left parties and our side of the house will continue to fight poverty in the way they describe.',25928,25956),(1402,10,'So a large part of the argument is missing.',25957,25965),(1403,10,'They say parties on government side don\'t deal with economic issues, but the second part to prove why left parties do this is missing.',25966,25989),(1404,10,'I would note empirically a large reason why these parties rose on government side is because centre left parties first abandoned the working class.',25990,26013),(1405,10,'It was first the case that the democratic party ran towards the middle or new labour occurred in the UK and then you got the rise of far right parties on their side of the house.',26014,26049),(1406,10,'No thank you.',26050,26052),(1407,10,'So it\'s not necessarily clear why these parties continue to fight poverty on our side.',26053,26067),(1408,10,'CO\'s case has a far lower burden.',26068,26074),(1409,10,'We don\'t necessarily need to prove to you that the left right spectrum as exists in our counterfactual world will do all of the good things that opening opposition suggests necessarily.',26075,26105),(1410,10,'All we need to prove is that the structural incentives of those parties means that they are less likely to peddle in hate, xenophobia and the other kinds of rhetoric that Chris describes to you.',26106,26140),(1411,10,'No thank you.',26141,26143),(1412,10,'Let\'s talk about what Chris gives you.',26144,26150),(1413,10,'The framing I would give for Chris\'s argument is that democracy and its survival as a political system and not just a collection of rules is prior to everything else.',26151,26180),(1414,10,'If the democracy no longer functions then none of the voters can be represented.',26181,26194),(1415,10,'CJ says a few things to this.',26195,26201),(1416,10,'The first thing they say is there is more radicalisation on our side when you only have the left right spectrum.',26202,26222),(1417,10,'I would note that it\'s true you can run to the ends of the spectrum on our side but there are no votes there.',26223,26246),(1418,10,'The reason these are fringe positions is because they are ones in which voters don\'t exist without the political realignment which only exists on government side of the house.',26247,26275),(1419,10,'No thank you.',26276,26278),(1420,10,'The second thing they say is that people outside the establishment are the ones who push these policies on their side.',26279,26299),(1421,10,'That\'s precisely the problem.',26300,26303),(1422,10,'Chris shows to you why the only way in which people outside the establishment can enter the political arena without backing from for example major donors or other political parties currently is to peddle in hateful rhetoric, blame immigrants and all those kinds of things. Opening.',26304,26349),(1423,10,'In India the only option for an opposition party that cares for the minorities or for the millions of disregarded farmers by the BJP seems to be anti-Hindu nationalist.',26350,26379),(1424,10,'So you do not succeed.',26380,26384),(1425,10,'Only the shift away from this where you have more fractions of individuals who can in fact go up that can be a solution for the ways that we describe that CG doesn\'t weigh against and you don\'t at all respond to.',26385,26426),(1426,10,'Yeah so the point is that it is only seen as anti-Hindu nationalist in your world when Hindu nationalism comes to the fore which is precisely the problem described in Chris\'s speech.',26427,26459),(1427,10,'What is the counterfactual on our side and why is it that establishment parties don\'t peddle in hate in the way we suggest?',26460,26482),(1428,10,'They\'re systemically less likely to do this because they are more subject to things like the public sphere of media regulation.',26483,26503),(1429,10,'When the Guardian or the BBC calls out a major party for engaging in hateful rhetoric that is much more likely to stick because that party has been around for far longer and they can\'t just simply run away to a new party.',26504,26546),(1430,10,'That\'s why party loyalty contrary to OG and CG is a good thing because people who have loyalty to the party are more likely to hold it to account.',26547,26575),(1431,10,'The people who hold the Labour Party to account in the UK are precisely the strongest Labour supporters.',26576,26593),(1432,10,'They are the ones who criticize it and tell it when it\'s going in the wrong direction.',26594,26610),(1433,10,'What does OG then say?',26611,26615),(1434,10,'OG says you need to compromise.',26616,26621),(1435,10,'OO\'s response is you won\'t compromise because issues are important.',26622,26631),(1436,10,'Chris\'s extension proves even if you get compromise it is bad compromise.',26632,26643),(1437,10,'We shouldn\'t be compromising with the AFD in Germany or Eric Zemmour in France because that just drives us to the far right and compromise gives you a less functional government because these coalitions are much more likely to be fractious and unstable.',26644,26686),(1438,10,'Finally when it comes to social issues they are likely to be accepted on our side when they gain large social traction and otherwise there\'s no solvency on their side.',26687,26716),(1439,10,'For all of those reasons, oppose.',26717,26722),(1440,9,'This is a debate about courts and justices and judges.',26736,26745),(1441,9,'So judges will interpret this as a sit and preside over certain appeals over mergers and acquisitions after regulators have regulated and companies sue if they think that this is unfair.',26746,26776),(1442,9,'Secondly, I think this framing is important because regulators will know that this is a legal doctrine that judges and courts will adopt, so therefore the way we approach regulated mergers and acquisitions will keep a broader scope in mind.',26777,26816),(1443,9,'For instance, they know they can get away with being able to complain that this merger acquisition has failed to conduct some environmental information surveys on how this merger acquisition would deplete energy resources or for instance, pollute in the local environment.',26817,26858),(1444,9,'This is important because our first argument is that the consumer welfare declines on the consumer welfare doctrines in the SAS rule are too narrow.',26859,26883),(1445,9,'The first thing I want to talk about here is that we\'re going to be able to essentially create companies to self-regulate before they go into the merger and acquisition process.',26884,26915),(1446,9,'So here\'s the through line, right?',26916,26921),(1447,9,'They will perceive that regulators will regulate against them if they do not give out information on things like the way they influence them like that.',26922,26947),(1448,9,'So therefore, they will preemptively have this information on hand in order to prevent things like like getting sued and stuff like that.',26948,26970),(1449,9,'The reason why this is important and the reason why they\'ll do this is threefold.',26971,26985),(1450,9,'Firstly, investors are wary, so they\'re going to be scared if this is too risky because of the media attention that will happen if they get sued for instance, other considerations.',26986,27016),(1451,9,'Secondly, legal fees are incredibly expensive to have lawyers and things like that.',27017,27029),(1452,9,'Thirdly, if you fail the first process in the district courts, you have to appeal again and appeal again and appeal again.',27030,27051),(1453,9,'These appeal phrases take a long time for you as well.',27052,27062),(1454,9,'So as a company, you want to make sure that you cover all your cases.',27063,27077),(1455,9,'Fourthly, you are acquiring another firm.',27078,27083),(1456,9,'So there\'s likely that that other firm might get cold feet if they feel as though you have not prepared enough for this process as well.',27084,27109),(1457,9,'The importance for this is because we reduce information asymmetry.',27110,27119),(1458,9,'So here\'s how I want to contextualize this.',27120,27127),(1459,9,'If a company knows that they will have the merchant acquisition locked because they have not provided information on pollution, what they will do is they\'ll go and hire consultants to make sure they conduct stuff like an environmental impact statement that reveals the total GHG emissions that will result from this merchant acquisition.',27128,27181),(1460,9,'The reason why we think this is fine is because if you have a locked capital, this means you have to have a locked capital in order to acquire another firm.',27182,27212),(1461,9,'So this is going to be calculated in your decision-calculating process.',27213,27224),(1462,9,'The reason why this information is good, firstly, I think it\'s good for the public to be aware of this information.',27225,27245),(1463,9,'That\'s to say, if you live in these communities that are going to be near these factories, near these data centers, your energy prices might increase if they come in.',27246,27275),(1464,9,'It\'s good for you to do things like have a long campaign or local campaigning for your local politicians to create ordinances against these type of companies coming in.',27276,27304),(1465,9,'But also secondly, I think it\'s really good for investors too.',27305,27315),(1466,9,'You might want to invest in this company after the merchant acquisition to know the type of things that they\'re getting into.',27316,27337),(1467,9,'Revealing all this information to the public is good for the company and for the market as a whole.',27338,27356),(1468,9,'The second argument I want to talk about is that we have more political will on our side of the house.',27357,27377),(1469,9,'I want to note that regulators do want to regulate.',27378,27387),(1470,9,'Because on their side of the house, they might think that regulators are going to do this in a bad way.',27388,27408),(1471,9,'But firstly, the reason why they do so in a bad way in the status quo is because they have too narrow a stick to adjudicate mergers and acquisitions.',27409,27437),(1472,9,'If it is the case that you can only block an M&A if they reduce the competition and prices, it is a lot easier for a company to budge the numbers, to hire people to say actually no, the competitor prices are going to decrease.',27438,27483),(1473,9,'But because we\'ve seen a proliferation of M&As in the hundreds, like 450 in the past year, the Department of Justice and the FTC currently do not have enough resources to investigate all of this.',27484,27519),(1474,9,'That\'s to say it is easy for M&As to go unnoticed.',27520,27531),(1475,9,'Secondly, they also have a repeal preference for doing this because for instance, they want to get promoted, they want to get more funding from Congress, they want to get more media attention on their agency so that they\'re able to do this.',27532,27574),(1476,9,'And thirdly, because there\'s bipartisan support.',27575,27580),(1477,9,'That\'s why for instance, a lot of people like Lisa Tan, because Lisa Tan is really harsh against tech, that\'s why we have things like conservatives, right?',27581,27607),(1478,9,'And for instance, because of this bipartisan support, we\'ve seen a lot of interest in this as a whole.',27608,27626),(1479,9,'The reason why this independently is not enough for them to block more M&As is because using this as a legal document is too low.',27627,27652),(1480,9,'The second argument I want to, the third argument I want to posit this week is that on the comparative, it\'s far worse.',27653,27675),(1481,9,'On the comparative, it is far worse to get environmental accountability and privacy accountability on their side.',27676,27692),(1482,9,'The first thing behind this is because things like privacy laws and environmental laws lag behind industry.',27693,27709),(1483,9,'So for instance, laws regarding the protection of consumer, like a welfare on data and AI, oftentimes have to catch up to the technology that\'s emerging in the South.',27710,27738),(1484,9,'Things like the environment, like EPA and stuff like that, constantly get repealed in the South.',27739,27754),(1485,9,'Like for instance, the Halliburton loophole, which allows the crafting companies to not have to dispose of wastewater properly is something that was repealed by corporate interest.',27755,27781),(1486,9,'I\'m going to prove to you why these type of regulators are not susceptible to these flaws, but before I continue, I\'ll take a few questions.',27782,27807),(1487,9,'Just still kind of explain why you think you have to solve any of these problems.',27808,27823),(1488,9,'Like why does it help with the environment?',27824,27831),(1489,9,'Why does it help with privacy?',27832,27837),(1490,9,'Why does it help with any of these things?',27838,27846),(1491,9,'So the thing that I mentioned is that it\'s a preemptive thing, right?',27847,27859),(1492,9,'So this is proactive.',27860,27863),(1493,9,'So before the M&A already occurs, they have to prove that, they have to use data studies and stuff like that to prove that there\'s going to be less environmental harm and stuff like that.',27864,27899),(1494,9,'So this happens before the M&A happens.',27900,27907),(1495,9,'And then I\'m going to prove to you why you now give courts more ability to have legal prosecution on R7,000.',27908,27929),(1496,9,'All right, so what happens on R7,000?',27930,27937),(1497,9,'What we think happens is when regulators, I don\'t know, when regulators, like in the deal chain and stuff like that, like regulating M&As, they\'re not susceptible to acts and stuff like that.',27938,27971),(1498,9,'They get to create their own doctrine when they\'re trying to do this.',27972,27984),(1499,9,'So when these regulators get sued, if other companies think that this is weak, what happens is this goes through the court process and this creates cases for future court decisions to be based on them.',27985,28020),(1500,9,'So for instance, if a utility company gets sued, then the facts of that case gets to be applied to future courts and things like that.',28021,28046),(1501,9,'The reason why this is important is because these doctrines actually give future courts legal basis to determine this information