55,8,'They often have a good amount of charisma, which is what allowed them to organically rise to the top of these revolutions in the first place.',110600,110625),(6056,8,'And there is a track record of their efforts and their success.',110626,110637),(6057,8,'And, most importantly, these leaders are also likely to run in the same party or same government to actively capitalize on the July Revolution\'s support, which is unprecedentedly large compared to any efforts they have made in the past.',110638,110676),(6058,8,'Why is this terrible, then?',110677,110681),(6059,8,'Because this actively increases the fear of further democratic backsliding.',110682,110691),(6060,8,'If you are already a person on the fence and skeptical of the revolution, when there is last-line support for these figures that cannot really be challenged in any meaningful way because the Aon government and their credibility is down the drain, you fear they become a missusina even if they never end up like that.',110692,110748),(6061,8,'This is a comparative argument. Why?',110749,110754),(6062,8,'Because if you counterfactualize, there are still going to be parties that run to appeal to these students of protesters.',110755,110774),(6063,8,'But there are going to likely be more competing parties and political figures because they cannot all unite under the silver bullet that we took down the Aon government.',110775,110803),(6064,8,'They all would have to do so much more to differentiate themselves and to make sure they are passing or they are proposing new policies.',110804,110828),(6065,8,'But also, second, these leaders face less political accountability on their side.',110829,110840),(6066,8,'The students who led the protests are, while very affable people, no, not actually infallible.',110841,110855),(6067,8,'Power tends to corrupt even when there are good intentions in some cases.',110856,110868),(6068,8,'When you give large amounts of power to a small group, they often have selfish incentives to engage in things like ethnic favoritism because you need to do that.',110869,110897),(6069,8,'You need some level of majority support to make sure that you can stay in government for a bit to pass the policies and ideals you want to pass because you generally believe ideologically these are good policies and you want to help the Bangladeshi people.',110898,110943),(6070,8,'You also sometimes might engage in corruption because you believe if there is no way out to get funds, besides actively pander to the military in some cases, even if you think that these are all done for good causes, this often leads to democratic backsliding even when intentions are not harmful.',110944,110995),(6071,8,'Often I say that no, no, but these leaders are good.',110996,111006),(6072,8,'We agree, they are probably good, but when they get into power, they oftentimes change.',111007,111021),(6073,8,'And we argue that change is likely, especially in these circumstances of large amounts of instability.',111022,111037),(6074,8,'But third, what this means is that leaders cannot be held accountable to the ballot box.',111038,111053),(6075,8,'And this is no longer that different from the old regime.',111054,111064),(6076,8,'This is terrible.',111065,111067),(6077,8,'The counterfactual is we likely think there is a multiplicity of parties and leaders are likely to contest in these elections.',111068,111088),(6078,8,'Student leaders likely support a variety of parties.',111089,111096),(6079,8,'Political competition is good. Why?',111097,111101),(6080,8,'Because you often do panders to more groups, more minorities, and so it\'s harder for you to alienate them.',111102,111120),(6081,8,'You often prevent backsliding and they hold each other accountable, which is crucial in a moment of democratic transition.',111121,111139),(6082,8,'Second argument, before that closing.',111140,111144),(6083,8,'Yeah, so if the anti-discrimination student movement doesn\'t climb, what other force has the institutional capacity to form a party?',111145,111165),(6084,8,'Or is it just going to be lots of small, independent groups of two or three, all split up, fighting against each other?',111166,111188),(6085,8,'Well, I mean, I think this is a question to be answered in a round.',111189,111203),(6086,8,'I\'m not exactly sure right now.',111204,111209),(6087,8,'Brian will do it.',111210,111213),(6088,8,'Second argument, this reduces immediate political instability.',111214,111220),(6089,8,'It is not even clear that we will get elections or when we will get elections.',111221,111236),(6090,8,'So therefore, we need stability to make sure the transitional government works in the interim because these are lives of people at stake.',111237,111259),(6091,8,'There\'s families to feed and there\'s people we need in the house.',111260,111271),(6092,8,'Why does this actively benefit the transitional government then?',111272,111280),(6093,8,'Because here\'s the comparative.',111281,111284),(6094,8,'It is far more likely they get a military coup on opposition.',111285,111296),(6095,8,'The way you should weigh this argument is that even if this happens, like 30% more likely, that is a huge risk we would not wish to take because often that leads to blood on the streets.',111297,111333),(6096,8,'Often that means that people\'s livelihoods are destroyed and you lose faith in democracy, which ends up being irreversible on their side.',111334,111355),(6097,8,'The military currently is very powerful in Bangladesh for inclusive reasons.',111356,111366),(6098,8,'They\'ve always been given huge levels of control of the Bangladeshi economy since the Cold War.',111367,111382),(6099,8,'They\'re politically influential with the connections to political parties.',111383,111391),(6100,8,'Problem, the military backs the interim government, but they have qualms with the leaders of protest movements. Why?',111392,111409),(6101,8,'They do not want the leaders of protest movements involved because the leaders specifically are people and icons and figureheads of the anti-military movement then when they were fighting in the revolution.',111410,111442),(6102,8,'When student organizers begin running for office, you view democracy as a threat to Germany\'s inequal grasp of power.',111443,111461),(6103,8,'This is a perception issue on their side.',111462,111469),(6104,8,'The counterfactual by no means is perfect on our side, but student protesters are more able to portray democracy as good for the military.',111470,111493),(6105,8,'We can better ally with the military rather than go directly against it.',111494,111506),(6106,8,'And even if it\'s not a coup on our side, they still undermine elections.',111507,111520),(6107,8,'There\'s more violence against protesters and it\'s all on that far worse.',111521,111532),(6108,8,'Very proud to be a post.',111533,111538),(6109,8,'We now welcome the leader of opposition.',111539,111545),(6110,8,'Please begin the opposition campaign.',111546,111550),(6111,8,'Explaining two simple things in opening opposition.',111551,111557),(6112,8,'First, why leadership is better.',111558,111562),(6113,8,'Secondly, why the world in which these student leaders engage outside the democratic system is far worse.',111563,111579),(6114,8,'I\'m going to prove how you get instability that OJ thinks is so important as well as better policies.',111580,111598),(6115,8,'So firstly, why don\'t we get better leadership within the system?',111599,111609),(6116,8,'So we agree with the premise of opening government that these student leaders are likely to win because they\'re all uniquely inspiring.',111610,111631),(6117,8,'Many people supported the movement in the first place in order to overcome the dictatorship, indicating a large effect of support.',111632,111652),(6118,8,'The current leader of the provincial government, Mohamed Younis, is likely to back these individuals as well.',111653,111669),(6119,8,'They\'re just generally relatable to youth and uniquely inspiring because they represented the movement that gave them democracy, that represented the fall of the dictatorship that oppressed so many individuals.',111670,111699),(6120,8,'Why is this so good?',111700,111704),(6121,8,'I have a couple of reasons why people make better leaders that are uniquely able to represent the youth and make their voices important in the political sphere in the first place.',111705,111736),(6122,8,'And the premise here directly challenges what we get in an opening government that says there will be incentives to represent the youth on either side of the house.',111737,111765),(6123,8,'If we prove that youth otherwise would be politically disengaged, this means there\'s less political incentives when we cater to them because you don\'t see them as viable voters to the upper house in the first place.',111766,111802),(6124,8,'Why is this the case?',111803,111807),(6125,8,'Firstly, youth deeply distrust the establishment not only due to decades of oppression under a dictatorship or a pseudo-dictatorship and a legacy of corruption associated with other parties like the BNP as well, but also that youth is generally associated with the state itself as a source of oppression, but also that structurally youth often have incentives not to engage in rational ignorance where you believe that your vote doesn\'t matter',111808,111879),(6126,8,'and you have less incentives therefore to be politically engaged.',111880,111889),(6127,8,'It\'s not worth it to put in the efforts to vote and to try and learn about political parties if you don\'t think your vote is going to matter in the first place.',111890,111922),(6128,8,'And even when you do consume media, it often feeds you negative narratives about, for example, why democracy is a sham, why horrible things in your country are happening.',111923,111951),(6129,8,'It\'s not worth voting because media often sensationalizes the worst things that happen because it\'s the most eye-catching.',111952,111970),(6130,8,'Which means youth that often disproportionately, who appeal to these things, often have no incentives to want to engage politically otherwise.',111971,111991),(6131,8,'So it\'s uniquely only the leaders that are presented in transition to democracy that you are going to fight to be passed or that make you engage in the political system and have some degree of trust to want to vote in the first place.',111992,112036),(6132,8,'On their side, they have no doubt or challenge.',112037,112045),(6133,8,'The second reason why youth are going to be disproportionately not politically represented is that they are economically weak as well.',112046,112066),(6134,8,'For A, this is just likely because youth are quite young, they have less time to accumulate wealth in the long term which gives you political and economic leverage.',112067,112095),(6135,8,'This time itself hurts.',112096,112099),(6136,8,'But secondly, Bangladesh is currently going through an economic transition where they are going to be relatively poor, to a more middle and upper income country which has resulted in large amounts of unemployment in the short term.',112100,112137),(6137,8,'Because as the country becomes richer, as wages rise, it means that as a country gets to transition, many people are left behind and left unemployed.',112138,112163),(6138,8,'This disproportionately harms the youth because their lack of experience and time means they are not likely to be, for example, high skilled and middle manager type people and more likely to be workers which has left many of them economically in the dust.',112164,112207),(6139,8,'This means that they have less, for example, lobbying power but also less economic power to be engaged in the system, to be able to take time off to vote as well and to do things like attend political rallies.',112208,112247),(6140,8,'Especially in the context of a post-revolution society where a lot of their will to engage and their will to protest has been exhausted as well.',112248,112274),(6141,8,'This means that in the absence of these individuals, they get no representation for the youth.',112275,112290),(6142,8,'Furthermore, I want to explain why youth leaders themselves are likely to be incredibly good.',112291,112305),(6143,8,'This is because there is likely to be a selection effect where the youth leaders are likely to be people that have strong liberal democratic values to the extent that they were willing to fight and potentially be incarcerated or die for these values in an oppressive regime in the first place which suggests a deep ideological attachment to things like democracy and the liberalization of society.',112306,112372),(6144,8,'What is the impact of all of this?',112373,112380),(6145,8,'I think it\'s first and foremost good that we have good democratic liberal values against corruption and pro-things like meritocracy.',112381,112401),(6146,8,'But also on a political policy level, it\'s good that we have demands to cater to people that have long term interests, i.e.',112402,112425),(6147,8,'the youth that are going to live for much longer than people who are not the youth.',112426,112442),(6148,8,'This is important because this demands for example a transition away from textiles and other forms of low skilled industry which is bad in the long term even if it is desirable for politicians in the short term.',112443,112480),(6149,8,'Why are these things bad in the long term?',112481,112489),(6150,8,'Things like for example textiles or other forms of manufacturing often are unsustainable as Bangladesh becomes more economically wealthy.',112490,112508),(6151,8,'As incomes rise, exports become less competitive because you have higher costs of inputs.',112509,112522),(6152,8,'This is bad because it\'s hard to outcompete other industries.',112523,112532),(6153,8,'But also things like automation and the rise of automation would otherwise force these countries like Bangladesh to pretty much really de-industrialize as well.',112533,112557),(6154,8,'Why is it the case that alternatives are bad and are going to care for these short term interests?',112558,112576),(6155,8,'First, let\'s begin by characterizing who these alternatives are.',112577,112585),(6156,8,'Firstly, it\'s likely to be for example the BNP, an old establishment party which is likely to have the resources and the legitimacy to succeed by being an opposition party to the Obama league.',112586,112619),(6157,8,'And this is a problem because they have a long history of corruption and institutionalized corruption which is likely to continue because as a leader of a party that is engaged in corruption and tax economics, they adapt to have large amounts of wealth as a source.',112620,112666),(6158,8,'But also B, are likely to be politically short termists as well.',112667,112678),(6159,8,'They\'re likely to have connections with, because as an old establishment party, you have large connections with industry, for example manufacturing or textiles, which is often the tipping point for you catering to their interests.',112679,112713),(6160,8,'Not only because they have personal connections, but because they\'re self-competing on alternative incentives.',112714,112728),(6161,8,'So for example, many other workers in society are somewhat short termists, going to keep their job in the short term because it\'s bad for the country in the long term.',112729,112759),(6162,8,'So these two aspects of the Bangladesh are often the tipping points for alternative parties like the BNP to overvalue the short term and prioritize things like textiles as opposed to adapting and investing in better long term economic plans.',112760,112799),(6163,8,'Secondly, another alternative is likely to be Islamist parties as well because Bangladesh has a large Islamist population, or Muslim population, which is likely to be incredibly oppressive towards Hindu minorities.',112800,112830),(6164,8,'This is likely to be the case because Islamist parties have used this as a primary means of differentiation as a political party against other parties, which is likely to lead to a more extremist type of rhetoric and policy stance.',112831,112871),(6165,8,'Secondly, I want to explain why it is worse for these leaders to engage outside the political system before that political movement.',112872,112893),(6166,8,'UNIS has been a powerful democratizing and anti-corruption force, even just in August, and over time has seen it with the cause of momentum, which can be channeled to and strengthen existing political institutions, which young people now, and not in the past, have desired.',112894,112939),(6167,8,'Right, but the problem is it\'s associated with these student leaders alongside UNIS, who are seen as a front for pro-democracy movements when they don\'t run.',112940,112966),(6168,8,'They\'re also likely to do things like be critical of the otherwise democratic system, where the upper parties and the counter-factionals are going to exploit now.',112967,112993),(6169,8,'In the short run, we think in a world where these people do not run, it leads to more instability, where, for example, these student leaders are more likely to do things like stop protests, because they believe these other, alternative parties are bad and anti-democratic, which they\'re also likely to be, which we proved in our previous point.',112994,113053),(6170,8,'So they do things like protest on the streets, even if it\'s not all of you, it\'s likely to be the leaders and the close followers who now are not choosing to run but still have the original passion and desire to be engaged.',113054,113097),(6171,8,'This is a problem because these types of protests, firstly, lead to things like democratic backsliding and the stress of the government that OG thinks is so harmful, but also creates instability itself, which leads to things like investor flights.',113098,113137),(6172,8,'Finally, I think the overwhelming dominance of the student party that would run on our side is good in terms of avoiding gridlock that occurs more when you have other plurality of parties on their steeping-hoes that are likely to have an overwhelming majority, and gridlock itself is often inherently bad when you have one, for example, like parties that have an incentive to sabotage the main party in a coalition',113138,113209),(6173,8,'because they want them to succeed less, they can win in the next electoral cycle, which is really harmful because you don\'t pass basic infrastructure bills that the big banks of the nation need in a closed public society.',113210,113248),(6174,8,'Thank you very much.',113249,113252),(6175,8,'Thank you very much.',113253,113256),(6176,8,'Thank you very much.',113257,113260),(6177,8,'and the protest movements who need to be represented by their lovely figure, Mugabe.',113261,113274),(6178,8,'We all know how that story ends, hence not well.',113275,113284),(6179,8,'The thesis of opening up is straightforward. Power corrupts.',113285,113293),(6180,8,'The student protesters did a good thing by toppling Hasina\'s regime.',113294,113304),(6181,8,'The best thing they can now do is to stay on the outside, to criticize, yes, to protest, as opposed to become corrupted by the political establishment.',113305,113331),(6182,8,'Three points in this speech.',113332,113336),(6183,8,'First, on youth voting.',113337,113340),(6184,8,'Second, the counterfactual.',113341,113343),(6185,8,'Finally, democratic consolidation.',113344,113346),(6186,8,'First, on youth establishment.',113347,113350),(6187,8,'This is opening opposition\'s main point.',113351,113356),(6188,8,'I have four lines of response.',113357,113362),(6189,8,'Response number one, the premise of opening opposition\'s claim is that the youth are otherwise unlikely to engage in politics.',113363,113382),(6190,8,'That\'s unlikely because student organizers don\'t disappear on our side of the house.',113383,113395),(6191,8,'They still exist, they still have charisma, they still have cults of personality, and have strong incentives to encourage youth voters to turn out and vote.',113396,113421),(6192,8,'And the reason why this is probably a message that will be received well by many individuals is for all the reasons that opening opposition themselves name.',113422,113448),(6193,8,'For example, the fact that these leaders have unique power over student protesters.',113449,113461),(6194,8,'The fact that these people have charisma.',113462,113468),(6195,8,'The fact that these individuals are seen as having accomplished a revolutionary movement.',113469,113481),(6196,8,'Which means even if there is a marginal reduction in the youth vote, it\'s not 60%.',113482,113497),(6197,8,'Rather, you\'re probably getting a high level of youth turnout on either side of the house mitigating opening opposition\'s claim.',113498,113517),(6198,8,'Response number two, political parties, most obviously the BNP, have strong structural incentives on our side of the house to appeal to our youth voters.',113518,113542),(6199,8,'And this is for three reasons.',113543,113548),(6200,8,'Reason number one, the August protest movement energized youth votes in a way that was not true historically.',113549,113566),(6201,8,'So opening opposition is right.',113567,113571),(6202,8,'Historically, young voters in Bangladesh did not participate in democratic politics.',113572,113582),(6203,8,'But the fact that they\'ve now been energized and mobilized by the most powerful moment in post-independence Bangladeshi history means that youth voters probably feel empowered to participate in democratic politics, even if their leaders do not run.',113583,113621),(6204,8,'Reason number two, political parties have strong incentives to appeal to our youth voters to deter any potential violence.',113622,113640),(6205,8,'Hint, what did student protesters do this August when political parties did not represent them?',113641,113655),(6206,8,'They turned to the streets. They rioted.',113656,113662),(6207,8,'Foreign investment plummeted, which means even if the BNP is corrupt and selfish, its corrupt and selfish interests align with appealing towards youth voters to ensure there is not a second July revolution.',113663,113695),(6208,8,'Reason number three, on our side of the house, we have durable fiat to assume these leaders do not suddenly form a political party.',113696,113719),(6209,8,'But if you\'re the BNP, in the next 5, 10, 15 years, you\'re always concerned that if you do not appeal sufficiently well to youth voters, these guys might come out of retirement and actually run against you, which means the theoretical potential that these organizers could potentially run in elections is a powerful preemptive deterrent against neglecting or ignoring the youth vote.',113720,113782),(6210,8,'Response number three to opening opposition, we\'ll flip their claim.',113783,113792),(6211,8,'Youth distrust will be worse on their side of the house.',113793,113803),(6212,8,'This is a argument that applies to the longer term prospect, not the immediate term.',113804,113818),(6213,8,'Both teams agree in the short term that people who participate in the protests vote overwhelmingly for these figures.',113819,113837),(6214,8,'But what happens in the long term?',113838,113844),(6215,8,'Student protesters are likely to feel enormously disillusioned with Bangladeshi politics.',113845,113855),(6216,8,'Why is that?',113856,113858),(6217,8,'Because when these people become political operators, when they run parties, when they\'re in government, they have to make decisions in trade-offs that protesters feel violated the principles they pushed for.',113859,113890),(6218,8,'These people have to accept campaign contributions from corporations.',113891,113899),(6219,8,'They have to engage in political horse trading to overcome the gridlock opening opposition describes.',113900,113914),(6220,8,'Here\'s the problem.',113915,113917),(6221,8,'When the person that you saw leading the revolution is now engaging in the very same kind of political shenanigans that you opposed and you fought against, that is when you are much more likely on their side of the house to feel personally attacked.',113918,113962),(6222,8,'Now, to be clear, on both sides of the house, some political party has to engage in horse trading, campaign contributions, et cetera.',113963,113985),(6223,8,'It\'s better when that\'s a mainstream political party like the BNP, as opposed to the figureheads of the movement violating their trust, which flips their claim on engagement long term and also increases the probability of protests on the streets.',113986,114025),(6224,8,'This comprehensively flips opening opposition\'s claim and mitigates it to the point they cannot win on this argument.',114026,114043),(6225,8,'Point number two, what is the counterfactual?',114044,114050),(6226,8,'Opening opposition says that the likely counterfactual is the Bangladesh Nationalist Party. We agree.',114051,114064),(6227,8,'There\'s three reasons.',114065,114067),(6228,8,'First, this party has existing infrastructure to defend the main opposition movement.',114068,114079),(6229,8,'Since its rise to power in 2009, they\'ve been the main secondary party in Bangladesh since the 80s.',114080,114097),(6230,8,'Reason number two, the AL is overwhelmingly unlikely to win votes in the upcoming election.',114098,114112),(6231,8,'Why is that?',114113,114115),(6232,8,'They\'ve been enormously discredited.',114116,114119),(6233,8,'Their leader literally fled to, I think, India.',114120,114127),(6234,8,'The point is that the AL is at an all-time low, which means the BNP has enormous capacity to win the election because they have existing infrastructure.',114128,114155),(6235,8,'Reason number three, just empirically, polling shows that the BNP is probably going to be the largest party, whatever the next election happens to be.',114156,114180),(6236,8,'Now, opening opposition\'s claim here is twofold.',114181,114187),(6237,8,'First, they say that radical Islamist parties gain power.',114188,114196),(6238,8,'Second, they say the BNP is bad.',114197,114203),(6239,8,'Let\'s work through both.',114204,114207),(6240,8,'Their first claim on radical Islamist parties doesn\'t work for three reasons.',114208,114219),(6241,8,'Reason number one, there\'s a huge problem with this argument, which is they\'re right.',114220,114233),(6242,8,'Islamist parties can get some power.',114234,114239),(6243,8,'But the groups that are voting for Islamist parties would never be the student protesters on our side of the house.',114240,114260),(6244,8,'The reason why is that the vast majority of people involved in the student protest movement were liberal, democratic, or university students.',114261,114282),(6245,8,'They are overwhelmingly secular or not highly religious, which means if the organizers of the protests don\'t run for office, then the people who would have voted for them are not suddenly turning to radical Islamist parties because they do not believe that is a legitimate conception of democracy.',114283,114331),(6246,8,'Therefore, the likely alternative is they move towards secular parties, like, for example, the BNP.',114332,114346),(6247,8,'Response number two, it\'s unlikely that Islamist parties gain substantial power because there\'s a game theoretic disincentive to vote for these parties.',114347,114368),(6248,8,'The reason why is that you know that the percentage of votes that goes to any individual Islamist party is low, especially because there are multiple of them and they split the vote.',114369,114401),(6249,8,'So you as a voter have a game theoretic incentive to vote for a secular party that\'s likely to get a greater share of votes because that\'s more likely to affect the outcome of an election. See ya.',114402,114439),(6250,8,'Why are you drawing a distinction between, why are you saying that the BNP is the secular party?',114440,114457),(6251,8,'They\'re clearly the non