ount of time, so they depend on them for their resources.',116343,116361),(6362,8,'They owe them favors.',116362,116365),(6363,8,'They have family members appointed to top positions in a bunch of textile industries that didn\'t want the Iwami League to win.',116366,116387),(6364,8,'Why does this matter?',116388,116391),(6365,8,'Because Bangladesh needs to stop industrial policy favoring textile industries.',116392,116401),(6366,8,'As wages rise in Bangladesh, it can no longer compete with countries like Ethiopia on textiles.',116402,116417),(6367,8,'Plus, Bangladesh is in the midst of an emerging market crisis and needs to transition up the value chain in order to grow, and this growth is necessary to do things that create youth unemployment.',116418,116452),(6368,8,'Even if the BNP cares about the youth, it cares about the funding it gets from corporate backers which it can use to win way more votes than just the votes of the youth.',116453,116486),(6369,8,'This means the BNP will be structurally politically corrupt when we take stock on opening opposition.',116487,116502),(6370,8,'And we now welcome the member of government to begin her remarks. Thank you.',116503,116516),(6371,8,'We provide a variety of reasons to believe that even if students are uncorruptible, immoral, politically accountable, they will be ineffectual and they will cause the greatest degree of harm in this day.',116517,116549),(6372,8,'I\'m gonna start by positioning this extension against the government.',116550,116559),(6373,8,'Open government, I think, assumes that students are very likely to win when they explain that young people will be almost automatically behind them.',116560,116583),(6374,8,'But then they also explain that there are a series of perception issues that might block young people out of power, which I think kind of mitigates the harms that that can trigger.',116584,116616),(6375,8,'I think then, subsequently, it provides a series of good potential risks that could occur where students are, for instance, corrupt or overly radical, but I think this potentially requires a relatively uncharitable view of the incentives of these students, who I think, to their credit, are pretty pro-democracy, are pretty enlivened, and subject to kind of counter-billing incentives that both are queen\'s names.',116617,116682),(6376,8,'So, we will make a more modest, certain, and internally consistent claim.',116683,116694),(6377,8,'Regardless of the virtues of these students, if they pursue their demands, it will damage the legacy of the revolution and its touched political program, which is bad for our national law.',116695,116726),(6378,8,'I want to explain is that the perception of these students is cynical.',116727,116739),(6379,8,'And open composition points to generic reasons why people are suspicious of politicians, which I think are true, but I think there are some more specific reasons why people are likely to be particularly skeptical of students.',116740,116776),(6380,8,'Crucially, I think youth and radicalism is something that is viewed more fondly without politics than within, which is just to say that people understand things like youth and radicalism as virtues in many instances, but they also understand, for instance, that they were far more soridid in their youth, that their views change over time, that young people are still developing and do not always have broad ideological views,',116777,116846),(6381,8,'which I think is, in general, pretty bad in terms of young people getting elected, in terms of them attacking older people\'s votes.',116847,116869),(6382,8,'But I would additionally note that it poses a barrier to collaboration with older people in politics.',116870,116886),(6383,8,'Like, I think it is quite likely that even probably pretty good politicians who are older, who are more experienced, are pretty skeptical of these young people, making it far more difficult for them to achieve their aims.',116887,116924),(6384,8,'The second thing I would note is that the specific demands, not all of them, but some of them, made by these young people are both unachievable and undesirable.',116925,116953),(6385,8,'And I think this is probably a better explanation of the problems of these young people than open government, who I think assumes that they\'ll do things that are particularly corrupt and particularly immoral.',116954,116987),(6386,8,'But we\'re just going to explain that if they pursue the political program outlined in their 9-point plan, that is bad.',116988,117009),(6387,8,'What is their political program?',117010,117014),(6388,8,'A couple of things.',117015,117018),(6389,8,'Firstly, they want to fire a tract of police, commissioners and superintendents who are involved in student casualties.',117019,117036),(6390,8,'Secondly, they want to fire a tract of vice chancellors of universities all across Bangladesh.',117037,117051),(6391,8,'And thirdly, they want to remove security personnel at large from university campuses in Bangladesh.',117052,117066),(6392,8,'The first thing I\'m going to do here is explain why those political positions are likely inflexible, and then I will explain why they are bad.',117067,117092),(6393,8,'The importance of explaining why they\'re inflexible is it shows why you would sort of, no thank you, will not be strategic and adopt particular positions in order to avoid this backlash that I\'ll explain later.',117093,117128),(6394,8,'Why are they inflexible?',117129,117132),(6395,8,'Well firstly, I would note that these things are highly public, highly published, they\'re in the 9-point plans, which I think makes them relatively difficult to walk back, because they are a stated ideological commitment of these young people.',117133,117172),(6396,8,'But secondly, I think that they likely believe them, both ideologically, but also incredibly emotionally, because fundamentally these sorts of demands represent revenge on the kinds of people whose students viewed as being particularly punitive or particularly immoral.',117173,117210),(6397,8,'That, I think, additionally means that this is likely an expectation of their peers in the movement, making it socially very difficult to avoid.',117211,117234),(6398,8,'Additionally though, I would note that the most radical young people are likely to be at the forefront of politics.',117235,117254),(6399,8,'Those are the people with potentially the most charisma, those are the people with the strongest belief that pushes them into political roles relative to more moderate young people.',117255,117283),(6400,8,'Finally, I would note that they probably have a pretty rational desire to disempower political enemies like vice chancellors, like police, but I\'m going to explain now why that is probably a very bad thing.',117284,117318),(6401,8,'So, young people enter politics with a list of demands about disempowering people like vice chancellors, like police.',117319,117336),(6402,8,'This, I think, is highly unachievable.',117337,117342),(6403,8,'I think that this is a set of people who are obviously incredibly empowered in society, they have access to incredibly important institutions like universities, like police, and they are important, like obviously it is important to have police in Bangladesh, and that means I think that they are particularly powerful enemies.',117343,117394),(6404,8,'And this provides to you, I think, a far more specific reason why I think that who\'s all more moderately institutional resistance is inevitable, because the set of people specifically who these young people have upset are the ones who their political programs irritate, who are the ones who their political programs continue to provoke.',117395,117449),(6405,8,'But I would also note that these are numerous enemies, like we\'re talking not just about powerful people, but about like hundreds, probably thousands of police.',117450,117475),(6406,8,'These are people who are going to be motivated against these students.',117476,117487),(6407,8,'It also alienates you from potential political allies, because it\'s pretty unlikely that, for instance, the BNP is particularly keen on like mass firing security from university campuses.',117488,117515),(6408,8,'I think a lot of adults are probably pretty like, they think security is a good thing to have at university in some level.',117516,117539),(6409,8,'I\'ll take you in a second.',117540,117545),(6410,8,'All of that, I think, means that young people are likely to suffer in terms of getting traction, in achieving their political goals.',117546,117568),(6411,8,'They\'re likely to be ineffective, and that, I think, is something which is far more likely to lead to problems with youth engagement described by both opening teams.',117569,117596),(6412,8,'I\'ll take that opinion now.',117597,117601),(6413,8,'If the impact is a coup, then there\'s still a coup.',117602,117612),(6414,8,'But if they attempt to pursue an anti-dressalist policy, if the impact is a coup, and just they lose political support, they lose the next election, they will never find a new policy.',117613,117646),(6415,8,'I already explained why they\'re likely to be euphristic, why they\'re likely to be strident and inflexible.',117647,117663),(6416,8,'But I also just explained why there are a series of less-high things about a coup that are still bad.',117664,117684),(6417,8,'Things like institutions refusing to cooperate with you, things like potential political allies refusing alliances with you.',117685,117701),(6418,8,'The next thing I want to explain is that formal politics uniquely leads to division and confiding, which is bad.',117702,117721),(6419,8,'And I don\'t know, I think this is actually kind of conceded by everything Oxford does, who says, well, it\'s fine if they become a bit corrupt because they can just condemn their peers.',117722,117755),(6420,8,'Formal politics is more divisive than civil society, firstly, because you are required to take far more 